{
  "raw": {
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195093": {
      "title": "Director of the School of Interior Architecture - UT College of Architecture and Design - Start date: July 1, 2027",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: College of Architecture and Design Location: Knoxville, TN The University of Tennessee, Knoxville invites applications and nominations for Director of the School of Interior Architecture, a bold intellectual and organizational leader ready to guide a talented, ambitious community of faculty, students, and staff at a pivotal moment of growth. The director sets the school's strategic and intellectual direction, embracing a plurality of approaches to teaching, research, and practice while synthesizing the discipline's most pressing forces, including technological change, environmental and social justice, visualization, and shifting global economies, into a coherent vision for the field's future. Beyond intellectual leadership, the role demands a fiscal and organizational acumen; managing budget, advocating for the school internally and externally, fostering collaboration across the college's schools of architecture, design, and landscape architecture, and connecting daily decisions to long-term ambition. The director should understand the shared mission among the varied constituencies of the college and the school and should be able to listen and communicate sensibly to a wide range of faculty, administrators, students, alumni, and others. The position offers a competitive salary and benefits package commensurate with the successful candidate’s qualifications and experience, as part of a 12-month full-time appointment at the rank of full professor. As a faculty member, the director is expected to teach and maintain knowledge at the leading edge of the field. The successful candidate will serve a renewable 5-year appointment as director subject to annual performance reviews from the dean of the College of Architecture and Design. The positions preferred start date is July 1, 2027. ABOUT THE SCHOOL The School of Interior Architecture offers a Bachelor of Science in Interior Architecture within a highly competitive professional program. Faculty, students and staff in our school advance the discipline by situating human-centered, spatially driven interior architecture, maker culture and theories of interiority at the forefront of research, creative practice, scholarship, and teaching. The curriculum is uniquely created to encourage cross-disciplinary studies and collaborations within the university as well as the community and to prepare students for professional practice. Recent national and international student and faculty awards distinguish the school, including Brinkman Scholarships, Donghia Scholarships, Graham Foundation Grants, and IDEC and IIDA Faculty Awards. The school currently has more than 200 students and 16 full- and part-time faculty and is supported by one dedicated administrative assistant and 25 other staff members across the college. Admission targets bringing in 56 freshmen annually with a goal of maintaining four cohorts of roughly that size. The school has attracted over 550 freshmen applications in each of the last four years from both within the state of Tennessee and nationally. Generous state scholarship programs make education accessible to students from a broad range of backgrounds and income levels. Over 36% of our students are from out of state or international. The school is in the College of Architecture and Design alongside the Schools of Architecture, Design, and Landscape Architecture. We also share a building with the School of Art. As such, the position offers rich opportunities for cross-disciplinary partnerships and collegiality. ABOUT THE COLLEGE AND UNIVERSITY The College of Architecture and Design is comprised of 850 students, 57 full-time faculty, 26 staff organized across four schools—architecture, design, interior architecture, landscape architecture. Faculty deliver curriculum in three main facilities: the 160,000 square-foot Art+Architecture Building, in the center of UT Knoxville’s campus, the 27,000 square-foot Southern Depot and the 20,000 square-foot Fab Lab, an advanced maker space. Both the Southern Depot and the Fab Lab are in downtown Knoxville. The “A+A” is an award-winning building which features open studios, a four-story atrium, the Ewing Gallery, a woodshop, review spaces, and lecture halls. The Southern Depot is an historic adaptation of a train station comprised of two buildings built in the early 1900s, and houses studios and instruction spaces as well as faculty offices and administrative space. Our Fab Lab featuring advanced machinery for digital and robotic fabrication, allows our faculty and students to integrate state-of-the-art fabrication technology into design education and research. The University of Tennessee, Knoxville is a public land-grant Research-1 university and the flagship campus of the University of Tennessee system. There are just over 41,000 students and 2,200 instructional faculty across its 14 degree-granted colleges and over 900 programs of study. ABOUT THE AREA Known as ‘The Maker City,’ Knoxville is the third largest city in the state with a metropolitan statistical area population of over 960,000 people. The region is a hub for research and design with a focus on environmental research, urban studies, and advanced technologies such as additive manufacturing technology and advanced fiber composites. East Tennessee benefits from collaborative synergies with Oak Ridge National Laboratory, Tennessee Valley Authority, and the Great Smoky Mountains National Park. Downtown Knoxville boasts an effervescent nightlife with theater, film, art, music, and well recognized events and festivals like Big Ears, a vibrant live music scene across all genres, excellent restaurants, heritage celebration events, farmers’ markets, and community gardens. Unique and walkable residential and business districts include Knoxville’s historic Market Square and Old City, North and South Waterfronts, and the Urban Wilderness recreational area. Knoxville and the surrounding region also benefit from the energy and advocacy of civic, business, religious, neighborhood, and community organizations invested in local quality of life. Within a fifteen-minute commute of campus, Knoxville offers a diverse range of housing from downtown lofts to homes in historic tree-lined neighborhoods. The city and county also offer strong public and private K-12 educational options including public international baccalaureate, art and science magnet programs, as well as private, religious, and Montessori schools. Knoxville’s neighborhoods also benefit from easy access to parks, multi-use trail systems, and the Urban Wilderness. The nearby Great Smoky Mountains National Park and TVA reservoir-lakes provide stellar outdoor recreation including biking, water sports, climbing, and hiking. Overall, the University, Knoxville, and the School's existing national and international connections, Knoxville’s position at the intersection of Appalachia, the South, and post-industrial America, together with its traditions of making and distinct cultural identity and the interdisciplinary resources of an R1 university, makes UTK a compelling place to teach, conduct research, and continue building the program. REQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS An established Associate or Full Professor. A Master’s degree or higher in Interior Architecture, Interior Design, Architecture, or allied discipline. A record of leadership. Experience as an accomplished supervisor and/or mentor. Teaching with an emphasis on interior architecture or interior design. Eligible for tenure at the rank of Full Professor. DESIRED/PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS ON LEADERSHIP Demonstrated record of visionary, strategic, and creative leadership. Demonstrated skills in communications and active listening in collaborating with diverse constituencies, such as campus community, professional community, local community, alumni, and other stakeholders. Interpersonal skills that indicate a high level of professionalism, initiative, resourcefulness, and personal accountability. Demonstrated understanding of the research, instructional, service, and engagement demands of a Research-1, land-grant university. Established experience with fiscal management, facilitation, and negotiation including fundraising and a forward-thinking approach to growth and management. Demonstrated record of identifying and partnering with professional and industry partners who advance curricular and research initiatives and strategic goals. ON RESEARCH, CREATIVE ACCOMPLISHMENTS, DESIGN, AND EXCELLENCE A portfolio of research, scholarship, creative work, engagement, and/or practice that demonstrates the ability to pursue self-directed intellectual objectives. Accomplishments demonstrating a clear understanding of undergraduate design education in support of excellent studio-based design instruction. Practice experience with an emphasis on interior architecture or interior design. Demonstrated support for traditional and emergent technologies and the development of innovative, future-oriented curricula. Demonstrated support for a plurality of approaches to research, scholarship, creative work, and engagement, and experience in supporting faculty to realize those diverse research objectives. Established record of working across academic or disciplinary design units and programs. NCIDQ Certification. ON TEACHING The ability to instruct undergraduate interior architecture studios. The ability to integrate digital and analog skills in the design studio and classroom. SUBMISSION MATERIALS Please submit the following application materials in the form of a PDF Statement of Interest that summarizes the candidate’s accomplishments and articulates a clear vision for our school that pertains to research, scholarship, creative work; teaching; service Current curriculum vitae Additional materials will be requested for candidates under consideration. Initial review of applications will begin on December 1, 2026, with continued review until the position is filled. For questions, please contact Search Committee Chairperson, Courtney Childers: childers@utk.edu."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194222": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Socio-Cultural Anthropology - Fall 2027",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: College of Arts & Science: Division of Social Sciences: Anthropology Location: UTK The Department of Anthropology at the University of Tennessee, Knoxville invites applicants for a full-time tenure-track position in Socio-Cultural Anthropology at the rank of Assistant Professor beginning August 1, 2027. The position will focus on food sovereignty in relation to indigenous or other marginalized communities.  The University is engaged in a next-level initiative aimed both at substantially elevating the University’s national reputation for impactful research, scholarship, and creative activity and at assembling a roster of faculty committed to delivering one of the nation’s best educational experiences for undergraduate and graduate students. We seek to hire a scholar with broad training in socio-cultural anthropology or closely related disciplines with a strong background in ethnographic methods and community engagement. While regional expertise is open, we seek a scholar whose regional focus complements those of existing faculty. This position is part of a new College-wide faculty hiring constellation intended to bring together tenure-line faculty members whose research, scholarship, and creative activity can help bring new, interdisciplinary perspectives to the area of Societal Resilience to Environmental Hazards . Candidates who can contribute to this interdisciplinary constellation will be preferred. The Department of Anthropology at the University of Tennessee, Knoxville is a three-field department that includes cultural anthropology, anthropological archaeology, and biological anthropology. The Department of Anthropology hosts the Disaster, Displacement and Human Rights Program and collaborates with interdisciplinary programs on campus like Native American and Indigenous Studies, Latin American, Caribbean and Latinx Studies, and Africana Studies. We are seeking a scholar who strengthens the department’s socio-cultural anthropology subfield and can offer courses of relevance to students across the department and university. The successful candidate will be expected to teach two courses per semester, maintain a robust research profile, and contribute to departmental and university service. An earned doctorate in Socio-Cultural Anthropology or a closely related field must be in hand by the time of appointment. Required Qualifications Ph.D. in Cultural Anthropology, Social Anthropology, Socio-Cultural Anthropology, or a closely related field. Ph.D. in hand before the start date of August 1, 2027. Use of ethnographic field methods for research. Research focus on food sovereignty and/or indigeneity. Ability to teach upper-division undergraduate and graduate courses in cultural anthropology, specifically in ethnographic methods and theory. Preferred Qualifications Ability to teach courses focused on the geographical area of their expertise. Ability to teach courses pertinent to themes such as vulnerability and resilience in the context of environmental hazards, climate change impacts on vulnerable communities, environmental justice and indigenous societies. Applicants should submit a cover letter that addresses relevant research and teaching experience, a CV, research statement, and the names and contact information of three referees. Letters of reference, sample syllabi, and a statement of teaching philosophy will be requested at a later stage. Please submit application materials via Interfolio at https://apply.interfolio.com/194222 .  Review of applications will begin on November 15, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. Preference will be given to applications received before the November 15 deadline. Direct any questions to Dr. Arsalan Khan, Search Committee Chair, at akhan53@utk.edu . About the University of Tennessee, Knoxville The new hire will be joining an active faculty at the University of Tennessee – Knoxville, a research-intensive university with more than 31,000 undergraduate and 7,000 graduate students. The city of Knoxville was recently named by Trip Advisor as a “Top Ten Destination on the Rise,” boasting an attractive and walkable downtown that hosts celebrated restaurants, shopping venues, and year-round cultural events and community-gathering activities. The Knoxville metropolitan area has a population of nearly 700,000 and is within easy driving distance to Atlanta, Nashville, Asheville, Cherokee, Cincinnati, and the Great Smoky Mountains National Park."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192058": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Computational Biology, Biochemistry & Cellular and Molecular Biology, Fall 2027",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: College of Arts & Sciences: Division of Natural Sciences & Mathematics: Biochem/Cellular & Molecular Biology Location: University of Tennessee-Knoxville Successful candidates will be expected to develop an innovative, nationally recognized, and externally funded research program that uses bioinformatics, computation, and/or modeling to address questions at the cutting edge of genomics, molecular, cellular, or systems biology.  We are particularly interested in applicants that build on emerging areas in BCMB ( https://bcmb.utk.edu ), including nucleus organization, epigenetics, and gene regulation. The BCMB department spans many sub-disciplines, with existing areas of strength in molecular genetics, biophysics, and biochemistry, as well as cell, neuro-, cancer, and plant biology. BCMB is a dynamic, interdisciplinary, and collegial department. In order to best support these priorities and business needs, the anticipated start date for this role is August 1, 2027 . This position offers office and dry-lab research space in the modern Ken and Blaire Mossman Building, which houses high-level research and instructional space. Shared high-performance computing resources and data storage solutions are available through the university. While no wet lab facilities will be dedicated to this hire, extensive opportunities exist for collaboration with experimental colleagues who use shared, next generation sequencing, microscopy, and bioanalytical facilities. The successful candidate will have opportunities to participate in communities of scholars in membrane biology and inflammation, and a convergent research initiative in targeted radiotherapeutics. Interactions with nearby Oak Ridge National Lab are fostered by the UT-Oak Ridge Innovation Institute, including a graduate program in Genome Science and Technology. BCMB faculty also collaborate with colleagues at the UT Ag-Vet campus, the UT Medical Center, and the National Institute for Modeling Biological Systems. The University is engaged in a next-level initiative aimed both at substantially elevating the University's national reputation for impactful research, scholarship, and creative activity and at assembling a roster of faculty committed to delivering one of the nation's best educational experiences for undergraduate and graduate students. Applicants must have a doctorate (Ph.D. or equivalent) and postdoctoral experience in an appropriate discipline with evidence of high-quality research. The successful candidate will also show potential to contribute to teaching within the core departmental curriculum at the undergraduate and graduate levels. Teaching experience and experience applying for external funding are preferred. Applicants are to submit application materials to apply.interfolio.com/192058 . Applications will require (i) a cover letter, (ii) a curriculum vitae with a complete list of publications, (iii) a 2–3-page statement of current and future research interests, (iv) a 1-page statement of teaching experience and interests, and (v) names and contact information of three references (Note: letters will be requested later and should not be uploaded at this time). The review of applications will begin on October 1. Please direct any inquiries regarding the posting to Dr. Rachel Patton McCord, committee chair, at rmccord@utk.edu ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191908": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Chemistry, Fall 2027 (Materials Discipline)",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: College of Arts & Sciences: Division of Natural Sciences & Mathematics: Chemistry Location: Knoxville, TN The Department of Chemistry at the University of Tennessee, Knoxville (UTK; https://chem.utk.edu/) invites applications for a tenure-track faculty position at the rank of Assistant Professor of Chemistry in Materials Chemistry.  The anticipated start date for the position is August 1, 2027. The successful candidate will have established an exemplary record of research that includes significant contributions to the candidate’s field of study and that is indicative of future promise.  These qualities can be demonstrated through publications in peer-reviewed journals, citation statistics, invited and contributed talks, evidence of leadership roles, and the candidate’s Statement of Research.  The successful candidate will be expected to establish a nationally recognized, externally-funded research program in the Department and provide training to undergraduate and graduate students. The successful candidate will also be expected to contribute to the teaching mission for the Department.  We are looking for candidates committed to effective undergraduate and graduate teaching, as well as, to innovation in the development of curricula and teaching methods.  These qualities are demonstrated through the candidate’s pedagogical experience as articulated in the  Statement of Teaching. The Knoxville campus of the University of Tennessee is seeking candidates who have the ability to contribute in meaningful ways to the goals of the University.  The University is engaged in a next-level initiative aimed both at substantially elevating the University's national reputation for impactful research, scholarship, and creative activity and at assembling a roster of faculty committed to delivering one of the nation's best educational experiences for undergraduates and graduate students. UTK is the flagship campus of the University of Tennessee system.  Faculty in the Department of Chemistry have the opportunity to work alongside and collaborate with researchers across our campuses, as well as at the nearby Oak Ridge National Laboratory (ORNL) and Y-12 National Security Complex.  The City of Knoxville has a beautiful and walkable downtown, varied nightlife, active neighborhoods, and eclectic shopping and restaurants.  Knoxville and UTK are located within easy driving distance to Nashville, Atlanta, and the Great Smoky Mountains. While experimental Materials Chemistry is broadly defined, experimental chemists with interests in solid state, quantum, and soft materials are strongly encouraged to apply. Appointment at the rank of Assistant Professor requires a completed Ph.D. degree in chemistry at time of application, and at least one full year of postdoctoral research experience is required. All candidates should submit a single pdf document containing the following: (1) Cover letter addressing why this position is of interest; (2) Curriculum vitae including, at the end, names and contact information (institutions, phones, emails, and websites if available) of at least three references; (3) Statement of research plans with a proposed startup budget and budget justifications (maximum 8 pages including references); (4) Brief statement of teaching philosophy (maximum 2 pages). Applicants should apply at http://apply.interfolio.com/191908 . They should also arrange for three letters of recommendation to be sent to this site. Review of applications will begin on October 5, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled.  Search Chair, Bin Zhao, bzhao@utk.edu. This institution is using Interfolio's Faculty Search to conduct this search. Applicants to this position receive a free Dossier account and can send all application materials, including confidential letters of recommendation, free of charge."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/173153": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor (Microbial Drivers of Chronic Disease), Department of Microbiology, Fall 2026",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: College of Arts & Sciences: Division of Natural Sciences & Mathematics: Microbiology Location: UTK The Department of Microbiology at the University of Tennessee is seeking candidates for a tenure-track faculty appointment at the Assistant Professor level studying microbial drivers of inflammation and chronic disease. Specific areas of interest include, but are not limited to microbial drivers of cancer, autoimmune diseases, and metabolic disorders. Research programs can be focused on either the microbial or host-response in the context of inflammatory disease. Our faculty ( http://micro.utk.edu/ ) have broad interests in microbial pathogenesis and microbial ecology. Candidates with experimental approaches that complement or strengthen any of these areas will be given full consideration. The Department of Microbiology is an active participant and leader in the Community of Scholars on Immunology and Inflammation (CoSI2), Community of Scholars on Microbial Consortia (CoSMiC), and Biomembranes Community of Scholars at UTK, which brings together engineering, chemistry, veterinary, and material sciences, UT Institute of Agriculture (UTIA), Oak Ridge National Lab (ORNL), and the University of Tennessee Medical Center (UTMC) in Knoxville. Faculty have access to multiple core facilities on campus including animal facility, genomics core, imaging core, mass-spectrometry core, flow cytometers and cell sorters ( https://research.utk.edu/oried/core-facilities/ ). UTK is the flagship university of the University of Tennessee System and is designated as a Carnegie ‘Very High Research Activity’ University. A competitive 9-month salary and generous start-up package, as well as laboratory space in a state-of-the-art building are available. The Department of Microbiology values the quality of life of all its members and is committed to promoting an atmosphere where all members of the university community feel welcome and can thrive.  Located in the foothills of the Smoky Mountains, The University of Tennessee Knoxville was founded in 1794 and is one of the oldest public universities in the nation. There are over 40,000 students enrolled at the Knoxville campus, who train and study in a city where excellence in academia meets the natural beauty of the southern Appalachian region and in the company of the artists, makers, and entrepreneurs that call Knoxville their home. The University is beginning a next-level initiative aimed both at substantially elevating the University's national reputation for impactful research, scholarship, and creative activity and at assembling a roster of faculty committed to delivering one of the nation's best educational experiences for undergraduate and graduate students. The minimum requirement is a PhD in a related field and at least two years of postdoctoral experience, including academic, research, government, and industry settings. The successful candidate is expected to establish an innovative, externally funded research program and to contribute to the departmental teaching mission at the undergraduate and graduate levels. The successful candidate is anticipated to start this position August 1, 2026. Interested applicants should submit a (i) a cover letter; (ii) CV; (iii) contact information for at least three references; (iv) research statement describing accomplishments and future plans (not to exceed 3 pages total, excluding references); (v) teaching philosophy (not to exceed 1 page); and (vi) two papers that best represents the applicant’s contribution to science/society along with a brief statement (1 paragraph/paper) of why these publications were chosen. Application materials must be submitted to Interfolio at: https://apply.interfolio.com/173153 Review of applications will begin on November 21, 2025 and will continue until the position is filled. For questions, contact MicrobeInflammationSearch@utk.edu"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193391": {
      "title": "Teaching Assistant Professor or Lecturer of Violin",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: Natalie L. Haslam College of Music Location: Knoxville, TN Appointment Type: 9-month, full-time, non-tenure-track appointment beginning Fall Semester, August 1, 2027 Salary & Benefits: Competitive and commensurate with rank and experience, includes competitive fringe benefits Responsibilities Studio Instruction: Teach applied violin at the undergraduate and graduate levels, and maintain a strong, active violin studio Chamber Music: Teach chamber music at the undergraduate and graduate levels, and coordinate undergraduate and graduate chamber music ensembles. Courses: Teach courses in string pedagogy and string literature, as assigned. Recruitment: Conduct active and ongoing recruitment activities to attract, build, and maintain a high-quality violin studio. Maintain/build a national profile as a pedagogue and performer. Required Qualifications Degree: Master’s degree (minimum requirement). Professional Profile: Demonstrate ability or commitment to maintaining a national presence as a performer. Preferred Qualifications Education: Doctorate or equivalent professional profile preferred Teaching Experience: Demonstrated success in college-level violin teaching Literature & Pedagogy: Extensive knowledge of string/violin literature and pedagogy methods, trends, and practices. Repertoire: Strong understanding of diverse solo and chamber music repertoires. Professional Skills: Excellent organizational and communication skills, with an interest in or experience with interdisciplinary collaboration and community outreach activities. Candidates for the rank of Teaching Assistant Professor must meet the qualifications for being a Teaching Assistant Professor within UTK's faculty handbook and the NLH College of Music bylaws; among these qualifications include holding a doctorate in music by the time of appointment and showing promise for excellence in areas of responsibility assigned to them. Candidates for the rank of Lecturer must meet the qualifications for being a Lecturer within UTK's faculty handbook and the NLH College of Music bylaws; among these qualifications include holding a degree appropriate to their discipline by the time of appointment and showing promise for excellence in areas of responsibility assigned to them Review of applications will begin on October 1, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. Applications received after this date may not receive full consideration. Applicants should submit a CV, cover letter, 3 references, and links to recordings of recent performances. Send any questions about the position to ahuntoon@utk.edu."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193705": {
      "title": "Assistant or Associate Professor of Bassoon and Chamber Music",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: Natalie L. Haslam College of Music Location: Knoxville, TN Assistant or Associate Professor; nine-month tenure-track appointment beginning August 1, 2027. Salary and fringe benefits are competitive and commensurate with rank and experience. The Knoxville campus of the University of Tennessee seeks candidates who have the ability to promote an atmosphere where all members of the university community feel welcome and can thrive. The responsibilities of this faculty member include teaching bassoon to undergraduate and graduate students through applied lessons and studio classes, as well as teaching the bassoon component of the Double Reed Methods course within the music education curriculum. In addition, the faculty member will coach chamber ensembles and collaborate closely with the Woodwind Area on student recitals, hearings, proficiencies, juries, and the Woodwind Literature and Pedagogy course. The successful candidate will be responsible for the effective recruitment of quality bassoon majors and high rates of student retention at the undergraduate and graduate level. The candidate will also collaborate closely with university band and orchestra directors to support placement, development, and performance of bassoon students within large ensembles. Additionally, this faculty member will also provide leadership for the Natalie L. Haslam College of Music’s chamber music program by developing a sustainable organizational and curricular infrastructure that supports and advances chamber music throughout the College. Responsibilities will include coordinating chamber music activities across areas; working collaboratively with faculty; integrating community outreach and student recruitment initiatives through chamber music; and advancing innovative opportunities for cross-area collaboration, performances, residencies, and related programming. The successful candidate will be highly organized and collaborative, possess significant experience in chamber music, and demonstrate proficiency and adaptability with administrative processes and technology. They will also bring the vision, initiative, and creativity necessary to develop an innovative, dynamic, and sustainable chamber music program that strengthens artistic collaboration, enriches the student experience, and expands the College’s engagement with its broader community. The candidate will be expected to maintain a record of national and international creative/scholarly activity and provide service contributions appropriate for a university faculty member. Required Qualifications 1) Doctorate in the area of specialty required by time of appointment or equivalent professional experience 2) Evidence of successful teaching, recruitment and retention at the college/university level 3) Emerging or established national visibility as a performer as appropriate to rank 4) Ability to organize, communicate, and work effectively with faculty, students, and colleagues 5) Commitment to preparing versatile and innovative musicians for careers in the twenty-first century 6) Additional background or educational experience in chamber music. Preferred Qualifications 1) Experience building, coordinating, or leading a chamber music program or comparable cross-area curricular initiative 2) Engagement with new music, including commissioning, premieres, or recording. 3) Experience with residencies, community outreach, or public programming built around chamber music. Candidates for the rank of Assistant Professor must: Meet the qualifications for being an Assistant Professor within UTK’s faculty handbook and the Natalie L. Haslam College of Music bylaws; these are covered in the above Required Qualifications. Candidates for the rank of Associate Professor must: Meet the qualifications for being an Associate Professor within UTK’s faculty handbook and the Natalie L. Haslam College of Music bylaws. Qualifications typically include having served as an assistant professor for at least 5 years and having served as mentor to junior faculty. Applicants should submit via Interfolio a letter of interest, a list of five references, a curriculum vitae, and links to 3-6 audio or video recordings of contrasting styles of repertoire, including 1-2 chamber music performances (include date of performances). Review of applications will begin October 19, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. Contact ahuntoon@utk.edu with any questions."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194178": {
      "title": "Open Rank Position in Music Education, Natalie L. Haslam College of Music, Fall 2027",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: Natalie L. Haslam College of Music Location: Knoxville, TN Assistant, Associate or Full Professor; nine-month tenure-track appointment beginning Fall Semester, August 1, 2027. Salary and fringe benefits are competitive and commensurate with rank and experience. The University of Tennessee's Natalie L. Haslam College of Music is seeking a dynamic music educator to join our faculty. The ideal candidate will demonstrate a collaborative spirit, bring expertise in elementary/general music education and contribute to our undergraduate and graduate music education offerings. The candidate should work collaboratively with other College of Music faculty to sustain and support the music education program, maintain a research/creative/scholarly activity profile commensurate with appointed rank, and demonstrate a commitment to forward-thinking curriculum development, innovation, and community engagement. Primary responsibilities include teaching undergraduates in elementary music education, leading graduate courses in areas of expertise, coordinating field experience internships, and supervising student teachers/interns. The successful candidate will actively participate in professional development for elementary/general music educators, develop regional and national visibility as an elementary/general music educator, and develop and maintain positive relationships with inservice music educators. The candidate is also expected to serve on graduate committees and participate in various education and professional development opportunities including TMEA (ETGMEA), CNAfME, and NAfME. Web Page: music.utk.edu The University of Tennessee Natalie L. Haslam College of Music offers a broad and comprehensive professional music program at both the undergraduate and graduate levels and is an accredited institutional member of the National Association of Schools of Music. The College has approximately 65 faculty members and approximately 400 music majors. General Information: The University of Tennessee, Knoxville, is the state’s flagship research institution, a campus of choice for outstanding undergraduates, and a premier graduate institution. As a land-grant university, it is committed to excellence in learning, scholarship, and engagement with society. In all its activities, the university aims to advance the frontiers of human knowledge and enrich and elevate society. Knoxville’s cultural resources include a symphony orchestra, an opera company, a professional jazz orchestra, theatrical productions, ballet and modern dance, art galleries, and museums. Within an hour’s drive is the Great Smoky Mountains National Park with its scenic beauty and wide variety of recreational activities, plants, and wildlife. Required Qualifications: 1) Doctorate/PhD or ABD in music education or related area; 2) at least three years of successful public school experience; 3) an emerging and/or established (dependent on rank of appointment) record of research, presentations, or scholarly/creative activity; 4) demonstrated abilities in organization, communication and working effectively with faculty, students, colleagues, and administrators. Preferred Qualifications: 1) Successful teaching experience in a music teacher education program; 2) ability to lead classes in secondary general music or other specializations such as music technology or popular music pedagogy; 3) experience teaching graduate online music education courses; 4) experience with elementary music teaching methodologies. Candidates for the rank of Assistant Professor must: Meet the qualifications for being an Assistant Professor within UTK’s faculty handbook and the Natalie L. Haslam College of Music bylaws; these are covered in the above Required Qualifications. Candidates for the rank of Associate Professor must: Meet the qualifications for being an Associate Professor within UTK’s faculty handbook and the Natalie L. Haslam College of Music bylaws; these qualifications normally include having served as an assistant professor for at least 5 years and having served as mentor to junior faculty. Candidates for the rank of Full Professor must: Meet the qualifications for being a Full Professor within UTK’s faculty handbook and the Natalie L. Haslam College of Music bylaws; these qualifications normally include having served as an associate professor for at least 5 years and having served as mentor to junior faculty. Review of applications will begin on October 22, 2026 and continue until the position is filled. Applicants should submit a one-page letter of interest that summarizes qualifications and interest in working in a student-centered music education program at the University of Tennessee, a list of 3-5 references with contact information, curriculum vitae, and a statement of teaching philosophy. Select candidates may be asked to provide brief examples of research and videos of collegiate teaching. Contact ahuntoon@utk.edu with any questions."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194546": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate/Full Professor of Music, Science, and Health, Natalie L. Haslam College of Music, Fall 2027",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: Natalie L. Haslam College of Music Location: Knoxville, TN The Knoxville campus of the University of Tennessee seeks candidates who have the ability to promote an atmosphere where all members of the university community feel welcome and can thrive. The Natalie L. Haslam College of Music invites applications for a 9-month tenure-track position at the Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor level in Music, Science, and Health. This is a rare and forward-looking appointment designed for a scholar-teacher who believes that a fuller understanding of music — how we hear it, perform it, feel it, analyze it, and create it — is enriched by understanding the human brain, human behavior, and the science behind how we interact with the art of music and human health. The successful candidate will occupy a distinctive role at the intersection of music, medicine and health, neuroscience, psychology, cognitive science, or other theoretical and applied sciences, contributing to both the core curriculum of the College and to an emerging cross-disciplinary research culture. The candidate will work in the Natalie L. Haslam College of Music with potential for collaboration with other colleges, and relevant health organizations.The successful candidate is expected to have either existing or strong potential for obtaining extramural funding. Teaching Responsibilities may include: Core lab-sequence instruction in music integrated with perspectives from the natural or social sciences appropriate to the candidate's expertise Seminars in areas such as music and health, music perception and psychoacoustics, the neuroscience of musical experience, and other topics relevant to the candidate’s field Student mentorship and research supervision in the candidate’s core field and related interdisciplinary fields Online course development and delivery, with interest in expanding the College's reach into digital learning environments Research Profile We seek a candidate with an active research agenda that intersects with one or more of the following areas: Scientific applications of health, wellbeing, and/or and human development, including scientific approaches to understanding music’s role in health-related outcomes. The neuroscience of musical experience, including brain-music interaction, with particular attention to musical and cognitive aspects such as rhythm, harmony, melody, tonality, memory, and emotion. Computational, data-driven, and/or psychological approaches to music perception, emotion, musical structure, and performance, with a focus on their relationships to human health Machine learning and artificial intelligence applications for understanding how music interacts with human health, behavior, and perception Web Page: www.music.utk.edu The University of Tennessee Natalie L. Haslam College of Music offers a broad and comprehensive professional music program at both the undergraduate and graduate levels and is an accredited institutional member of the National Association of Schools of Music. The College has approximately 65 faculty members and approximately 400 music majors. General Information: The University of Tennessee, Knoxville, is the state’s flagship research institution, a campus of choice for outstanding undergraduates, and a premier graduate institution. As a land-grant university, it is committed to excellence in learning, scholarship, and engagement with society. In all its activities, the university aims to advance the frontiers of human knowledge and enrich and elevate society. Knoxville’s cultural resources include a symphony orchestra, an opera company, a professional jazz orchestra, theatrical productions, ballet and modern dance, art galleries, and museums. Within an hour’s drive is the Great Smoky Mountains National Park with its scenic beauty and wide variety of recreational activities, plants, and wildlife. Required Qualifications PhD or related doctorate in a health-related science, neuroscience, psychology, cognitive science, or a closely related field (ABD considered with degree conferred by August 1, 2027) Emerging or established national visibility in the field as appropriate to rank Demonstrated research activity and publication in relevant areas Demonstrated ability to communicate, organize work effectively, and collaborate productively with faculty, staff, and students. Candidates for the rank of Assistant Professor should demonstrate a credible plan for developing an externally funded research program. Associate and Full Professor candidates should demonstrate a record of obtaining and/or leading external funding appropriate to their field. Preferred Qualifications Evidence of successful teaching at the college/university level. Candidates for the rank of Assistant Professor must: Meet the qualifications for being an Assistant Professor within UTK’s faculty handbook and the Natalie L. Haslam College of Music bylaws; these are covered in the position's Required Qualifications. Candidates for the rank of Associate Professor must: Meet the qualifications for being an Associate Professor within UTK’s faculty handbook and the Natalie L. Haslam College of Music bylaws; these qualifications normally include having served as an assistant professor for at least 5 years and having served as mentor to junior faculty. Candidates for the rank of Full Professor must: Meet the qualifications for being a Full Professor within UTK’s faculty handbook and the Natalie L. Haslam College of Music bylaws; these qualifications normally include having served as an associate professor for at least 5 years and having served as mentor to junior faculty. Submit the following attachments with your application: Cover letter detailing your interests and qualifications relating to research, teaching, and service. Current CV Research statement (2-3 pages) Teaching and mentoring philosophy Two-three representative publications Contact information for three references listing names, email addresses, and phone numbers Review of applications will begin on November 2, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. Any questions about the search can be directed to Dr. Nathan Fleshner (fleshner@utk.edu)."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194816": {
      "title": "Assistant, Associate or Full Professor of Music Theory and Emerging Technologies, Natalie L. Haslam College of Music, Fall 2027",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: Natalie L. Haslam College of Music Location: Knoxville, TN 9-month open rank Assistant, Associate or Full Professor of Music Theory and Emerging Technologies, Natalie L. Haslam College of Music, starting in the Fall 2027 The University of Tennessee’s Natalie L. Haslam College of Music is seeking a dynamic and accomplished scholar and creative practitioner for a tenure-track position in Music Theory and Emerging Technologies. The successful candidate will provide leadership and vision in music theory while advancing innovative approaches to the study, creation, analysis, and teaching of music through emerging technologies, including but not limited to artificial intelligence, data science, and computational methods. The candidate should also: Work collaboratively with College of Music faculty to sustain and strengthen undergraduate and graduate programs in music theory, musicianship, composition, technology, and related areas; Maintain a record of regional, national, and international research, creative, and/or scholarly activity appropriate to the candidate’s area of specialization, while participating in service appropriate for a university faculty member; Demonstrate expertise or developing expertise in emerging technologies and their applications to music, including areas such as artificial intelligence, computer music, digital audio, music information retrieval, robotics and hardware, interactive technologies, music production, or other evolving technological practices; Demonstrate a commitment to collaboration with the creativity and innovative thinking to envision and develop new professional pathways at the intersection of music and emerging technologies; Bring demonstrated experience and vision that will strengthen the excellence, visibility, and enrollment of the College’s academic programs; and Demonstrate a commitment to collaboration, innovation, and engagement with students, colleagues, and communities through evolving practices in music and technology. Web Page: www.music.utk.edu The University of Tennessee Natalie L. Haslam College of Music offers a broad and comprehensive professional music program at both the undergraduate and graduate levels and is an accredited institutional member of the National Association of Schools of Music. The College has approximately 65 faculty members and approximately 400 music majors. General Information: The University of Tennessee, Knoxville, is the state’s flagship research institution, a campus of choice for outstanding undergraduates, and a premier graduate institution. As a land-grant university, it is committed to excellence in learning, scholarship, and engagement with society. In all its activities, the university aims to advance the frontiers of human knowledge and enrich and elevate society. Knoxville’s cultural resources include a symphony orchestra, an opera company, a professional jazz orchestra, theatrical productions, ballet and modern dance, art galleries, and museums. Within an hour’s drive is the Great Smoky Mountains National Park with its scenic beauty and wide variety of recreational activities, plants, and wildlife. Send any questions about the position to ahuntoon@utk.edu. Required Qualifications: Doctorate in a related field required by time of appointment A consistent, well-rounded record of or demonstrated potential for research, presentations, and scholarly/creative activity Demonstrated abilities in organization, communication, and working effectively with faculty, students, colleagues, and administration Demonstrate a commitment to collaboration and inclusive teaching, with the creativity and innovative thinking to envision and develop new professional pathways at the intersection of music and emerging technologies. Preferred Qualifications: Experience teaching music theory at the undergraduate and graduate levels, including a demonstrated ability to integrate analytical, aural, compositional, and technology-based approaches. Demonstrated experience incorporating emerging technologies into music teaching, research, composition, performance, or creative practice, including areas such as digital audio, music production, coding, interactive technologies, AI, or computer-assisted music. Experience developing and delivering online, hybrid, or technology-enhanced learning opportunities in music theory, musicianship, composition, or related areas. Strong communication skills and cross-cultural competencies that support effective collaboration with colleagues and community members, particularly in technology-mediated and interdisciplinary environments. Candidates for the rank of Assistant Professor must: Meet the qualifications for being an Assistant Professor within UTK’s faculty handbook and the Natalie L. Haslam College of Music bylaws; these are covered in the above Required Qualifications. Candidates for the rank of Associate Professor must: Meet the qualifications for being an Associate Professor within UTK’s faculty handbook and the Natalie L. Haslam College of Music bylaws; among these qualifications include normally having served as an Assistant Professor for at least 5 years and having served as mentor to junior faculty. Candidates for the rank of Full Professor must: Meet the qualifications for being a Full Professor within UTK’s faculty handbook and the Natalie L. Haslam College of Music bylaws; among these qualifications include normally having served as an Associate Professor for at least 5 years and having served as mentor to junior faculty. Review of applications will begin on November 2, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled.  Applications received after this deadline may not receive full consideration. Applicants should submit: 1) a one-page letter of interest that summarizes their qualifications and interest in the position at the University of Tennessee, 2) a list of 3-5 references with contact information, 3) curriculum vitae, 4) a one page statement of teaching philosophy addressing approaches to music and the integration of emerging technologies, 5) a list of projects, current research and publications."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193893": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Philosophy, Fall 2027",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: College of Arts & Sciences: Division of Arts & Humanities: Philosophy Location: Knoxville, Tennessee The Department of Philosophy at the University of Tennessee, Knoxville invites applications for a tenure-line position at the rank of Assistant or Associate Professor, beginning Fall 2027.  The Knoxville campus of the University of Tennessee is on the academic semester system, and the default teaching load is two courses per semester, totaling four per year.  Research and teaching expectations are high.  Tenure-line faculty in the department are highly research-active.  Teaching expectations include undergraduate courses at all levels, graduate teaching in area(s) of specialization and competence, and mentoring of student research at all levels, including directing theses and serving on thesis committees.  Faculty are also responsible for service to the department, university, and profession, of a kind and degree appropriate to rank.  Interest in exploring the possibility of developing a new logic lab on campus would be desirable.  UTK is a land-grant university and values publicly-engaged research, teaching, and service, and considers evidence of commitment to public engagement in the records of applicants. PhD in Philosophy must be completed by the position start date, 8/1/2027.  Some preference will be given to candidates at the advanced Assistant or early Associate level.  Candidates for appointment at the Assistant Professor level must demonstrate potential to develop a sustained record of excellence in teaching and research.  Candidates for appointment at the Associate Professor level must demonstrate a sustained record of excellence in teaching and research, as well as a record of significant service. Area of Specialization: Philosophy of Language or Logic Area of Competency: Open but ability to teach PHIL 235 (Formal Logic) is required.  Highly desirable would be one or more of (i) ability to teach PHIL 435 (Intermediate Formal Logic, required for all Ph.D. students); (ii) ability to contribute to some interdisciplinary area (e.g. linguistics, neuroscience, AI, game development); (iii) ability to contribute to the department's degree concentrations in Legal and Political Philosophy or Philosophy of Science and Medicine. This position is part of UTK's \"Envisioning and Narrating Emerging Futures\" hiring constellation; for details see https://artsci.utk.edu/about/employment/ Please submit (i) a cover letter, which should include some discussion of possible service contributions to the department; (ii) a CV; (iii) a fully anonymized research statement; (iv) a fully anonymized writing sample of 5000-10000 words; (v) at least three letters of recommendation; and (vi) a teaching portfolio that includes a teaching statement, representative syllabi in the candidate's AOS, and other evidence of teaching effectiveness.  In addition, candidates may describe how they would help promote students' access to and inclusion in their teaching and research.  Applications must be received by November 2, 2026 to receive full consideration.  The committee will begin its deliberations by considering anonymized research statements and writing samples in isolation. Please submit materials electronically to http://apply.interfolio.com/193893 .  Questions may be directed to Clerk Shaw at jshaw15@utk.edu."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192710": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor - Physics, Quantum Information Theory - Fall 2027",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: College of Arts & Sciences: Division of Natural Sciences & Mathematics: Physics Location: Knoxville, TN The Department of Physics and Astronomy at the University of Tennessee Knoxville (UTK) invites applications for a tenure-track faculty position at the rank of Assistant Professor in Quantum Information Theory , with an anticipated start date of August 1, 2027. We seek candidates capable of developing an innovative research program in theoretical quantum information science and quantum computing, broadly construed . We welcome applications spanning the full quantum information science landscape, from fundamental theory to theory closely connected with quantum technologies and physical platforms. Relevant areas include but are not limited to: quantum information and computation; quantum algorithms and complexity; quantum error correction and fault-tolerant quantum computing; quantum simulation; quantum sensing and metrology; quantum communication and networks; quantum cryptography; quantum control; quantum many-body information science; theoretical quantum optics; quantum architectures and resource estimation; and hardware-aware theory or hardware–software co-design. We are particularly interested in candidates whose research will complement and extend existing theoretical and experimental strengths at UTK and Oak Ridge National Laboratory (ORNL) and who can build intellectual connections across different layers of quantum science and technology. Candidates whose work links fundamental quantum information concepts to realistic devices, architectures, experiments, or applications are especially encouraged to apply. The search is not restricted to any quantum-computing platform, application area, or methodological approach. UTK offers a rich environment for quantum science. Faculty across Physics and Astronomy and other departments pursue research in quantum computation, quantum materials, quantum optics, quantum sensing, quantum communication and networking, and related areas. UTK researchers have extensive collaborations with ORNL, including activities associated with the Quantum Science Center, as well as interdisciplinary collaborations across the university. The successful candidate will have opportunities to develop collaborations spanning fundamental physics, computation, engineering, and emerging quantum technologies. The University of Tennessee, Knoxville is Tennessee’s flagship State research institution, a campus of choice for outstanding undergraduates, and a premier graduate institution. The successful candidate will benefit greatly from available computational resources, and by the proximity to unique research facilities at Oak Ridge National Laboratory. The campus is in one of the most beautiful areas of the country with easy access to miles of inland waterways, pristine state and national parks, diverse cultural opportunities, and a blend of convenient urban and rural living settings. Downtown Knoxville, adjacent to campus, is a thriving neighborhood filled with restaurants, shops, and indoor and outdoor entertainment venues, and is home to the Knoxville Symphony and Knoxville Opera, as well as to the annual Big Ears Music Festival, Jazz on the Square, Shakespeare on the Square, and other events. The successful candidate is expected to establish a vigorous, externally funded research program; supervise graduate and undergraduate researchers; contribute to the department's graduate and undergraduate teaching missions; and participate in the continued development of quantum information science research and education at UTK. Applicants must hold a Ph.D. in Physics or a closely related field by the time of appointment . Candidates must have demonstrated research expertise in theoretical quantum information science, broadly construed, together with a record of research accomplishments appropriate to their career stage. Candidates must demonstrate the potential to establish an independent, externally funded research program of high quality, the potential for effective teaching at the undergraduate and graduate levels, and the potential for successful mentoring of undergraduate and graduate students. Candidates must also demonstrate the ability to contribute effectively to a collaborative research and educational environment within the Department of Physics and Astronomy and across the broader quantum science community at UTK and ORNL. Applicants should include (1) a cover letter describing their interest in the position; (2) a curriculum vitae including full list of publications; (3) a research statement outlining previous and future directions with detail on aligned sources of extramural funding; (4) a teaching and mentoring statement; (5) complete letters of recommendation from 3 references (requested through Interfolio). All application materials should be submitted on-line at https://apply.interfolio.com/192710 . Only electronic applications will be considered, and acceptable file formats are .pdf or .docx.  Review of applications will start on December 1, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. For questions, please contact Dr. George Siopsis, Search Committee Chair, via gsiopsis@utk.edu."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/189584": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Cognitive and Computational Neuroscience - Department of Psychology and Neuroscience - Fall 2027",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: College of Arts & Science: Division of Social Sciences: Psychology Location: Knoxville, TN The Department of Psychology and Neuroscience at the University of Tennessee at Knoxville invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position in Cogniti ve and/ or Computational Neuroscience . We are looking for a faculty who can enrich and expand existing cognitive neuroscience research. We invite applications from individuals who use behavioral, computational, and/ or neuroscience approaches to study cognitive processes including perception, attention, learning, memory systems, language, executive function, motor control, or decision-making. The position will begin on August 1, 2027. About the Department of Psychology and Neuroscience: The Department of Psychology and Neuroscience has three Ph.D. programs (Counseling, Clinical, and Experimental) and one M.A. (Experimental) program and comprises 37 tenure-line faculty, and 8 full-time teaching faculty, and 3 full-time clinical faculty. The Experimental program is a vibrant and diverse community of scientists who conduct a wide range of animal and human research. It includes three sub-disciplines (Cognitive and Developmental Sciences, Neuroscience & Behavior, and Social). The successful candidate will join the Cognitive and Developmental Sciences area which consists of 8 tenure-line faculty. The department is committed to mentoring new faculty to be successful in their academic careers. New faculty are assigned a mentoring committee. The Department has an excellent record in helping faculty successfully earn promotion and tenure. Information about the department, our undergraduate Psychology and Neuroscience programs, an d our graduate programs is online at http://psychology.utk.edu . About the University of Tennessee, Knoxville: The University of Tennessee at Knoxville is the flagship university and the premier public research institute in the State of Tennessee. The University is beginning a next-level initiative aimed both at substantially elevating the University’s national reputation for impactful research, scholarship, and creative activity and at assembling a roster of faculty committed to delivering one of the nation’s best educational experiences for undergraduate and graduate students. There are extensive opportunities for collaborations with members of the Neuroscience Network of East Tennessee ( NeuroNET ) , National Institute for Mathematical and Biological Synthesis ( NIMBioS ) , and nearby Oak Ridge National Laboratory . About Knoxville: The city of Knoxville is a hidden gem with a beautiful and walkable downtown, thriving music scene ( https://bigearsfestival.org/ ), active neighborhoods, hundreds of miles of greenways, and eclectic shopping and restaurants. Knoxville is located within easy driving distance to Asheville, Nashville, Atlanta, and the Great Smoky Mountains National Park ( https://www.nps.gov/grsm/index.htm ) . More information about Knoxville can be found at https://www.visitknoxville.com/ and https://insideofknoxville.com/ . Applicants must have Ph .D. in Cognition, Neuroscience, or a related field by August 1, 2027. The ideal candidate should have a strong research record and enthusiasm for teaching at the graduate and undergraduate levels. Position responsibilities include maintaining a productive research program, teaching at both undergraduate and graduate levels, and directing graduate student research. The successful candidate will be expected to have either existing or strong potential for obtaining extramural fundin g, build a widely recognized research program, provide high-quality instruction and advising to a diverse group of graduate and undergraduate students, actively engage with colleagues from a wide range of disciplines , and engage in departmental, institutional, and professional service . All materials should be electronically submitted to Interfolio (https:// apply.interfolio.com/189584 ). Applicants should submit : A cover letter detailing your interests and qualifications relating to research, teaching, and service. A current CV A research statement (2-3 pages) A teaching and mentoring philosophy Three representative publications Contact information for three references (names, email addresses, and phone numbers) Review of applications will begin on Oc tober 1, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. Any questions about the search can be directed to Dr. Caglar Tas ( caglartas@utk.edu )."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192475": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Acting (Stage/Screen), Fall 2027",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: College of Arts & Sciences: Division of Arts & Humanities: Theatre Location: Knoxville, TN The Department of Theatre at the University of Tennessee invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant/Associate Professor specializing in Acting (Stage & Screen). The successful candidate will be a accomplished acting teacher with extensive training in one or more recognized approaches to actor training and significant professional experience in theatre. The ideal candidate will have demonstrated excellence teaching acting at the university, conservatory, or equivalent professional-training level, with experience teaching both undergraduate and graduate actors. Experience in acting for the camera and/or performance in emerging media and/or filmmaking is required. Duties include active participation in both graduate and undergraduate programs, service to the department, university, and profession, and creative activity and/or research. The faculty member will teach courses in acting, including advanced scene study and advanced scene study. Other classes may include acting for the camera, audition technique, performance across emerging media, on-camera technique for non-majors or other areas of expertise. The successful candidate will contribute to the continued development of a rigorous, sequential actor-training curriculum and mentor students preparing for professional careers across stage and screen. The faculty member will contribute as a company member to the Clarence Brown Theatre, a LORT D professional theatre in residence, as an actor, director, or in other capacities based on their expertise. UTK is a land-grant university and values engaged forms of research/scholarship/creative activity, teaching, and service and considers evidence of these commitments in the records of applicants. The University is engaged in a next-level initiative aimed both at substantially elevating the University's national reputation for impactful research, scholarship, and creative activity and at assembling a roster of faculty committed to delivering one of the nation's best educational experiences for undergraduate and graduate students.​ This position is part of a new constellation of interdisciplinary faculty positions in the UT College of Arts and Sciences emphasizing Envisioning and Narrating Emerging Futures (ENEF). We are seeking tenure-line faculty who will have overlapping interests that foster faculty development and collaboration. The goal is to bring together and invest in faculty across departments whose research, scholarship and creative work is linked through this initiative and to attract scholars who can bring different perspectives to an important issue or area. Minimum qualifications include an MFA or equivalent terminal degree, or at least five years of professional experience at the regional theatre level or above, and a demonstrated record of, or potential for, excellence in teaching. Candidates must also demonstrate professional experience in theatre and experience in acting for the camera, filmmaking, and/or performance in emerging media. Candidates for appointment at the Assistant Professor level must demonstrate the potential to develop a sustained record of excellence in teaching and professional creative activity and/or research appropriate to a tenure-track faculty appointment. Candidates for appointment at the Associate Professor level must demonstrate a substantial and sustained record of excellence in teaching and professional creative activity and/or research, along with a record of service and professional accomplishment commensurate with appointment at the rank of Associate Professor. Candidates should submit a cover letter, CV/resume, and contact information for three professional references to Interfolio (apply.interfolio.com/192475) . The cover letter should address the candidate’s qualifications for the position, approach to actor training, professional experience, and teaching experience. Review of applications will begin on November 9th and continue until the position is filled. Questions related to this search should be directed to Casey Sams at jsams1@utk.edu."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/187644": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Business Analytics, AI/ML, Fall 2027",
      "description": "Posted: Jun 05, 2026 Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: Haslam College of Business: Business Analytics & Statistics Location: University of Tennessee, Knoxville The Department of Business Analytics and Statistics at the Haslam College of Business, University of Tennessee, Knoxville (UTK) invites applications for a tenure-track assistant professor position of business analytics with a focus on applied artificial intelligence (AI) or machine learning (ML). Faculty members are expected to publish in top academic journals, to teach at undergraduate and graduate levels, and contribute to the intellectual life of the university. Effective date of employment will be August 1, 2027, or when filled. Compensation will be competitive. Successful candidates for this position will have: A Ph.D. degree in Information Systems, Operations Research, Computer Science, Artificial Intelligence, Machine Learning, Statistics, or related fields, with a business background or a focus on business applications, in hand (including August 2027 graduation) as of the starting date of employment. A commitment to a scholarly research record as evidenced by publications (or work toward publications) in top-tier academic journals. The ability to communicate effectively to audiences including faculty, undergraduate students, and graduate students. Qualified individuals should send the following application materials as ONE PDF to apply.interfolio.com/187644 : cover letter, CV, research statement, teaching statement (with teaching evaluations if applicable), sample research paper, and contact information for three references. Review of applications will begin on 6/25/2026 and continue until the position is filled. Full consideration will be given to applications received before July 31, 2026. A background check is required before the successful candidate can be hired. For questions, please contact: BAS@UTK.EDU or 865-974-9311. Department of Business Analytics and Statistics The Department of Business Analytics and Statistics is one of seven departments within the Haslam College of Business. The department currently offers bachelors, masters, and Ph.D. degrees in Business Analytics and leads UTK's Intercollegiate Graduate Statistics and Data Science Program. The department's MSBA program received the 2018 INFORMS Smith Prize. The Business Analytics faculty emphasizes working with businesses to apply analytics to real world challenges. The department includes 18 tenured or tenure-track faculty, many of whom serve in editorial positions at leading journals and have been recognized with international awards including the Edelman Award and the Wagner Prize."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/187985": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Operations Management, Fall 2027",
      "description": "Posted: Jun 22, 2026 Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: Haslam College of Business: Business Analytics & Statistics Location: The University of Tennessee, Knoxville, Haslam College of Business The University of Tennessee, Knoxville, Haslam College of Business invites applications for one tenure-track, Assistant Professor of Operations Management (OM) position in its Department of Business Analytics and Statistics. Applicants will be expected to have expertise in an OM area (e.g., behavioral operations, health care operations, service operations, supply chain management, sustainable operations). The applicant's research program should reflect the potential for ongoing thought leadership in the discipline. The Knoxville campus of the University of Tennessee is seeking to fill the position with a candidate who can contribute in meaningful ways to the University. Effective date of employment will be August 1, 2027, or when filled. Program Information The Department of Business Analytics and Statistics (BAS) is housed in the Haslam College of Business at the University of Tennessee. The Department enjoys a strong position within the Haslam College, and faculty members are active participants in college-wide programs and priorities. The University of Tennessee's Haslam College of Business is consistently ranked highly in national surveys. Duties/Responsibilities The candidate will be expected to contribute to the teaching mission at undergraduate and graduate levels.  Research requirements include evidence or strong potential for publication in top-tier journals in OM, and an interest in working with other faculty members and graduate students on collaborative research. Required: Ph.D. degree in business administration or a related discipline (such as management science, industrial engineering, economics) with qualifications at time of hire to be appointed at the rank of assistant professor.  In addition, the candidate should have at least one publication or working paper that has received a “major revision” or better decision at a top-tier OM journal. Preferred: The candidate should be able to demonstrate their ability to conduct analytical, behavioral, or empirical research that is methodologically rigorous, and managerially relevant. The candidate should also work effectively as a contributing member of a faculty team, with a scholarly research record focused on top-tier journal publications in the field, such as the journals listed in the UT-Dallas list of business journals . The ideal candidate will also possess a demonstrated history of teaching excellence (or show the potential to become an outstanding teacher, such as experience as a teaching assistant in its Ph.D.-granting institution). Faculty in the Haslam College can expect opportunities to teach across a variety of programs including traditional undergraduate, master’s, and Ph.D. as well as executive education programs. The preferred candidate will teach undergraduate and/or graduate courses offered by the BAS department. Preference will be given to candidates with track record or articulated future willingness to engage in Graduate and Executive Education programs. Qualified individuals are invited to submit a vita, letter of interest, and names and contact information for three references as ONE PDF through the online system apply.interfolio.com/187985 . Formal letters of reference will be required during a later phase of the selection process (references will not be contacted until a final pool of candidates is approved and finalists have cleared via general assessment as required by UTK policy). Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. To receive full consideration, applications should be submitted by October 15, 2026. Preliminary interviews may be conducted via Zoom or in person at the INFORMS Annual Meeting, October 31–November 3, 2026. Please apply through the online application system. For questions, please contact: BAS@UTK.EDU or 865-974-2556."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191870": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor, Finance, Fall 2027",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: Haslam College of Business: Finance Location: Knoxville, TN The Haslam College of Business at the University of Tennessee invites applications and nominations for a tenure-track Assistant/Associate Professor position in the Department of Finance.  Faculty members are expected to publish in top finance journals, teach at undergraduate and graduate levels, and contribute to the intellectual life of the university.  Candidates must be able to work effectively within a Department and a College that places significant emphasis on interaction and cross-functional integration.  Effective date of employment will be August 1, 2027, or when filled. Compensation will be competitive. Program Information The Department of Finance is one of seven departments within the Haslam College of Business at the University of Tennessee. The Department’s mission includes excellence in research, teaching, and service, while working within the framework of the College’s overall mission. The Department holds a strong, meaningful position within the College, and faculty members actively participate in College-wide programs and priorities. The Department of Finance is the largest major at The University of Tennessee and includes curriculum at the undergraduate, MBA, and doctoral levels; and it also houses the Master’s Investment Learning Center. The Department values a collegial environment where faculty members work well with each other and with doctoral students. Location Knoxville, Tennessee is the gateway to the Smoky Mountains and is frequently listed as one of the “Best Places to Live” based on the low cost of living and abundance of outdoor activities. Duties/Responsibilities The successful candidate will be expected to contribute to the teaching mission at the undergraduate level and, eventually, at the MBA and/or Ph.D. levels. Research requirements include a strong, ongoing record of publication in top-tier finance journals and an interest in working with other faculty and graduate students on research projects. Successful Candidates for Assistant Professor rank for this position will have: Ph.D. degree in finance or a related field in hand (including August 2027 graduation) as of the starting date of employment. A commitment to a scholarly research record as evidenced by publications (or work toward publications) in top-tier journals in the field of finance. The ability to communicate effectively to audiences including faculty, undergraduate students, and graduate students. Successful Candidates for Associate Professor rank for this position will – in addition to the above – have: Evidence of an established research agenda as evidenced by top-tier publications within the field of Finance. Evidence of service at the department, college, and discipline levels. The ability and desire to teach and/or develop programming in our graduate and executive education (GEE) programs, such as the Master’s in Financial Economics, MBA, Executive MBA, Physicians Executive MBA, or Aerospace and Defense MBA. Qualified individuals are invited to submit their application materials via Interfolio apply.interfolio.com/191870 . The application package should include a cover letter indicating interest, a curriculum vitae, evidence of successful prior teaching (course materials, teaching evaluations, etc.), a recent publication or working paper, and three (3) confidential letters of reference.  We will begin initial review of application materials on September 18, 2026 and will continue reviewing applications until the position is filled. The search will remain open until the position is filled. Interviews will be conducted via videoconference (e.g., ZOOM) starting in September-October 2026, and we will invite the finalists to campus. For further information, please contact Jeannie Goodman (goodmanj@utk.edu or 865-974-3218) or the Chair of the Search Committee, Scott Guernsey (sguernsey@utk.edu)."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191881": {
      "title": "Non-Tenure Track Faculty Positions in Finance, Fall 2027 (2 openings)",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: Haslam College of Business: Finance Location: University of Tennessee, Knoxville The Department of Finance at the Haslam College of Business, University of Tennessee, Knoxville, invites applications and nominations for two full-time, non-tenure-track faculty positions . These positions will be classified under the Lecturer , Teaching Professor , or Professor of Practice tracks, with the specific title and rank dependent on qualifications. Interviews will be conducted via videoconference (e.g., ZOOM) starting in October 2026 and we will invite the finalists for each position to campus. Position Details The Department of Finance anticipates hiring two non-tenure-track faculty members to support teaching needs across the finance curriculum, which includes courses in corporate finance, investments, financial institutions, FinTech, and real estate. The specific course assignments, as well as the separate determinations of track (Lecturer, Teaching Professor, Professor of Practice) and rank of all hires, will be based on departmental needs and candidate strengths. Successful candidates are expected to engage with students beyond the classroom to help them enhance their skills and career readiness. Faculty must be able to collaborate effectively within the Department, College, and University. Start Date: August 1, 2027 Salary: Competitive and commensurate with experience and qualifications. Department Overview The Department of Finance is one of seven academic departments within the Haslam College of Business . It offers programs at the undergraduate, MBA, Executive MBA, and doctoral levels, and houses the Masters Investment Learning Center . Finance is a popular major and minor (collateral) at UTK. The department is committed to excellence in teaching, research, and service, and prides itself on a collegial and collaborative environment among faculty and students. Location The University of Tennessee is located in Knoxville , a vibrant city nestled near the Great Smoky Mountains . Knoxville offers a low cost of living , abundant outdoor activities , and is frequently recognized as one of the “Best Places to Live” in the United States. Qualifications Track and Rank Qualifications: Consistent with the University of Tennessee, Knoxville Faculty Handbook and college/department bylaws, non-tenure-track (NTT) titles and ranks are determined based on credentials and a documented record of effectiveness in assigned responsibilities (primarily teaching). Standards and procedures related to appointment and promotion standards are additionally governed by department and college bylaws. Lecturer Series (Lecturer, Senior Lecturer, Distinguished Lecturer) Lecturer – Holds (or will hold at the time of appointment) a Master’s degree or higher in Finance or a related field and shows promise for excellence in assigned responsibilities. Senior Lecturer – As above, with a demonstrated record of excellence in assigned responsibilities. Distinguished Lecturer – As above, with a sustained, consistent record of excellence in assigned responsibilities. Teaching Professor Series (Assistant, Associate, Teaching Professor) Teaching Assistant Professor – Holds a Ph.D. (or will hold at the time of appointment) in Finance or a related field with promise for excellence in teaching and related responsibilities, evidenced by early effectiveness and contributions. Teaching Associate Professor – Terminal degree with a demonstrated record of excellence in teaching and related responsibilities. Teaching Professor (Full) – Terminal degree with a sustained, consistent record of excellence and evidence of instructional leadership (e.g., curriculum development, mentoring, pedagogical innovation) commensurate with senior rank. Professor of Practice Series (Assistant, Associate, Professor of Practice) Assistant Professor of Practice – Holds a Ph.D. (or will hold at the time of appointment) in Finance or a related field and/or appropriate professional licensure/certification and significant professional experience; shows promise for excellence in assigned responsibilities. Associate Professor of Practice – As above, with a demonstrated record of excellence in assigned responsibilities. Professor of Practice – As above, with a sustained, consistent record of excellence in assigned responsibilities; often includes evidence of professional or instructional leadership. Notes on Evidence and Evaluation: Appointment rank and later promotion is determined based on evidence of achievement in assigned duties, which may include course evaluations, peer reviews, curricular or program leadership, mentoring, pedagogical innovation, student success contributions, and service aligned with instructional roles. Expectations are calibrated to workload and unit bylaws. Preferred Qualifications: Demonstrated effectiveness in undergraduate-level teaching . A strong commitment to student success . Industry experience and/or relevant certifications (e.g., CFP, CFA, real estate licensure) are desirable but not required. The ability and desire to teach and/or develop programming in our graduate and executive education (GEE) programs, such as the Masters in Financial Economics, MBA, Executive MBA, Physicians Executive MBA, or Aerospace and Defense MBA. Application Process Interested candidates should apply online via Interfolio: https://apply.interfolio.com/XXXXXX Application materials should include: Cover letter indicating position interest and relevant experience Curriculum vitae (CV) Evidence of teaching effectiveness (e.g., syllabi, course materials, student evaluations) Contact information for three professional references Application Review Begins: Wednesday, September 16, 2026 Positions remains open until filled. For inquiries, contact Jeannie Goodman (goodmanj@utk.edu) or the Search Committee Chair, Tracie Woidtke (twoidtke@utk.edu)"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191984": {
      "title": "Non-Tenure Track Faculty in Real Estate, Finance, Fall 2027",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: Haslam College of Business: Finance Location: University of Tennessee, Knoxville The Department of Finance at the Haslam College of Business, University of Tennessee, Knoxville, invites applications and nominations for one full-time, non-tenure-track faculty position . This position is for a faculty member whose teaching responsibilities will primarily be in the area of real estate . This position will be classified under the Lecturer , Teaching Professor , or Professor of Practice tracks, with the specific title and rank dependent on qualifications. Interviews will be conducted via videoconference (e.g., ZOOM) starting in October 2026 and we will invite the finalists for this position to campus. Position Details The Department of Finance anticipates hiring one non-tenure-track faculty member who can contribute to course offerings in real estate .  Current offerings include Real Estate Principles, Real Estate Law, Real Estate Finance and Investment Analysis, and Real Estate Cases. The specific course assignments, as well as the separate determinations of track (Lecturer, Teaching Professor, Professor of Practice) and rank of any hire, will be based on departmental needs and candidate strengths. Successful candidates are expected to engage with students beyond the classroom to help them enhance their skills and career readiness. Faculty must be able to collaborate effectively within the Department, College, and University. Start Date: August 1, 2027 Salary: Competitive and commensurate with experience and qualifications. Department Overview The Department of Finance is one of seven academic departments within the Haslam College of Business . It offers programs at the undergraduate, MBA, Executive MBA, and doctoral levels.  Real estate classes are offered within the Finance department and is one of the fastest growing areas of interest in the College.  We currently offer a three-class collateral in real estate and anticipate offering a cross-disciplinary Real Estate Minor starting in the 2027-2028 academic year.  We have a vibrant, student-run real estate club (UTK Real Estate Club – recutk.edu ) and enjoy strong support from alumni in the real estate industry.  Our students participate in case competitions, Argus training, and other experiential learning opportunities.  The department is committed to excellence in teaching, research, and service, and prides itself on a collegial and collaborative environment among faculty and students. Location The University of Tennessee is located in Knoxville , a vibrant city nestled near the Great Smoky Mountains . Knoxville offers a low cost of living , abundant outdoor activities , and is frequently recognized as one of the “Best Places to Live” in the United States. Track and Rank Qualifications: Consistent with the University of Tennessee, Knoxville Faculty Handbook and college/department bylaws, non-tenure-track (NTT) titles and ranks are determined based on credentials and a documented record of effectiveness in assigned responsibilities (primarily teaching). Standards and procedures related to appointment and promotion standards are additionally governed by department and college bylaws. Lecturer Series (Lecturer, Senior Lecturer, Distinguished Lecturer) Lecturer – Holds (or will hold at the time of appointment) a Master’s degree or higher in Finance, Real Estate, or a related field and shows promise for excellence in assigned responsibilities. Senior Lecturer – As above, with a demonstrated record of excellence in assigned responsibilities. Distinguished Lecturer – As above, with a sustained, consistent record of excellence in assigned responsibilities. Teaching Professor Series (Assistant, Associate, Teaching Professor) Teaching Assistant Professor – Holds a Ph.D. (or will hold at the time of appointment) in Finance, Real Estate, or a related field with promise for excellence in teaching and related responsibilities, evidenced by early effectiveness and contributions. Teaching Associate Professor – Terminal degree with a demonstrated record of excellence in teaching and related responsibilities. Teaching Professor (Full) – Terminal degree with a sustained, consistent record of excellence and evidence of instructional leadership (e.g., curriculum development, mentoring, pedagogical innovation) commensurate with senior rank. Professor of Practice Series (Assistant, Associate, Professor of Practice) Assistant Professor of Practice – Holds a Ph.D. (or will hold at the time of appointment) in Finance, Real Estate, or a related field and/or appropriate professional licensure/certification and significant professional experience; shows promise for excellence in assigned responsibilities. Associate Professor of Practice – As above, with a demonstrated record of excellence in assigned responsibilities. Professor of Practice – As above, with a sustained, consistent record of excellence in assigned responsibilities; often includes evidence of professional or instructional leadership. Notes on Evidence and Evaluation: Appointment rank and later promotion is determined based on evidence of achievement in assigned duties, which may include course evaluations, peer reviews, curricular or program leadership, mentoring, pedagogical innovation, student success contributions, and service aligned with instructional roles. Expectations are calibrated to workload and unit bylaws. Preferred Qualifications: Demonstrated effectiveness in undergraduate-level teaching . A strong commitment to student success . Industry experience and/or relevant certifications (e.g., CCIM, MAI, etc.) are desirable but not required. The ability and desire to teach and/or develop programming in our expanding real estate offerings at the undergraduate and graduate level. Application Process Interested candidates should apply online via Interfolio: https://apply.interfolio.com/191984 Application materials should include: Cover letter indicating position interest and relevant experience Curriculum vitae (CV) Evidence of teaching effectiveness (e.g., syllabi, course materials, student evaluations) Contact information for three professional references Application Review Begins: Wednesday, September 16, 2026 Positions remains open until filled. For inquiries, contact Jeannie Goodman (goodmanj@utk.edu) or the Search Committee Chair, Tracie Woidtke (twoidtke@utk.edu)"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/189266": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor (Organizational Behavior/Human Resources), Department of Management & Entrepreneurship (Fall, 2027)",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: Haslam College of Business: Management Location: University of Tennessee, Knoxville Haslam College of Business University of Tennessee, Knoxville Tenure-Track Faculty Position, Department of Management & Entrepreneurship The University of Tennessee’s Haslam College of Business and the Department of Management & Entrepreneurship invite applications for a tenure-track position in Organizational Behavior / Human Resources at the Assistant Professor level. The effective date of employment is August 1, 2027, or when filled. Compensation will be competitive. Haslam College of Business and Department Background The Haslam College of Business is well-funded with an endowment exceeding $260 million dollars. The Haslam College of Business is AACSB accredited, with over 200 full-time faculty serving approximately 11,000 undergraduate and graduate students. Classes are taught in the state-of-the-art James A. Haslam II Business Building. The college has experienced exceptional growth, more than doubling since the Haslam Business Building opened. To meet the needs of this growth, we are hiring new faculty, and constructing a 240,000 square foot additional business building, Boyd Hall, which will open in 2028. The Department of Management and Entrepreneurship offers two undergraduate degrees (Management and Human Resource Management), a master’s degree in management and human resources, two university minors (Entrepreneurship and Social Entrepreneurship), and the Ph.D. degree in Strategy, Entrepreneurship, & Organizations. Research faculty members include Savannah Asay, Brian Bergman, Melissa Cardon, David Gras, Joseph Harrison, Christina Hymer, Tom Lumpkin, Eduardo Melendez, Alex Miller, Tim Munyon (Department Head), Tim Pollock, Anne Smith, Jinyuan Song, Ruixiang Song, Sherry Thatcher, and Dave Williams. Our faculty publish in top journals including AMJ, AMR, ASQ, JAP, JBV, JIBS, JOM, Org Science, PPsych, and SMJ. The Knoxville Area Knoxville is located in east Tennessee, with a metro area population of more than 900,000. Knoxville enjoys a mild climate, a beautiful and walkable downtown, varied nightlife, active neighborhoods, and eclectic shopping and restaurants. Knoxville has been consistently named as one of the top 25 best places to live by USA Today and a top 10 “America’s best towns” by CNN Travel. It is home to several large corporations and located within easy driving distance of Oak Ridge National Laboratory and the Great Smoky Mountain National Park. Asheville, Atlanta, Charlotte, and Nashville are readily accessible by car, and the Knoxville airport offers direct flights to 40 destinations. Required Candidates must hold a Ph.D. from an accredited institution and demonstrate evidence of quality scholarship and teaching effectiveness in Organizational Behavior / Human Resources or a related discipline. The Ph.D. must be earned by August 1, 2027. Candidates should show promise in developing a program in disciplinary research and scholarship that is gaining external recognition. Preferred Our preferred candidates will possess: An ongoing program of research in premier refereed journals. A desire to work with doctoral students. An identifiable methodological skillset. A record of demonstrated teaching success at an accredited institution. Essential Duties and Responsibilities Engage in human resource management and/or organizational behavior research that results in premier publications. Teach undergraduate and graduate courses in management. Participate in professional and university service activities. Candidates applying by August 7, 2026 will receive a complete review of their credentials. We will continue reviewing applications until the position is filled. We will conduct preliminary interviews via videoconference (e.g., ZOOM) and will invite the finalists for the position to campus. We will not conduct interviews nor meet with applicants at academic conferences (e.g., AOM). Applicants should submit a cover letter, current curriculum vitae, and the names and contact information of three references via Interfolio. In your cover letter, please explain your fit for the position. At the end of your CV, please list the names and contact information of three references. Reference letters are not needed at the time of application. Use the following link to apply for the position: https://apply.interfolio.com/189266 ---------- Dr. Joseph Harrison, Search Committee Chair Department of Management & Entrepreneurship Haslam College of Business 417 Stokely Management Center University of Tennessee, Knoxville Knoxville, TN 37996-0545"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/189276": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor (Strategic Management), Department of Management & Entrepreneurship (Fall, 2027)",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: Haslam College of Business: Management Location: University of Tennessee, Knoxville Haslam College of Business University of Tennessee, Knoxville Tenure-Track Faculty Position, Department of Management & Entrepreneurship The University of Tennessee’s Haslam College of Business and the Department of Management & Entrepreneurship invite applications for a tenure-track position in Strategic Management at the Assistant Professor level. The effective date of employment is August 1, 2027, or when filled. Compensation will be competitive. Haslam College of Business and Department Background The Haslam College of Business is well-funded with an endowment exceeding $260 million dollars. The Haslam College of Business is AACSB accredited, with over 200 full-time faculty serving approximately 11,000 undergraduate and graduate students. Classes are taught in the state-of-the-art James A. Haslam II Business Building. The college has experienced exceptional growth, more than doubling since the Haslam Business Building opened. To meet the needs of this growth, we are hiring new faculty and constructing a 240,000 square foot additional business building, Boyd Hall, which will open in 2028. The Department of Management and Entrepreneurship offers two undergraduate degrees (Management and Human Resource Management), a master’s degree in management and human resources, two university minors (Entrepreneurship and Social Entrepreneurship), and the Ph.D. degree in Strategy, Entrepreneurship, & Organizations. Research faculty members include Savannah Asay, Brian Bergman, Melissa Cardon, David Gras, Joseph Harrison, Christina Hymer, Tom Lumpkin, Eduardo Melendez, Alex Miller, Tim Munyon (Department Head), Tim Pollock, Anne Smith, Jinyuan Song, Ruixiang Song, Sherry Thatcher, and Dave Williams. Our faculty publish in top journals including AMJ, AMR, ASQ, JAP, JBV, JIBS, JOM, Org Science, PPsych, and SMJ. The Knoxville Area Knoxville is located in east Tennessee, with a metro area population of more than 900,000. Knoxville enjoys a mild climate, a beautiful and walkable downtown, accessible waterways, varied nightlife, active neighborhoods, and eclectic shopping and restaurants. Knoxville has been consistently named as one of the top 25 best places to live by USA Today and a top 10 “America’s best towns” by CNN Travel. It is home to several large corporations and located within easy driving distance of Oak Ridge National Laboratory and the Great Smoky Mountain National Park. Asheville, Atlanta, Charlotte, and Nashville are readily accessible by car, and the Knoxville airport offers direct flights to 40 destinations. Required Candidates must hold a Ph.D. from an accredited institution and demonstrate evidence of quality scholarship and teaching effectiveness in Strategic Management or a related discipline. The Ph.D. must be earned by August 1, 2027. Candidates should show promise in developing a program in disciplinary research and scholarship that is gaining external recognition. Preferred Our preferred candidates will possess: An ongoing program of research in premier refereed journals. A desire to work with doctoral students. An identifiable methodological skillset. A record of demonstrated teaching success at an accredited institution. Essential Duties and Responsibilities Engage in strategy research that results in premier publications. Teach undergraduate and graduate courses in management and strategy. Participate in professional and university service activities. Candidates applying by August 7, 2026 will receive a complete review of their credentials. We will continue reviewing applications until the position is filled. We will conduct preliminary interviews via videoconference and will invite the finalists for the position to campus. We will not be conducting interviews nor meeting with applicants at academic conferences (e.g., AOM). Applicants should submit a cover letter, current curriculum vitae, and the names and contact information of three references via Interfolio. In your cover letter, please explain your fit for the position. At the end of your CV, please list the names and contact information of three references. Reference letters are not needed at the time of application. Use the following link to apply for the position: https://apply.interfolio.com/189276 ---------- Dr. Joseph Harrison, Search Committee Chair Department of Management & Entrepreneurship Haslam College of Business 417 Stokely Management Center University of Tennessee, Knoxville Knoxville, TN 37996-0545"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194496": {
      "title": "Open Rank, Non-Tenure Track Faculty, College of Communication and Information (Fall 2027)",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: College of Communication and Information Location: University of Tennessee, Knoxville The College of Communication and Information (CCI) at the University of Tennessee invites applications and nominations for a non-tenure track faculty position to start August 1, 2027. The position will be under one of the following: the lecturer series, the teaching professor series, or the faculty of practice series, with classification and rank depending upon the candidate’s qualifications. The selected candidate will be appointed to the school within CCI that most closely aligns with their training, qualifications, and experience. The primary responsibility of NTT faculty is teaching. This position will teach undergraduate classes in the new college-wide professional development series that launched in fall 2025 and help students outside of the classroom enhance their career readiness skills. Working with a curriculum managed by the CCI Office of Professional Development, the position will teach courses in the 3-course series (CCI 101, CCI 201, and CCI 401) and collaborate with other professors to ensure consistent facilitation of the course series. Additionally, this position will teach college-wide introductory course for non-majors, CCI 150. The CCI professional development series is comprised of three, one-hour courses that creates a cohesive professional development approach and embeds professional development in a student's academic journey so that all CCI majors are: Exposed to information and experiences that will improve their success as students and graduates Develop an appreciation for their major as well as how their major fits into the larger field of communication and information Encouraged to explore; tying students’ Clifton Strengths to their academic, co-curricular, and career explorations; and fosters greater development of career competencies Integrating career exploration into the academic experience early for CCI students The College of Communication and Information The College of Communication and Information (CCI) consists of four schools: Advertising and Public Relations, Communication Studies, Information Sciences, and Journalism and Media. CCI has approximately 1,700 undergraduate majors (across six undergraduate majors in advertising, public relations, communication studies, information sciences, journalism and media and the online BSCI) and approximately 500 graduate students. The University of Tennessee and Knoxville The University of Tennessee is the state of Tennessee’s flagship university and a public Carnegie Research 1 institution. As a land-grant university and as Volunteers, serving the state and nation is key to its mission. The University culture is guided by adherence to core values that define the Volunteer spirit and include seeking knowledge, leading with innovation and integrity, engaging locally and globally, and embracing responsible stewardship of resources. Nestled in the foothills of the Great Smoky Mountains, Knoxville has a metropolitan statistical area population of more than 850,000 people. This moderate-sized city has a vibrant nightlife that includes numerous theaters and museums, a great music scene, a symphony orchestra and an opera company, world-class restaurants, many cultural festivals, and a downtown market square. Surrounded by eight lakes and the Tennessee River, the Knoxville area boasts wonderful year-round water activities, including boating, fishing, and swimming that make for scenic and breathtaking views of the area. Knoxville is also home to many leading corporations, including Warner Brothers/Discovery, Regal Cinemas, Bush Brothers & Company, Tennessee Valley Authority, Ruby Tuesday, Pilot Flying J, and Alcoa. It also has several prominent advertising/public relations firms and is the fifth largest video production center in the United States. Required : The ideal cand idate will have a strong interest in student success and must be able to work effectively across all levels of the University. Preferred : Demonstrated effectiveness in teaching at the undergraduate level and experience effectively engaging many students (course materials, course design, teaching evaluations, etc.). Prior experience delivering professional development programs or experiential learning opportunities, such as internships, service-learning courses, etc. Ability to engage students from their freshman year to senior experience . Industry experience is desirable but not required . Per the University of Tennessee faculty handbook, each NTT series is described below: Lecturer Series: Faculty appointed to this title series hold a degree (or its professional equivalent) appropriate to their disciplines. Lecturer: Individuals holding this rank have shown promise for excellence in areas of responsibility assigned to them. Senior lecturer: Individuals holding this rank have demonstrated excellence in areas of responsibility assigned to them. Distinguished lecturer: Individuals holding this rank have demonstrated and maintained a consistent record of excellence in areas of responsibility assigned to them. Teaching Professor Series: Faculty appointed under this title series hold the terminal degree appropriate to their disciplines at time of appointment. The following ranks or titles may be assigned: teaching assistant professor, teaching associate professor, and teaching professor. Teaching assistant professor: Individuals holding this rank have shown promise for excellence in areas of responsibility assigned to them. Teaching associate professor: Individuals holding this rank have demonstrated excellence in areas of responsibility assigned to them. Teaching professor: Individuals holding this rank have demonstrated a consistent record of excellence in areas of responsibility assigned to them. Faculty of Practice Series: Appointment to the faculty of practice title series is for those who have achieved distinction in their chosen field of practice, and who bring to the university unique practical experiences and talents that will benefit students. The expectation is that those appointed to a faculty of practice position have professional experience in the field. The following ranks or titles may be assigned to faculty of practice: assistant professor of practice, associate professor of practice, and professor of practice. Faculty appointed to this title series should hold the terminal degree in the field at time of appointment and/or have equivalent capabilities by virtue of professional experience, professional licensure and/or certification. Assistant professor of practice: Individuals holding this rank have shown promise for excellence in areas of responsibility assigned to them. Associate professor of practice: Individuals holding this rank have demonstrated excellence in areas of responsibility assigned to them. Professor of practice: Individuals holding this rank have demonstrated and maintained a consistent record of excellence in areas of responsibility assigned to them. To apply, please submit a letter of application with a cover letter indicating interest, curriculum vitae or resume, and the names and contact information of at least three references on Interfolio . Additionally, if you have classroom experience, you may send teaching evaluations. Please email cci@utk.edu if you have any questions about the position. Review of applications begins November 1, 2026 , and continues until the position is filled. This institution is using Interfolio's Faculty Search to conduct this search. Applicants to this position receive a free Dossier account and can send all application materials, including confidential letters of recommendation, free of charge."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192339": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Educational Leadership, Fall 2027",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: College of Education, Health, and Human Sciences: Educational Leadership & Policy Studies Location: Knoxville, TN The College of Education, Health, and Human Sciences at The University of Tennessee is seeking applicants for an Assistant Professor to join the Department of Educational Leadership and Policy Studies for a nine-month tenure-track faculty position at the rank of assistant professor beginning in August 2027. We are looking for a pre-K-12 leadership faculty member, preferably with training and expertise in special education, to contribute to the master’s, EdS, EdD, and PhD degree programs focused on educational leadership. Duties and responsibilities include: Maintaining a cutting-edge scholarly research agenda Supporting the graduate programs in Educational Leadership and Policy Studies through teaching and advising Participating in professional associations at the local, state, regional, and national/international levels Service to the department, college, university, and other educational organizations Collaborating with college and department faculty and staff and with school districts and school administrators. The College of Education, Health and Human Sciences is a multidisciplinary unit with 90+ tenured/tenure-track faculty members, housed in six departments. Educational Leadership and Policy Studies is a thriving department with more than 200 graduate students and 20 full-time faculty working with programs in pre-K-12 education, higher education, adult and continuing education, and evaluation, statistics, and methodology, and an undergraduate minor in leadership studies. The Department is an active member of the University Council for Educational Administration, and its licensure programs are accredited by CAEP. Located near the beautiful Smoky Mountains, The University of Tennessee, Knoxville (UTK) was founded in 1794 and is one of the oldest public universities in the country. UTK is a vibrant and diverse urban campus, located in the heart of Knoxville. It is the flagship campus for the UT system, a participating institution of the Association of Public and Land-grant Universities (APLU), and is officially designated as a Research (Very High Research Activity) and Community Engagement University by the Carnegie Foundation. For more information about UTK and Knoxville, please visit: http://www.utk.edu/aboutut/ Review of applications will begin on October 15, 2026 , and continue until the position is filled. Inquiries about the position should be directed to: Dr. Mary Lynne Derrington Associate Professor and Search Committee Chair mderring@utk.edu Required qualifications include: A doctorate (completed by August 2027) in educational administration, educational leadership, or a related field. Evidence of strong potential for scholarly productivity in the field of educational administration, including the strong potential to secure external funding. Experience working with educational leaders, as demonstrated through teaching, research, or employment history. Evidence of a strong potential for teaching excellence in graduate level leadership preparation programs. Demonstrated ability to work collegially with others, including faculty, staff, and students. A willingness to plan and deliver instruction in online formats. Preferred qualifications include: Experience teaching graduate level courses. Experience in pre-K-12 special education. Experience with distance learning methodologies. Experience as a pre-K-12 school leader. Experience with leading or teaching mixed or multiple methods research. To apply, please submit the following using the Interfolio Faculty Search platform: (1) a cover letter, which should consist of a summary of teaching and research and how the applicant meets the required and preferred qualifications, (2) a current curriculum vita, (3) a maximum of two publications, and (4) the names and contact information for three (3) references who are able to speak specifically to your qualifications for the position."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192584": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Kinesiology, Recreation, and Sport Studies, Fall 2027",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: College of Education, Health, and Human Sciences: Kinesiology Recreation & Sport Studies Location: Knoxville, TN Description : The Department of Kinesiology, Recreation, and Sport Studies at the University of Tennessee, Knoxville is seeking a nine-month, tenure-track Assistant Professor with a focus in socio-cultural aspects of sport. Duties/Responsibilities : - Teach undergraduate and graduate courses in socio-cultural foundations of sport and recreation, sociological aspects of sport, social theories in sport, and related areas of recreation and sport management - Conduct research leading to publication in referred journals - Participate in professional, university, and community service - Mentor undergraduate and graduate students and direct graduate projects, theses, and dissertations - Pursue external funding and grant writing when appropriate About the Department of Kinesiology, Recreation, and Sport Studies : The Department of Kinesiology, Recreation, and Sport Studies is home to over 50 faculty and staff members. There are two undergraduate majors: (1) Kinesiology and (2) Recreation and Sport Management. The department has more than 2,000 undergraduate majors, along with multiple master's degree programs and a PhD program in Kinesiology and Sport Studies, serving nearly 200 graduate students. Its doctoral program is ranked among the top 20 in the nation by the National Academy of Kinesiology. About the University of Tennessee, Knoxville : The University of Tennessee, Knoxville, is the state’s flagship institution, a campus of choice for outstanding undergraduates, and a premier graduate institution. The university is currently experiencing unprecedented growth in enrollment, with more than 41,000 students. The University is also a Carnegie Foundation Research (R1) University with high research activity and has achieved a Carnegie Classification for Community Engagement. Forbes Magazine recently ranked UT as one of the best places to work, including a ranking of ninth among public higher education institutions in the United States. The university is committed to excellence in learning, scholarship, and engagement with society. In all its activities, the university aims to advance the frontiers of human knowledge and enrich and elevate society. About Knoxville : The city of Knoxville is a hidden gem with a metro area population of more than 900,000. Located on the Tennessee River, Knoxville is surrounded by several gorgeous lakes and has a beautiful and walkable downtown, a diverse music scene including internationally recognized festivals, active neighborhoods, unique restaurants, and a robust offering of diverse cultural and outdoor activities. The university is located within easy driving distance to the Great Smoky Mountains, Asheville, Atlanta, and Nashville. Qualifications : We are seeking candidates who will pursue an innovative, rigorous research program; teach undergraduate and graduate courses; and provide department, college, and professional service. An earned doctorate in sport studies, sport management, or a related field is required at time of appointment. Candidates should have experience in, or potential for conducting high-impact research, publishing in top peer reviewed journals, and seeking external funding to support their scholarship. They should be passionate and enthusiastic toward student learning and demonstrate a commitment to teaching excellence. We are seeking applications from candidates with expertise related to socio-cultural aspects of sport. Required Qualifications : - Earned doctorate in sport studies, sport management, or a related field at time of appointment - Evidence of scholarly achievement/potential in area of expertise - Scholarly publications in high-quality peer-reviewed journals - Effective communication and interpersonal skills Desired Qualifications : - University teaching experience - Demonstrated commitment to professional organizations - Evidence of external funding Application Instructions : Review of applications will begin October 19 and continue until the position is filled.  Individuals interested in applying for this position should submit electronically at https://apply.interfolio.com/192584 . Submission materials should include a letter of application, curriculum vita, research statement, teaching statement, and list of three references who can address the applicant’s scholarly and teaching capabilities with complete addresses, phone numbers, and email addresses. Questions can be directed to Search Committee Chair: Dr. Rob Hardin Professor Department of Kinesiology, Recreation, and Sport Studies robh@utk.edu 865-974-3340 Equal Employment Opportunity Statement : All qualified applicants will receive equal consideration for employment and admission without regard to race, color, national origin, religion, sex, pregnancy, marital status, sexual orientation, gender identity, age, physical or mental disability, genetic information, veteran status, and parental status, or any other characteristic protected by federal or state law. In accordance with the requirements of Title VI of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972, Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, and the Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990, the University of Tennessee affirmatively states that it does not discriminate on the basis of race, sex, or disability in its education programs and activities, and this policy extends to employment by the university. Requests for accommodation of a disability should be directed to the ADA Coordinator at Equal Opportunity and Accessibility, 1840 Melrose Avenue, Knoxville, TN 37996-3560, by email to eoa@utk.edu, or by phone at 865-974-2498. Inquiries and charges of violation of Title VI (race, color, and national origin), Title IX (sex), Section 504 (disability), the ADA (disability), the Age Discrimination in Employment Act (age), sexual orientation, or veteran status should be directed to the Office of Investigation and Resolution, 216 Business Incubator Building, E J. Chapman Drive, Knoxville, TN 37996-3560, by email to investigations@utk.edu, or by phone at 865-974-0717."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/182795": {
      "title": "Associate / Full Professor, Center for Applied Digital Health and Optimization Methods, Nutrition, Spring 2027",
      "description": "Posted: Mar 09, 2026 Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: College of Education, Health, and Human Sciences: Nutrition - DO NOT USE - USE NPHS Location: Knoxville, TN The College of Education, Health, and Human Sciences (CEHHS) at the University of Tennessee Knoxville invites applications for a 12-month Tenure-Track faculty position at the rank of Associate Professor or Full Professor. This individual will serve as the Associate Director of the new Center for Applied Digital Health and Optimization Methods led by Dr. Graham Thomas. The Center for Applied Digital Health and Optimization Methods is designed to enhance the University of Tennessee Knoxville’s (UTK’s) national footprint in digital health and optimization methods. The Center’s work emphasizes the development and application of digital health technologies, such as Just-in-Time Adaptive Interventions (JITAIs), wearable sensors, and predictive analytics to address complex health challenges. This initiative leverages cutting-edge frameworks and methodologies including the Multiphase Optimization Strategy (MOST), Sequential Multiple Assignment Randomized Trials (SMART), Micro-Randomized Trials (MRTs), and control systems engineering approaches. This position will join a diverse network of faculty across the College of Education, Health, and Human Sciences (CEHHS) and will serve as a catalyst for multidisciplinary collaborations within CEHHS and across colleges. The Center is dedicated to four primary foci: 1.       Conducting and supporting externally funded research capitalizing on digital health; 2.       Supporting multidisciplinary collaborations to bridge disciplines such as behavioral science and engineering; 3.       Providing training in digital health and optimization methods as a part of undergraduate, graduate, and postdoctoral training programs; 4.       Promoting the uptake of digital health and optimization methods in health research both locally and in the health sciences broadly. This position carries a 12-month appointment with a primary faculty home in the merged Department of Nutrition and Public Health Sciences. Depending on the candidate's background and expertise, a joint appointment with another relevant department may be possible. The distribution of responsibilities is designed to ensure the successful leadership of the Center while maintaining a robust program of scholarly research and instruction: Center Leadership (35%) : The successful candidate will dedicate 35% of their effort to the leadership of the Center for Applied Digital Health and Optimization Methods as its Associate Director. In this capacity, the individual will provide high-level strategic direction to support the official launch and foundational growth of the Center within CEHHS. A key priority for this role is identifying and cultivating opportunities for multidisciplinary research collaborations that capitalize on digital health and optimization methods across the university, (e.g., with the Tickle College of Engineering, College of Arts and Sciences, College of Nursing). The Associate Director will provide instrumental support for the incorporation of digital health and optimization frameworks such as MOST, SMARTs, and MRTs into research studies and grant proposals to be submitted to funders such as the NIH and NSF that will be led by faculty across the college and university. The role also involves co-developing center-led training initiatives designed to enhance the proficiency of students at all levels, as well as faculty, in applied digital health and optimization science. Research & Scholarship (30%) : The candidate is expected to maintain an independent and impactful research program involving digital health and/or optimization methods. For example, this may include applying frameworks and methodologies such as MOST, SMART, and MRTs, or development and testing of just-in-time adaptive interventions (JITAIs) and wearable sensor technologies. Teaching and Instructional Responsibilities (25%) : Beginning in the second year of the appointment, the successful candidate will provide high-quality instruction, maintaining a standard 1:1 annual teaching load. Consistent with the University’s commitment to research, there may be opportunities for instructional buyout or teaching relief via externally funded research. Beyond formal classroom instruction, a key component of this role involves providing mentorship to postdoctoral fellows within the Center for Applied Digital Health and Optimization Methods. Additionally, depending on the candidate's specific interests and the evolving needs of training programs, there may be opportunities to mentor undergraduate and/or graduate students. Service (10%) : The successful candidate will have opportunities to provide service at the departmental, college, and university levels. This may include participation in faculty governance, contributing to departmental and college-level committees, and supporting the broader administrative goals of the University. Other forms of professional service may include cultivating industry partnerships, participating in federal grant review panels, and promoting the uptake of digital health and optimization methods within the broader scientific community. This 12-month, fulltime (1.0 FTE), position begins February 1, 2027 and is negotiable. Starting salary is commensurate with experience. UTK has a competitive benefits package. The start-up package is based upon scholarship needs. UTK is Tennessee's flagship land grant university and is currently in the midst of unprecedented growth in enrollment, with approximately 38,000 enrolled students. The University is also a Carnegie Foundation Research (R1) University with the highest research activity and has achieved a Carnegie Classification for Community Engagement. We seek an established and recognized scholar whose work targets advancing health outcomes through the innovative application of digital health and/or optimization methods. Candidates must have earned their doctorate in psychology, public health, epidemiology, biostatistics, computer science, engineering, or a related discipline in the social, behavioral, or technical sciences. Candidates must meet the specific qualifications for these ranks as defined by the UTK Faculty Handbook. Candidates for the Associate Professor rank must have an established research agenda, a strong record of scholarly activity, and successful experience acquiring extramural funding (e.g., NIH, NSF) as a lead investigator. Candidates for the Full Professor rank must demonstrate an extensive record of extramural funding as a lead investigator, an extensive record of scholarly productivity and national/international recognition, and proven experience in successful faculty mentoring and leadership within team science environments. Preference will be given to candidates with a robust history of successfully applying and supporting the applied use of optimization methods such as the Multiphase Optimization Strategy (MOST), Sequential Multiple Assignment Randomized Trials (SMART), and/or Micro-Randomized Trials (MRT). Candidates with demonstrated experience in productive interdisciplinary collaborations, successful team leadership, and administrative experience in a research center environment are highly desired. Additional preferred qualifications include expertise in developing Just-in-Time Adaptive Interventions (JITAIs), the use of wearable sensor technologies, experience with control systems engineering approaches, and a proven history of cultivating successful industry partnerships to advance digital health research. Review of applications will begin on July 6, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. Individuals interested in applying for this position should submit electronically at apply.interfolio.com/182795 , and include a letter of application outlining how the candidate fits the qualifications and expectations of the position, curriculum vita, and list of three references who can address the applicant’s capabilities with complete addresses, phone numbers, and email addresses. Questions can be directed to Dr. Hollie Raynor, search committee chair, at hraynor@utk.edu."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191956": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor, Food and Consumer Behavior in Retail, Hospitality & Tourism Management Position, Fall 2027",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: College of Education, Health, and Human Sciences: Retail, Hospitality, and Tourism Mgmt Location: Knoxville, TN The Department of Retail, Hospitality, and Tourism Management (RHTM), in the College of Education, Health and Human Sciences (CEHHS) at the University of Tennessee, Knoxville (UTK) seeks applications for a nine-month, tenure-track Assistant or Associate Professor in the Department of Retail, Hospitality and Tourism Management. This position will be part of a cluster hire that will strengthen the University of Tennessee’s ability to develop a national reputation in the area of food and nutrition security, using a food systems perspective. The cluster will include six faculty in the home units of Nutrition or Public Health; Retail, Hospitality and Tourism Management; Agricultural and Resource Economics; Food Science; and Family and Consumer Sciences. Faculty hired as part of this cluster will collaboratively work across the University of Tennessee Knoxville (UTK) and University of Tennessee Institute of Agriculture (UTIA) campuses, using a team science approach, in building a strategic plan that emphasizes enhancing collaboration and impact by creating an interdisciplinary, translational human health and wellness identity at UT in food and nutrition security. This strategic plan will include a focus on interdisciplinary academic programming and scholarship. Within the area of scholarship, this cluster hire will help lead the University of Tennessee in building a research enterprise that includes community-engaged scholarship and research that enhances population representativeness. The focus of this position in the Department of Retail, Hospitality and Tourism Management will be household food acquisition and consumption behavior with implications for food retailer behavior and household food security or health, as well as topics related to community food availability. REQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS Requirements include an earned doctorate in retail, hospitality, or a closely related field Tenure-Track Associate Professors will have achieved and maintain a recognized record in disciplinary research/scholarship, have achieved and maintained a record of institutional and professional service, and show evidence that they work well with students, colleagues, and interdisciplinary teams. In most cases they will have served as an assistant professor for at least five years. Tenure-Track Assistant Professors should show promise as teachers, show promise of developing a program in disciplinary research/scholarship commensurate with appointment, have a developing record of institutional and/or professional service, and show evidence that they work well with students, colleagues, and interdisciplinary teams. DESIRED QUALIFICATIONS: Demonstrated experience in productive interdisciplinary collaborations and experience in a team science environment Screening of applications will begin on October 1, 2026 and continue until the position is filled. The expected start date for the position is August 1, 2027. To apply, submit the following materials: (1) a letter detailing your interest in the position, (2) a current curriculum vitae, and (3) three letters of reference through the Interfolio link. Please submit all applications materials to Direct any questions regarding the position to Dr. Youn-Kyung Kim, Search Committee Chair, Department of Retail, Hospitality & Tourism Management, yk im13@utk.edu."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192253": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in STEM (Mathematics) Education (Tenure Track), Fall 2027",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: College of Education, Health, and Human Sciences: Theory & Practice in Teacher Education Location: Knoxville, TN, United States Assistant Professor in STEM (Mathematics) Education (Tenure Track) Department of Theory and Practice in Teacher Education College of Education, Health, and Human Science The University of Tennessee, Knoxville The Department of Theory and Practice in Teacher Education (TPTE) in the College of Education, Health and Human Sciences at The University of Tennessee seeks a dynamic scholar for a full-time (9-month), tenure-track assistant professor position in STEM education with expertise in mathematics education. About the Program The University of Tennessee is an R1 doctoral institution with very high research activity and considerable emphasis on community engagement. The Department of TPTE brings together experts across several education fields to prepare individuals for impactful careers in educational settings and beyond, to bridge innovative research and practice, and to serve communities through educational excellence. The department is organized into three units, and this position resides within the Interdisciplinary Learning and Teaching (ILT) Unit. Collaboration across the units is frequent and expected.  ILT programs span early childhood education, STEM education, learning design and technology, and computing, AI and data science in education. Our programs are led by twelve tenure-track/tenured faculty and eleven full-time clinical faculty and instructors. We are brought together by our embrace of interdisciplinary approaches to education across classroom, community, and digital contexts and a dedication to educational innovation from cradle to career. Our STEM education faculty have unique opportunities to work with two research centers, the Center for Enhancing Education in Mathematics and Science (CEEMS) and the AI, Data, and Educational Partnerships (ADEPT) center to develop collaborations across colleges and departments, school districts, and community organizations to enhance PreK-16 STEM education and support community-engaged scholarship. Position Details We desire a new colleague who engages in substantial research to address important challenges facing students, teachers, families, schools, and communities in PreK-16 mathematics education. The candidate’s research may focus on (but is not limited to): 1) mathematics teaching and/or learning through STEM integration, especially with a focus on connecting mathematics and engineering, emerging technologies, or career/technical education; 2) the influence of shifting educational policies or broader societal trends on mathematics teaching and learning; 3) the preparation of educators for mathematics instruction in formal school or informal contexts; 4) mathematics learning as understood through innovative theories and methodologies. Responsibilities include: teaching preservice teachers in mathematics education courses; teaching and advising master’s and doctoral students; conducting and publishing research on PreK-16 mathematics or STEM education; pursuing and obtaining external funding to build a strong research agenda; working cooperatively and developing relationships with other university departments and organizations related to STEM education; providing service and leadership in STEM education programs; and participating in STEM education outreach and engagement activities. Qualifications Candidates must have a doctoral degree in Mathematics Education, or a related field, by August 2027; substantive teaching experience in an educational context relevant to their expertise; a record of scholarship and pursuit of external funding commensurate with experience; and a demonstrated commitment to collaborations across research and/or teaching grounded in trusting, respectful, and mutually beneficial relationships with colleagues and communities. In addition, applicants must demonstrate knowledge of the current landscape of mathematics education, including key challenges and future directions. Application Process Interested applicants should submit: 1) a letter of interest outlining their research and teaching experiences and qualifications for the position (maximum of 2 pages, single-spaced); 2) a research statement detailing their scholarly agenda, including past and current research as well as future directions (maximum of 2 pages, single-spaced); 3) a curriculum vita (not to exceed 10 pages) that demonstrates qualifications and fit for the position; and 4) the names and contact information for three references who are prepared to submit letters of recommendation for semi-finalists. Application materials should be submitted electronically via the Interfolio portal to [link]. General inquiries can be sent directly to Dr. Frances K. Harper, Search Committee Chair, at fharper1@utk.edu . Review of materials will begin on October 16, 2026 and continue until the position is filled. The position is available beginning Fall Semester, 2027. For more information about the College of Education, Health, and Human Sciences, please visit http://cehhs.utk.edu/ Note regarding the use of generative AI: The committee understands that some candidates may elect to use generative AI to help prepare application materials. We view using AI for brainstorming, editing, and formatting as appropriate and ethical; however, candidates should not submit fully unedited, AI-generated text or falsified information."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192263": {
      "title": "Assistant or Associate Professor of Literacy Education (Tenure-Track), Fall 2027",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: College of Education, Health, and Human Sciences: Theory & Practice in Teacher Education Location: Knoxville, TN, United States Assistant/Associate Professor of Literacy Education (Tenure-Track) Theory and Practice in Teacher Education College of Education, Health, and Human Sciences The University of Tennessee, Knoxville The College of Education, Health and Human Sciences at The University of Tennessee is seeking an assistant or associate professor for a position in literacy education in the Department of Theory and Practice in Teacher Education (TPTE). We seek a colleague to join an active research and teaching community. Salary is competitive and commensurate with experience and qualifications. Position begins August 2027. The College of Education, Health, and Human Sciences is a multidisciplinary unit with faculty members housed in eight departments. TPTE, the College’s largest department, brings together experts across various education fields to prepare individuals for impactful careers in educational settings and beyond, to bridge innovative research and practice, and to serve communities through educational excellence. The department is organized into three units, and this position resides within the Language, Culture, and Teacher Education (LCTE) Unit. Literacy education faculty conduct consequential research on reading and writing interventions, assessment development, motivation and affect, teacher preparation and professional development. Further, faculty members are committed to preparing educators for effective and engaging teaching in high need urban and rural communities. The successful candidate will join an active, collaborative, and interdisciplinary faculty committed to improving public education through exemplary research, teaching, and service. The successful candidate has a strong understanding of current literacy research and effective practices for teaching literacy in K-12 educational settings. Responsibilities include : ● Designing, conducting and publishing scholarly research ● Pursuing external funding to support an active research agenda ● Teaching undergraduate and/or graduate classes in literacy education ● Collaborating with a team of faculty in development, delivery, and ongoing program evaluation of undergraduate and graduate courses ● Developing collaborative relationships with local schools, teachers, and community members ● Providing service and leadership to local/state and national/international professional organizations ● Fulfilling advising and administrative duties as needed by the department and the university Required Qualifications at the Assistant Professor Rank : ● Demonstrated content area expertise in literacy education, drawing on evidence-based practices ● Evidence of scholarly potential and potential for external funding ● Evidence of supporting K-12 literacy teaching and/or teacher preparation ● An earned doctorate by start date in literacy education, reading education, language and literacy, or a closely related field with a scholarly focus on literacy ● A commitment to working as part of a collaborative team on research and teaching Required Qualifications at the Associate Professor Rank : ● All of the required qualifications listed for the assistant professor rank ● Demonstrated record of scholarship and external funding ● Five or more years of experience as an assistant professor in literacy education or closely related field Desired Qualifications: Additional desired qualifications for assistant or associate ranks include: ● Evidence of successful work with teacher education students in culturally and linguistically diverse contexts ● A record of scholarship and expertise/experience in grant writing ● A literacy/reading specialist advanced degree or endorsement ● Expertise in research related to multilingual learners, writing development, or reading-writing connections Review of applications will begin on November 1, 2026 and continue until the position is filled. Please direct questions about the position to the search committee co-chair, Dr. Marianne Rice, Assistant Professor of Literacy Education , mrice44@utk.edu . Please apply online at https://apply.interfolio.com/192263 and include a letter expressing your interest and describing your qualifications, current curriculum vitae, names and contact information of three references, and two scholarly writing samples (e.g., reprints of publications, manuscripts, or works in progress). Applications received outside of the Interfolio system via Indeed or any other application website or system will not be considered. For more information about the College of Education, Health, and Human Sciences, please visit http://cehhs.utk.edu . Note regarding the use of generative AI: The committee understands that some candidates may elect to use generative AI to help prepare application materials. We view using AI for brainstorming, editing, and formatting as appropriate and ethical; however, candidates should not submit fully unedited, AI-generated text or falsified information."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192264": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Research Methodology Cluster Hire, Fall 2027",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: College of Education, Health, and Human Sciences: Theory & Practice in Teacher Education Location: Knoxville, TN, United States The College of Education, Health, and Human Sciences at The University of Tennessee, Knoxville seeks applicants for three nine-month, tenure-track Assistant Professor positions beginning August 1, 2027. These positions are part of a strategic investment in research methodology to strengthen graduate education and research across the college. We welcome applications from early career scholars with an active and rigorous research agenda in one or more of the following areas: Advanced Quantitative Methods Education Data Science Methods Qualitative Research Methods We are interested in candidates whose work addresses emerging methodological challenges, critically examines how research methods shape educational inquiry, and explores the opportunities, limitations, and implications of artificial intelligence for research. We seek scholars who are committed to preparing the next generation of researchers. The Qualitative Research Methods and Advanced Quantitative Methods positions will be housed in the Evaluation, Statistics, and Methodology program in the Department of Educational Leadership and Policy Studies. The Education Data Science Methods position will be housed in the Computing, AI, and Data Analytics in Education program in the Department of Theory and Practice in Teacher Education. Program faculty work collaboratively across departments to provide methodology courses throughout the College. Duties and responsibilities include: Maintaining an active and innovative scholarly research agenda. Pursuing external funding opportunities to support one’s research agenda. Teaching undergraduate and graduate-level research methods courses. Advising master’s and doctoral students and serving on graduate committees. Contributing to the development and refinement of research methods curricula, programs, and initiatives. Engaging in service to the department, college, university, profession, and broader educational community. Required qualifications include: A doctorate (completed by August 2027) in educational research, evaluation, statistics, data science, qualitative inquiry, survey methodology, educational measurement, educational psychology, learning sciences, or a closely related field. Evidence of, or potential for, an active and rigorous scholarly program, including the potential to secure external funding. Demonstrated expertise in one or more of the following areas: Advanced quantitative methods (e.g., Bayesian methods, longitudinal or growth modeling, latent variable modeling), survey methodology, assessment, measurement, or related approaches; Education data science, computational approaches for analyzing educational data, the use of AI as a research method, natural language processing, or related approaches; or Qualitative research methodologies, with experience in multiple qualitative research designs and traditions of inquiry (e.g., basic qualitative research, case study, narrative, phenomenology, ethnographic methods, grounded theory, critical/discourse approaches). Evidence of potential for excellence in teaching and mentoring. Demonstrated ability to work collegially with others, including faculty, staff, and students. Willingness to teach in multiple instructional formats, including in-person, online, and hybrid environments. Preferred qualifications include: Experience teaching graduate-level research methods courses. Scholars interested in the intersection(s) between quantitative methods, education data science, and qualitative methods Experience mentoring student research projects. Record of, or strong potential for, pursuing or securing externally funded research. Experience with multiple statistical software packages (e.g., R, Python, SPSS, Stata, SAS), qualitative data analysis software (e.g., Dedoose, NVivo, Atlas.ti, MAXQDA), or other computational tools and emerging technologies, such as artificial intelligence, relevant to the candidate's area of expertise. To apply, please submit the following using the Interfolio Faculty Search platform at Interfolio LINK (1) a two page cover letter, which should consist of a summary of how the applicant meets the required and preferred qualifications, and identify the research area for which the candidate would like to be considered (2) a curriculum vitae, (3) one representative publication, and (4) the names and contact information for three references who are able to speak to the candidate’s qualifications for the position. No other submitted documents will be considered. Note regarding the use of generative AI : The committee understands that some candidates may elect to use generative AI to help prepare application materials. We view using AI for brainstorming, editing, and formatting as appropriate and ethical; however, candidates should not submit fully unedited, AI-generated text or falsified information. The College of Education, Health and Human Sciences is a multidisciplinary unit with 90+ tenured/tenure-track faculty members, housed in six departments. Our Departments of Educational Leadership and Policy Studies and Theory and Practice in Teacher Education collaborate extensively across research, graduate education, and methodological training. The College is committed to advancing innovative research and preparing scholars who address consequential educational and societal challenges. Located near the beautiful Smoky Mountains, The University of Tennessee, Knoxville (UTK) was founded in 1794 and is one of the oldest public universities in the country. UTK is a vibrant and diverse urban campus located in the heart of Knoxville. It is the flagship campus for the UT system, a participating institution of the Association of Public and Land-grant Universities (APLU) and is officially designated as a Research (Very High Research Activity) and Community Engagement University by the Carnegie Foundation. For more information about UTK and Knoxville, please visit: http://www.utk.edu/aboutut/ Review of applications will begin on October 15, 2026 , and continue until the position is filled. Inquiries about the position should be directed to co-chairs: Dr. Louis Rocconi Associate Professor and Search Committee Co-Chair lrocconi@utk.edu Dr. Josh Rosenberg Associate Professor and Search Committee Co-Chair jrosenb8@utk.edu"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192908": {
      "title": "Professor & Associate Dean for Research",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: Tickle College of Engineering Location: Knoxville, TN The College of Engineering at the University of Tennessee, Knoxville, invites applications for the position of Professor & Associate Dean for Research (ADR). Reporting directly to the Dean, the ADR will provide broad leadership and direction for all research/technology-related activities including grants/contracts proposals, funding opportunities, partnership agreements, and statistical data analysis related to the research enterprise. The ADR will work closely with the Dean, other associate deans, department heads, and other members in the college leadership team to support faculty development and mentoring, development efforts, and other strategic priorities in support of the college's mission. Reporting to the ADR is an eight-person pre-award team to support faculty proposal submission and a two-person research development team to facilitate funding successes for early career members, plus assist the development of collaborative research teams for larger funding opportunities. This Engineering Research Office is well respected for its quality service to the TCE faculty and College’s research success ( https://tickle.utk.edu/research/support/) . Expectations for this position broadly include maintaining excellent pre-award and faculty support for proposal development, as well as strategic conception, planning, and execution of initiatives designed to elevate the College's research enterprise over time. About the Tickle College of Engineering The Tickle College of Engineering (TCE) is in the midst of an unprecedented period of growth and success, including adding more than 30 new faculty at all levels to the college over 4 years as part of ambitious hiring campaigns led by Chancellor Donde Plowman and Dean Matthew Mench. The college has set records in research expenditures, enrollment, incoming student GPA, intellectual property development, and USNWR rank in the past three years. Key partnerships with Oak Ridge National Laboratory (ORNL) through the University of Tennessee Oak Ridge Innovation Institute (UT-ORII) and Consolidated Nuclear Security (CNS) that manages the Y-12 National Security Complex have created an expanded opportunity space for faculty and students in the region.   New facilities include the state-of-the art Zeanah Engineering Complex, the University of Tennessee Manufacturing and Design Enterprise (TN-MADE) facility, and the Innovation South building that houses UTK’s Fibers and Composites Manufacturing Facility (FCMF), a National Prototype Research Center (NSPC) as part of a partnership with CNS Y-12 under construction, and new facilities for the Biomedical Engineering department and a quantum foundry in planning stages. TCE currently has 213 tenured/tenure track and more than 80 non-tenure track faculty in its nine academic departments and offers 11 undergraduate, 16 MS, and 15 PhD/DE degree programs. Affiliated with TCE and located in Tullahoma, Tennessee, the UT Space Institute is a hub of aerospace and defense research. The college is also home to eight research centers and three interdisciplinary institutes. With approximately 4,800 undergraduate and 1,500 graduate students, the college sits 28th among public universities in the most recent U.S. News and World Report graduate rankings. Faculty in the college have won 33 early career awards (NSF, DOE, DARPA, AFOSR, AHA, and Sloan) since 2016. In FY25, the college had annual research expenditures of $113.9M. Knoxville, TN is a vibrant city with a beautiful and walkable downtown, active neighborhoods, an effervescent nightlife that includes numerous theaters and museums, a rich live music scene across all genres, and eclectic restaurants. Knoxville is nestled in the foothills of the Great Smoky Mountains, surrounded by lakes and the Tennessee River, providing amazing access to various outdoor activities. Knoxville is within easy driving distance of four major metropolitan areas that regularly host larger cultural and entertainment events. Terminal degree in engineering or a closely related field and qualifications appropriate for holding the rank of full professor in one of the College’s academic units ( https://tickle.utk.edu/academics/departments/ ) Demonstrated leadership and administrative skills Strong interpersonal and communication skills Strong commitment to engineering research and education Demonstrated experience in scholarly work and excellence in research Applications should be submitted electronically at apply.interfolio.com/192908 . Applications should include: a cover letter describing your interest in the position and relevant experience (up to 2 pages) a research leadership statement describing your vision for the role and your leadership style (up to 3 pages) a curriculum vitae the names and contact information of three professional references Questions should be sent to Dr. Dacheng Ren, Search Committee Chair, at dren3@utk.edu. Review of applications will begin on October 1, 2026, and continue until the position has been filled. The college anticipates filling the position for appointment effective January 1, 2027, or shortly thereafter."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191046": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Chemical and Biomolecular Engineering Fall 2027",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: Tickle College of Engineering: Chemical  & Biomolecular Engineering Location: University of Tennessee, Knoxville  The Department of Chemical and Biomolecular Engineering (CBE) at the University of Tennessee, Knoxville seeks outstanding candidates for a tenure‑track assistant professor position in advanced energy storage and conversion—including batteries, electrochemistry, manufacturing, and related areas. Candidates must show strong potential for impactful research, effective teaching, and meaningful professional service. A Ph.D. in chemical engineering or a closely related field is required. UT Knoxville is the state’s flagship research institution, and the CBE Department maintains strong programs in advanced materials, biomolecular engineering, and sustainable energy. We welcome applicants who will strengthen existing areas or introduce new research directions. Extensive collaboration opportunities exist across campus, with Oak Ridge National Laboratory (ORNL), and with regional industry partners such as Eastman and Volkswagen. Additional interdisciplinary avenues include University of Tennessee–Oak Ridge Innovation Institute (UT‑ORII), Institute for Advanced Materials & Manufacturing (IAMM), and the Center for Renewable Carbon. The Tickle College of Engineering (TCE) is in the midst of an unprecedented period of growth and success, including adding over 30 new faculty at all levels to the college over 4 years as part of ambitious hiring campaigns led by Chancellor Donde Plowman and Dean Matthew Mench. The college has set records in research expenditures, enrollment, incoming student GPA, intellectual property development, and USNWR rank in the past three years. Key partnerships with Oak Ridge National Lab (ORNL), through UT-ORII, and Consolidated Nuclear Security (CNS), which manages the Y-12 National Security Complex, have created an expanded opportunity space for faculty and students in the region. New facilities include the state-of-the art Zeanah Engineering Complex, the University of Tennessee Manufacturing and Design Enterprise (TN-MADE) facility, and the Innovation South building that houses UTK’s Fibers and Composites Manufacturing Facility (FCMF), a National Prototype Research Center (NSPC) as part of a partnership with CNS Y-12 under construction, and new facilities for the Biomedical Engineering department and a quantum foundry in planning stages. TCE currently has 213 tenure/tenure track and over 80 non-tenure track faculty in its nine academic departments and offers 11 undergraduate, 16 MS, and 15 PhD/DE degree programs. Affiliated with TCE and located in Tullahoma, Tennessee, the UT Space Institute is a hub of aerospace and defense research. The college is also home to eight research centers and three interdisciplinary institutes. With approximately 4,800 undergraduate and 1,500 graduate students, the college sits 28th among public universities in the most recent U.S. News and World Report graduate rankings. Faculty in the college have won 33 early career awards (NSF, DOE, DARPA, AFOSR, AHA, and Sloan) since 2016. In FY25, the college had annual research expenditures of $113.9M. Knoxville offers a vibrant downtown, a strong arts and music scene, and abundant outdoor recreation. The region’s population exceeds 850,000, and the city is within easy reach of the Great Smoky Mountains and major metropolitan areas. A Ph.D. or equivalent doctoral-level degree in chemical engineering or a closely related field is required by the appointment start date. Candidates should submit the following documents: • Cover letter • Curriculum vitae • Statement of research • Statement of teaching • A PDF copy of the applicant’s best work (1 paper only) • Names and contact information of at least three references Review of applications will begin on November 2, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. Applicant questions can be directed to search chair, Dr. Gabriel Veith at cbe@utk.edu."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192289": {
      "title": "Teaching Assistant Professor or Lecturer",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: Tickle College of Engineering: Civil & Environ Engr Location: Knoxville, TN Teaching Faculty Position in Civil Engineering and Professional Development The Department of Civil and Environmental Engineering (CEE) at the University of Tennessee, Knoxville (UTK) seeks to hire a teaching-focused faculty member whose responsibilities will combine technical instruction in the candidate’s area of engineering expertise with a significant role in the department’s undergraduate professional development curriculum. The preferred start date is January 1, 2027. Salary and benefits are nationally competitive and commensurate with experience and qualifications. The position is a non-tenure track, renewable, 9-month appointment, with opportunities for research or to teach additional courses each summer. The appointment title will be determined based on the successful candidate’s qualifications and experience, consistent with the UTK Faculty Handbook. The successful candidate is expected build an exceptional record of accomplishment in teaching and student engagement, leading to career-track promotions. The successful candidate will contribute to the department’s established, highly successful professional communications course for second-year undergraduates, which emphasizes technical writing, oral presentations, and student professional development through a high-engagement, feedback-intensive instructional model. Initially, the faculty member will work closely with experienced colleagues to learn and contribute to the course’s existing structure, teaching approach, and support systems while supporting course delivery, providing individualized student feedback, and participating in coordination activities. Over time, the candidate will assume leadership responsibilities for the course, including instructional development, teaching assistant recruitment and training, recitation coordination, team grading, and oversight of the associated writing-center structure. The faculty member will also teach courses in their technical area within civil and environmental engineering; expertise in construction, geotechnical, or structural engineering is especially aligned with current departmental needs. The department has 26 tenure-line faculty lines and 5 non-tenured full-time teaching faculty and offers baccalaureate, masters, and PhD degrees with concentrations in construction, environmental, geotechnical and materials, structures, transportation, and water resources engineering. The successful candidate will join a community of innovators in engineering education both in the department and in The Tickle College of Engineering and will be supported through a strong commitment to faculty development. The Tickle College of Engineering (TCE) is in the midst of an unprecedented period of growth and success, including adding over 30 new faculty at all levels to the college over 4 years as part of ambitious hiring campaigns led by Chancellor Donde Plowman and Dean Matthew Mench. The college has set records in research expenditures, enrollment, incoming student GPA, intellectual property development, and USNWR rank in the past three years. Key partnerships with Oak Ridge National Lab (ORNL)  through the University of Tennessee Oak Ridge Innovation Institute (UTORII) and Consolidated Nuclear Security (CNS) that manages the Y-12 National Security Complex have created an expanded opportunity space for faculty and students in the region.   New facilities include the state-of-the art Zeanah Engineering Complex, the University of Tennessee Manufacturing and Design Enterprise (TN-MADE) facility, and the Innovation South building that houses UTK’s Fibers and Composites Manufacturing Facility (FCMF), a National Prototype Research Center (NSPC) as part of a partnership with CNS Y-12 under construction, and new facilities for the Biomedical Engineering department and a quantum foundry in planning stages. TCE currently has 213 tenure/tenure track and over 80 non-tenure track faculty in its nine academic departments and offers 11 undergraduate, 16 MS, and 15 PhD/DE degree programs. Affiliated with TCE and located in Tullahoma, Tennessee, the UT Space Institute is a hub of aerospace and defense research. The college is also home to eight research centers and three interdisciplinary institutes. With approximately 4,800 undergraduate and 1,500 graduate students, the college sits 28th among public universities in the most recent U.S. News and World Report graduate rankings. Faculty in the college have won 33 early career awards (NSF, DOE, DARPA, AFOSR, AHA, and Sloan) since 2016. In FY25, the college had annual research expenditures of $113.9M. Knoxville, TN is a vibrant city with a beautiful and walkable downtown, active neighborhoods, an effervescent nightlife that includes numerous theaters and museums, a rich live music scene across all genres, and eclectic restaurants. Knoxville is nestled in the foothills of the Great Smoky Mountains, surrounded by lakes and the Tennessee River, providing amazing access to various outdoor activities. Knoxville is within easy driving distance of four major metropolitan areas that regularly host larger cultural and entertainment events. Responsibilities The standard teaching load is four courses per semester; however, teaching assignments are determined holistically and may account for course enrollment, number of sections, level of student engagement and feedback, grading responsibilities, team-teaching arrangements, and other departmental responsibilities. Faculty teaching assignments across the department are made with consideration given both to departmental needs and teaching interests of the faculty member. Immediate teaching priorities for this position are: Technical Written and Verbal Communication Structural, geotechnical, and/or construction engineering · Working effectively within a collaborative teaching environment, mentoring and supervising student instructional assistants, support of students’ professional growth across the undergraduate program. Full engagement with the students, faculty, and programs of the department through participation in faculty meetings and committees, program development, student extracurricular activities, etc Earned M.S. or Ph.D. in Civil Engineering or a closely related field by the time of appointment. Candidates without a Ph.D. must have several years of relevant industry experience and will be appointed a Lecturer Ability to develop a diverse teaching portfolio of courses including technical communications and coursework within their technical expertise in the structural engineering, geotechnical engineering, and/or construction engineering. Exceptional communication skills and strong motivation to work collaboratively Eligibility to work in the US Registration as a professional engineer or ability to become registered within four years Applicants should submit 1) an application letter addressing qualifications, motivation, and teaching interests, 2) a complete curriculum vitae, and 3) the names and contact information (names, affiliation, email, and telephone number) for five references. Questions about the position should be directed to Dr. Sarah Mobley, Chair of the Search Committee (voice: 865-974-3355; email: sarahmobley@utk.edu ). Applications will be reviewed beginning October 1 and will continue until the position is filled."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192807": {
      "title": "Teaching Assistant Professor or Lecturer, Construction Engineering and Management, Spring 2027",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: Tickle College of Engineering: Civil & Environ Engr Location: Knoxville, TN The Department of Civil and Environmental Engineering (CEE) at the University of Tennessee, Knoxville (UTK) seeks to hire a teaching-focused faculty member whose responsibilities will be focused on construction engineering and management.  The preferred start date is January 1, 2027. Salary and benefits are nationally competitive and commensurate with experience and qualifications. The position is a non-tenure track, renewable, 9-month appointment, with opportunities for research or to teach additional courses each summer. The appointment title will be determined based on the successful candidate’s qualifications and experience, consistent with the UTK Faculty Handbook. The successful candidate is expected build an exceptional record of accomplishment in teaching, and student and industry engagement, leading to career-track promotions. The successful candidate will teach undergraduate and Master’s level courses in construction engineering and management, including teaching a new hands-on construction laboratory. They will join a growing and vibrant department with 26 tenure-line faculty and 5 non-tenured full-time teaching faculty and offers baccalaureate, masters, and PhD degrees with concentrations in construction, environmental, geotechnical and materials, structures, transportation, and water resources engineering. The successful candidate will join a community of innovators in engineering education both in the department and in The Tickle College of Engineering and will be supported through a strong commitment to faculty development. The Tickle College of Engineering (TCE) is in the midst of an unprecedented period of growth and success, including adding over 30 new faculty at all levels to the college over 4 years as part of ambitious hiring campaigns led by Chancellor Donde Plowman and Dean Matthew Mench. The college has set records in research expenditures, enrollment, incoming student GPA, intellectual property development, and USNWR rank in the past three years. Key partnerships with Oak Ridge National Lab (ORNL)  through the University of Tennessee Oak Ridge Innovation Institute (UTORII) and Consolidated Nuclear Security (CNS) that manages the Y-12 National Security Complex have created an expanded opportunity space for faculty and students in the region.   New facilities include the state-of-the art Zeanah Engineering Complex, the University of Tennessee Manufacturing and Design Enterprise (TN-MADE) facility, and the Innovation South building that houses UTK’s Fibers and Composites Manufacturing Facility (FCMF), a National Prototype Research Center (NSPC) as part of a partnership with CNS Y-12 under construction, and new facilities for the Biomedical Engineering department and a quantum foundry in planning stages. TCE currently has 213 tenure/tenure track and over 80 non-tenure track faculty in its nine academic departments and offers 11 undergraduate, 16 MS, and 15 PhD/DE degree programs. Affiliated with TCE and located in Tullahoma, Tennessee, the UT Space Institute is a hub of aerospace and defense research. The college is also home to eight research centers and three interdisciplinary institutes. With approximately 4,800 undergraduate and 1,500 graduate students, the college sits 28th among public universities in the most recent U.S. News and World Report graduate rankings. Faculty in the college have won 33 early career awards (NSF, DOE, DARPA, AFOSR, AHA, and Sloan) since 2016. In FY25, the college had annual research expenditures of $113.9M. Knoxville, TN is a vibrant city with a beautiful and walkable downtown, active neighborhoods, an effervescent nightlife that includes numerous theaters and museums, a rich live music scene across all genres, and eclectic restaurants. Knoxville is nestled in the foothills of the Great Smoky Mountains, surrounded by lakes and the Tennessee River, providing amazing access to various outdoor activities. Knoxville is within easy driving distance of four major metropolitan areas that regularly host larger cultural and entertainment events. Earned M.S. or Ph.D. in Civil Engineering or a closely related field by the time of appointment. Candidates without a Ph.D. must have several years of relevant industry experience and will be appointed as a lecturer. Ability to develop and teach a diverse teaching portfolio of construction engineering courses Exceptional communication skills and strong motivation to work collaboratively Eligibility to work in the US Registration as a professional engineer or ability to become registered within four years Applicants should submit 1) an application letter addressing qualifications, motivation, and teaching interests, 2) a complete curriculum vitae, and 3) the names and contact information (names, affiliation, email, and telephone number) for five references. Questions about the position should be directed to Dr. Nick Zhou, Chair of the Search Committee (voice: 865-974-0722; email: hzhou8@utk.edu ). Applications will be reviewed beginning October 1 and will continue until the position is filled."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195603": {
      "title": "Assistant or Associate Professor Position in Advanced Computing, Fall 2026",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: Tickle College of Engineering: Civil & Environ Engr Location: Knoxville, TN The Department of Civil and Environmental Engineering (CEE) at the University of Tennessee, Knoxville (UTK), invites applications and nominations for a tenure-track faculty position at the Assistant or Associate Professor rank at the intersection of advanced computing and civil and environmental engineering. Candidates should demonstrate a strong record of scholarship, commensurate with career stage, in one or more areas of civil and environmental engineering or in a closely related field with clear relevance to the built or natural environment. Areas of interest include, but are not limited to, artificial intelligence, machine learning, scientific computing, autonomous and cyber-physical systems, physical AI, high-performance computing, digital twins, and quantum computing. Particular consideration will be given to candidates who develop, adapt, integrate, or rigorously validate advanced computational methods to enable fundamental discovery, predictive modeling, design, decision-making, or autonomous operation in civil and environmental engineering systems. The department seeks candidates whose work extends beyond the routine application of established computational tools and has the potential to produce transformative advances across one or more CEE domains. The successful candidate will be expected to establish or sustain an externally funded research program and teach undergraduate and graduate courses within their area of expertise. The candidate will also contribute to the development and delivery of interdisciplinary or crossdepartmental curricula in advanced computing, mentor students, and foster collaborations that integrate advanced computational capabilities across CEE research and education. About the Tickle College of Engineering: The Tickle College of Engineering (TCE) is in the midst of an unprecedented period of growth and success, including adding over 30 new faculty at all levels to the college over 4 years as part of ambitious hiring campaigns led by Chancellor Donde Plowman and Dean Matthew Mench. The college has set records in research expenditures, enrollment, incoming student GPA, intellectual property development, and USNWR rank in the past three years. Key partnerships with Oak Ridge National Lab ( ORNL)  through the University of Tennessee Oak Ridge Innovation Institute (UTORII) and Consolidated Nuclear Security (CNS) that manages the Y-12 National Security Complex have created an expanded opportunity space for faculty and students in the region.   New facilities include the state-of-the art Zeanah Engineering Complex, the University of Tennessee Manufacturing and Design Enterprise (TN-MADE) facility, and the Innovation South building that houses UTK’s Fibers and Composites Manufacturing Facility (FCMF), a National Prototype Research Center (NSPC) as part of a partnership with CNS Y-12 under construction, and new facilities for the Biomedical Engineering department and a quantum foundry in planning stages. TCE currently has 213 tenure/tenure track and over 80 non-tenure track faculty in its nine academic departments and offers 11 undergraduate, 16 MS, and 15 PhD/DE degree programs. Affiliated with TCE and located in Tullahoma, Tennessee, the UT Space Institute is a hub of aerospace and defense research. The college is also home to eight research centers and three interdisciplinary institutes. With approximately 4, 8 00 undergraduate and 1,500 graduate students, the college sits 2 8 th among public universities in the most recent U.S. News and World Report graduate rankings. Faculty in the college have won 33 early career awards (NSF, DOE, DARPA, AFOSR, AHA, and Sloan ) since 2016. In FY2 5 , the college had annual research expenditures of $113. 9 M. All candidates must hold an earned doctorate in civil or environmental engineering or closely related field by the appointment start date.  All candidates must have research and teaching interests at the nexus of advanced computing and civil and environmental engineering. All candidates must have strong communication skills and the ability to work effectively in an interdisciplinary environment. All candidates must be legally authorized to work in the United States by the appointment start date. Candidates for Assistant Professor must have a record of scholarly achievement and high quality publications and must demonstrate potential to develop a nationally recognized, externally funded research program. Candidates for Associate Professor must have a strong record of scholarly achievement and high-quality publications and must have established a nationally recognized, externally funded research program. Applicants should submit: An application letter addressing qualifications and motivation, Statements of research and teaching interests A complete curriculum vitae Names of and contact information for five references Questions about the position should be directed to Lee Han, Chair of the Search Committee (voice: 865-974-7707; email lhan@utk.edu). Applications will be reviewed beginning November 15 and will continue until the position is filled."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192671": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track Assistant Professor Positions in Electrical Engineering, Fall 2027, 2 Positions Available",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: Tickle College of Engineering: Electrical Engr & Computer Science Location: Knoxville, TN, United States The Min H. Kao Department of Electrical Engineering and Computer Science (EECS ) at The University of Tennessee, Knoxville ( UTK ) is seeking candidates for two tenure-track faculty positions at the assistant professor level in electrical engineering with expertise in areas including high-frequency power electronics and magnetics, analog/mixed-signal/RF circuits, low power and energy efficient circuits, and electronics packaging (particularly on wide-bandgap/ultra-widebandgap semiconductors). Strong candidates in electrical engineering in areas not explicitly listed will also be considered. The successful candidates will be expected to (1) conduct and publish scholarly research; (2) pursue funding to support an active research agenda; (3) teach undergraduate and graduate courses in electrical engineering; (4) mentor graduate students; and (5) participate in departmental, college and/or university service. Information about the University, College, Department, and Knoxville UTK is the state’s flagship campus and leading research institution with a strong partnership with nearby Oak Ridge National Laboratory (ORNL), where many UTK faculty have ongoing joint positions and/or joint research projects. The Tickle College of Engineering (TCE) is in the midst of an unprecedented period of growth and success, including adding over 30 new faculty at all levels to the college over 4 years as part of ambitious hiring campaigns led by Chancellor Donde Plowman and Dean Matthew Mench. The college has set records in research expenditures, enrollment, incoming student GPA, intellectual property development, and USNWR rank in the past three years. Key partnerships with Oak Ridge National Lab (ORNL)  through the University of Tennessee Oak Ridge Innovation Institute (UTORII) and Consolidated Nuclear Security (CNS) that manages the Y-12 National Security Complex have created an expanded opportunity space for faculty and students in the region.   New facilities include the state-of-the art Zeanah Engineering Complex, the University of Tennessee Manufacturing and Design Enterprise (TN-MADE) facility, and the Innovation South building that houses UTK’s Fibers and Composites Manufacturing Facility (FCMF), a National Prototype Research Center (NSPC) as part of a partnership with CNS Y-12 under construction, and new facilities for the Biomedical Engineering department and a quantum foundry in planning stages. TCE currently has 213 tenure/tenure track and over 80 non-tenure track faculty in its nine academic departments and offers 11 undergraduate, 16 MS, and 15 PhD/DE degree programs. Affiliated with TCE and located in Tullahoma, Tennessee, the UT Space Institute is a hub of aerospace and defense research. The college is also home to eight research centers and three interdisciplinary institutes. With approximately 4,800 undergraduate and 1,500 graduate students, the college sits 28th among public universities in the most recent U.S. News and World Report graduate rankings. Faculty in the college have won 33 early career awards (NSF, DOE, DARPA, AFOSR, AHA, and Sloan) since 2016. In FY25, the college had annual research expenditures of $113.9M. EECS at UTK has 50 full-time T/TT faculty members, three members of the National Academy of Engineering Fellows, 15 IEEE Fellows, 15 NSF/DOE CAREER awardees, and 19 ranked in World’s Top 2% Scientists as compiled by Stanford. The department has a growing enrollment of more than 1000 undergraduate and more than 450 graduate students across the three majors of Electrical Engineering, Computer Engineering, and Computer Science. In addition, the department offers undergraduate minors in computer science, cybersecurity, datacenter technology and management, and machine learning. Successful faculty candidates will be expected to contribute to the continued growth and excellence of EECS. The city of Knoxville is a hidden gem with an elegant and walkable downtown, rich and varied nightlife, vibrant neighborhoods, eclectic restaurants, and amazing access to outdoor activities of all kinds as well as exciting cultural events throughout the year. UTK is in Knoxville, Tennessee, within an easy driving distance to Nashville, Atlanta, Asheville, and the Great Smoky Mountains National Park. From Knoxville’s TYS Airport, Knoxville has nonstop flights to 22 major airports in the US, including direct flights to cities such as DC, NYC, Atlanta, Boston, Charlotte, Chicago, Dallas, Denver, Detroit, Houston, Las Vegas, Miami, Orlando, Philadelphia, Phoenix. In addition, Knoxville and the surrounding areas boast great K-12 schools and one of the most highly educated populations in the entire US. With one of the lowest costs of living in the country, Knoxville was recently recognized in U.S. News and World Report as the 29th best place to live in the U.S. In 2024, US News ranked the State of Tennessee as #5 in fiscal stability, #12 in economy, and #21 in infrastructure. Applicants must hold a Ph.D. in electrical engineering or a closely related field at the time of appointment. The candidate is expected to show potential for obtaining funding for the research programs, and for participation in interdisciplinary teams. The candidate is also expected to show effective, high-quality teaching skills, and ability to effectively mentor undergraduate and graduate students. The application deadline is November 30, 2026. Applications received after the deadline may be considered until the positions are filled. Please submit the following items online in Interfolio to complete your application: ■          Cover Letter ■          Curriculum Vitae ■          Research Statement ■          Teaching Statement ■          Names and Contact Information of Three References For questions, please email the Search Committee Chair, Dr. JiangBiao He at jiangbiao.he@utk.edu ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192692": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track Assistant Professor Positions in Computer Science - Fall 2027 - 2 Positions Available",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: Tickle College of Engineering: Electrical Engr & Computer Science Location: Knoxville, TN, United States The University of Tennessee, Knoxville (UTK) seeks exceptional candidates for two tenure-track faculty positions in the Min H. Kao Department of Electrical Engineering and Computer Science ( EECS ) at the rank of Assistant Professor. Primary consideration will be given to candidates in the areas of security and privacy, computer graphics (including VR/AR), programming languages, robotics, or human-computer interaction. Strong candidates from other areas of research will also be considered. Candidates whose only research area is AI/ML will not be considered for this search. The successful candidate will be expected to (1) conduct and publish scholarly research; (2) pursue funding to support an active research agenda; (3) teach undergraduate and graduate courses in computer science and related areas; (4) mentor graduate students; and (5) participate in departmental service. Information about the University, College, Department, and Knoxville UTK is the state’s flagship campus and leading research institution with a strong partnership with nearby Oak Ridge National Laboratory (ORNL), where many UTK faculty have ongoing joint positions and/or joint research projects. The Tickle College of Engineering (TCE) is in the midst of an unprecedented period of growth and success, including adding over 30 new faculty at all levels to the college over 4 years as part of ambitious hiring campaigns led by Chancellor Donde Plowman and Dean Matthew Mench. The college has set records in research expenditures, enrollment, incoming student GPA, intellectual property development, and USNWR rank in the past three years. Key partnerships with Oak Ridge National Lab (ORNL) through the University of Tennessee Oak Ridge Innovation Institute (UTORII) and Consolidated Nuclear Security (CNS) that manages the Y-12 National Security Complex have created an expanded opportunity space for faculty and students in the region.   New facilities include the state-of-the art Zeanah Engineering Complex, the University of Tennessee Manufacturing and Design Enterprise (TN-MADE) facility, and the Innovation South building that houses UTK’s Fibers and Composites Manufacturing Facility (FCMF), a National Prototype Research Center (NSPC) as part of a partnership with CNS Y-12 under construction, and new facilities for the Biomedical Engineering department and a quantum foundry in planning stages. TCE currently has 213 tenure/tenure track and over 80 non-tenure track faculty in its nine academic departments and offers 11 undergraduate, 16 MS, and 15 PhD/DE degree programs. Affiliated with TCE and located in Tullahoma, Tennessee, the UT Space Institute is a hub of aerospace and defense research. The college is also home to eight research centers and three interdisciplinary institutes. With approximately 4,800 undergraduate and 1,500 graduate students, the college sits 28th among public universities in the most recent U.S. News and World Report graduate rankings. Faculty in the college have won 33 early career awards (NSF, DOE, DARPA, AFOSR, AHA, and Sloan) since 2016. In FY25, the college had annual research expenditures of $113.9M. EECS at UTK has 50 full-time T/TT faculty members, three members of the National Academy of Engineering Fellows, 15 IEEE Fellows, 15 NSF/DOE CAREER awardees, and 19 ranked in World’s Top 2% Scientists as compiled by Stanford. The department has a growing enrollment of more than 1000 undergraduate and more than 450 graduate students across the three majors of Electrical Engineering, Computer Engineering, and Computer Science. In addition, the department offers undergraduate minors in computer science, cybersecurity, datacenter technology and management, and machine learning. Successful faculty candidates will be expected to contribute to the continued growth and excellence of EECS. The city of Knoxville is a hidden gem with an elegant and walkable downtown, rich and varied nightlife, vibrant neighborhoods, eclectic restaurants, and amazing access to outdoor activities of all kinds as well as exciting cultural events throughout the year. UTK is in Knoxville, Tennessee, within an easy driving distance to Nashville, Atlanta, Asheville, and the Great Smoky Mountains National Park. From Knoxville’s TYS Airport, Knoxville has nonstop flights to 22 major airports in the US, including direct flights to cities such as DC, NYC, Atlanta, Boston, Charlotte, Chicago, Dallas, Denver, Detroit, Houston, Las Vegas, Miami, Orlando, Philadelphia, Phoenix. In addition, Knoxville and the surrounding areas boast great K-12 schools and one of the most highly educated populations in the entire US. With one of the lowest costs of living in the country, Knoxville was recently recognized in U.S. News and World Report as the 29th best place to live in the U.S. In 2024, US News ranked the State of Tennessee as #5 in fiscal stability, #12 in economy, and #21 in infrastructure. Applicants must hold a Ph.D. in Computer Science, or a closely related field, at the time of appointment. The candidate is expected to show potential to establish an independent research program, secure external funding, and contribute to interdisciplinary collaboration. The candidate is also expected to show effective, high-quality teaching skills, and the ability to effectively mentor undergraduate and graduate students. For full consideration, applications should be received by October 15, 2026. Review of applications will continue until the position is filled. Please submit the following items via Interfolio to complete your application: ■          Cover Letter ■          Curriculum Vitae ■          Research Statement ■          Teaching Statement ■          Names and Contact Information of Three References For questions, please email the Search Committee Chair, Dr. Scott Ruoti ( ruoti@utk.edu )."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192921": {
      "title": "Endowed Dongarra Professorship in High Performance Computing (HPC) / Director of the Innovative Computing Laboratory - Associate Professor/Professor - Fall 2027",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: Tickle College of Engineering: Electrical Engr & Computer Science Location: Knoxville, TN, United States The Min H. Kao Department of Electrical Engineering and Computer Science (EECS) at The University of Tennessee, Knoxville (UTK) invites applications and nominations for a faculty position at the Associate Professor or Full Professor level for the Dongarra Professorship in High Performance Computing (HPC), beginning Fall 2027. This prestigious endowed position honors Turing Award laureate and UTK Distinguished Professor Emeritus Jack Dongarra , whose pioneering work established UTK and the Innovative Computing Laboratory (ICL) as a global epicenter for high-performance computing, numerical linear algebra, and scientific software systems. The successful candidate will have the opportunity to serve as the Director of the Innovative Computing Laboratory (ICL), guiding one of the world's premier academic HPC research institutions into its next era of innovation, interdisciplinary collaboration, and international impact. We seek an exceptional, internationally recognized scholar with demonstrated leadership and a strong track record of sustained external research funding, impactful publications, and effective teaching and mentorship. The successful candidate will advance the department's strategic vision in high-performance computing, lead high-impact research endeavors, and leverage UTK’s unique research ecosystem, including deep research partnerships with nearby Oak Ridge National Laboratory (ORNL). Areas of research interest include, but are not limited to, numerical algorithms and high-performance scientific software; parallel and distributed computing; performance analysis, modeling, and benchmarking; programming models, runtime systems, and compilers; exascale and heterogeneous computing, including GPUs and emerging accelerators; high-performance AI and data-intensive computing; HPC storage, I/O, interconnects, and resilience; and emerging computing paradigms. Candidates need not have expertise across all areas. Of particular interest are researchers whose work bridges algorithms, software, and evolving computing architectures to advance high-performance scientific computing. The successful candidate will be expected to (1) maintain an internationally recognized, externally funded research program in high-performance computing; (2) provide visionary academic and research leadership as the Dongarra Professor, with the opportunity to serve as Director of the Innovative Computing Laboratory (ICL); (3) publish high-impact scholarly work in top-tier HPC venues and journals; (4) teach undergraduate and graduate courses in computer science and computer engineering; (5) mentor graduate and undergraduate students, with a strong commitment to graduating doctoral students; and (6) contribute meaningful professional and institutional service at the departmental, college, university, and national levels. UTK is the state’s flagship campus and leading research institution with a strong partnership with nearby Oak Ridge National Laboratory (ORNL), where many UTK faculty have ongoing joint positions and/or joint research projects. The Tickle College of Engineering (TCE) is in the midst of an unprecedented period of growth and success, including adding over 30 new faculty at all levels to the college over 4 years as part of ambitious hiring campaigns led by Chancellor Donde Plowman and Dean Matthew Mench. The college has set records in research expenditures, enrollment, incoming student GPA, intellectual property development, and USNWR rank in the past three years. Key partnerships with Oak Ridge National Lab (ORNL)  through the University of Tennessee Oak Ridge Innovation Institute (UTORII) and Consolidated Nuclear Security (CNS) that manages the Y-12 National Security Complex have created an expanded opportunity space for faculty and students in the region.   New facilities include the state-of-the art Zeanah Engineering Complex, the University of Tennessee Manufacturing and Design Enterprise (TN-MADE) facility, and the Innovation South building that houses UTK’s Fibers and Composites Manufacturing Facility (FCMF), a National Prototype Research Center (NSPC) as part of a partnership with CNS Y-12 under construction, and new facilities for the Biomedical Engineering department and a quantum foundry in planning stages. TCE currently has 213 tenure/tenure track and over 80 non-tenure track faculty in its nine academic departments and offers 11 undergraduate, 16 MS, and 15 PhD/DE degree programs. Affiliated with TCE and located in Tullahoma, Tennessee, the UT Space Institute is a hub of aerospace and defense research. The college is also home to eight research centers and three interdisciplinary institutes. With approximately 4,800 undergraduate and 1,500 graduate students, the college sits 28th among public universities in the most recent U.S. News and World Report graduate rankings. Faculty in the college have won 33 early career awards (NSF, DOE, DARPA, AFOSR, AHA, and Sloan) since 2016. In FY25, the college had annual research expenditures of $113.9M. EECS at UTK has 50 full-time T/TT faculty members, three members of the National Academy of Engineering Fellows, 15 IEEE Fellows, 15 NSF/DOE CAREER awardees, and 19 ranked in World’s Top 2% Scientists as compiled by Stanford. The department has a growing enrollment of more than 1000 undergraduate and more than 450 graduate students across the three majors of Electrical Engineering, Computer Engineering, and Computer Science. In addition, the department offers undergraduate minors in computer science, cybersecurity, datacenter technology and management, and machine learning. Successful faculty candidates will be expected to contribute to the continued growth and excellence of EECS. The city of Knoxville is a hidden gem with an elegant and walkable downtown, rich and varied nightlife, vibrant neighborhoods, eclectic restaurants, and amazing access to outdoor activities of all kinds as well as exciting cultural events throughout the year. UTK is in Knoxville, Tennessee, within an easy driving distance to Nashville, Atlanta, Asheville, and the Great Smoky Mountains National Park. From Knoxville’s TYS Airport, Knoxville has nonstop flights to 22 major airports in the US, including direct flights to cities such as DC, NYC, Atlanta, Boston, Charlotte, Chicago, Dallas, Denver, Detroit, Houston, Las Vegas, Miami, Orlando, Philadelphia, Phoenix. In addition, Knoxville and the surrounding areas boast great K-12 schools and one of the most highly educated populations in the entire US. With one of the lowest costs of living in the country, Knoxville was recently recognized in U.S. News and World Report as the 29th best place to live in the U.S. In 2024, US News ranked the State of Tennessee as #5 in fiscal stability, #12 in economy, and #21 in infrastructure. Minimum Qualifications: Applicants must hold a Ph.D. in Computer Science, Computer Engineering, Electrical Engineering, or a closely related field prior to the start of appointment. The department will pursue tenure consideration for qualified candidates who demonstrate accomplishments that align with holding an endowed professorship and directing a major research laboratory. For an appointment at the Associate Professor rank, the candidate is expected to have conducted nationally and internationally recognized scholarly research in high performance computing, demonstrated a sustained track record of securing external research funding from agencies such as NSF, DOE, DARPA, or industry, showed evidence of effective teaching and graduate student advising, and exhibited strong academic leadership potential to fulfill the expectations of an endowed chair and laboratory directorship. For an appointment at the Full Professor rank, the candidate is expected to demonstrate established international leadership in high performance computing, an outstanding track record of major external grant support, high-impact publications in top venues, and successful completion of doctoral students. The candidate should possess proven capabilities and vision to lead major research centers, large-scale interdisciplinary initiatives, or academic laboratories such as the Innovative Computing Laboratory (ICL), along with a record of distinguished professional service. Preferred qualifications include demonstrated experience or interest in center- or laboratory-level leadership (such as directorship of research units like ICL); experience leading multi-investigator or multi-institutional research grants funded by DOE, NSF, DOD, or industry; experience with joint or collaborative appointments with national laboratories or industrial research facilities; and experience in curriculum development and educational innovation in high performance computing and computer systems. Applicants should electronically submit the following via Interfolio. CV Cover Letter Teaching Statement Research Statement Names and Contact Information of Three References Review of applications will begin October 26, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. Please submit your application by October 26, 2026, to ensure full consideration. Please contact the search chair, Dr. James Plank, ( jplank@utk.edu ) if you have any questions."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193072": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track Faculty Positions in Computer Engineering - Assistant Professor - Fall 2027, 2 Positions Available",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: Tickle College of Engineering: Electrical Engr & Computer Science Location: Knoxville, TN, United States The University of Tennessee, Knoxville (UTK) seeks exceptional candidates for 2 tenure-track computer engineering faculty positions in the Min H. Kao Department of Electrical Engineering and Computer Science (EECS) at the rank of Assistant Professor, beginning August 2027. We welcome candidates whose work advances one or more of the following: -   Secure and trustworthy hardware: hardware and systems security, embedded and cyber-physical systems -  Connected and distributed systems: Internet of Things, next-generation sensing, wireless and networked systems, edge computing -   Computer architecture and emerging computing:  hardware–software co-design, electronic design automation Exceptional candidates in other areas of computer engineering are also encouraged to apply. The successful candidate will be expected to (1) conduct and publish scholarly research; (2) pursue funding to support an active research agenda; (3) teach undergraduate and graduate courses in computer engineering and related areas (4) mentor graduate students; and (5) participate in departmental service. The Knoxville campus of the University of Tennessee is seeking candidates who have the ability to promote an atmosphere where all members of the university community feel welcome and can thrive. Information about the University, College, Department, and Knoxville UTK is the state’s flagship campus and leading research institution with a strong partnership with nearby Oak Ridge National Laboratory (ORNL), where many UTK faculty have ongoing joint positions and/or joint research projects. The Tickle College of Engineering (TCE) is in the midst of an unprecedented period of growth and success, including adding over 30 new faculty at all levels to the college over 4 years as part of ambitious hiring campaigns led by Chancellor Donde Plowman and Dean Matthew Mench. The college has set records in research expenditures, enrollment, incoming student GPA, intellectual property development, and USNWR rank in the past three years. Key partnerships with Oak Ridge National Lab (ORNL)  through the University of Tennessee Oak Ridge Innovation Institute (UTORII) and Consolidated Nuclear Security (CNS) that manages the Y-12 National Security Complex have created an expanded opportunity space for faculty and students in the region.   New facilities include the state-of-the art Zeanah Engineering Complex, the University of Tennessee Manufacturing and Design Enterprise (TN-MADE) facility, and the Innovation South building that houses UTK’s Fibers and Composites Manufacturing Facility (FCMF), a National Prototype Research Center (NSPC) as part of a partnership with CNS Y-12 under construction, and new facilities for the Biomedical Engineering department and a quantum foundry in planning stages. TCE currently has 213 tenure/tenure track and over 80 non-tenure track faculty in its nine academic departments and offers 11 undergraduate, 16 MS, and 15 PhD/DE degree programs. Affiliated with TCE and located in Tullahoma, Tennessee, the UT Space Institute is a hub of aerospace and defense research. The college is also home to eight research centers and three interdisciplinary institutes. With approximately 4,800 undergraduate and 1,500 graduate students, the college sits 28th among public universities in the most recent U.S. News and World Report graduate rankings. Faculty in the college have won 33 early career awards (NSF, DOE, DARPA, AFOSR, AHA, and Sloan) since 2016. In FY25, the college had annual research expenditures of $113.9M. EECS at UTK has 50 full-time T/TT faculty members, three members of the National Academy of Engineering Fellows, 15 IEEE Fellows, 15 NSF/DOE CAREER awardees, and 19 ranked in World’s Top 2% Scientists as compiled by Stanford. The department has a growing enrollment of more than 1000 undergraduate and more than 450 graduate students across the three majors of Electrical Engineering, Computer Engineering, and Computer Science. In addition, the department offers undergraduate minors in computer science, cybersecurity, datacenter technology and management, and machine learning. Successful faculty candidates will be expected to contribute to the continued growth and excellence of EECS. The city of Knoxville is a hidden gem with an elegant and walkable downtown, rich and varied nightlife, vibrant neighborhoods, eclectic restaurants, and amazing access to outdoor activities of all kinds as well as exciting cultural events throughout the year. UTK is in Knoxville, Tennessee, within an easy driving distance to Nashville, Atlanta, Asheville, and the Great Smoky Mountains National Park. From Knoxville’s TYS Airport, Knoxville has nonstop flights to 22 major airports in the US, including direct flights to cities such as DC, NYC, Atlanta, Boston, Charlotte, Chicago, Dallas, Denver, Detroit, Houston, Las Vegas, Miami, Orlando, Philadelphia, Phoenix. In addition, Knoxville and the surrounding areas boast great K-12 schools and one of the most highly educated populations in the entire US. With one of the lowest costs of living in the country, Knoxville was recently recognized in U.S. News and World Report as the 29th best place to work. Applicants must hold a Ph.D. in computer engineering, electrical engineering, computer science or a closely related field at the time of appointment. The candidate is expected to show potential for obtaining funding for the research programs, and for participation in interdisciplinary teams. The candidate is also expected to show effective, high-quality teaching skills, and ability to effectively mentor undergraduate and graduate students. The application deadline is November 30, 2026. Applications received after the deadline may be considered until the position is filled. Please submit the following items via Interfolio to complete your application: ■          Cover Letter ■          Curriculum Vitae ■          Research Statement ■          Teaching Statement ■          Names and Contact Information of Three References For questions, please email the Search Committee Chair, Dr. Ahmedullah Aziz ( aziz@utk.edu )."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192401": {
      "title": "Tenure-track Assistant Professor of Industrial and Systems Engineering, Fall 2027 (1/3)",
      "description": "Posted: Sep 01, 2026 Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: Tickle College of Engineering: Industrial & Systems Engineering Location: Knoxville, TN Applications and nominations are invited for three tenure-track Assistant Professor positions in the Department of Industrial and Systems Engineering (ISE) at the University of Tennessee, Knoxville (UTK). The ISE Department offers BS, MS, Ph.D., and Doctor of Engineering degrees in Industrial Engineering and an MS in Engineering Management. The Department currently has 14 tenured or tenure-track faculty members and enrolls more than 600 students, including 294 UG, 138 MS, and 116 Doctoral students. The Department will increase its faculty size to 25 by 2027, including 16 tenured or tenure-track faculty members and nine full-time teaching or research faculty members. ISE actively engages with on-campus centers, including the Institute for a Secure and Sustainable Environment, the Reliability and Maintainability Center, the Center for Transportation Research, UT-OR Innovation Institute, the state’s Institute for Public Service, and others. Departmental strengths include optimization, data analytics, machine learning, AI, quantum computing, human factors, reliability and maintainability, and operational excellence, and their applications in manufacturing, logistics and transportation, supply chain, energy systems, sustainability, and healthcare systems. The annual externally funded research expenditure is about $5M. The department was ranked 28th among all public institutions in the 2026 U.S. News Best Graduate School Ranking. For additional information about the Department, please visit https://tickle.utk.edu/ise . The Tickle College of Engineering (TCE) is in the midst of an unprecedented period of growth and success, including adding over 30 new faculty at all levels to the college over 4 years as part of ambitious hiring campaigns led by Chancellor Donde Plowman and Dean Matthew Mench. The college has set records in research expenditures, enrollment, incoming student GPA, intellectual property development, and USNWR rank in the past three years. Key partnerships with Oak Ridge National Lab (ORNL)  through the University of Tennessee Oak Ridge Innovation Institute (UTORII) and Consolidated Nuclear Security (CNS) that manages the Y-12 National Security Complex have created an expanded opportunity space for faculty and students in the region.   New facilities include the state-of-the art Zeanah Engineering Complex, the University of Tennessee Manufacturing and Design Enterprise (TN-MADE) facility, and the Innovation South building that houses UTK’s Fibers and Composites Manufacturing Facility (FCMF), a National Prototype Research Center (NSPC) as part of a partnership with CNS Y-12 under construction, and new facilities for the Biomedical Engineering department and a quantum foundry in planning stages. TCE currently has 213 tenure/tenure track and over 80 non-tenure track faculty in its nine academic departments and offers 11 undergraduate, 16 MS, and 15 PhD/DE degree programs. Affiliated with TCE and located in Tullahoma, Tennessee, the UT Space Institute is a hub of aerospace and defense research. The college is also home to eight research centers and three interdisciplinary institutes. With approximately 4,800 undergraduate and 1,500 graduate students, the college sits 28th among public universities in the most recent U.S. News and World Report graduate rankings. Faculty in the college have won 33 early career awards (NSF, DOE, DARPA, AFOSR, AHA, and Sloan) since 2016. In FY25, the college had annual research expenditures of $113.9M. Available effective August 1, 2027, the positions will include competitive compensation and a startup package.  The Knoxville campus of the University of Tennessee seeks candidates who can promote an atmosphere where all members of the university community feel welcome and can thrive. The successful candidate will be expected to establish and maintain a scholarly and externally funded research program; supervise graduate students; develop and teach courses at both the undergraduate and graduate levels; and provide service to the Department.  The department is interested in candidates who conduct research in quantum computing, manufacturing systems, healthcare systems, human-robotics interaction, building construction, AI foundation and applications, agentic AI, and other ISE-related areas. An earned doctorate in Industrial Engineering, Systems Engineering, Operations Research, Mathematics, Physics, Computer Science, or a closely related field is required, and the PhD should be in hand by the time of appointment. The successful applicants will have strong problem-solving and communication skills and the ability to work in an interdisciplinary environment. A strong commitment to teaching excellence and publications in leading journals is expected. Preferred qualifications are teaching experience, industrial experience, and demonstrated ability to attract external funding. Individuals interested in applying should apply through Interfolio. Applicants must attach to their application a letter of interest addressing qualifications, research interests and teaching interests; a curriculum vitae; and a document containing the names and contact information for at least three professional references. Review of applications will begin on October 5, 2026 and continue until the position is filled. Inquiries should be directed to Dr. Jim Ostrowski, the search committee chair, at jostrows@utk.edu ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190979": {
      "title": "Faculty Position in Materials Science and Engineering: Synthesis, Processing, and Characterization of Quantum Materials - Fall 2027",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: Tickle College of Engineering: Materials Science & Engineering Location: Knoxville, Tennessee The Department of Materials Science and Engineering (MSE) in the Tickle College of Engineering (TCE) at the University of Tennessee, Knoxville (UTK), invites applications for an Associate or Full Professor position in the broad area of experimental synthesis, processing, and characterization of quantum materials.  The department will pursue tenure consideration for qualified candidates. This position is intended to build upon and expand the internationally recognized leadership in quantum materials established by the MSE Department at UTK in close partnership with Oak Ridge National Laboratory (ORNL), one of the world's premier centers for quantum materials research. The MSE Department at UTK is a vibrant and rapidly growing academic unit with internationally recognized strengths in quantum materials, electronic materials, ceramics, metals and alloys, polymers, composites, advanced characterization, computational modeling, AI/ML for materials informatics, and autonomous materials discovery. The department leverages the exceptional collaborative research ecosystem within the Institute for Advanced Materials and Manufacturing (IAMM), featuring world-class facilities for materials synthesis, processing, and advanced characterization. Combined with the department's close partnership with Oak Ridge National Laboratory (ORNL) and access to the Spallation Neutron Source (SNS), High Flux Isotope Reactor (HFIR), and Center for Nanophase Materials Sciences (CNMS), these resources provide one of the world's premier environments for quantum materials research. Visit the department website for more information: https://mse.utk.edu/ Research Areas of Interest We seek internationally recognized experimental researchers with demonstrated excellence in the synthesis, processing, and characterization of quantum materials. The successful candidate is expected to establish and lead a world-class, externally funded research program that advances the discovery, understanding, and development of quantum materials and quantum phenomena while strengthening the strategic partnership between UTK and ORNL. Areas of particular interest include, but are not limited to: · Synthesis and crystal growth of quantum materials, including bulk single crystals, thin films, heterostructures, nanostructures, and low-dimensional materials. · Processing and fabrication of quantum materials for fundamental studies and emerging quantum technologies. · Advanced structural, electronic, magnetic, optical, and spectroscopic characterization of quantum materials. · In-situ and operando characterization techniques to investigate quantum phase transitions, correlated electronic behavior, and structure-property relationships. · Quantum materials exhibiting strongly correlated electron phenomena, superconductivity, magnetism, topology, spintronics, quantum defects, low-dimensional physics, or other emergent quantum behaviors. · Materials development for quantum information science, quantum sensing, quantum computing, spin-based devices, or next-generation electronic technologies. · Integration of synthesis and characterization with advanced neutron, X-ray, electron microscopy, and spectroscopy techniques available through the unique UTK–ORNL research ecosystem. The successful candidate will be expected to provide scientific leadership within the department, establish and maintain a vigorous externally funded research program, leverage the exceptional experimental capabilities available through ORNL user facilities, foster multidisciplinary collaborations across UTK and ORNL, mentor graduate students and postdoctoral researchers, and contribute to the broader quantum materials community through outstanding scholarship, professional service, and leadership. Candidates whose research is primarily computational or theoretical will not be considered. Teaching Areas of Interest The successful candidate will contribute to the department's undergraduate and graduate teaching mission in electronic and quantum materials. Teaching areas may include, but are not limited to: · Electronic Materials · Quantum Materials · Structure and Properties of Materials · Materials Characterization · Crystallography and Diffraction · Graduate courses aligned with the candidate's research expertise The ability to develop new graduate-level courses in areas aligned with the candidate's research is strongly encouraged. About the Tickle College of Engineering The Tickle College of Engineering (TCE) is in the midst of an unprecedented period of growth and success, including adding over 30 new faculty at all levels to the college over 4 years as part of ambitious hiring campaigns led by Chancellor Donde Plowman and Dean Matthew Mench. The college has set records in research expenditures, enrollment, incoming student GPA, intellectual property development, and USNWR rank in the past three years. Key partnerships with Oak Ridge National Lab (ORNL)  through the University of Tennessee Oak Ridge Innovation Institute (UTORII) and Consolidated Nuclear Security (CNS) that manages the Y-12 National Security Complex have created an expanded opportunity space for faculty and students in the region.   New facilities include the state-of-the art Zeanah Engineering Complex, the University of Tennessee Manufacturing and Design Enterprise (TN-MADE) facility, and the Innovation South building that houses UTK’s Fibers and Composites Manufacturing Facility (FCMF), a National Prototype Research Center (NSPC) as part of a partnership with CNS Y-12 under construction, and new facilities for the Biomedical Engineering department and a quantum foundry in planning stages. TCE currently has 213 tenure/tenure track and over 80 non-tenure track faculty in its nine academic departments and offers 11 undergraduate, 16 MS, and 15 PhD/DE degree programs. Affiliated with TCE and located in Tullahoma, Tennessee, the UT Space Institute is a hub of aerospace and defense research. The college is also home to eight research centers and three interdisciplinary institutes. With approximately 4,800 undergraduate and 1,500 graduate students, the college sits 28th among public universities in the most recent U.S. News and World Report graduate rankings. Faculty in the college have won 33 early career awards (NSF, DOE, DARPA, AFOSR, AHA, and Sloan) since 2016. In FY25, the college had annual research expenditures of $113.9M. About Knoxville Knoxville, Tennessee, is a vibrant city with a beautiful and walkable downtown, thriving neighborhoods, outstanding restaurants, museums, theaters, and a rich live music scene. Nestled in the foothills of the Great Smoky Mountains and surrounded by lakes and the Tennessee River, Knoxville offers exceptional opportunities for outdoor recreation while remaining within easy driving distance of several major metropolitan areas. Its combination of natural beauty, affordability, and outstanding quality of life makes Knoxville an exceptional place to live and work. A Ph.D. in Materials Science and Engineering, Physics, Chemistry, or a closely related field is required. Qualifications appropriate for appointment at the rank of Associate Professor or Full Professor An internationally recognized record of research excellence in the experimental synthesis, processing, and characterization of quantum materials. Demonstrated record of sustained external research funding. Excellent record of scholarly publications and graduate student mentoring. Demonstrated commitment to excellence in undergraduate and graduate teaching. Evidence of successful multidisciplinary collaborations, particularly those involving national laboratories, large research centers, or major user facilities. Applicants at the rank of associate professor should be established researchers with national visibility, a strong record of externally funded research, and demonstrated potential to lead major interdisciplinary research initiatives. Applicants at the rank of full professor should be internationally recognized researchers with a distinguished record of externally funded research and demonstrated leadership of major interdisciplinary and/or multi-institutional research initiatives. Applicants should submit the following materials: 1.      Cover letter addressing qualifications and research/teaching interests (1–3 pages). 2.      Curriculum vitae, including a complete publication list. 3.      Statement of research accomplishments and future research vision (3–5 pages). 4.      Statement of teaching philosophy and interests (1–2 pages). 5.      Contact information for at least five professional references. Applications must be submitted through the University of Tennessee's online application system (insert link). To receive full consideration, applications should be received before November 15, 2026 . Review of applications will continue until the position is filled. For questions and inquiries about this position, please contact the Search Committee Chair, Mariya Zhuravleva, mzhuravl@utk.edu ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192700": {
      "title": "Y-12/UTK Joint Professor and Distinguished Chair in Advanced Manufacturing, Spring 2027",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: Tickle College of Engineering: Mechanical & Aerospace Engr Location: University of Tennessee-Knoxville The Y-12 National Security Complex and the University of Tennessee, Knoxville (UTK) invite applications for the position of Distinguished Chair in Advanced Manufacturing. This joint appointment represents a rare leadership role that bridges national security, academia, and the industry enterprise. The Distinguished Chair will serve simultaneously as a senior leader at Y-12, shaping the future of national security innovation and secure prototyping, and as a full professor at UTK, contributing to one of the nation’s fastest-growing engineering colleges. In this dual capacity, the Distinguished Chair will have the opportunity to influence research direction, inspire the next generation of engineers, and directly impact national security readiness through advanced manufacturing breakthroughs. The Distinguished Chair will provide the technical and strategic leadership for the National Security Prototyping Center (NSPC), which is a UT and Y-12 collaboration going beyond the shared facility, solving complex manufacturing problems by taking ideas from first principles to pilot-scale manufacturing processes, validated through development of qualified prototypes. The NSPC intends to make transformative progress that enables the United States to realize national security products faster than its adversaries – dramatically reducing the deployment time for high value, qualified components from decades to a matter of months.  Developments in advanced manufacturing leveraging smart factory principles will span topics such as hybrid additive and subtractive manufacturing, precision machining, convergent manufacturing, casting, forging, metrology, robotics, automation, in-process sensing and control, qualification and certification, integrated internet of things, digital twins, joining, artificial intelligence, machine learning, and expeditionary manufacturing. This position reflects a bold vision: to leverage the unparalleled ecosystem of East Tennessee that operates at the nexus of extraordinary facilities and strong regional and national partnerships.  In addition to directing world-class capabilities in the 100,000 ft 2 NSPC facilities, the Distinguished Chair will coordinate efforts with facilities at the Y-12 National Security Complex, a cornerstone of the U.S. nuclear security enterprise with unmatched expertise in secure manufacturing stewardship, and UTK’s Tickle College of Engineering, a top-ranked and rapidly growing engineering college with state-of-the-art facilities such as the Zeanah Engineering Complex, Defense and Development Applied Research Center (D 2 ARC), and the Institute for Advanced Materials and Manufacturing (IAMM).  The position will also engage regional collaborators such as Manufacturing USA’s Institute for Advanced Composites Manufacturing Innovation (IACMI), Oak Ridge National Laboratory’s Manufacturing Demonstration Facility (MDF), and a strong cohort of industry partners. For this position, we seek an established leader who combines technical excellence with the ability to inspire teams and drive impact. The successful candidate will be visionary and forward-thinking, with a proven track record of leading innovations in manufacturing. They will have demonstrated experience in implementing smart factory principles to transition prototypes into qualified products and navigating industry and national security environments. They will be collaborative and people-focused, committed to teamwork and multi-partner projects. They will also be a strategic communicator, equally comfortable engaging with engineers on the floor, executives in boardrooms, and leaders in government. The Distinguished Chair will be an employee of the University of Tennessee with their primary appointment at the rank of full professor in the Mechanical and Aerospace Engineering Department within the Tickle College of Engineering.  The Chair will also have a joint research faculty appointment with the Y-12 National Security Complex under the supervision of the Y-12 Senior Director for Nuclear Security Innovations. The Y-12 National Security Complex was established in 1943 during the Manhattan Project to enrich uranium for the first atomic bomb. Today, the facility plays a critical role in maintaining the U.S. nuclear deterrent by manufacturing, refurbishing, and dismantling weapon components. Additionally, Y-12 secures strategic global materials to prevent proliferation, supplies enriched uranium to fuel the Nuclear Navy, and undergoes extensive site modernization to support long-term national security, defense, and global non-proliferation objectives. The Tickle College of Engineering (TCE) is experiencing an unprecedented period of growth and success, including adding over 30 new faculty to the college over 4 years as part of ambitious hiring campaigns led by Chancellor Donde Plowman and Dean Matthew Mench. The college has set records in research expenditures, enrollment, incoming student GPA, intellectual property development, and USNWR rank in the past three years. With approximately 4,800 undergraduate and 1,500 graduate students, the college sits 28th among public universities in the most recent U.S. News and World Report graduate rankings. Knoxville, TN is a vibrant city with a beautiful and walkable downtown, active neighborhoods, an effervescent nightlife that includes numerous theaters and museums, a rich live music scene across all genres, and eclectic restaurants. Knoxville is nestled in the foothills of the Great Smoky Mountains, surrounded by lakes and the Tennessee River, providing amazing access to various outdoor activities. Knoxville is within easy driving distance of four major metropolitan areas that regularly host larger cultural and entertainment events. Minimum Qualifications: ·  Terminal degree (PhD or equivalent) in a science or engineering discipline with a research emphasis on advanced manufacturing and at least 10 years of post-PhD leadership experience across national security, academia, or industry. ·  Proven leadership of multi-disciplinary teams involving parties that span government, academia, or industry. ·  Proven collaboration with industry with demonstrated ability to transition novel manufacturing technologies to application. ·  Experience successfully navigating qualification and certification requirements. ·  Strong interpersonal and communication skills. ·  Ability to obtain a high-level DOE or DOW security clearance. Preferred Qualifications: ·       Internationlly recognized with at least 20 years of experience in the area of advanced manufacturing. ·       Proven leadership of large multi-disciplinary teams involving parties that span government, academia, and industry. ·       Demonstrated ability to transition novel manufacturing technologies to a qualified and certified product for national security or energy applications. ·       Successful record of teaching and mentoring graduate and undergraduate students. ·       Demonstrated record of publishing in relevant journals, of securing and executing externally funded research with federal and industrial sponsors. ·       Experience working in a classified research or manufacturing space. ·       U.S. citizen holding a high-level DOE or DOW security clearance. Priority will be given to applications received on or before October 15, 2026 and review will continue until the position is filled. The anticipated start date of the new position is January 1, 2027. Salary is commensurate with the position and experience of the applicant. Applications must include (1) a letter of intent concisely outlining the applicant’s qualifications and interest this position; (2) a comprehensive curriculum vitae; (3) a vision statement outlining the need and strategy for developing advanced manufacturing capabilities for national security; and (4) the names and contact information for five references. The application package must be submitted through the position listing on Interfolio ( https://apply.interfolio.com/192700) .  Inquiries should be addressed to the Search Chair, Professor Chad Duty at cduty@utk.edu ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193011": {
      "title": "Assistant or Associate Professor Electrochemical Energy Storage and Conversion, Fall 2027",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: Tickle College of Engineering: Mechanical & Aerospace Engr Location: Knoxville, TN The Tickle College of Engineering (TCE) at the University of Tennessee, Knoxville (UTK) is seeking applications for a tenure-track faculty position at the assistant or associate professor level in Electrochemical Energy Storage and Conversion in the Department of Mechanical and Aerospace Engineering. The position is part of a college-level cluster in electrochemical energy storage and conversion and will complement additional faculty hires in related areas. This initiative responds to substantial growth in the battery manufacturing sector in Tennessee and the broader region. The successful candidate will contribute to the teaching mission of the university in their discipline. Candidates at the associate professor level are expected to demonstrate an established record of research excellence and external funding. The successful candidate will bring expertise complementary to the cluster and the broader UTK community and should have a strong interest in collaborative research. Relevant areas include, but are not limited to, electrochemical energy storage and conversion, battery science and engineering, electrochemical testing and characterization, components, devices and systems, manufacturing, recycling, and other areas synergistic with electrochemical energy storage and conversion technologies. Candidates whose research connects materials or electrochemistry to device performance, manufacturing, safety, or system-level engineering are especially encouraged to apply. The search is not primarily focused on standalone materials synthesis or discovery; however, materials-focused candidates whose research connects to broader electrochemical, device, manufacturing, or engineering challenges are encouraged to apply. The Tickle College of Engineering (TCE) is in the midst of an unprecedented period of growth and success, including adding over 30 new faculty to the college as part of ambitious hiring campaigns led by Chancellor Donde Plowman and Dean Matthew Mench. The college has set records in research expenditures, enrollment, incoming student GPA, intellectual property development, and USNWR rank in the past three years. New facilities include the state-of-the art Zeanah Engineering Complex, the University of Tennessee Manufacturing and Design Enterprise (TN-MADE) facility, and the Innovation South building now under construction that will house UTK’s Fibers and Composites Manufacturing Facility (FCMF). TCE currently has 214 tenure/tenure track and 79 non-tenure track faculty in its 10 academic departments and offers 11 undergraduate, 16 MS, and 15 PhD/DE degree programs. Affiliated with TCE and located in Tullahoma, Tennessee, the UT Space Institute is a hub of aerospace and defense research. The college is also home to seven research centers and three interdisciplinary institutes. With approximately 4,800 undergraduate and 1,500 graduate students, the college sits 28th among public universities in the most recent U.S. News and World Report graduate rankings. Faculty in the college have won 29 early career awards (NSF, DOE, DARPA, AFOSR, and ARO) since 2016. In FY25, the college had annual research expenditures of $113.9M. Minimum Qualifications: ·   Applicants must have earned a PhD (or equivalent) in mechanical engineering or a closely related field at the time of appointment. ·   All candidates must be able to teach core and elective Mechanical Engineering courses at undergraduate and graduate level. ·   Candidates for the rank of Associate Professor must have at least five years of post-PhD (or equivalent degree) experience in the relevant field at the time of appointment. Preferred Qualifications: ·   Associate Professor: Primary consideration will be given to applicants who have established a successful research program with demonstrated national visibility, a strong record of teaching and student mentoring, and a compelling vision for future research at UTK. Candidates should demonstrate a record of publication in relevant journals, securing and executing externally funded research from federal, industrial, or other sponsors, and service to their institution and professional community. · Assistant Professor: Applicants should have demonstrated potential to succeed as a junior faculty at an R1 university with a clear and compelling vision for future research at UTK. · All candidates are expected to demonstrate strong communication skills and potential for multi-disciplinary, collaborative research. Applications should be submitted to the online listing (https://apply.interfolio.com/193011) and include: (1) a single-page cover letter articulating the applicant’s interest in and qualifications for this position, (2) a curriculum vitae, (3) a research statement outlining previous and future directions ( maximum four pages ), (4) a teaching and mentoring statement ( maximum three pages ), and (5) the names of three to five references. Applications received by November 1, 2026 will receive priority. Questions pertaining to the position may be sent to the search committee chair, Prof. Brett Compton at brettcompton@utk.edu."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192249": {
      "title": "Assistant or Associate Professor of Nuclear Engineering, Fall 2027",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: Tickle College of Engineering: Nuclear Engineering Location: Knoxville, TN The Department of Nuclear Engineering at the University of Tennessee, Knoxville (UTK) invites applications for a tenure-track faculty position at the Assistant or Associate Professor level. The department will pursue tenure consideration for qualified candidates. The anticipated start date is August 2027 . Duties include teaching undergraduate and graduate courses in nuclear engineering-related subjects, developing and sustaining an externally funded research program, advising graduate students, publishing scholarly work, and providing service to the department, college, university, and professional community. The UT Department of Nuclear Engineering is the largest Nuclear Engineering PhD program in the United States, with a diverse research portfolio and close collaborations with Idaho National Laboratory (INL), Oak Ridge National Laboratory (ORNL), the Y-12 National Security Complex, and more than 150 nuclear-related companies within 50 miles of the campus. The department is housed in the state-of-the-art Zeanah Engineering Complex, home to more than 20 nuclear engineering laboratories and computational resources. The department is committed to cultivating work/life balance and a family-friendly environment for faculty, staff, and students. The Tickle College of Engineering (TCE), comprised of nine departments that house 213 tenure-track and 80+ non-tenure-track faculty, is in the midst of unprecedented growth under Chancellor Donde Plowman and Dean Matthew Mench, having added 30+ new faculty over the past 4 years and set records in research expenditures ( $113.9M in FY25 ), enrollment (4,800 undergraduate and 1,500 graduate students), and increased ranking (now 28th among public universities ) in the US News and World Report list of top engineering colleges . Key partnerships with Oak Ridge National Laboratory and Consolidated Nuclear Security (Y-12) and growth are supported by new facilities including the Zeanah Engineering Complex and the Innovation South building. Knoxville is a vibrant city with a beautiful, walkable downtown, active neighborhoods, a lively nightlife with numerous theaters and museums, a rich live music scene, and eclectic dining. Nestled in the foothills of the Great Smoky Mountains and surrounded by lakes and the Tennessee River, Knoxville offers outstanding access to outdoor recreation and is within 200 miles of four major metropolitan areas. We seek candidates whose research and teaching interests strengthen and broaden the department's capabilities in one or more of the following areas: reliability and maintainability of nuclear systems and components; data analytics, artificial intelligence, digital twin technologies, and cyber security for nuclear applications; nuclear instrumentation and control; radiation instrumentation; robust decision-making, empirical/data-driven modeling, and process & equipment monitoring; thermal-fluid sciences and experimental reactor thermal-hydraulics . Candidates whose work bridges experimentation, computational modeling, sensing, and thermal-fluid or reactor systems analysis in support of reliable, sustainable nuclear power are especially encouraged to apply. Ideally, successful candidates will be able to contribute across more than one of these areas. Preference will be given to applicants with demonstrated (or clearly emerging, for more junior candidates) research success that complements existing departmental strengths and who have a vision for developing collaborative, sustainably funded research programs. Candidates must also show strong commitment to high-quality undergraduate and graduate teaching. Candidates for assistant professor must have a demonstrated potential to develop a nationally recognized, externally funded research program.  Candidates for associate professor must lead a nationally recognized externally funded research program and demonstrate the potential to develop an international recognized research program. Applicants must hold a doctorate in Nuclear Engineering or a closely related field by the effective date of appointment. Applications must be submitted electronically via Interfolio. A complete application should include: A curriculum vitae A statement of research interests and future directions A statement of teaching interests and philosophy A cover letter articulating the applicant's interest in and qualifications for the position The names and contact information of three to five professional references Applications will be reviewed continuously, with priority given to those received by November 30th . Questions may be directed to Dr. Jason Hayward (search committee chair) at jhayward@utk.edu ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193351": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Nuclear Engineering, Fall 2027",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: Tickle College of Engineering: Nuclear Engineering Location: Knoxville, TN The Department of Nuclear Engineering at the University of Tennessee, Knoxville (UTK) is seeking applications to fill a tenure-track faculty position at the Assistant Professor level starting August 1, 2027, across several disciplines in radiological engineering, including health physics, radiation protection, decommissioning, medical physics, nuclear space sciences, and radiation detection.  Duties will include teaching undergraduate and graduate courses in nuclear engineering-related subjects, generating externally funded research, advising graduate students, writing scholarly journal articles, and providing service to the department, college, university, and community. The UTK Department of Nuclear Engineering is part of the Tickle College of Engineering and is one of the largest nuclear programs in the country. It is ranked #1 in graduate enrollment (156), #2 in undergraduate enrollment (432), and #2 in BS and PhD degrees awarded. The department has 21 tenure/tenure-track faculty and 14 non-tenure track faculty. It is currently ranked #3 overall (#2 public university) in the U.S. News and World Report’s ranking of nuclear engineering graduate programs. With over $15.5M in external funding, the department supports outstanding facilities and laboratories, including the Accelerator Research Center featuring 6/9 MV and 450-kV electron accelerators, the Flexible Neutron Source featuring DD and DT generators, the Tennessee Ion Beam Materials Laboratory (TIMBL), the environmental measurements laboratory, and several radiation detection laboratories. It has strong, active collaborations with nearby ORNL (HFIR, SNS), Y12, UCOR, and many of the 150 nuclear-related companies in the area, as well as other national laboratories and companies across the nation. The Tickle College of Engineering (TCE) is in the midst of an unprecedented period of growth and success, including adding over 30 new faculty at all levels to the college over 4 years as part of ambitious hiring campaigns led by Chancellor Donde Plowman and Dean Matthew Mench. The college has set records in research expenditures, enrollment, incoming student GPA, intellectual property development, and USNWR rank in the past three years. Key partnerships with Oak Ridge National Lab (ORNL) through the University of Tennessee Oak Ridge Innovation Institute (UTORII) and Consolidated Nuclear Security (CNS) that manages the Y-12 National Security Complex have created an expanded opportunity space for faculty and students in the region.   New facilities include the state-of-the art Zeanah Engineering Complex, the University of Tennessee Manufacturing and Design Enterprise (TN-MADE) facility, and the Innovation South building that houses UTK’s Fibers and Composites Manufacturing Facility (FCMF), a National Prototype Research Center (NSPC) as part of a partnership with CNS Y-12 under construction, and new facilities for the Biomedical Engineering department and a quantum foundry in planning stages. TCE currently has 213 tenure/tenure track and over 80 non-tenure track faculty in its nine academic departments and offers 11 undergraduate, 16 MS, and 15 PhD/DE degree programs. Affiliated with TCE and located in Tullahoma, Tennessee, the UT Space Institute is a hub of aerospace and defense research. The college is also home to eight research centers and three interdisciplinary institutes. With approximately 4,800 undergraduate and 1,500 graduate students, the college sits 28th among public universities in the most recent U.S. News and World Report graduate rankings. Faculty in the college have won 33 early career awards (NSF, DOE, DARPA, AFOSR, AHA, and Sloan) since 2016. In FY25, the college had annual research expenditures of $113.9M. The department, college, and university strive to be a welcoming community where students, faculty, and staff from all backgrounds are able to belong, thrive, and contribute. The university has established a number of policies to promote work-life balance and support the professional development, success, and overall well-being of faculty. Knoxville, TN is a vibrant city with a beautiful and walkable downtown, active neighborhoods, an effervescent nightlife that includes numerous theaters and museums, a rich live music scene across all genres, and eclectic restaurants. Knoxville is nestled in the foothills of the Great Smoky Mountains, surrounded by lakes and the Tennessee River, providing amazing access to various outdoor activities. Knoxville is within easy driving distance of four major metropolitan areas that regularly host larger cultural and entertainment events. Earned doctorate in Nuclear Engineering or a closely related field at the time the position is filled Research and teaching interests in radiological engineering or related disciplines. Demonstrated record of publishing in high-quality academic journals. Strong communication skills and the ability to work in an interdisciplinary environment. Eligibility to work in the US Applicants should submit 1) an application letter addressing qualifications and motivation, 2) statements of research and teaching interests, 3) a complete curriculum vitae, and 4) the names and contact information for five references. Questions about the position should be directed to Dr. Lawrence Heilbronn, Chair of the Search Committee (voice: 865-974-0982; email lheilbro@utk.edu). Applications will be reviewed beginning November 30 th , 2026, and will continue until the position is filled."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190438": {
      "title": "Property and Real Estate Assistant or Associate Law Professor-UTK Winston College of Law-Fall 2027",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: Winston College of Law Location: Knoxville, TN  THE UNIVERSITY OF TENNESSEE WINSTON COLLEGE OF LAW invites applications from both entry-level and lateral candidates at the assistant or associate professor level for one full-time, tenure-track faculty position to begin at the start of the 2027-28 academic year. The College is interested in candidates with experience and scholarly aptitude in real estate and other dimensions of property law. Ideally, the successful candidate will teach property along with one or more courses in the real estate law area, including real estate finance, land use, or real estate transactions. Secondary interests include bankruptcy, secured transactions, intellectual property, business law, and related courses. Duties include maintaining a full (typically three-course) annual teaching load, engaging in and publishing scholarly research, and participating in law school and university governance and service. Candidates must hold a J.D. or equivalent law degree by the time of appointment and have a strong commitment to excellence in teaching, scholarship, and service. An impressive academic background and significant professional experience are desirable. The successful applicant will be appointed to a rank commensurate with their research, teaching, and service record. In particular, candidates for Assistant Professor should have a demonstrated potential to succeed as a junior faculty member at a state flagship campus such as The University of Tennessee, Knoxville. Candidates for Associate Professor should have a demonstrated record of thoughtful and effective teaching, continued engagement in scholarship, and engagement in their professional community. Applications must be submitted through Interfolio. Applicants should submit a letter of interest, a CV, and the names and contact information for three professional references. Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. To receive full consideration, applications from entry-level candidates not participating in the AALS Faculty Appointments Register process (or those who are not in the first distribution) should be submitted by August 25, 2026. Lateral candidate applications should be submitted by September 1, 2026. For questions, please contact Professor Michelle Kwon, Chair of the Faculty Appointments Committee, at mkwon2@utk.edu ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191659": {
      "title": "Teaching Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor and Program Director of Academic Success -UTK Winston College of Law-Fall 2027",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: Winston College of Law Location: Knoxville, TN  THE UNIVERSITY OF TENNESSEE WINSTON COLLEGE OF LAW invites applications for the position of Director of its Academic Success Program (ASP). This position, which will commence no later than July 1, 2027, is a full time, non-tenure track faculty position. It is a 12-month appointment. The ASP Director will coordinate, manage, and teach in the Academic Success Programs at Winston Law. The Academic Success programming currently includes a 1L acclimation program; an Academic Enrichment Program for selected spring semester 1L students; one-on-one advising for students in the Academic Alert program; a 3L bar preparation class; and other programs and advising for the full student body on topics of academic success and bar exam preparation. The ASP Director will have significant creative input in shaping the future direction of the program. The ASP Director will work with the ASP Associate Director and collaborate with the rest of the faculty to identify opportunities to change the curriculum, teaching methods, and assessment techniques to equip students with the tools to succeed in law school and on the bar exam. As a member of the faculty, the Director will hold the initial rank of Teaching Assistant Professor (or Teaching Associate  Professor or Teaching Professor, with rank depending on the level of appropriate relevant experience). The individual will have the opportunity for appointmentrenewal and promotion pursuant to the University of Tennessee’s Faculty Handbook . As a member of the faculty, the ASP Director will participate in faculty meetings, will exercise voting rights as defined by the Winston Law’s bylaws, and will have an annual allowance of funds to support travel to attend and speak at professional conferences. Candidates must hold A law degree from an accredited law school by the time of appointment , indicators of academic success in school, and U.S. bar membership are required. Additional desired qualifications include at least two years in an academic setting, ideally in the area of law school academic support; knowledge of learning theory (as evidenced by an advanced degree, publications, speeches, etc.); and an understanding of the NextGenUBE bar examination, the Legacy UBE bar examination, the Multistate Professional Responsibility Examination, and other licensing exams administered by various states. The candidate must also have the qualifications appropriate for one of the following non-tenure track academic appointments. Candidates for Teaching Assistant Professor must show promise for excellence in areas of responsibility assigned to them. Candidates for Teaching Associate Professor must have demonstrated excellence in areas of responsibility assigned to them. Candidates for Teaching Professor must have demonstrated a consistent record of excellence in areas of responsibility assigned to them. A candidate may demonstrate excellence or promise of excellence through their teaching record, knowledge of learning theory, experience in preparing students for the bar examination, administrative skills, and other relevant academic experience.  The initial rank will depend on the candidate’s experience and skill sets. Applications must be submitted through Interfolio. Applicants should submit a letter of interest, a CV, and the names and contact information for three professional references. Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. To receive full consideration, applications should be submitted by September 15, 2026. For questions, please contact Professor Lucy Jewel, Chair of the Faculty Search Committee, at ljewel@utk.edu ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190467": {
      "title": "Adjunct Clinical Assistant, Associate or Full Professor – Nurse Anesthesia - College of Nursing - Fall 2026_Spring 2027",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: College of Nursing Location: College of Nursing - Knoxville, Tennessee The College of Nursing at the University of Tennessee, Knoxville seeks a dynamic and engaging Certified Registered Nurse Anesthetist (CRNA) faculty for a part-time, adjunct position at the Clinical Assistant, Associate or Full Professor rank . Details on the College of Nursing Programs are available at https://nursing.utk.edu/ About the Position The Adjunct faculty position provides academic and clinical instructional support to the Doctor of Nursing Practice -Nurse Anesthesia program.  Depending on program needs and the faculty member’s expertise, responsibilities may include didactic, simulation, laboratory, clinical, or scholarly-project instruction. Assignments will be determined by the Assistant Dean of Graduate Programs in consultation with the Program Administrator/Concentration Coordinator and appropriate course director and/or clinical coordinator. The ideal candidate will demonstrate subject-matter expertise, current knowledge of nurse anesthesia practice, and the ability to facilitate student learning in an academically rigorous doctoral program.  The candidate should have a strong commitment to teaching, student success, professional practice, and collaborative engagement with students, faculty, staff, and clinical partners. Duties: 1. Teaching and student support a. Conduct didactic, simulation, laboratory, and/or clinical teaching as assigned by the Assistant Dean of Graduate Programs. b. Provide academic and clinical guidance to students within the scope of the assigned course or clinical responsibilities and refer significant academic, clinical, conduct, or progression concerns to the course director and Program Administrator/Concentration Coordinator. c. Evaluate student learning and performance using established course objectives, evaluation tools, grading criteria, and program policies, and provide timely, constructive feedback. d. Maintain appropriate documentation of student attendance, performance, clinical experiences, and evaluation outcomes as required by the program, college, university, and accrediting bodies. 2. Curriculum and course contributions a. Contribute to student scholarly projects when assigned and appropriately qualified and assist with curriculum and course activities such as course coordination, evaluation materials, and simulation development. b. Participate in program and course review and revision as requested and within the scope of the adjunct assignment. c. Review and revise assigned instructional content to reflect program outcomes, the AACN Essentials, applicable COA standards, Tennessee Board of Nursing requirements, current evidence, professional standards, and best practices. d. Teach graduate nursing courses in the Nurse Anesthesia Program and, when appropriately qualified and assigned, contribute to DNP scholarly-project courses. e. Apply evidence-based educational practices to curriculum development, teaching, simulation, student evaluation, and clinical instruction. 3. Clinical education a. Supervise nurse anesthesia students only when assigned, appropriately credentialed, and authorized by the program and clinical site. b. Collaborate with the Program Administrator/Concentration Coordinator, course director, clinical coordinator, and clinical partners to support student orientation, clinical objectives, schedules, supervision, evaluation, and achievement of required clinical experiences. c. Promptly report concerns related to student performance, patient safety, supervision, clinical-site quality, or the learning environment through established program channels. d. Maintain compliance with clinical-site requirements, including credentialing, orientation, health documentation, confidentiality, safety, and other applicable institutional requirements. e. Support a clinical learning environment that promotes patient safety, professional conduct, respectful communication, student well-being, and progressive development of competence. 4. Program participation and service a. Participate in the evaluation and ongoing enhancement of the Nurse Anesthesia curriculum as relevant to the faculty member’s assigned responsibilities. b. Contribute to the goals and strategic priorities of the Nurse Anesthesia Program, College of Nursing, and University within the scope of the adjunct appointment. c. Attend required program, course, orientation, calibration, and faculty-development meetings related to the adjunct assignment. d. Collaborate effectively with faculty, staff, students, and clinical partners to support interprofessional academic, clinical, simulation, and scholarly opportunities. e. Maintain professional competence, required licensure and certification, and current knowledge relevant to assigned teaching responsibilities. f. Complete required university, college, program, and clinical-site training within established timelines. g. Perform other instructional or program-related duties as assigned by the Program Director Administrator/Concentration Coordinator and consistent with the terms and effort allocation of the adjunct appointment. Required Qualifications: Earned doctorate (DNP or PhD) in Nursing from an accredited institution at time of appointment. Currently licensed as a registered nurse in Tennessee or eligible for licensure through the interstate Nurse Licensure Compact prior to employment. Current certification/recertification as a Certified Registered Nurse Anesthetist (CRNA) by the NBCRNA. 3+ years of experience as a practicing CRNA; Professional experience commensurate with rank. Evidence of successful classroom and/or clinical teaching experience. This position is open to those who qualify and can be appointed as one of the following ranks in the College of Nursing. Rank appointment is dependent on education and qualifications relative to the university and college standards and will be determined by academic leadership in accordance with faculty handbook, policies, and procedures. Adjunct Clinical Assistant Professor: This rank is for those who have completed a doctoral degree or terminal degree appropriate to the field or who are licensed or certified to practice the profession where appropriate. Individuals holding this rank have shown promise for excellence in areas of responsibility recorded in the hiring unit’s annual documentation of the NTT faculty workload distribution and responsibilities. Adjunct Clinical Associate Professor: This rank is for those who have completed a doctoral degree or a terminal degree appropriate to the field and who are licensed or certified to practice the profession where appropriate. Individuals holding this rank have demonstrated excellence in areas of responsibility recorded in the hiring unit’s annual documentation of the NTT faculty’s workload distribution and responsibilities. Adjunct Clinical Professor: This rank is for those who have completed a doctoral degree or a terminal degree appropriate to the field and who are licensed or certified to practice the profession where appropriate. Individuals holding this rank have demonstrated a consistent record of excellence in areas of responsibility recorded in the hiring unit’s annual documentation of the NTT faculty workload distribution and responsibilities. Application Instructions To apply, please submit your application and materials through our Interfolio application system.  Applications should include: (1) a letter of interest addressing teaching interests and how the applicant’s background and experience make them an ideal candidate, (2) a comprehensive curriculum vitae, and (3) the names and contact information (address, phone number, and e-mail address) for at least three professional references. Review of applicants will be two weeks from date of posting and will continue until the position is filled. Rank and salary based on qualifications and experience. For questions about the position, please email conhr@utk.edu ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190472": {
      "title": "Clinical Assistant, Associate or Full Professor - Assistant Program Administrator, Nurse Anesthesia Program – College of Nursing - Fall 2026",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: College of Nursing Location: College of Nursing - Knoxville, Tennessee The College of Nursing at the University of Tennessee, Knoxville, seeks an engaging nurse anesthesia educator and academic leader for a full-time, 12-month faculty appointment at the Clinical Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor rank. This faculty role a lso serves as the Assistant Program Administrator/ Assistant Concentration Coordinator for the Doctor of Nursing Practice - Nurse Anesthetist program . Details on the College of Nursing Programs are available at https://nursing.utk.edu/ About the Position The Clinical Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor - Assistant Program Administrator provides academic, educational, and clinical instruction , and administrative support to the Program Administrator/Concentration Coordinator of the Nurse Anesthesia Program, assisting with the implementation and evaluation of program operations and providing leadership for specifically delegated areas of responsibility. This faculty leadership position is integral to maintaining excellence in program quality, student outcomes, and regulatory compliance. Faculty Responsibilities Teach graduate nurse anesthesia and DNP courses in areas of expertise, including development of course objectives , learning activities, instructional materials, examinations, grading rubrics, and other assessments. Design, deliver, and evaluate simulation-based education, including high-fidelity anesthesia scenarios, skills laboratories, debriefing, and competency assessment. Provide direct clinical instruction and supervision of nurse anesthesia students in affiliated clinical settings, consistent with student level, patient acuity, case complexity, safety requirements, and accreditation expectations. Evaluate student performance in didactic, simulation, and clinical settings; provide timely formative and summative feedback; document achievement of competencies and clinical requirements; and contribute to remediation plans when needed. Serve as academic advisor and faculty mentor for nurse anesthesia students and chair or serve on DNP scholarly project committees as assigned. Maintain currency in nurse anesthesia clinical practice, educational methods, simulation pedagogy, accreditation standards, and certification requirements. Engage in clinical scholarship, evidence-based practice, quality improvement, professional dissemination, and/or educational innovation consistent with the clinical faculty role and assigned workload. Participate in faculty governance, college and university committees, professional service, recruitment, orientation, and other responsibilities expected of full-time faculty. Delegated Program Leadership and Administrative Responsibilities Assist the Program Administrator/Concentration Coordinator in administering the academic and clinical components of the Nurse Anesthesia Program while maintaining an active faculty teaching role. Lead or coordinate delegated aspects of curriculum implementation, course coordination, simulation education, clinical education, student assessment, and program evaluation. Support compliance with COA, CCNE, university, state, and federal requirements, including preparation of accreditation reports, self-study materials, site visits, and ongoing documentation. Collaborate with clinical sites, preceptors, clinical coordinators, and faculty to ensure appropriate placements , supervision, case experiences, student safety, and a supportive clinical learning environment. Monitor student achievement and clinical experiences; communicate significant academic, clinical, progression, conduct, or safety concerns to the Program Administrator/Concentration Coordinator and participate in timely intervention and remediation. Coordinate designated continuous quality improvement activities, including collection, analysis, and reporting of student outcomes, clinical data, stakeholder feedback, and other evaluation measures. Participate in admissions, recruitment, orientation, faculty onboarding, student success initiatives, and resource planning as delegated. Provide recommendations regarding curriculum, simulation, clinical sites, equipment, staffing, and other program resources. Assume delegated program leadership responsibilities during the Program Administrator/Concentration Coordinator’s absence while the Program Administrator retains ultimate authority and accountability. Required Qualifications Earned DNP or PhD from an accredited institution, conferred by the time of appointment. Current, unrestricted registered nurse licensure in Tennessee or eligibility for licensure through the Nurse Licensure Compact by the time of appointment. Current certification/recertification as a Certified Registered Nurse Anesthetist (CRNA) through the National Board of Certification and Recertification for Nurse Anesthetists (NBCRNA). At least three years of professional practice experience as a CRNA, with professional accomplishments commensurate with the proposed faculty rank. At least two years of teaching experience in a nurse anesthesia or related graduate nursing program. Documented effectiveness in didactic, simulation, and/or clinical teaching and in assessment of student learning. Demonstrated ability to develop, implement, and evaluate nurse anesthesia curriculum and clinical learning experiences. Demonstrated leadership, communication, collaboration, and organizational skills. Experience with COA accreditation, program evaluation, or continuous quality improvement. Preferred Qualifications Teaching experience in a nurse anesthesia or related graduate nursing program. Experience designing and facilitating high-fidelity simulation. Evidence of clinical scholarship , quality improvement, professional dissemination, or educational innovation. Salary range for this position $200,000 - $215,000 This position is open to those who qualify and can be appointed as one of the following ranks in the College of Nursing. Rank appointment is dependent on education and qualifications relative to the university and college standards and will be determined by academic leadership in accordance with faculty handbook, policies, and procedures. Clinical Assistant Professor: This rank is for those who have completed a doctoral degree or terminal degree appropriate to the field or who are licensed or certified to practice the profession where appropriate. Individuals holding this rank have shown promise for excellence in areas of responsibility recorded in the hiring unit’s annual documentation of the NTT faculty workload distribution and responsibilities. Clinical Associate Professor: This rank is for those who have completed a doctoral degree or a terminal degree appropriate to the field and who are licensed or certified to practice the profession where appropriate. Individuals holding this rank have demonstrated excellence in areas of responsibility recorded in the hiring unit’s annual documentation of the NTT faculty’s workload distribution and responsibilities. Clinical Professor: This rank is for those who have completed a doctoral degree or a terminal degree appropriate to the field and who are licensed or certified to practice the profession where appropriate. Individuals holding this rank have demonstrated a consistent record of excellence in areas of responsibility recorded in the hiring unit’s annual documentation of the NTT faculty workload distribution and responsibilities. To apply, please submit your application and materials through our Interfolio application system. Applications should include: (1) a letter of interest addressing teaching interests and how the applicant’s background and experience make them an ideal candidate, (2) a comprehensive curriculum vitae, and (3) the names and contact information (address, phone number, and e-mail address) for at least three professional references. Review of applicants will be two weeks from date of posting and will continue until the position is filled. Rank and salary based on qualifications and experience. For questions about the position, please email conhr@utk.edu ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190475": {
      "title": "Clinical Assistant, Associate or Full Professor- Program Administrator/Concentration Coordinator, Nurse Anesthesia Program - College of Nursing - Fall 2026",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: College of Nursing Location: College of Nursing - Knoxville, Tennessee The College of Nursing at the University of Tennessee, Knoxville, seeks an engaging nurse anesthesia educator and academic leader for a full-time, 12-month faculty appointment at the Clinical Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor rank. This faculty role also serves as the Program Administrator/Concentration Coordinator for the Doctor of Nursing Practice - Nurse Anesthetist program . Details on the College of Nursing Programs are available at https://nursing.utk.edu/ About the Position The Clinical Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor - Program Administrator/Concentration Coordinator provides academic, educational, and clinical instruction, and administrative leadership for the Nurse Anesthesia Program, ensuring excellence in program quality, student outcomes, and regulatory compliance. This faculty leadership position is responsible for maintaining full compliance with accreditation standards established by the Council on Accreditation of Nurse Anesthesia Educational Programs (COA) and the Commission on Collegiate Nursing Education (CCNE), as well as applicable university, state, and federal requirements. Reporting to the Assistant Dean of Graduate Programs, this faculty role provides oversight for all aspects of program administration and operations, including curriculum design and continuous improvement, clinical education and experiential learning, student mentorship, program assessment, and accreditation activities. Faculty Responsibilities · Teach graduate nurse anesthesia and DNP courses in areas of expertise, including development of course objectives, learning activities, instructional materials, examinations, grading rubrics, and other assessments. · Design, deliver, and evaluate simulation-based education, including high-fidelity anesthesia scenarios, skills laboratories, debriefing, and competency assessment. · Provide direct clinical instruction and supervision of nurse anesthesia students in affiliated clinical settings, consistent with student level, patient acuity, case complexity, safety requirements, and accreditation expectations. · Evaluate student performance in didactic, simulation, and clinical settings; provide timely formative and summative feedback; document achievement of competencies and clinical requirements; and implement or oversee remediation when needed. · Serve as academic advisor and faculty mentor for nurse anesthesia students and chair or serve on DNP scholarly project committees as assigned. · Maintain currency in nurse anesthesia clinical practice, educational methods, simulation pedagogy, accreditation standards, and certification requirements. · Engage in clinical scholarship, evidence-based practice, quality improvement, professional dissemination, and/or educational innovation consistent with the clinical faculty role and assigned workload. · Participate in faculty governance, college and university committees, professional service, recruitment, orientation, and other responsibilities expected of full-time faculty. Program Leadership and Administrative Responsibilities · Provide overall leadership, authority, and accountability for the academic and clinical components of the Nurse Anesthesia Program while retaining an active faculty teaching role. · Lead curriculum development, mapping, implementation, evaluation, and continuous revision to ensure coherence, appropriate sequencing, and alignment with DNP Essentials, nurse anesthesia competencies, certification expectations, and program outcomes. · Ensure compliance with Council on Accreditation of Nurse Anesthesia Educational Programs (COA), Commission on Collegiate Nursing Education (CCNE), university, state, and federal requirements; serve as the primary program contact for accreditation matters. · Provide oversight of clinical education, including clinical site quality, student placements, appropriate supervision, case and experience requirements, student safety, and the clinical learning environment. · Lead systematic program evaluation and continuous quality improvement using student outcomes, clinical data, stakeholder feedback, certification results, attrition data, and other measures. · Mentor and coordinate program faculty; contribute to faculty recruitment, onboarding, workload planning, development, and evaluation in accordance with college and university policies. · Oversee student admissions, progression, remediation, conduct, and dismissal processes in collaboration with appropriate faculty and academic leaders. · Develop and administer the program budget and recommend allocation of faculty, staff, clinical, simulation, equipment, and other resources. · Maintain effective partnerships with clinical agencies, preceptors, faculty, students, alumni, professional organizations, and accreditation bodies. · Delegate appropriate responsibilities to the Assistant Program Administrator and other faculty while retaining final program authority and accountability. Required Qualifications · Earned DNP or PhD from an accredited institution, conferred by the time of appointment. · Current, unrestricted registered nurse licensure in Tennessee or eligibility for licensure through the Nurse Licensure Compact by the time of appointment. · Current certification/recertification as a Certified Registered Nurse Anesthetist (CRNA) through the National Board of Certification and Recertification for Nurse Anesthetists (NBCRNA). · At least three years of professional practice experience as a CRNA, with professional accomplishments commensurate with the proposed faculty rank. · At least two years of teaching experience in a nurse anesthesia or related graduate nursing program. · Documented effectiveness in didactic, simulation, and/or clinical teaching and in assessment of student learning. · Demonstrated ability to develop, implement, and evaluate nurse anesthesia curriculum and clinical learning experiences. ·   Demonstrated leadership, communication, collaboration, and organizational skills. Experience in academic program leadership or administration. Experience with COA accreditation, program self-study, reporting, and continuous quality improvement Preferred Qualifications · Experience with COA accreditation, program self-study, reporting, and continuous quality improvement. · Experience designing and facilitating high-fidelity simulation. · Evidence of clinical scholarship, quality improvement, professional dissemination, or educational innovation. Salary range for this position $230,000 - $260,000 This position is open to those who qualify and can be appointed as one of the following ranks in the College of Nursing. Rank appointment is dependent on education and qualifications relative to the university and college standards and will be determined by academic leadership in accordance with faculty handbook, policies, and procedures. Clinical Assistant Professor: This rank is for those who have completed a doctoral degree or terminal degree appropriate to the field or who are licensed or certified to practice the profession where appropriate. Individuals holding this rank have shown promise for excellence in areas of responsibility recorded in the hiring unit’s annual documentation of the NTT faculty workload distribution and responsibilities. Clinical Associate Professor: This rank is for those who have completed a doctoral degree or a terminal degree appropriate to the field and who are licensed or certified to practice the profession where appropriate. Individuals holding this rank have demonstrated excellence in areas of responsibility recorded in the hiring unit’s annual documentation of the NTT faculty’s workload distribution and responsibilities. Clinical Professor: This rank is for those who have completed a doctoral degree or a terminal degree appropriate to the field and who are licensed or certified to practice the profession where appropriate. Individuals holding this rank have demonstrated a consistent record of excellence in areas of responsibility recorded in the hiring unit’s annual documentation of the NTT faculty workload distribution and responsibilities. To apply, please submit your application and materials through our Interfolio application system.  Applications should include: (1) a letter of interest addressing teaching interests and how the applicant’s background and experience make them an ideal candidate, (2) a comprehensive curriculum vitae, and (3) the names and contact information (address, phone number, and e-mail address) for at least three professional references. Review of applicants will be two weeks from date of posting and will continue until the position is filled. Rank and salary based on qualifications and experience. For questions about the position, please email conhr@utk.edu ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192673": {
      "title": "Adjunct Clinical Instructor - College of Nursing - Academic Year 2026 - 2027",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: College of Nursing Location: Knoxville, TN The College of Nursing at the University of Tennessee, Knoxville, seeks a dynamic and engaging Adjunct Clinical Instructor (part-time) to contribute to educating the next generation of health care professionals. Details on the College of Nursing Programs are available at https://nursing.utk.edu/ About the College of Nursing With a longstanding tradition of excellence in nursing education, research, and clinical practice, we are proud to be a guiding force in healthcare education. We embrace innovation while honoring the values that have shaped our success. By offering cutting-edge academic programs, supporting impactful research, and forging strong community partnerships, we prepare healthcare professionals who deliver high-quality, culturally responsive care in a diverse and ever-changing world. Join the Vol Nurse Family! Get to know CON About the Position This is a adjunct faculty role that focuses on delivering high-quality undergraduate and/or graduate nursing education, including clinical supervision. Responsibilities encompass instruction, student assessment, and collaboration on clinical placements and curriculum revisions. Appointments are made on a semester-by-semester basis, contingent upon the College’s teaching needs. Duties: Participate in the ongoing development, implementation, evaluation, and enhancement of nursing academic programs: 1. Participate in program and course revisions. a. Teach graduate and undergraduate nursing courses as assigned b. Supervise nursing students as assigned c. Participate in the evaluation and ongoing enhancement of the curricula d. Apply evidence from best education practices to curriculum development and to teaching 2. Serve the College of Nursing, University, community and profession: a. Participate in community and professional activities as appropriate 3. Contribute to the mission and strategic priorities of the College of Nursing and the University of Tennessee Knoxville. 4. Collaborate effectively with other faculty and staff members both within the College of Nursing and throughout the University and community to develop inter-professional academic, clinical, and research opportunities. Work Location Various clinical sites in the Knoxville, Tennessee area Clinical models anticipate two days a week either Monday, Tuesday or Wednesday, Thursday Required Qualifications: Bachelor of Science in Nursing (BSN) with at least 5 years clinical experience Licensure as a registered nurse in Tennessee prior to employment Effective interpersonal skills and cultural competence Ability to work with a diverse group of students and colleagues Desirable Qualifications: Master of Science in Nursing (MSN) at time of appointment Evidence of successful clinical and/or classroom teaching experience Eligibility for APRN licensure in Tennessee Experience with instructional media and technology To apply, please submit your application and materials through our Interfolio application system. Applications should include: (1) a letter of interest addressing teaching interests and how the applicant’s background and experience make them an ideal candidate, (2) a comprehensive curriculum vitae, and (3) the names and contact information (address, phone number, e-mail addresses for at least three professional references, and a copy of your TN RN licensure or Multi-state compact. R eview of applicants will begin two weeks after the position is posted and will be ongoing throughout the academic year. For questions about the position, please email conhr@utk.edu For more information on the College of Nursing, visit our website: http://nursing.utk.edu/ ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192675": {
      "title": "Adjunct Clinical Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor - College of Nursing - Academic Year 2026 - 2027",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: College of Nursing Location: Knoxville, TN The College of Nursing at the University of Tennessee, Knoxville, seeks a dynamic and engaging Adjunct Clinical Assistant, Associate or Full Professor (part-time) to contribute to educating the next generation of health care professionals. Details on the College of Nursing Programs are available at https://nursing.utk.edu/ About the College of Nursing With a longstanding tradition of excellence in nursing education, research, and clinical practice, we are proud to be a guiding force in healthcare education. We embrace innovation while honoring the values that have shaped our success. By offering cutting-edge academic programs, supporting impactful research, and forging strong community partnerships, we prepare healthcare professionals who deliver high-quality, culturally responsive care in a diverse and ever-changing world. Get to know CON About the Position This is a adjunct faculty role that focuses on delivering high-quality undergraduate and/or graduate nursing education, including clinical supervision. Responsibilities encompass instruction, student assessment, and collaboration on clinical placements and curriculum revisions. Appointments are made on a semester-by-semester basis, contingent upon the College’s teaching needs. Duties: Participate in the ongoing development, implementation, evaluation, and enhancement of nursing academic programs: 1. Participate in program and course revisions. a. Teach graduate and undergraduate nursing courses as assigned b. Supervise nursing students as assigned c. Participate in the evaluation and ongoing enhancement of the curricula d. Apply evidence from best education practices to curriculum development and to teaching 2. Serve the College of Nursing, University, community and profession: a. Participate in community and professional activities as appropriate 3. Contribute to the mission and strategic priorities of the College of Nursing and the University of Tennessee Knoxville. 4. Collaborate effectively with other faculty and staff members both within the College of Nursing and throughout the University and community to develop inter-professional academic, clinical, and research opportunities. Required Qualifications: · Earned doctorate (PhD or DNP) at time of appointment. · Licensure as a registered nurse in Tennessee prior to employment. · Minimum of 3 years of nursing practice experience. · Effective interpersonal skills and cultural competence. · Ability to work with a diverse group of students and colleagues. Desirable Qualifications: · Evidence of successful classroom and/or clinical teaching experience · Eligibility for APRN licensure in Tennessee · If teaching NP students, national certification as a nurse practitioner and prior to employment recognition by the Tennessee Board of Nursing as an advanced practice registered nurse · Experience with instructional media and technology This position is open to those who qualify and can be appointed as one of the following ranks in the College of Nursing. Rank appointment is dependent on education and qualifications relative to the university and college standards and will be determined by academic leadership in accordance with faculty handbook, policies, and procedures. Adjunct Clinical Assistant Professor: This rank is for those who have completed a doctoral degree or terminal degree appropriate to the field or who are licensed or certified to practice the profession where appropriate. Individuals holding this rank have shown promise for excellence in areas of responsibility recorded in the hiring unit’s annual documentation of the NTT faculty’s workload distribution and responsibilities. Adjunct Clinical Associate Professor: This rank is for those who have completed a doctoral degree or a terminal degree appropriate to the field and who are licensed or certified to practice the profession where appropriate. Individuals holding this rank have demonstrated excellence in areas of responsibility recorded in the hiring unit’s annual documentation of the NTT faculty’s workload distribution and responsibilities. Adjunct Clinical Professor: This rank is for those who have completed a doctoral degree or a terminal degree appropriate to the field and who are licensed or certified to practice the profession where appropriate. Individuals holding this rank have demonstrated a consistent record of excellence in areas of responsibility recorded in the hiring unit’s annual documentation of the NTT faculty’s workload distribution and responsibilities. To apply, please submit your application and materials through our Interfolio application system.  Applications should include: (1) a letter of interest addressing teaching interests and how the applicant’s background and experience make them an ideal candidate, (2) a comprehensive curriculum vitae, and (3) the names and contact information (address, phone number, and e-mail address) for at least three professional references, (4) A copy or certification letter of your TN RN licensure or multi-state compact. The review of applicants will begin two weeks after the posting date and will be ongoing throughout the academic year. Rank and salary based on qualifications and experience. For questions about the position, please email conhr@utk.edu.  For more information on the College of Nursing, visit our website: http://nursing.utk.edu/"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193187": {
      "title": "Associate Dean of Academic Affairs and Associate, Full Professor, Clinical Associate Professor, or Clinical Full Professor – College of Nursing – Spring 2027 or Fall 2027",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: College of Nursing Location: College of Nursing - Knoxville, Tennessee The College of Nursing at the University of Tennessee, Knoxville, seeks a n Associate Dean of Academic Affairs and Associate Professor, Full Professor, Clinical Associate Professor or Clinical Full Professor; the position is open to both clinical and tenure-track senior level ranks in the title of the position. Details on the College of Nursing Programs are available at https://nursing.utk.edu/ About the College of Nursing With a longstanding tradition of excellence in nursing education, research, and clinical practice, we are proud to be a guiding force in healthcare education. We embrace innovation while honoring the values that have shaped our success. By offering cutting-edge academic programs, supporting impactful research, and forging strong community partnerships, we prepare healthcare professionals who deliver high-quality, culturally responsive care in a diverse and ever-changing world. Come j oin the Vol Nurse Family! Get to know CON About the Position The Associate Dean for Academic Affairs is the College of Nursing’s senior academic administrator who provides strategic leadership and operational oversight for the College of Nursing’s educational programs, faculty development, and academic standards. R eport ing directly to the Dean of the College , this role serves a s a vital member of the executive team, driving the implementation of the college 's academic mission, ensuring curriculum quality, supporting faculty excellence, and maintaining compliance with accreditation standards . Responsibilities : Provides overall strategic direction, vision, and leadership for academic programs of the College of Nursing. Represent the College of Nursing's interests internally on matters related to academic programs within the University and externally to constituents. Manage overall academic budget; makes recommendations and implements actions that increase funds. Maintain personal academic faculty obligations in teaching, service, and research in accordance with area of expertise. Curriculum & Program Oversight Direct the development, assessment, and continuous improvement of academic programs. Oversee curriculum review processes, course scheduling, and academic catalog updates. Create synergy across the responsibilities of the Assistant Dean for Undergraduate Programs, Assistant Dean for Graduate Programs, PhD and DNP Program Directors and Program Coordinators. Responsible for all academic documents and processes including academic calendar, policies and procedures, master schedule, timetables , handbooks, and catalog. Responsible for all clinical affiliation agreements, adjunct faculty, and external academic reports and surveys. Provide development, support, and guidance to administrators for whom are responsible . P repar e all administrative reports on academic programs. Develop and implement enrollment plans. Accreditation & Quality Assurance Holds ultimate responsibility for maintaining accreditation status of the School of Nursing's academic programs. Assume administrative accountability for ensuring the quality of the degree-granting educational programs. Manage accreditation compliance, self-study reports, and external agency reviews. Use data-driven insights and learning outcome assessments to enhance academic quality. I mplement a continuous quality improvement process for monitoring academic outcomes. Creates a seamless data management system for monitoring and facilitating academic programs. Faculty Affairs & Governance Responsible for faculty recruitment, onboarding, evaluation, promotion, and tenure processes. Support faculty development, mentorship, and teaching excellence. Facilitate shared governance and resolve faculty or academic policy grievances. Conduct annual review of clinical faculty and academic affairs staff. Establish productivity and workload standards in consultation with faculty and other administrators. Student Success & Administration Oversee delivery of student services and evaluates the Director of Student Services. Coordinate activities of academic programs with campus/university academic support systems to ensure a well-integrated plan of operation compatible with the College of Nursing's goals and mission. Address complex student academic concerns, appeals, and petitions. Advise admissions, student services, and advising teams to support retention and progression. Required Qualifications: Earned doctorate (PhD or DNP) in nursing, education or related field. Licensure as a registered nurse in Tennessee prior to employment. Five years’ experience in academic nursing education with increasing administrative responsibility. Professional experience commensurate with rank. Experience with accreditation, curriculum development, and academic assessment. Demonstrated success in recruiting, mentoring, retaining , and advocating for outstanding faculty, staff, and students. Experience with faculty reappointment, promotion, and governance processes. This position is open to those who qualify and can be appointed as one of the following ranks in the College of Nursing. Rank appointments are dependent on education and qualifications relative to the university and college standards and will be determined by academic leadership in accordance with faculty handbook, policies, and procedures. Tenure/Tenure Track Ranks Professors are expected to 1. hold the doctorate or other terminal degree of the discipline, or present equivalent training and experience appropriate to the particular appointment 2. be accomplished teachers 3. have achieved and maintain a nationally recognized record in disciplinary research / scholarship / creative activity/ engaged scholarship 4. have achieved and maintain a record of significant institutional, disciplinary, and/or professional service or outreach engagement 5. serve as mentors to junior colleagues 6. have normally served as an associate professor for at least five years 7. have shown beyond doubt that they work well with colleagues and students in performing their university responsibilities Associate professors are expected to 1. hold the doctorate or other terminal degree of the discipline, or to present equivalent training and experience as appropriate to the particular appointment 2. be good teachers 3. have achieved and maintain a recognized record in disciplinary research / scholarship / creative activity/ engaged scholarship 4. have achieved and maintain a record of institutional, disciplinary, and/or professional service or outreach engagement 5. have normally served as an assistant professor for at least five years 6. have demonstrated that they work well with colleagues and students in performing their university responsibilities Non-Tenure Track Ranks: Clinical professor: This rank is for those who have completed a doctoral degree or terminal degree appropriate to the field and who are licensed or certified to practice the profession where appropriate . Individuals holding this rank have demonstrated a consistent record of excellence in areas of responsibility assigned to them. Clinical associate professor: This rank is for those who have completed a doctoral degree or terminal degree appropriate to the field and who are licensed or certified to practice the profession where appropriate . Individuals holding this rank have demonstrated excellence in areas of responsibility assigned to them. To apply, please submit your application and materials through our Interfolio application system.  Applications should include: (1) a letter of interest addressing teaching interests and how the applicant’s background and experience make them an ideal candidate, (2) a comprehensive curriculum vitae, and (3) the names and contact information (address, phone number, and e-mail address) for at least three professional references. The review of applicants will begin two weeks after the posting date and will continue throughout the academic year until the position is filled . Rank and salary are based on qualifications and experience. For questions about the position, please email conhr@utk.edu.  For more information on the College of Nursing, visit our website: http://nursing.utk.edu/"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194032": {
      "title": "Associate Professor - College of Nursing - Fall 2027 (2 Positions)",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: College of Nursing Location: Knoxville, TN The College of Nursing at the University of Tennessee, Knoxville, invites applications for two open 9-month full-time , tenure track faculty positions at the rank of Associate Professor to start August 1, 2027. We are seeking a visionary scholar who brings innovative and forward-thinking approaches to advancing nursing education, research, and practice in the evolving health landscape. Details on the College of Nursing Programs are available at https://nursing.utk.edu/ About the College of Nursing  - With a longstanding tradition of excellence in nursing education, research, and clinical practice, we are proud to be a guiding force in healthcare education. By offering cutting-edge academic programs, supporting impactful research, and forging strong community partnerships, we prepare healthcare professionals who deliver high-quality, culturally responsive care in a diverse and ever-changing world. Come join the Vol Nurse Family! Get to know CON Highlights: Generous start-up package including relocation stipend and protected scholarship time for the first 2 years. Scholarship and grant writing support from the college (Office of Research & Scholarship) and the university (Office of Research, Innovation, and Economic Development). Inter-professional education and research opportunities Member of the National Hartford Centers for Gerontological Nursing Excellence. State-of-the-art accredited Health Innovation & Simulation Lab run in collaboration with the College of Engineering. Collaborations with Pat Summitt Alzheimer’s clinic, University of Tennessee Medical Center, Dolly Parton Children’s Hospital and Oak Ridge National Laboratory. The college owns and operates Vine Clinic, a nurse-managed school-based health center with telehealth to other underserved schools. About the Position The ideal candidate will demonstrate a record of scholarly productivity, sustained externally funded research and a commitment to mentoring students and collaborating with faculty colleagues in a dynamic academic environment.  The successful candidate must possess a strong record of excellence in teaching, along with a demonstrated history of impactful research supported by extramural funding. Key responsibilities include delivering high-quality, engaging instruction; mentoring and advising students; conducting influential research with external funding; and fostering interdisciplinary collaboration across diverse academic and professional domains. Position Responsibilities: Research and Scholarship Establish and maintain an active program of scholarly research with a clear agenda and measurable outcomes toward extramural funding. Disseminate research findings through peer-reviewed publications, presentations, and conferences. Pursue external funding and/or lead scholarly initiatives aligned with college priorities and disciplinary needs. Mentor junior faculty and collaborate across disciplines, clinical partners, and community partners as appropriate. Teaching and Mentorship Foster interdisciplinary academic collaborations across health, engineering, informatics, and design Teach undergraduate and/or graduate courses as assigned. Mentor and advise students in academic and professional development. Contribute to curriculum development, assessment, and continuous improvement of academic programs. Service and Leadership Serve on college, university, and professional committees; contribute to shared governance. Participate in accreditation support, program evaluation, and strategic initiatives. Engage in professional and community service that advances the discipline, strengthens partnerships, and promotes the mission of the college. Work collaboratively with interdisciplinary teams to enhance educational programs and research initiatives. Work location: Knoxville, Tennessee - This is not a distance-based position.  Faculty are expected to be present on campus. Required Qualifications: Earned doctorate in nursing by time of appointment. Eligibility for appointment at the rank of Associate Professor with evidence consistent with tenure-track expectations (scholarly productivity, peer-reviewed publications, and recognized contributions). Demonstrated effectiveness in teaching and student mentorship. Current, unencumbered RN license in the state where they are a permanent resident, and eligibility for RN licensure in Tennessee, as required for the position. Strong foundation in human-centered design and human factors in clinical or educational settings. Evidence of research in health and wellness Desirable Qualifications: Experience in academic nursing Evidence of team science and translational research Currently funded with the potential to transfer funding to UTKCON Experience with instructional media and technology Rank and Salary: Associate professors are expected to hold the doctorate or other terminal degree of the discipline, or to present equivalent training and experience as appropriate to the particular appointment be good teachers have achieved and maintain a recognized record in disciplinary research / scholarship / creative activity/ engaged scholarship have achieved and maintain a record of institutional, disciplinary, and/or professional service or outreach engagement have normally served as an assistant professor for at least five years have demonstrated that they work well with colleagues and students in performing their university responsibilities. Salary will be commensurate with qualifications and experience. A competitive benefits package is included. Applications should include: (1) a letter of interest addressing research and teaching interests and how the applicant’s background and experience make them an ideal candidate, (2) a comprehensive curriculum vitae, (3) the names and contact information (address, phone number, and e-mail address) for at least three professional references, and (4) a copy of your RN license.  Review of applications will begin November 6, 2026. For additional information please contact College of Nursing Human Resources at conhr@utk.edu or via mail at The University of Tennessee, Knoxville, College of Nursing, Human Resources 1200 Volunteer Blvd., Knoxville, TN 37996."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194034": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor - College of Nursing - Fall 2027",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: College of Nursing Location: Knoxville, TN The College of Nursing at the University of Tennessee, Knoxville, invites applications for a 9-month full-time , tenure track position at the rank of Assistant Professor to start August 1, 2027. We are seeking dynamic, innovative, and collaborative candidates who are committed to advancing nursing education, research, and practice in the evolving health landscape. Details on the College of Nursing Programs are available at https://nursing.utk.edu/ About the College of Nursing With a longstanding tradition of excellence in nursing education, research, and clinical practice, we are proud to be a guiding force in healthcare education. By offering cutting-edge academic programs, supporting impactful research, and forging strong community partnerships, we prepare healthcare professionals who deliver high-quality, culturally responsive care in a diverse and ever-changing world. Come join the Vol Nurse Family! Get to know CON Highlights: Generous start-up package including relocation stipend and protected scholarship time for the first 2 years Scholarship and grant writing support from the college (Office of Research & Scholarship) and the university (Office of Research, Innovation and Economic Development) Inter-professional education and research opportunities Member of the National Hartford Centers for Gerontological Nursing Excellence State-of-the-art accredited Health Innovation & Simulation Lab run in collaboration with the College of Engineering Collaborations with Pat Summitt Alzheimer’s clinic, University of Tennessee Medical Center, Dolly Parton Children’s Hospital and Oak Ridge National Laboratory. The college owns and operates Vine Clinic, a nurse-managed school-based health center with telehealth services to other underserved schools. About the Position This position is ideal for an emerging scholar and educator who will advance the College’s mission through high-quality teaching, developing a sustainable program of research/scholarship, and service to the College, University and profession.  The successful candidate will demonstrate a commitment to sustain externally funded research, student mentorship, and the ability to contribute to a collegial, outcomes-focused academic environment.  Key responsibilities include delivering high-quality, engaging instruction; mentoring and advising students; conducting influential research with strong potential for external funding; and fostering interdisciplinary collaboration across diverse academic and professional domains. Position Responsibilities: Research and Scholarship Establish and maintain a focused program of scholarly research with a clear agenda and measurable outcomes with potential for extramural funding. Disseminate research findings through peer-reviewed publications, presentations, and conferences. Pursue external funding and/or lead scholarly initiatives aligned with college priority and disciplinary needs. Collaborate with faculty, clinical partners, and interdisciplinary teams to strengthen research impact and translation to practice Teaching and Mentorship Teach undergraduate and/or graduate courses as assigned. Use evidence-based teaching strategies and valid assessment to support student learning outcomes. Advise and mentor students (academic progression, research mentorship, project/dissertation support). Contribute to curriculum development, assessment, and continuous improvement of academic programs. Service and Leadership Participate in college, university, and professional committees; contribute to shared governance. Contribute to accreditation readiness, program evaluation, and strategic initiatives as assigned. Engage in professional and community service that advances the discipline, strengthens partnerships and promotes the mission of the college. Work collaboratively with interdisciplinary teams to enhance educational programs and research initiatives. Work Location: Knoxville, TN - This is not a distance-based postion.  Faculty are expected to be present on campus. Required Qualifications: Earned doctorate in nursing by time of appointment. Eligibility for appointment at the rank of Assistant Professor with evidence consistent with tenure-track expectations (scholarly productivity, peer-reviewed publications, and recognized contributions). Demonstrated scholarly productivity. Current/unencumbered RN license in the state where they are a permanent resident, and eligibility for RN licensure in Tennessee, as required for the position. Strong foundation in human-centered design and human factors in clinical or educational settings. Evidence of research in health and wellness Desirable Qualifications: Experience in academic nursing Evidence of team science and translational research Currently funded with the potential to transfer funding to UTKCON Experience with instructional media and technology Rank and Salary: Assistant professors are expected to hold the doctorate or other terminal degree of the discipline, or to present equivalent training and experience as appropriate to the particular appointment show promise as teachers show promise of developing a program in disciplinary research / scholarship / creative activity that is gaining external recognition have a developing record of institutional, disciplinary, and/or professional service show evidence that they work well with colleagues and students in performing their university responsibilities. S alary will be commensurate with qualifications and experience. A competitive benefits package is included. For additional information please contact College of Nursing Human Resources at conhr@utk.edu or via mail at The University of Tennessee, Knoxville, College of Nursing, Human Resources 1200 Volunteer Blvd., Knoxville, TN 37996 Applications should include: (1) a letter of interest addressing research and teaching interests and how the applicant’s background and experience make them an ideal candidate, (2) a comprehensive curriculum vitae, (3) the names and contact information (address, phone number, and e-mail address) for at least three professional references, and (4) a copy of current RN license.  Review of applications will begin November 6, 2026."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195042": {
      "title": "Clinical Instructor - College of Nursing - Fall 2027",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: College of Nursing Location: Knoxville, TN The College of Nursing at the University of Tennessee, Knoxville, seeks a versatile and student-centered Clinical Instructor to contribute to educating the next generation of health care professionals.  This faculty position is a full time, nine month appointment beginning in August 1, 2027. Details on the College of Nursing Undergraduate Programs can be found at https://nursing.utk.edu/undergraduate-programs/ About the College of Nursing With a longstanding tradition of excellence in nursing education, research, and clinical practice, we are proud to be a guiding force in healthcare education. We embrace innovation while honoring the values that have shaped our success. By offering cutting-edge academic programs, supporting impactful research, and forging strong community partnerships, we prepare healthcare professionals who deliver high-quality, culturally responsive care in a diverse and ever-changing world. Come join the Vol Nurse Family! Get to know CON About the Position The ideal candidate will have a record that demonstrates excellent teaching potential with expertise in teaching topics in nursing.  Additionally, the ideal candidate should have a strong commitment to teaching and service and should thrive in a diverse and intellectually stimulating environment. Duties: Participate in the ongoing development, implementation, evaluation, and enhancement of nursing academic programs: Teach nursing courses as assigned. While the candidate may be hired to primarily teach in a specific program or program level, teaching assignments are based on UT CON needs as well as individual faculty member expertise and performance; faculty may be asked to teach across both undergraduate and graduate programs. Supervise and provide written evaluation of nursing students as assigned Collaborate with clinical agencies in arranging student clinical experiences (e.g. orientation to objectives of clinical experience, course syllabus, clinical schedule, et al) as appropriate. Ensure courses reflect program outcomes, AACN essentials, state board of nursing minimum standards, current evidence and best practice Participate in program and course revisions Participate in the evaluation and ongoing enhancement of the curricula Apply evidence from best education practices to teaching Contribute to the mission and strategic priorities of the College of Nursing and the University of Tennessee Knoxville Work Location: · Various clinical sites in the Knoxville, Tennessee area · Clinical models may vary the days of the week each semester Required Qualifications: Earned Master’s degree in Nursing (MSN) within six (6) months of appointment. 3+ years' of nursing practice experience. Current, unencumbered RN license in the state where they are a permanent resident, and eligibility for RN licensure in Tennessee, as required for the position. Evidence of successful clinical and/or classroom teaching experience Effective interpersonal skills and cultural competence Ability to work with a diverse group of students and colleagues Desirable Qualifications: Doctor of Nursing Practice (DNP) or Doctor of Philosophy in Nursing (PhD) Eligibility for RN/APRN license in Tennessee Experience with instructional media and technology To apply, please submit your application and materials through our Interfolio application system. Applications should include: (1) a letter of interest addressing teaching interests and how the applicant’s background and experience make them an ideal candidate, (2) a comprehensive curriculum vitae, (3) the names and contact information (address, phone number, and e-mail address) for at least three professional references, and (4) a copy of your current, unencumbered RN license in your state of residency. R eview of applicants will begin two weeks after posting date and will continue until the position is filled. For questions about the position, please email conhr@utk.edu For more information on the College of Nursing, visit our website: http://nursing.utk.edu/ ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195046": {
      "title": "Clinical Assistant, Associate or Full Professor - College of Nursing - Fall 2027",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: College of Nursing Location: Knoxville, TN The College of Nursing at the University of Tennessee, Knoxville, seeks an innovative and compelling Clinical Assistant, Associate or Full Professor to contribute to educating the next generation of health care professionals. This faculty position is a full time, nine month appointment beginning in August 1, 2027. Details on the College of Nursing Programs are available at https://nursing.utk.edu/ About the College of Nursing With a longstanding tradition of excellence in nursing education, research, and clinical practice, we are proud to be a guiding force in healthcare education. We embrace innovation while honoring the values that have shaped our success. By offering cutting-edge academic programs, supporting impactful research, and forging strong community partnerships, we prepare healthcare professionals who deliver high-quality, culturally responsive care in a diverse and ever-changing world. Come join the Vol Nurse Family! Get to know CON About the Position The ideal candidate will have a record that demonstrates excellent teaching potential with expertise in teaching topics in nursing.  Additionally, the ideal candidate should have a strong commitment to teaching and service and should thrive in a diverse and intellectually stimulating environment. Duties: Participate in the ongoing development, implementation, evaluation, and enhancement of nursing academic programs: Participate in program and course revisions. Revise assigned courses to reflect program outcomes, AACN essentials, state board of nursing minimum standards, current evidence, and best practice. Collaborate with emphasis area coordinators, clinical concentration coordinators, program directors, and the Associate and Assistant Deans to identify the types of clinical experiences and sites needed for optimal clinical education of students. Teach graduate and undergraduate nursing courses as assigned. While the candidate may be hired to primarily teach in a specific program or program level, teaching assignments are based on UT CON needs as well as individual faculty member expertise and performance; faculty may be asked to teach across both undergraduate and graduate programs. Supervise nursing students as assigned. Collaborate with clinical agencies in arranging student clinical experiences (e.g. orientation to objectives of clinical experience, course syllabus, clinical schedule, et al) as appropriate. Participate in the development of academic policies affecting the admission, progression, graduation and dismissal of students. Participate in the evaluation and ongoing enhancement of the curricula. Apply evidence from best education practices to curriculum development and to teaching. Serve the College of Nursing, University, community and profession: Participate in College of Nursing faculty, standing committee, task force and other meetings. Participate in Faculty Senate Committees and ad hoc committees, as elected. Participate in community and professional activities as appropriate. Participate in scholarly projects as appropriate. Contribute to the mission and strategic priorities of the College of Nursing and the University of Tennessee Knoxville Collaborate effectively with other faculty and staff members both within the College of Nursing and throughout the University and community to develop inter-professional academic, clinical, and research opportunities. Work Location: Faculty work arrangements may be on-site or full remote, depending on the program to which they are assigned. This position is open to those who qualify and can be appointed as one of the following ranks in the College of Nursing. Rank appointments are dependent on education and qualifications relative to the university and college standards and will be determined by academic leadership in accordance with faculty handbook, policies, and procedures. Clinical Assistant Professor: This rank is for those who have completed a doctoral degree or terminal degree appropriate to the field or who are licensed or certified to practice the profession where appropriate. Individuals holding this rank have shown promise for excellence in areas of responsibility recorded in the hiring unit’s annual documentation of the NTT faculty workload distribution and responsibilities. Clinical Associate Professor: This rank is for those who have completed a doctoral degree or a terminal degree appropriate to the field and who are licensed or certified to practice the profession where appropriate. Individuals holding this rank have demonstrated excellence in areas of responsibility recorded in the hiring unit’s annual documentation of the NTT faculty’s workload distribution and responsibilities. Clinical Professor: This rank is for those who have completed a doctoral degree or a terminal degree appropriate to the field and who are licensed or certified to practice the profession where appropriate. Individuals holding this rank have demonstrated a consistent record of excellence in areas of responsibility recorded in the hiring unit’s annual documentation of the NTT faculty workload distribution and responsibilities. Required Qualifications: Earned doctorate (PhD or DNP) at time of appointment. Current, unencumbered RN license in the state where they are a permanent resident, and eligibility for RN licensure in Tennessee, as required for the position. Minimum of 3 years of nursing practice experience. Effective interpersonal skills and cultural competence. Ability to work with a diverse group of students and colleagues. Desirable Qualifications: Evidence of successful classroom and/or clinical teaching experience Eligibility for APRN licensure in Tennessee If teaching NP students, national certification as a nurse practitioner and prior to employment recognition by the Tennessee Board of Nursing as an advanced practice registered nurse Experience with instructional media and technology To apply, please submit your application and materials through our Interfolio application system.  Applications should include: (1) a letter of interest addressing teaching interests and how the applicant’s background and experience make them an ideal candidate, (2) a comprehensive curriculum vitae, (3) the names and contact information (address, phone number, and e-mail address) for at least three professional references, and (4) a copy of your current, unencumbered RN license in your state of permanent residency. The review of applicants will begin two weeks after the posting date and will continue throughout the academic year. Rank and salary are based on qualifications and experience. For questions about the position, please email conhr@utk.edu.  For more information on the College of Nursing, visit our website: http://nursing.utk.edu/"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195123": {
      "title": "Clinical Assistant, Associate or Full Professor – TBSN Program Director – College of Nursing – Spring 2027",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: College of Nursing Location: Knoxville, TN The College of Nursing at the University of Tennessee, Knoxville, seeks an engaging nurse educator and academic leader for a full-time, 12-month faculty appointment at the Clinical Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor rank. This faculty role also serves as the Director for the TBSN (Traditional Bachelor of Science in Nursing) program . About the College of Nursing With a longstanding tradition of excellence in nursing education, research, and clinical practice, we are proud to be a guiding force in healthcare education. We embrace innovation while honoring the values that have shaped our success. By offering cutting-edge academic programs, supporting impactful research, and forging strong community partnerships, we prepare healthcare professionals who deliver high-quality, culturally responsive care in a diverse and ever-changing world. Our top-ranked, direct admit, four-year Bachelor of Science in Nursing program offers a strong foundation in nursing science, clinical practice, and compassionate care.  The Bachelor of Science in nursing program combines the unique resources of the UT campus with health care agencies in a manner that enables both faculty and students to participate fully in all facets of the health care delivery system. Facilities at the College of Nursing include high fidelity labs, clinical practice labs, and technology enhanced classrooms. Clinical instruction is offered for students in major hospitals, community clinics, health departments, and schools in the region. About the Position The TBSN Program Director reports to the Assistant Dean of Undergraduate Programs and serves as the primary leader responsible for the coordination and day-to-day oversight of the TBSN program. This role represents the TBSN program on the college leadership team and provides direction for key program functions to ensure a high-quality, consistent student and faculty experience.  This position will maintain personal academic faculty obligations in teaching, service, and research in accordance with area of expertise. Duties: Responsible for coordinating the development, implementation, and evaluation of the courses required for completion of the BSN degree. Monitor and ensure consistent implementation of all CON and UTK policies specific to the TBSN program. Participate in program evaluation work groups, task forces, and committees as appropriate/assigned. Collaborate with clinical faculty related to specialty clinical rotations. Collaborate with the Director of Student Services in recruiting and admission activities. Collaborate with the Academic Affairs Manager in planning junior and senior orientation activities. Maintain on-line communication strategies with TBSN faculty and students. Collaborate and coordinate program development with university departments [Online Education, Office of Information Technology, Registrar/MyUTK, etc.]. Communicate with the Assistant Dean of Undergraduate Programs on relevant course related matters related to individual student behavior and learning outcomes as appropriate. Approve final student schedules. Determine needed faculty resources for clinicals and simulation/learning labs. Responsible for holding Course Coordinator Meetings each semeste. Approve out of sequence students’ (OOS) Academic Plans. Assist with annual update of the Undergraduate Student Handbook. Participate in the teaching, research, and service missions of the CON. Participate in the ongoing development, implementation, evaluation, and enhancement of nursing academic programs: Participate in program and course revisions. Revise assigned courses to reflect program outcomes, AACN essentials, state board of nursing minimum standards, current evidence, and best practice. Collaborate with emphasis area coordinators, clinical concentration coordinators, program directors, and the Associate and Assistant Deans to identify the types of clinical experiences and sites needed for optimal clinical education of students. Teach graduate and undergraduate nursing courses as assigned. While the candidate may be hired to primarily teach in a specific program or program level, teaching assignments are based on UT CON needs as well as individual faculty member expertise and performance; faculty may be asked to teach across both undergraduate and graduate programs. Supervise nursing students as assigned. Collaborate with clinical agencies in arranging student clinical experiences (e.g. orientation to objectives of clinical experience, course syllabus, clinical schedule, et al) as appropriate. Participate in the development of academic policies affecting the admission, progression, graduation and dismissal of students. Participate in the evaluation and ongoing enhancement of the curricula. Apply evidence from best education practices to curriculum development and to teaching. Serve the College of Nursing, University, community and profession: Participate in College of Nursing faculty, standing committee, task force and other meetings. Participate in Faculty Senate Committees and ad hoc committees, as elected. Participate in community and professional activities as appropriate. Participate in scholarly projects as appropriate. Contribute to the mission and strategic priorities of the College of Nursing and the University of Tennessee Knoxville Collaborate effectively with other faculty and staff members both within the College of Nursing and throughout the University and community to develop inter-professional academic, clinical, and research opportunities. Work location: onsite Required Qualifications: Earned doctorate (PhD or DNP) at time of appointment. Current, unencumbered RN license in your state of permanent residency, and eligibility for RN licensure in Tennessee, as required for the position. Minimum of 3 years of nursing practice experience. Effective interpersonal skills and cultural competence. Ability to work with a diverse group of students and colleagues. Desirable Qualifications: Evidence of successful classroom and/or clinical teaching experience. Demonstrated ability to provide effective leadership, foster collaboration, and manage complex academic program operations. If teaching NP students, national certification as a nurse practitioner and prior to employment recognition by the Tennessee Board of Nursing as an advanced practice registered nurse. Experience with instructional media and technology. This position is open to those who qualify and can be appointed as one of the following ranks in the College of Nursing. Rank appointments are dependent on education and qualifications relative to the university and college standards and will be determined by academic leadership in accordance with faculty handbook, policies, and procedures. Clinical Assistant Professor: This rank is for those who have completed a doctoral degree or terminal degree appropriate to the field or who are licensed or certified to practice the profession where appropriate . Individuals holding this rank have shown promise for excellence in areas of responsibility recorded in the hiring unit’s annual documentation of the NTT faculty workload distribution and responsibilities. Clinical Associate Professor: This rank is for those who have completed a doctoral degree or a terminal degree appropriate to the field and who are licensed or certified to practice the profession where appropriate . Individuals holding this rank have demonstrated excellence in areas of responsibility recorded in the hiring unit’s annual documentation of the NTT faculty’s workload distribution and responsibilities. Clinical Professor: This rank is for those who have completed a doctoral degree or a terminal degree appropriate to the field and who are licensed or certified to practice the profession where appropriate . Individuals holding this rank have demonstrated a consistent record of excellence in areas of responsibility recorded in the hiring unit’s annual documentation of the NTT faculty workload distribution and responsibilities. To apply, please submit your application and materials through our Interfolio application system.  Applications should include: (1) a letter of interest addressing teaching interests and how the applicant’s background and experience make them an ideal candidate, (2) a comprehensive curriculum vitae, (3) the names and contact information (address, phone number, and e-mail address) for at least three professional references, and (4) a copy of your c urrent, unencumbered RN license in your state of permanent residency . The review of applicants will begin two weeks after the posting date and will continue throughout the academic year until the position is filled. Rank and salary are based on qualifications and experience. For questions about the position, please email conhr@utk.edu.  For more information on the College of Nursing, visit our website: http://nursing.utk.edu/"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195244": {
      "title": "Clinical Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor - DNP PMHNP Concentration Coordinator - College of Nursing - Spring 2027",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: College of Nursing Location: Knoxville The College of Nursing at the University of Tennessee, Knoxville, seeks a dynamic and engaging Clinical Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor to contribute to educating the next generation of Psychiatric Mental Health Nurse Practitioners . The ideal candidate will demonstrate a strong potential for teaching excellence , with expertise in psychiatric-mental health nursing . Additionally, the ideal candidate should have a strong commitment to education and service and be able to thrive in a diverse and intellectually stimulating environment. This faculty position is a full-time, nine-month appointment beginning January 1, 2027. PRIMARY FUNCTION : Reporting to the DNP Director and Assistant Dean of Graduate Programs, this position is r esponsible for coordinating student admissions, development, implementation, and evaluation of the courses required for the PMHNP degree and certification , as well as p receptor and site acquisition, student placement, and concentration evaluation . This position will maintain personal academic faculty obligations in teaching, service, and research in accordance with area of expertise. PRIMARY RESPONSIBILITIES: Serve as the concentration advisor for DNP students from admission until graduation in the clinical specialty Respond to specific inquiries about the DNP -PMHNP concentration Implement recruitment activities in conjunction with the Office of Student Services, DNP Director , and Assistant Dean of Graduate Programs Ensure published materials [website, handbooks, etc ] related to concentration are up-to-date and accurate Serve as member of DNP Admission Committee by completing primary and secondary application reviews and interviews as assigned Confer with DNP Director and Assistant Dean of Graduate Programs on individual programs of study including prerequisite and required courses to be taken, course sequenc ing , and changes to program of study Ensure individual student files are complete including documentation of advising sessions, clinical evaluations, and record of clinical experiences Attend DNP Student Orientation and meet with new students during D octoral I ntensives T each in courses as assigned Ensure student knowledge of and compliance with CON and Graduate School deadlines and form completion Attend and participate in appropriate commencement exercises Assist C ollege of Nursing Academic Affairs in preparing documents for state licensure and certification as necessary Provide leadership in the development/revision of specialty-specific curriculum Participate in program curriculum mapping of Essentials of DNP Education as well as specialty-specific competencies Propose curriculum changes as needed to general graduate faculty and attend Graduate Curriculum Committee meetings as necessary Ensure all specialty courses are suitable for the distance education environment and updated annually and prepare materials for class schedules, methods of student evaluation, quizzes, examinations, learning materials, and other concentration/specialty requirements Create and manage appropriate Canvas course sites Delineate specific responsibilities for each faculty member assigned to specialty courses Coordinate appropriate TNVoice evaluation for assigned concentration faculty and confer with the Assistant Dean or Graduate Programs and the Associate Dean of Academic Affairs about these Identify and schedule appropriate preceptors and clinical facilities to be used for clinical experiences for student Identify , solicit , and establish effective relationship s with clinical preceptors/sites C oordinate c linical sites visits with individual PMHNP faculty and students to establish student evaluation sessions with preceptors Collect student evaluation s of clinical site s and preceptors Ensure all student clinical hours logs are complete and verified by preceptors at least monthly Communicate with the DNP Director and Assistant Dean of Graduate Programs on all course - related matters related to individual student behavior and program outcomes ( e . g., deficient, inappropriate, or dangerous student behavior; inadequacies of classroom or clinical facilities; student or faculty difficulties which cannot be resolved at the course level) Participate in program evaluation work groups, task forces, and committees as assigned Required Qualifications: Earned doctorate (PhD or DNP) at time of appointment. Professional experience commensurate with rank. Evidence of teaching experience. Current, unencumbered RN license in the state where they are a permanent resident, and eligibility for RN licensure in Tennessee, as required for the position. National certification as a PMHNP and Board of Nursing as an advanced practice Registered Nurse prior to employment recognition by the Tennessee. 3 years of nursing practice experience. Effective interpersonal skills and cultural competence. Ability to work with a diverse group of students and colleagues. This position is open to those who qualify and can be appointed as one of the following ranks in the College of Nursing. Rank appointments are dependent on education and qualifications relative to the university and college standards and will be determined by academic leadership in accordance with faculty handbook, policies, and procedures. Clinical Assistant Professor: This rank is for those who have completed a doctoral degree or terminal degree appropriate to the field or who are licensed or certified to practice the profession where appropriate . Individuals holding this rank have shown promise for excellence in areas of responsibility recorded in the hiring unit’s annual documentation of the NTT faculty workload distribution and responsibilities. Clinical Associate Professor: This rank is for those who have completed a doctoral degree or a terminal degree appropriate to the field and who are licensed or certified to practice the profession where appropriate . Individuals holding this rank have demonstrated excellence in areas of responsibility recorded in the hiring unit’s annual documentation of the NTT faculty’s workload distribution and responsibilities. Clinical Professor: This rank is for those who have completed a doctoral degree or a terminal degree appropriate to the field and who are licensed or certified to practice the profession where appropriate . Individuals holding this rank have demonstrated a consistent record of excellence in areas of responsibility recorded in the hiring unit’s annual documentation of the NTT faculty workload distribution and responsibilities. To apply, please submit your application and materials through our Interfolio application system. Applications should include: (1) a letter of interest addressing teaching interests and how the applicant’s background and experience make them an ideal candidate, (2) a comprehensive curriculum vitae, (3) the names and contact information (address, phone number, and e-mail address) for at least three professional references, and (4) a copy of your RN license. Screening of applicant credentials will begin two weeks after posting date and continue until the position is filled. Rank and salary based on qualifications and experience. For more information, visit our website: ( http://nursing.utk.edu/ ). For questions regarding this position, please reach out to conhr@utk.edu."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192269": {
      "title": "Betsey R. Bush Endowed Professor in Behavioral Health-Social Work-Fall 2027 (Associate or Full Professor)",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: Social Work Location: Nashville, TN The University of Tennessee, Knoxville (UTK) College of Social Work is seeking a highly regarded scholar to fill the position of the Betsey R. Bush Endowed Professor in Behavioral Health . This endowed professorship offers an outstanding opportunity for an experienced behavioral health researcher to pursue their program of research, with significant potential for collaboration with creative colleagues and doctoral students. \"The ideal candidate will bring an interprofessional lens to community-engaged research and education; a strong record of scholarly activity; experience as a mentor to graduate students, post-docs, and pre-tenure faculty; and have the ability to promote an atomosphere where all members of the univerisity community feel welcome and can thrive.\" They also will meet the requirements for the appropriate rank for the University of Tennessee.  To this end, the candidate should have an established record of substantial extramural funding (for professor level) or intramural and some extramural funding and indication of future potential for a sustained record of extramural funding (for associate level). The successful candidate will help the CSW move forward our vision and mission . Candidates for this position must have earned their Ph.D. in social work or related field. Successful candidates would need to meet the qualifications to be hired as Associate or Professor.  This 9-month position is located on the Nashville campus. The start date will be August 1, 2027. The University and the College of Social Work The University of Tennessee Knoxville is a land-grant, flagship research institution, that imagines “ a world enriched by our ideas, improved through action, and inspired by the Volunteer Spirit of service and leadership .” ( https://chancellor.utk.edu/vision/ ). The university is in the midst of unprecedented growth, with current enrollment of approximately 37,000 students. The University is one of 38 land-grant universities with Carnegie Foundation classification as both a Research 1: Very High Spending and Doctorate Production, and the elective Classification for Community Engagement. Forbes Magazine recently ranked UTK as one of the best places to work in the US, including ranking it ninth among public higher education institutions in the United States. UTK offers a competitive benefits package, and 9-month tenure-track faculty are eligible for additional summer salary The University of Tennessee Knoxville College of Social Work (UTCSW) has almost 1,000 students enrolled in undergraduate, MSSW, DSW, and PhD programs in Knoxville, Nashville, and online. Ranked 24 th nationally by the US News and World Report (2024), the college prepares highly skilled and culturally sensitive social workers to address areas of need across the state, the country, and the globe. UTCSW is home to four research centers and several faculty research labs. The college’s research-active faculty conduct interdisciplinary research in in partnership with faculty from other UTK colleges/departments (e.g., the Tickle College of Engineering, the College of Nursing, and Departments of Public Health, Child and Family Studies, Kinesiology, Nutrition, and Psychology, among others), the UT Health Science Center in Memphis, and UT Extension. UTCSW also has community-based partnerships with numerous state and community agencies. Campus Location Nashville is an action-packed and vibrant metropolis with so much more to offer than its famous Country Music Hall of Fame and Grand Ole Opry. As the state capital, Nashville is home to state government, multiple statewide child-serving nonprofit agencies, and 16 colleges and universities, including Vanderbilt University and several HBCUs (Tennessee State University, Fisk, Meharry Medical College). with whom our college regularly partners. It is currently listed as #50 on the U.S. News and World report’s best Places to Live (2024) and offers events such as the CMA Festival, the Nashville Film Festival, Tomato Art Fest, and the Nashville Pride Festival. Outside of Nashville’s busy downtown Broadway scene, there are lots of unique neighborhoods filled with fantastic ethnic food options and cultural events, with unlimited opportunities to explore gorgeous parks and greenways, both inside and outside the city limits. Nashville is a short distance away from national parks that are great for camping and hiking. Required Qualifications: Ph.D. in social work or other related field An established, active program of research related to behavioral health care. Evidence of a commitment to social work values in alignment with CSWE accreditation standards in their practice and research Evidence of high-quality teaching at the graduate level, across modalities Evidence of commitment to mentoring graduate students, pre-tenure faculty, and/or post-doctoral fellows who are developing programs of externally-funded research Evidence of commitment to creating positive classroom and research settings where all people can thrive Candidates for Associate Professor should have preliminary evidence of external funding, demonstrated scholarly impact on practice or policy, and clear potential for sustained external funding. Candidates for Professor should have evidence of successful, sustained external funding, demonstrated scholarly impact on practice or policy and experience leading research teams. Preferred Qualifications: Master’s degree in social work Commitment to interdisciplinary research and collaboration Experience leading team-based research groups Interested individuals should provide: (1) a cover letter addressing research, teaching, and practice experience as well as experience relevant to each required and preferred qualification, (2) curriculum vitae, (3) three sample publications, and (4) contact information for three professional references. In your letter, pay specific attention to your experiences that are not easily found on your CV. Priority consideration will be given to applications submitted by January 15, 2027 though review may continue until an appointment is made. Materials should be submitted electronically https://apply.interfolio.com/192269 . Inquiries may be addressed to the search committee chair, Dr. Bridget Freisthler at bfreisth@utk.edu ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192283": {
      "title": "Urban Child Institute Endowed Professorship in Child Health and Well-Being-Social Work-Fall 2027 (Associate or Full Professor)",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: Social Work Location: Nashville, TN The University of Tennessee, Knoxville College of Social Work (UTCSW) is seeking a highly regarded scholar to fill the position of Urban Child Institute Endowed Professorship in Child Health and Well-Being on our Nashville campus. The successful candidate will also be the founding director of a new center, focusing on research, education, and engagement around topics related to child and family well-being. This position provides a unique opportunity for a visionary leader to help us shape the mission and vision of the center and provide leadership and oversight to the center activities. This endowed professorship offers an outstanding opportunity for an experienced researcher to pursue their program of research in child health and well-being. Topic areas around child health and wellbeing are open, though scholars with expertise in early childhood are especially welcome. Research programs founded within the social determinants of health framework along with intervention models to promote health and well-being with children and families will find significant potential for collaboration with creative colleagues and doctoral students.  The ideal candidate will bring an interprofessional lens to community-engaged research and education; a strong record of scholarly activity; experience as a mentor to graduate students, and/or post-docs and pre-tenure faculty; and have the ability to promote an atmosphere where all members of the university community feel welcome and can thrive. They also will meet the requirements for the appropriate rank for the University of Tennessee. To this end, the candidate should have an established record of substantial extramural funding (for professor level) or intramural and some extramural funding and indication of future potential for a sustained record of extramural funding (for associate level). As an endowed professor, the candidate must commit to a collaborative partnership with the Urban Child Institute and to nurturing existing and building new collaborations with community and research agencies in Shelby County, TN and the surrounding area. The successful candidate will help the UTCSW move forward our mission . Candidates for this position must have earned their Ph.D. in social work or related field. Successful candidates would need to meet the qualifications to be hired as Associate or Professor. This 9-month position will be located on the Nashville campus. Start date will be August 1, 2027. The University and the College of Social Work The University of Tennessee Knoxville is a land-grant, flagship research institution, that imagines “ a world enriched by our ideas, improved through action, and inspired by the Volunteer Spirit of service and leadership .” ( https://chancellor.utk.edu/vision/ ). The university is in the midst of unprecedented growth, with current enrollment of approximately 37,000 students. The University is one of 38 land-grant universities with Carnegie Foundation classification as both a Research 1: Very High Spending and Doctorate Production, and the elective Classification for Community Engagement. Forbes Magazine recently ranked UTK as one of the best places to work in the US, including ranking it ninth among public higher education institutions in the United States. UTK offers a competitive benefits package, and 9-month tenure-track faculty are eligible for additional summer salary. The University of Tennessee Knoxville College of Social Work (UTCSW) has almost 1,000 students enrolled in undergraduate, MSSW, DSW, and PhD programs in Knoxville, Nashville, and online. Ranked 24 th nationally by the US News and World Report (2024), the college prepares highly skilled and culturally sensitive social workers to address areas of need across the state, the country, and the globe. UTCSW is home to four research centers and several individual faculty research labs. The college’s research-active faculty conduct interdisciplinary research in in partnership with faculty from other UTK colleges/departments (e.g., the Tickle College of Engineering, the College of Nursing, and Departments of Public Health, Child and Family Studies, Kinesiology, Nutrition, and Psychology, among others), the UT Health Science Center in Memphis, and UT Extension. UTCSW also has community-based partnerships with numerous state and community agencies. Campus Location Nashville is an action-packed and vibrant metropolis with so much more to offer than its famous Country Music Hall of Fame and Grand Ole Opry. As the state capital, Nashville is home to state government, multiple statewide child-serving nonprofit agencies, and 16 colleges and universities, including Vanderbilt University and several HBCUs with whom our college regularly partners. It is currently listed as #50 on the U.S. News and World report’s best Places to Live (2024) and offers events such as the CMA Festival, the Nashville Film Festival, Live on the Green, Tomato Art Fest, and the Nashville Pride Festival. Outside of Nashville’s busy downtown Broadway scene, there are lots of unique neighborhoods filled with fantastic ethnic food options and cultural events, with unlimited opportunities to explore gorgeous parks and greenways, both inside and outside the city limits. Nashville is a short distance away from national parks that are great for camping and hiking. Required Qualifications: Ph.D. in social work or related field An established, active program of research in child health and well-being, with a priority for early childhood and the social determinants of health, along with intervention models to promote health and well-being with children and families. Evidence of a commitment to social work values in alignment with CSWE accreditation standards in their practice and research Evidence of high-quality teaching at the graduate level, and across modalities Evidence of commitment to mentoring graduate students, pre-tenure faculty, and/or post-doctoral fellows who are developing programs of externally-funded research Evidence of commitment to creating a welcoming, an inclusive, supportive positive climate in classroom and research settings, in alignment with where all participants students can succeed and thrive Experience leading large research projects and/or research teams with evidence of ability to assume responsibilities of a Director Candidates for Associate Professor should have evidence of external funding and clear potential for sustained external funding. Candidates for Professor should have evidence of successful, sustained external funding and experience leading research teams. Preferred Qualifications: Master’s degree in social work Experience and expertise in interdisciplinary research and collaboration Experience as Director or Assistant/Associate Director of an academic research center I nterested individuals should provide: (1) a cover letter addressing research, teaching, and practice experience, initial vision for a Center for Child and Family Health and Well-being, and experience relevant to each required and preferred qualification, (2) curriculum vitae, (3) three sample publications, and (4) contact information for three professional references. In your letter, pay specific attention to your experiences that are not easily found on your CV. Priority consideration will be given to applications submitted by January 15, 2027 though review may continue until an appointment is made. Materials should be submitted electronically https://apply.interfolio.com/192283 . Inquiries may be addressed to the search committee chair, Dr. Bridget Freisthler at bfreisth@utk.edu ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192481": {
      "title": "Assistant or Associate Professor-Social Work-Fall 2027 (2 Tenure Track positions)",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: Social Work Location: Knoxville, TN The University of Tennessee College of Social Work (UTCSW) invites applications for an assistant or associate professor in social work in any areas of social work practice and research. We seek candidates with an independent trajectory of research who can also build on and/or collaborate with current faculty’s areas of research strength and contribute to our broadening interdisciplinary and community-based research partnerships.  Successful candidates will demonstrate a strong program of scholarship and potential for extramural funding at the Assistant Professor level, or an established record of scholarship and externally funded research appropriate for appointment at the Associate Professor level. Further, the successful candidate will contribute to the highest-quality graduate and undergraduate social work education, have the ability to promote an atmosphere where all members of the university community feel welcome and can thrive. We are open to all research areas but have special interest in research related to health and well-being, use of artificial intelligence and computing for the social good, and community-engaged research that focuses on local community conditions. All applicants who apply by the priority deadline will be considered. Candidates for this position must have earned their Ph.D. in social work or a related health, social, or behavioral science field, including public health and public policy. Current doctoral students who are ABD and will finish their dissertation by July 31, 2027 are also invited to apply. The positions will be located on the Knoxville campus. The start date will be August 1, 2027. The University and the College of Social Work The University of Tennessee Knoxville is a land-grant, flagship research institution, that imagines “ a world enriched by our ideas, improved through action, and inspired by the Volunteer Spirit of service and leadership .” ( https://chancellor.utk.edu/vision/ ). The university is currently in the midst of unprecedented growth in enrollment, with approximately 40,000 enrolled students. The University is a Carnegie Foundation Research (R1) University with the designation of highest research activity and has achieved a Carnegie Classification for Community Engagement. UTK offers a competitive benefits package, and 9-month tenure-track faculty are eligible for additional summer salary. The University of Tennessee Knoxville College of Social Work has almost 1,000 students enrolled in undergraduate, MSW, DSW, and PhD programs in Knoxville, Nashville, and online. The College is preparing highly skilled and culturally sensitive social workers to address areas of need across the state, the country, and the globe. The College is home to four research centers and several faculty research labs. College faculty conduct interdisciplinary research addressing pressing social, behavioral, and health challenges across the lifespan and at individual, family, community, and systems levels. The UTCSW is committed to the success of our tenure-track faculty. We offer a strong mentoring program, manageable teaching load, competitive start-up funds, pre-tenure teaching releases, and additional opportunities for internal research funding. The university also offers structural supports for teaching and research innovation. Campus Location The City of Knoxville is a hidden gem, located in the foothills of the Great Smoky Mountains. Knoxville has a beautiful and walkable downtown, a diverse music scene, active neighborhoods, unique restaurants, outstanding athletic opportunities, and a robust offering of outdoor and cultural activities. Knoxville enjoys the charm of a small city with activities that range from green energy initiatives and community greenways to thriving festival culture, music, culinary, and outdoor recreation scenes. Knoxville’s community brings events such as the Asian Festival, Knoxville Opera’s Rossini Festival, Kuumba Festival, Hola Festival, Dogwood Arts Festival to name a few. The city is also home to the Knoxville Museum of Art, the Beck Cultural Center, Women’s Basketball Hall of Fame, the Frank H. McClung Museum of Natural History, and the Museum of Appalachia is a short drive from Knoxville. Citing affordability and quality of life, U.S. News and World Report ranks Knoxville among the best places to live. Required Qualifications · A Ph.D. in Social Work or a related field (ABD’s considered, if the dissertation is expected to be completed by July 31, 2026) · Assistant Professor Evidence of a research agenda with potential for external funding A record of high-quality scholarship evidenced in peer-reviewed publications and/or strong potential for policy and public impact Evidence of effective teaching at a university level Evidence of commitment to creating an inclusive, supportive climate in classroom and research settings, in alignment with CSWE accreditation standards Associate Professor All of the above, and A demonstrated record of pursuing/securing external funding appropriate to the candidate’s area of scholarship A strong, well-established, and continuous record of scholarship and peer-reviewed publications and/or demonstration of policy and public impact Preferred Qualifications Master’s degree in social work Two years post MSW practice experience Specific research interests that overlap with one or more of our current faculty Commitment to interdisciplinary research and collaboration Evidence of high-quality teaching experience such as course development, innovations in teaching, or end of course evaluations Experience teaching courses across programs (e.g., undergrad, masters) and modalities (e.g., online, in person) Experience working in team-based research groups Interested individuals should provide: (1) a cover letter addressing research, teaching, and practice experience as well as experience relevant to each required and preferred qualification, (2) curriculum vitae, (3) a writing sample (preferably a published article), and (4) contact information for three professional references. In your letter, pay specific attention to your experiences that are not easily found on your CV. Priority consideration will be given to applications submitted by September 30, 2026, though review may continue until an appointment is made. Materials should be submitted electronically to https://apply.interfolio.com/192481. Inquiries may be addressed to the search committee chair, Dr. Bridget Freisthler at bfreisth@utk.edu."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/182355": {
      "title": "Veterinary Diagnostic Pharmacologist Faculty",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: UT Institute of Agriculture: College of Veterinary Medicine: Biomedical & Diagnostic Sciences Location: Knoxville, TN, United States Veterinary Diagnostic Pharmacologist Faculty Position Announcement Assistant/Associate Professor Department of Biomedical and Diagnostic Sciences, College of Veterinary Medicine University of Tennessee The University of Tennessee invites applications from all qualified individuals for a full-time faculty appointment as Veterinary Pharmacology Director in the Department of Biomedical and Diagnostic Sciences at the College of Veterinary Medicine. The position offers the successful candidate a unique opportunity to blend diagnostic service, discovery, and teaching in the area of veterinary pharmacology. The Diagnostic Pharmacology Service (DPS) ( https://vetmed.tennessee.edu/vmc/dls/pharmacology/ ) is involved in numerous pharmacokinetic studies in various species. Applicants must have a strong interest in clinical and collaborative research with a special interest in analytical analysis and drug pharmacokinetics. The service is recognized nationally and internationally for both its quality and expertise in analytical methodology for clinical research. The laboratory develops and validates assays intended to support both intramural and extramural research, thus encouraging interaction between the UT College of Veterinary Medicine, the pharmaceutical industry, other universities, zoos and aquariums. The position advises residents, clinical and research faculty in the development of pharmacokinetic projects in a variety of species, interprets high performance liquid chromatography results and provides pharmacokinetic interpretations and consultations. The successful candidate will direct a lab that has one lab supervisor, one laboratory technician, and typically, 3 undergraduate students. The faculty member will: have oversight of all laboratory operations ensuring day-to-day requirements are met in an accurate and timely fashion; ensure that the facility and equipment are maintained; have an understanding of laboratory quality control, and laboratory safety; develop new techniques to meet evolving needs; communicate policies, prices, and laboratory changes to our clients; budget oversight for all laboratory services including revenues and fee structure, expenses, and approval of new expenditures; supervision, management, and annual evaluation of laboratory staff; and must possess excellent interpersonal and communication skills and a demonstrated ability to work with others in a collegial team atmosphere. The remaining effort allocation will include teaching activities and scientific advancement in proportions that are negotiable based on candidate interest. Contributions to the teaching mission of the College may be through the DVM curriculum, graduate program, and/or continuing education symposia. Engagement in scientific advancement will be commensurate with the research effort allocation, with a higher research effort expected for a tenure track position. The position will be offered at the rank of assistant or associate professor, in either a tenure or clinical (non-tenure) academic track, dependent on the individual’s qualifications and goals, and will be available as early as August 1, 2026. Tenure track Assistant Professors should demonstrate promise as teachers, and in the development of a program in disciplinary research/scholarship commensurate with appointment, have a developing record of institutional and/or professional service, and display evidence that they work well with colleagues and students. Tenure-track Associate Professors will have achieved and maintained a recognized record in disciplinary research/scholarship, have achieved and maintained a record of institutional and professional service, and demonstrate evidence that they work well with colleagues and students.  In most cases they will have served as an assistant professor for at least five years. Clinical track faculty primarily provide diagnostic expertise as well as participating in teaching, scholarly activities, and service to the university and professional organizations commensurate with their appointment. To be considered for a clinical associate professor appointment at hire, the candidate’s application must demonstrate that they are a leading teacher and/or provider of public service in accordance with their primary assigned responsibilities. The College of Veterinary Medicine at the University of Tennessee is seeking candidates who have the ability to contribute in meaningful ways to the collegial, inclusive, and accessible environment of the University.  Qualified applicants must have: (1) a DVM (or equivalent degree) from an institution accredited by the American Veterinary Medical Association or a relevant PhD; (2) post-graduate experience and/or a strong documented interest in analytical methodology and clinical veterinary pharmacology; (3) excellent verbal and written communication skills and the ability to work with others in a collegial, team atmosphere. Preferred qualifications include board certification by the American College of Veterinary Clinical Pharmacology or the European College of Veterinary Pharmacology and Toxicology. Salary will be commensurate with qualifications and experience. For consideration, please apply at https://apply.interfolio.com/182355 and include a letter of intent, names and contact information for at least three professional references, and current curriculum vitae. Informal inquiries are welcome and may be directed to: Ms. Elizabeth Allen Department of Biomedical and Diagnostic Sciences College of Veterinary Medicine University of Tennessee 2407 River Drive Knoxville, TN 37996 Email address: eallen6@utk.edu"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/187126": {
      "title": "Assistant or Associate Professor of Anesthesiology",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: UT Institute of Agriculture: College of Veterinary Medicine: Large Animal Clinical Sciences Location: College of Veterinary Medicine The Department of Large Animal Clinical Sciences, College of Veterinary Medicine at The University of Tennessee invites applications for a full-time, tenure-track faculty position in Veterinary Anesthesia at the Assistant or Associate Professor rank. Responsibilities of the faculty include small and large animals’ clinical service; clinical and didactic teaching of anesthesia and analgesia to professional students, interns and residents; development of a basic science or clinical research program commensurate with effort allocation; and participation in continuing education at the local and national level. The anesthesia service provides anesthetic care for patients primarily in the small animal and large animal hospitals with occasional exotic animals. The University of Tennessee Veterinary Medical Center (VMC) is equipped with all the resources and expertise of specialty services needed to provide first class patient care, teaching, and clinical and basic science research. The VMC has a comprehensive ICU and four faculty members board certified in Veterinary Emergency Medicine and Critical Care; a minimally invasive procedures unit and faculty members with expertise in endoscopy, laparoscopy, and stenting; a state-of-the-art physical therapy facility including hyperbaric oxygen therapy; and onsite stem cell development. Clinical services are supported by excellent clinical pathology and diagnostic pathology departments.  A full-time Veterinary Social Work program offers counseling services for clients, faculty, staff, and students. Other support services include behavior and nutrition. Requirements for the applicant include a DVM or equivalent degree, completion of a formal residency in veterinary anesthesiology, and certification or eligibility for certification by the American or European College of Veterinary Anesthesia and Analgesia. Applications will be accepted until the position is filled. Qualified individuals are invited to submit an application, including a letter of intent with a statement of professional goals, curriculum vitae, and the names of three professional references to apply.interfolio.com/187126 . For additional information, applicants are directed to visit the University of Tennessee, College of Veterinary Medicine web site at https://vetmed.tennessee.edu/careers/"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/188343": {
      "title": "Clinical or Tenure Track Assistant/Associate Professor of Farm Animal Medicine and Surgery",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: UT Institute of Agriculture: College of Veterinary Medicine: Large Animal Clinical Sciences Location: Large Animal Clinical Sciences The successful applicant will join a dynamic Farm Animal Medicine and Surgery Service comprised of three clinicians (two ACVIM-LAIM and one ACVS-LA board certified), four residents (two ACVIM-LAIM and two ACVS-LA), and two service Veterinary technicians. The Farm Animal Medicine and Surgery group is a subset of the larger Farm Animal Alliance group comprised of 12 faculty members (4 ACVIM-LAIM, 3 ACT, 1 ACVS-LA, 1 ACVCP, and 4 general practitioners) offering ambulatory services and hospital care to livestock and companion farm animals in the large Knoxville area. While the successful applicant will still share some clinical emergency responsibilities with other members of the Farm Animal Medicine and Surgery Service to provide support after hours, they will have the benefit of the 3 faculty on the Large Animal Emergency and Critical Care Service (1 ACVS and 1 ACVECC board certified) providing the majority of emergency coverage. The Farm Animal Medicine and Surgery Service sees a diverse caseload of production and companion farm animal species. Beef cattle, small ruminants, pigs, camelids compose the majority of cases along with dairy cattle and limited exotic hoofstock. Expectations for this position include primarily clinical service and clinical teaching, for a minimum of 24 weeks a year, with negotiable amounts of preclinical teaching and research depending on the applicant’s interests and on choice of clinical track or tenure track. The faculty member will share in the clinical and didactic teaching of students in the veterinary curriculum and the training of interns and residents in specialty emphasis areas. Responsibilities include individual animal care for food and fiber animal species (beef and dairy cattle, sheep and goats, camelids, and swine). The faculty member will participate in clinical emergency duty with other faculty. Depending on negotiated effort allocations, the successful candidate may be expected to develop new teaching and education courses and tools, contribute to the discovery of new knowledge, and participate in outreach to veterinarians, producers, and other stake holders. Candidates must have a DVM, VMD, or equivalent degree and have completed a clinical residency program recognized by the American College of Veterinary Surgeons (ACVS-LA) or the American College of Veterinary Internal Medicine (ACVIM-LAIM). Consideration will be given both to board-certified and board-eligible candidates. Preference will be given to applicants with experience with livestock species as well as those having interest in companion farm animals (pet pigs, small ruminants, and camelids). Candidates should be able to demonstrate surgical proficiency for routine and emergency procedures in farm animals. Applicants should submit a letter of intent, current curriculum vitae, and names, addresses, e-mail address, and phone numbers of at least three professional references to https://apply.interfolio.com/188343"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/147172": {
      "title": "College of Veterinary Medicine- Assistant or Associate Professor of Diagnostic Imaging",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: UT Institute of Agriculture: College of Veterinary Medicine: Small Animal Clinical Sciences Location: Knoxville, TN The Department of Small Animal Clinical Sciences of the University of Tennessee, College of Veterinary Medicine, invites applications for a full-time tenure-track or full-time or part-time clinical-track faculty position in veterinary diagnostic imaging. The position is at the Assistant, Associate or Professor level. The successful candidate is expected to engage in the departmental mission by participating in the teaching, service, and scholarship programs of the College. Responsibilities may include clinical, didactic, and laboratory teaching of radiology principles to professional students, interns, and residents; participation in continuing education at local and national levels; providing mentorship to residents; and contributing to the clinical or basic science research program depending on the desired effort allocation. Imaging equipment at the University of Tennessee, College of Veterinary Medicine includes one large animal and two small animal radiology rooms equipped with digital radiography; digital fluoroscopy; portable radiology units for the large animal field service; three ultrasound units; a 128-slice CT scanner; a 1.5T large bore MRI scanner, and a nuclear medicine suite with two gamma cameras. An interventional suite equipped with digital fluoroscopy and a state-of-the-art 3D printing lab are on-site. All imaging modalities are interfaced with a PACS system. The Department of Small Animal Clinical Sciences has more than 50 tenure and clinical-track faculty, including board-certified specialists in radiology, anesthesiology, surgery, internal medicine, emergency and critical care, cardiology, behavior, dermatology, dentistry, nutrition, oncology, ophthalmology, sports medicine and rehabilitation, and avian and zoological medicine. The University of Tennessee College of Veterinary Medicine boasts a collegial environment committed to mentoring new faculty to be successful in their academic career. This includes a Master Teaching Program, which provides resources, programs and leadership to support the highest quality of professionalism and instruction within the various educational missions of the college, and a Veterinary Social Work program that provides training and support in the areas of communication and conflict management, compassion fatigue, animal-related grief and bereavement, and mindfulness-based stress reduction. New faculty are assigned an experienced faculty mentor, and the Department has an excellent record in helping clinical faculty successfully earn promotion and tenure. The University of Tennessee Veterinary Medical Center is adjacent to UT Gardens, the state botanical garden of Tennessee. The campus is in Knoxville, TN, in the foothills of the Great Smoky Mountains National Park. Knoxville was recently named by Trip Advisor as a “Top 10 Destination on the Rise.” It is a short drive away from the Great Smoky Mountains, several lakes, as well as several dog-friendly state parks. It also offers a wide array of outdoor recreational activities, including hiking, rock climbing, kayaking, and extensive road and mountain biking opportunities. Knoxville features a vibrant cultural downtown with excellent restaurants; a bustling farmer’s market; a world-class jazz orchestra; one of the nation’s most revered college theater troupes; frequent art and cultural festivals; and men’s and women’s sporting events. Knoxville is within a day's driving distance from two-thirds of the population of the United States, with Chattanooga, Nashville, Atlanta, Asheville, and Cincinnati being some of the many nearby destinations. The University of Tennessee is Tennessee’s flagship land grant university and is noted for its high level of research activity. Requirements include a DVM/VMD or equivalent degree, completion of a formal residency training program in radiology/diagnostic imaging, and certification or eligibility for certification by the American College of Veterinary Radiology (ACVR) or the European College of Veterinary Diagnostic Imaging (ECVDI). Applications will be accepted until the position is filled. Qualified individuals are invited to submit an application, including a letter of intent with a statement of professional goals, curriculum vitae, and the names of three professional references For additional information, applicants are directed to visit the University of Tennessee, College of Veterinary Medicine web site at https://vetmed.tennessee.edu/careers/ or to contact Ms. Amy Van Skyhawk at avanskyh@utk.edu."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/168127": {
      "title": "Assistant / Associate Professor of Soft Tissue / Orthopedics or Combined Position",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: UT Institute of Agriculture: College of Veterinary Medicine: Small Animal Clinical Sciences Location: Knoxville, TN, United States The Department of Small Animal Clinical Sciences at the University of Tennessee, College of Veterinary Medicine invites applications for a full-time tenure or clinical track faculty position in small animal soft tissue, orthopedic surgery, or a combined soft tissue/orthopedic surgery position. Depending on qualifications and experience, the position is at the Assistant Professor or Associate Professor level. The successful candidate is expected to engage in the departmental mission by participating in the teaching, service, and scholarship programs of the College. Responsibilities include clinical, didactic, and laboratory teaching of small animal surgery to professional students, interns, and residents; participation in continuing education at local and national levels; providing mentorship to residents; and contributing to the clinical or basic science research program. Faculty within the small animal surgery section currently include three ACVS board-certified surgeons; one ACVS residency-trained veterinarian; four surgery residents; three neurosurgeons (DACVIM-Neurology); three neurology residents; one sports medicine specialists (DACVSMR); four anesthesiologists (DACVA); three anesthesia residents; three ophthalmologists (DACVO); two ophthalmology residents, and one dentist (DACVD). Current equipment available for use in surgery includes an endoscopic tower, LigaSure device, ultrasonic aspirator, continually updated equipment for thoracoscopic and laparoscopic surgery, stenting, arthroscopy, total hip system, and a full complement of implants, including plating, pinning, external fixation, circular fixation, interlocking nails, and 3-D printing capabilities. Equipment and operating space are also available for interventional radiology techniques, which are currently performed at the University of Tennessee. The Small Animal Clinic is equipped to provide state-of-the-art surgical and medical service, teaching, and research. The facilities and equipment of the Small Animal Clinic support 24 specialties and include surgical and therapeutic lasers, 128 slice computed tomography unit, 1.5 Tesla MRI, nuclear medicine, video endoscopy, intraoperative fluoroscopy, lithotripsy, and DEXA scanning. Also available to the surgery service are a fully developed nutrition service, a regenerative medicine laboratory, four emergency and critical care specialists, an Instron materials testing machine, and an on-site 3D modeling and printing laboratory. The hospital has a state-of-the-art rehabilitation facility with 3 underwater treadmills, a therapeutic exercise pool, a hyperbaric oxygen chamber, many wound management modalities, and a fully equipped gait analysis laboratory with kinetic and kinematic testing capability. The University of Tennessee College of Veterinary Medicine boasts a collegial environment committed to mentoring new faculty to be successful in their academic career. This includes a Master Teaching Program, which provides resources, programs and leadership to support the highest quality of professionalism and instruction within the various educational missions of the college, and a Veterinary Social Work program that provides training and support in the areas of communication and conflict management, compassion fatigue, animal-related grief and bereavement, and mindfulness-based stress reduction. New faculty are assigned an experienced faculty mentor and the Department has an excellent record in helping clinical faculty successfully earn promotion and tenure. The University of Tennessee Veterinary Medical Center is adjacent to UT Gardens, the state botanical garden of Tennessee. The campus is located in Knoxville, TN, in the foothills of the Great Smoky Mountains National Park. Knoxville was recently named by Trip Advisor as a “Top 10 Destination on the Rise.” It is a short drive away from the Great Smoky Mountains, several lakes, as well as a number of dog-friendly state parks. It also offers a wide array of outdoor recreational activities, including hiking, rock climbing, kayaking, and extensive road and mountain biking opportunities. Knoxville features a vibrant cultural downtown with excellent restaurants; a bustling farmer’s market; a world-class jazz orchestra; one of the nation’s most revered college theater troupes; frequent art and cultural festivals; and men’s and women’s sporting events. Knoxville is within a day's driving distance from two-thirds of the population of the United States, with Chattanooga, Nashville, Atlanta, Asheville, and Cincinnati being some of the many nearby destinations. The University of Tennessee is Tennessee’s flagship land grant university and is noted for its high level of research activity. Requirements include the DVM/VMD or equivalent degree and completion of a formal surgery residency or certification by the American College of Veterinary Surgeons (ACVS) or the European College of Veterinary Surgeons (ECVS). Applications will be accepted until the position is filled. Qualified individuals are invited to apply, including a letter of intent with a statement of professional goals, curriculum vitae, and the names of three professional references. For additional information, applicants are directed to visit the University of Tennessee, College of Veterinary Medicine web site at https://vetmed.tennessee.edu/Pages/utcvm_home.aspx or contact Dr. Karen Tobias with soft tissue surgery questions - ktobias@utk.edu or Dr. Cassio Ferrigno with orthopedic questions - cferrign@utk.edu"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/183440": {
      "title": "Clinical Instructor in Small Animal Emergency",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: UT Institute of Agriculture: College of Veterinary Medicine: Small Animal Clinical Sciences Location: Knoxville, TN, United States The Department of Small Animal Clinical Sciences, College of Veterinary Medicine, University of Tennessee invites applications for full-time clinical instructor positions in small animal emergency. The successful candidate will share an enthusiasm for teaching excellence, strong communication skills, a collaborative mindset, and compassionate patient care while actively supporting an inclusive teaching and work environment. Responsibilities will include clinical care of patients presented to the emergency service which operates 24/7. The successful candidate is expected to add to the department’s reputation and make contributions to the teaching and service missions of the college. The University of Tennessee Veterinary Medical Center (VMC) is equipped with all the resources and expertise of specialty services needed to provide first class patient care, teaching, and clinical and basic science research.  Specialty services include internal medicine, oncology, neurology and neurosurgery, soft tissue surgery, orthopedic surgery, ophthalmology, cardiology, dermatology, dentistry, radiology, nutrition, anesthesiology, physical therapy, behavior, radiation oncology, zoo medicine and zoologic companion animal medicine and surgery. There are excellent therapeutic and diagnostic imaging facilities that include on-site MRI, CT unit, video endoscopy, laparoscopy, DEXA scanning, physical therapy and hyperbaric oxygen therapy.  Clinical services are supported by comprehensive endocrinology, virology, microbiology, parasitology, clinical pathology and anatomic pathology services.  A full-time Veterinary Social Work program offers counseling services for clients, faculty, staff and students.  A state-of-the-art Teaching, Learning and Simulation Instruction Center is on site for teaching and skills practice.  Faculty development is supported by the Master Teacher’s Program. The successful candidate will be joining an expanding Emergency and Critical Care (ECC) service that includes three diplomates in Veterinary Emergency and Critical Care, five ECC residents and 28 licensed veterinary technicians (2 VTS ECC). The ECC service is supported by rotating interns, veterinary assistants, veterinary technician interns and student workers. The state-of-the-art critical care unit includes 2 Puritan Bennett Covidien ventilators, 2 Vapotherm and 1 Optiflow high-flow oxygen therapy units, NOVA pHOX ultra blood gas/ critical care analyzer, multiple point of care analyzers, a new SonoSite ultrasound unit and a DRE 4 channel telemetric ECG. The ECC service also has an in-house blood bank to serve clinical patients. The hospital provides emergency and critical care services 24 hours per day 7 days per week. Candidates should be willing to work flexible hours to support the emergency and critical care service. Knoxville is home to the University of Tennessee flagship campus and is a beautiful and friendly place to live. It is a short drive to the Great Smoky Mountains, as well as several dog-friendly state parks and recreation areas. Outdoor recreational opportunities include, but are not limited to, hiking, biking, trail running, climbing and water sports. Knoxville has a diverse and inclusive culture, a vibrant downtown with excellent shopping and restaurants; bustling farmer’s markets featuring locally grown organic produce, meats and crafts; live music of all genres, ballet and theater; frequent art and cultural festivals; and men’s and women’s NCAA and semiprofessional sporting events. Requirements include the DVM or equivalent degree, completion of a small animal internship or 1-2 years of practice experience. The position is available starting July 1, 2026. Applications are accepted immediately and will be accepted until the position is filled. For further information, please contact Dr. Kristen Marshall at (865) 974-8387 or by email at kmarsh23@utk.edu.  Qualified individuals are invited to submit an application consisting of: 1) letter of professional goals, 2) curriculum vitae, and 3) names of three professional references"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/188566": {
      "title": "Assistant / Associate Professor in Zoological Companion Animal Medicine",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: UT Institute of Agriculture: College of Veterinary Medicine: Small Animal Clinical Sciences Location: Knoxville, TN, United States The Department of Small Animal Clinical Sciences at the University of Tennessee College of Veterinary Medicine invites applications for a full-time clinical or tenure track faculty position in Zoological Companion Animal Medicine. Depending on qualifications and experience, the position is at the Assistant Professor or Associate Professor level. The successful candidate is expected to engage in the departmental mission by participating in clinical and didactic teaching to veterinary students and house officers; providing clinical service in zoological companion animal medicine with reciprocated coverage in zoological medicine; contributing to the development of a basic science or clinical research program commensurate with effort allocation; and participating in continuing education at local, national, and international levels. Excellent interpersonal and communication skills and the ability to work within a team are required. The position has a major clinical commitment to the Zoological Companion Animal and Wildlife Service, and faculty are responsible for instruction and supervision of veterinary students, interns, and residents. This service provides health care for privately owned zoo companion animals and native wildlife brought by members of the public and wildlife agencies. Faculty also work synergistically with the general zoo medicine service which is the other service within the Zoological Medicine Section and provides care to Zoo Knoxville, Tiger Haven (large felid sanctuary), Appalachian Bear Rescue (a black bear rehabilitation facility), other local zoos, and wildlife agencies. Teaching responsibilities include lectures and laboratories for DVM students, instruction and mentoring of house officers, and instruction to clinical year veterinary students. The successful candidate will help build a cohesive and positive working environment in the services for students, house officers, technicians, and faculty. The University of Tennessee Veterinary Medical Center is a modern, fully equipped facility with extensive therapeutic and diagnostic equipment. The Zoological Medicine Section is currently supported by 4 faculty members, 3 house officers and 3 veterinary technicians. Hospital space and equipment are available to diagnose and treat all zoo companion, wildlife, and zoological patients presented to the hospital. The department includes tenured and clinical track faculty in supporting specialty services including anesthesiology, behavior, cardiology, dermatology, emergency/critical care, internal medicine, neurology, ophthalmology, nutrition, dentistry, medical oncology, radiology, radiation oncology, physical therapy/sports medicine, and soft tissue and orthopedic surgery. Additionally, there are robust equine and farm animal patient populations and pathologists with strong interests in zoological medicine. The University of Tennessee College of Veterinary Medicine boasts a collegial environment committed to mentoring new faculty to be successful in their academic career. This includes a Master Teaching Program, which provides resources, programs and leadership to support the highest quality of professionalism and instruction within the various educational missions of the college and a Veterinary Social Work program that provides training and support in the areas of communication and conflict management, compassion fatigue, animal-related grief and bereavement, and mindfulness-based stress reduction. New faculty are assigned an experienced faculty mentor, and the Department has an excellent record in helping faculty successfully earn promotion and tenure. Knoxville is home to the University of Tennessee flagship campus and is a beautiful and friendly place to live. It is a short drive from the Great Smoky Mountains, as well as several dog-friendly state parks and recreation areas. Outdoor recreational opportunities include, but are not limited to, hiking, biking, trail running, climbing, and water sports. Knoxville has a vibrant cultural downtown with excellent restaurants; bustling farmer’s markets; live music of all genres, ballet, and theater; frequent art and cultural festivals; and men’s and women’s sporting events. Requirements include a DVM/VMD or equivalent degree and certification or eligibility for certification by the American College of Zoological Medicine (ACZM). Applications will be accepted until the position is filled. Qualified individuals are invited to apply. Applications should include a letter of intent with a statement of professional goals, curriculum vitae, and the names of three professional references"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192516": {
      "title": "Director and Associate/Full Professor of Shelter Medicine",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: UT Institute of Agriculture: College of Veterinary Medicine: Small Animal Clinical Sciences Location: Knoxville, TN, United States The University of Tennessee College of Veterinary Medicine (UTCVM) is seeking applications from an innovative and collaborative veterinarian to lead its shelter medicine service and outreach activities. Board certification by the American Board of Veterinary Practitioners (ABVP) in Shelter Medicine Practice is preferred. If not currently certified, progress demonstrated toward specialty certification is required. The service currently consists of two clinical faculty members, two veterinary nurses, and one administrative staff. The team provides spay/neuter training and didactic education to DVM students, clinical services and consults to local animal shelters and rescues, outreach clinics to the community, and conducts research. The ideal candidate will have experience leading teams, strengthening existing relationships and programs, and developing new initiatives to support both DVM training and community needs. The college seeks to expand its outreach efforts in the areas of shelter medicine and access to care. Startup funds are available to support outreach program development and preliminary research projects. The Director of Shelter Medicine will collaborate with other groups in the CVM including the Center for Veterinary Social Work, The Program for Pet Health Equity, the Office of Outreach and Global Engagement, the Office of Advancement, and the Associate Dean for Academic Affairs. Allocation of responsibilities would be commensurate with either a tenure track or clinical track position and would include: Administrative Service and Program Development (45%) · Provide leadership and strategic direction for the shelter medicine program in the areas of clinical service and teaching , research, and outreach · Lead the vision, creation, and implementation of community outreach and teaching program s · Supervise Companion Animal Initiative of Tennessee (CAIT) program and staff · Establish and oversee a shelter medicine residency program · Oversee financial accounts and budget · Lead and assist others with grant writing to solicit and maintain program donors Clinical Service (15-35%) · C onduct shelter consultations throughout the region and provide recommendations for improvements, outbreak management, design and implementation of SOPs and best practices · Provide back-up for spay/neuter service as needed · Provide clinical service for access to care clinics Didactic teaching (5-10%) · Teach in elective courses in the professional veterinary medicine curriculum with other Shelter Medicine faculty · Provide lectures for the core DVM curriculum Research (5-25%) · Conduct collaborative research on topics associated with administrative and clinical activities or other innovative topics. Academic service (5%) · Serve on CVM and university committees. · Demonstrate leadership in the discipline of shelter medicine through participation in the Assoc of Shelter Veterinarians and/or other relevant organizations. · Design and participate in continuing education to local and regional groups Knoxville is home to the University of Tennessee flagship campus, and is a beautiful and friendly place to live. It is a short drive to the Great Smoky Mountains, as well as a number of dog-friendly state parks and recreation areas. Outdoor recreational opportunities include, but are not limited to, hiking, biking, trail running, climbing and water sports. Knoxville has a diverse and inclusive culture, a vibrant downtown with excellent shopping and restaurants; bustling farmer’s markets featuring locally grown organic produce, meats and crafts; live music of all genres, ballet, and theater; frequent art and cultural festivals; and men’s’ and women’s’ NCAA and semiprofessional sporting events. Qualifications for a successful candidate: Required DVM or equivalent degree Board certification in Shelter Medicine Practice preferred; if not currently certified, progress demonstrated toward specialty certification required Minimum of 5 years of veterinary clinical experience Excellent interpersonal and communication skills and the ability to work cooperatively Leadership and program development experience Preferred A post-graduate degree in shelter medicine practice, preventive medicine, epidemiology, population medicine, public health, and/or information systems Didactic and/or laboratory teaching experience Applications are accepted immediately and will be accepted until the position is filled. For further information, please contact Dr. Rajeev Nair at (865) 974-8387 or by email at vrajeev@utk.edu."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193888": {
      "title": "Assistant / Associate Professor of Small Animal Primary Care",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: UT Institute of Agriculture: College of Veterinary Medicine: Small Animal Clinical Sciences Location: Knoxville, TN, United States The Department of Small Animal Clinical Sciences of the University of Tennessee, College of Veterinary Medicine invites applications for a full-time clinical track faculty position in Small Animal Primary Care. The position is at the Clinical Assistant or Associate Professor level, depending on qualifications and experience. The missions of the Department are to educate veterinary students and graduate veterinarians to meet the needs of the pet owning public, to provide advanced patient care, and to generate new knowledge in pet health through basic and applied research. Clinical track faculty at the University of Tennessee are expected to participate in teaching, service, and scholarship programs of the College. Responsibilities of the successful candidate will include clinical service and instruction in small animal primary care to DVM students and house officers, didactic program instruction within the preclinical curriculum, and collaboration in clinical discovery or scholarship. The University of Tennessee Tickle Small Animal Hospital is a comprehensive and fully equipped facility with the expertise needed to provide advanced medical care. Specialty services supported by departmental faculty include: internal medicine, oncology, neurology, soft tissue and orthopedic surgery, dentistry, ophthalmology, dermatology, behavior, radiology, radiation oncology, anesthesiology, nutrition, physical therapy, shelter medicine, zoo, and exotic and avian medicine and surgery. The College also houses a nationally renowned Veterinary Social Work program. Facilities include: nuclear medicine, computerized tomography, MRI, radiation therapy, video endoscopy, laparoscopy, hyperbaric unit, and physical therapy. The hospital provides VECCS Level I emergency and critical care service 24 hours per day, 365 days a year. The Primary Care clinic serves dogs and cats belonging to students, faculty, and staff of the University of Tennessee and is not open to the general public. Pets are seen primarily for wellness, illness, minor surgery, dentistry, and senior screening. The clinic will soon relocate to a newly constructed, full-service primary care clinic separate from the tertiary referral hospital. The successful candidate will join a collaborative service currently staffed by 3 faculty, 2 technicians, and 1 veterinary assistant. Primary care faculty are also committed to developing and supporting spectrum of care opportunities within our college and community. The University of Tennessee College of Veterinary Medicine boasts a collegial environment committed to mentoring new faculty to be successful in their academic career. This includes a Master Teaching Program, which provides resources, programs and leadership to support the highest quality of professionalism and instruction within the various educational missions of the college and a Veterinary Social Work program that provides training and support in the areas of communication and conflict management, compassion fatigue, animal-related grief and bereavement, and mindfulness-based stress reduction. New faculty are assigned an experienced faculty mentor, and the Department has an excellent record in helping faculty successfully earn promotion and tenure. The University of Tennessee is located in Knoxville Tennessee, a vibrant small city located between the Great Smoky Mountains and Cumberland plateau, an area with rich biodiversity and natural beauty. The Knoxville Urban Wilderness provides over 50 miles of trails and greenways within the city offering ample opportunity for outdoor recreation. Knoxville has an energetic cultural downtown area with excellent restaurants, bustling farmer’s markets, live music of all genres, ballet, theater, frequent art and cultural festivals, and men’s and women’s sporting events. The metropolitan areas of Nashville, Atlanta, Asheville, and Chattanooga are only a short drive away. Candidates must have a DVM or equivalent degree. All applicants should also have a minimum of 2 years clinical experience in companion animal medicine and/or have completed a small animal internship in medicine and surgery. The successful candidate will be proficient in teaching spay/neuter, minor surgery, and dental procedures. The ideal applicant will have clinical teaching experience as well as board certification or eligibility by an AVMA-recognized veterinary specialty organization. Applications will be accepted until the position is filled. Qualified individuals are invited to apply. Applications should include a letter of intent with a statement of professional goals, curriculum vitae, and the names of three professional references For additional information, applicants are directed to visit the University of Tennessee, College of Veterinary Medicine web site at https://vetmed.tennessee.edu/Pages/utcvm_home.aspx or contact search committee chair - Dr. Chad Lothamer - clothame@utk.edu"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/189540": {
      "title": "Open Rank Professor of Public Administration/Public Management Fall 2027",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: Baker School Location: Knoxville, TN The Howard H. Baker, Jr. School of Public Policy and Public Affairs at the University of Tennessee, Knoxville, is seeking to fill two tenure-line faculty positions at the senior assistant through full professor rank in public administration and public management beginning August 1, 2027. These are 9-month positions. Areas of particular interest include state and local governance, nonprofit financial management, and emergency management, though outstanding candidates in other substantive areas will receive full consideration. For a candidate with a distinguished record in state and local government/governance, one of the two positions may be filled as a named professorship. Named Professorship in State and Local Government/Governance Dependent on qualifications, one applicant may be considered for a named professorship, focused on state and local government and governance. This is a senior appointment carrying an endowed title and commensurate research support. We are especially interested in candidates whose scholarship has shaped the study of state and local governance, intergovernmental relations, or related areas, and whose national reputation would strengthen the Baker School’s profile in this domain. The named professorship is closely linked to the Baker School’s new Tennessee Center for Local Governance (described below). Uniquely qualified candidates with distinguished records of research, teaching, and professional leadership are encouraged to apply. We seek scholars who will contribute to teaching across our undergraduate major in Public Affairs and our Master of Public Administration (MPA), including a rapidly growing online MPA program. Candidates should demonstrate experience with, or clear potential for, high-quality teaching in both in-person and online formats across core public administration courses and specialized electives aligned with their expertise. Candidates should identify in their cover letter the core courses they would be able to teach. The teaching load is 2-2. The successful candidate should have an active research agenda addressing contemporary questions in public administration. The Baker School places particular value on scholarship that engages practitioners, contributes to evidence-informed decision-making, and addresses policy and management challenges facing Tennessee communities. As part of its continued growth, the Baker School is launching the Tennessee Center for Local Governance, offering faculty a platform to develop applied research partnerships with state and local governments and nonprofit organizations. Successful candidates will have the opportunity to contribute to this effort and help shape its development. All Baker School faculty are expected to provide service to the School, University, State, and community, and to engage with local policymakers and leaders. Who We Are The University of Tennessee, Knoxville, is the state’s flagship research institution, a campus of choice for outstanding undergraduates, and a premier graduate institution. As a land-grant university, it is committed to excellence in learning, scholarship, and engagement with society. The Baker School was established in 2023 to honor the distinguished life and legacy of Howard H. Baker Jr., a former US Senator, Senate Majority Leader, White House Chief of Staff, and Ambassador to Japan. Located in a 51,000-sq. In a purpose-built facility, the Baker School evolved from the Baker Center, which for 20 years has operated as a think tank producing research, public engagement programming, and an active set of student programs. The Baker School is very excited to add degree programs and to grow its research programming into independent research centers. In addition, the Baker School houses the Institute of American Civics, which is responsible for encouraging civic knowledge and civic participation among UT undergraduates and the state at large. We are a dynamic and close-knit team of dedicated and hardworking individuals who are devoted to research, education, and public service initiatives that address real-world problems in local, state, national, and global communities. Each member of our team is a valuable contributor to achieving our mission, and we work together to find innovative approaches to moving the Baker School forward. We are fast-moving and growing. Our work is constantly evolving to meet changing needs. Who We Are Looking For We are in search of a solution-finder, someone who enthusiastically embraces opportunities to address novel situations through teamwork and collaboration. We need a hardworking and flexible professional who thrives in developing out-of-the-box approaches and addressing new challenges. We are looking for an achiever, someone who is searching for a chance to grow their skills and who is open to learning from others. We need a critical thinker who exercises good judgment and who can be relied upon and trusted to provide sound counsel to colleagues and leadership inside and beyond the academy. We need teammates who are dedicated to public service. Minimum Qualifications Candidates must possess a PhD in Public Administration, Public Management, or a related field at the time of appointment, along with a demonstrated record of research publication and university-level teaching experience. Rank-Specific Qualifications Senior Assistant Professor: Candidates must have a completed PhD and demonstrate an established, active research agenda, evidenced by a record of publication in high-quality peer-reviewed journals or presses. Candidates must provide evidence of a sustained record of teaching excellence (including teaching evaluations) in both in-person and/or online formats, as well as a commitment to institutional and professional service. Associate Professor: Candidates must demonstrate a strong, ongoing record of publication in leading peer-reviewed journals, a sustained track record of teaching excellence and course development, and meaningful service and leadership contributions to the department, university, and profession. Experience securing and managing externally funded research or applied research partnerships is strongly preferred. Full Professor: Candidates must demonstrate a distinguished, sustained record of scholarly achievement with a substantial body of impactful peer-reviewed publications and a recognized national or international reputation in their field. Candidates must possess a sustained record of teaching excellence, mentorship of students and junior faculty, and significant leadership and service. A strong record of securing and managing external research fundings is preferred. Applicants should submit the following via Interfolio: §  A cover letter explaining their research and teaching experience §  A current CV §  A sample of scholarly writing §  Teaching evaluations §  Names and contact information for three references § Named Professorship Consideration: To be considered for the endowed professorship, applicants should include a brief statement in their cover letter detailing their major scholarly contributions to state and local government/governance and any experience building practitioner partnerships or centers. Successful candidates will be required to submit official transcripts and undergo a background check. Review of applications will begin on September 15, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. For questions or inquiries, contact the search chair: Dr. Tara Bryan, tbryan29@utk.edu ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190352": {
      "title": "Institute of American Civics - Open Rank Tenure Track Professor in American Civics Fall 2027",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: Baker School Location: Knoxville, TN The Institute of American Civics, located within the Howard H. Baker, Jr. School of Public Policy and Public Affairs at the University of Tennessee, Knoxville, is seeking to fill a tenure-line 9-month faculty position at the assistant through full professor rank in American Civics to begin August 1, 2027. Successful candidates will be expected to teach undergraduate classes on a 2-2 load and carry out a program of research and scholarship. They will also be expected to be actively engaged in the Institute’s activities. Who We Are The I nstitute of American Civics was created to enhance education in politics, economics, philosophy, American history, and American government as well as increase understanding of the rights and responsibilities of American citizenship; fundamental democratic principles; and the ways in which those principles are expressed in and through our federal, state, and local institutions. In pursuit of this mission, the Institute invites applications from scholars at all ranks with teaching and research profiles in American political thought and American political institutions (including federalism). Additional interests in ancient to early modern political philosophy and/or political economy are desirable. Willingness to engage in student programming and public outreach is also required. The Baker School was established in 2023 to honor the distinguished life and legacy of Howard H. Baker Jr., a former US Senator, Senate Majority Leader, White House Chief of Staff, and Ambassador to Japan. The Baker School evolved from the Baker Center, which produced research, public engagement programming, and an active set of student programs for twenty years. The Baker School has now added degree programs and grown its research programming into several independent research centers. We are a dynamic and close-knit team of dedicated and hardworking individuals who are devoted to our mission of research, education, and public service initiatives, and are dedicated to moving the Baker School forward. The University of Tennessee, Knoxville, is the state’s flagship research institution, a campus of choice for outstanding undergraduates, and a premier graduate institution. As a land-grant university, it is committed to excellence in learning, scholarship, and engagement with society. Minimum Qualifications Candidates must possess a PhD in political science, history, economics or a related discipline at the time of appointment (August 1, 2027). Rank-Specific Qualifications Assistant Professor: Candidates should have a demonstrated potential for publishing in appropriate venues, including a strong Ph.D. dissertation which has been published in some form or for which the candidate has a viable strategy for publication, and exceptional teaching. Advanced candidates for Assistant Professor should have a record of actual publications, and should show development of a trajectory that goes beyond the dissertation. Associate Professor: Candidates should have a demonstrated record of publishing in appropriate venues, exceptional teaching, and internal and external service to their professional community. Full Professor: Candidates should have a demonstrated record of publishing in appropriate venues, exceptional teaching, and internal and external service to their professional community. In addition, the candidate should have national/international recognition in their field of study. Appropriate venues can include books, journal articles, and contributions to edited volumes. Some demonstrated capacity to attract external grant or fellowship funding is a plus. Applicants should send a cover letter explaining their research, teaching experience, record of service (if any), commitment to the Institute’s mission, and which IAC courses they are prepared to teach. They should also send a current CV, a research statement, a sample of scholarly writing, and recent student teaching evaluations (if available). All applicants should provide a list of references. Applicants should submit the following via Interfolio: -CV -Cover Letter -List of three references with contact information -Research Statement -Sample of scholarly writing -Student Evaluations if available. Successful candidates will be required to submit official transcripts and undergo a background check. Screening of applicants will begin on September 1, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. For questions or inquiries, contact the search chair: Dr. Andrew Busch, abusch1@utk.edu ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193527": {
      "title": "Lecturer or Teaching Assistant Professor, Engineering Fundamentals, Spring 2027",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: Tickle College of Engineering: Engineering Fundamentals Instruction Location: Knoxville, TN Engineering Fundamentals (EF) at the University of Tennessee, Knoxville, seeks two dynamic and innovative faculty at the rank of Lecturer (non-terminal degree) or Teaching Assistant Professor (terminal degree) to contribute to its first-year engineering program. EF is the home of the engage Engineering Fundamentals program, an active-learning focused, first-year curriculum that is responsible for teaching 8-12 credit hours of common coursework for Tickle College of Engineering’s first-year students, depending on their major. EF course content, delivered in a flipped classroom model, primarily focuses on introductory level physics, computing tools (e.g., Excel and MATLAB), hands-on lab activities, and project design experiences. Additional topics that may be included in the first-year courses are: the design process, technical communication, teaming, perspectives of the engineering profession, and student success-related topics (time management, study skills, etc.). Engineering Fundamentals is also responsible for teaching an upper-division active-learning introductory programming course utilizing primarily MATLAB for engineering problem solving. All faculty in EF are expected to contribute 90% of their time to teaching and 10% to the service mission of EF, the college, the university, and/or the field. The workload for the position includes teaching one to two EF courses each semester (courses may include multiple sections up to 128 students), mentoring both undergraduate and graduate teaching assistants, and facilitating the day-to-day operations of the EF unit. Details about the engage Engineering Fundamentals program are available at https://engage.utk.edu/ . As a faculty member of the Engineering Fundamentals program , you join the University of Tennessee community. The Tickle College of Engineering (TCE) is in the midst of an unprecedented period of growth and success, including adding more than 30 new faculty at all levels to the college over 4 years as part of ambitious hiring campaigns led by Chancellor Donde Plowman and Dean Matthew Mench. The college has set records in research expenditures, enrollment, incoming student GPA, intellectual property development, and USNWR rank in the past three years. Key partnerships with Oak Ridge National Laboratory (ORNL) through the University of Tennessee Oak Ridge Innovation Institute (UT-ORII) and Consolidated Nuclear Security (CNS) that manages the Y-12 National Security Complex have created an expanded opportunity space for faculty and students in the region . New facilities include the state-of-the art Zeanah Engineering Complex, the University of Tennessee Manufacturing and Design Enterprise (TN-MADE) facility, and the Innovation South building that houses UTK’s Fibers and Composites Manufacturing Facility (FCMF), a National Prototype Research Center (NSPC) as part of a partnership with CNS Y-12 under construction, and new facilities for the Biomedical Engineering department and a quantum foundry in planning stages. TCE currently has 213 tenured/tenure track and more than 80 non-tenure track faculty in its nine academic departments and offers 11 undergraduate, 16 MS, and 15 PhD/DE degree programs. Affiliated with TCE and located in Tullahoma, Tennessee, the UT Space Institute is a hub of aerospace and defense research. The college is also home to eight research centers and three interdisciplinary institutes. With approximately 4,800 undergraduate and 1,500 graduate students, the college sits 28th among public universities in the most recent U.S. News and World Report graduate rankings. Faculty in the college have won 33 early career awards (NSF, DOE, DARPA, AFOSR, AHA, and Sloan) since 2016. In FY25, the college had annual research expenditures of $113.9M. Knoxville, TN is a vibrant city with a beautiful and walkable downtown, active neighborhoods, an effervescent nightlife that includes numerous theaters and museums, a rich live music scene across all genres, and eclectic restaurants. Knoxville is nestled in the foothills of the Great Smoky Mountains, surrounded by lakes and the Tennessee River, providing amazing access to various outdoor activities. Knoxville is within easy driving distance of four major metropolitan areas that regularly host larger cultural and entertainment events. The faculty position is a full-time, non-tenure-track, 9-month appointment at the rank of Lecturer or Assistant Teaching Professor, based on degree. Candidates are required to have earned at least a master’s degree in engineering , engineering physics, or computer science . Candidates should possess excellent communication skills, a solid commitment to innovative teaching methods ( both traditional and technology - enabled ) , and the ability to promote a n inclusive learning environment . Demonstrated interest in first- year engineering and computing education programs is expected. Candidates with experience teaching computing applications or engineering physics, experience teaching project-based courses , experience creating and managing student projects, and/ or degrees in engineering education will also be given preference. Applications must include the following four documents. Please upload documents as PDF s to ensure readability : 1. a one-page cover letter addressing qualifications and motivation, 2. a teaching statement, including the candidate’s background and experience that make them an ideal candidate. This should include, but is not limited to, the following , e xamples of how the candidate has , or would employ innovative, active learning strategies in the classroo m , e xamples of how the candidate has , or would engag e in large , diverse class es , e xamples of how the candidate has , or would assess learning outcomes for large , diverse classes 3. a comprehensive curriculum vitae, and 4. the names and contact information (address, phone number, and e-mail address) for at least three professional references . Questions about the position should be directed to Dr. Baker Martin , Chair of the Search Committee, (email: bmarti50@utk.edu ) . Positions to be filled for a January 2027 start . Review of application s will begin September 30th and will continue until all position s are filled. Applications without all four required documents uploaded will not be reviewed."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193530": {
      "title": "Assist Director & Teaching Asst/Assoc/Full Professor, Tickle College of Engineering, Spring 2027",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: Tickle College of Engineering: Engineering Fundamentals Instruction Location: Knoxville, TN The Cook Grand Challenge Honors Program in the Tickle College of Engineering at the University of Tennessee, Knoxville, seeks a dynamic and innovative educator for the position of Teaching Assistant/Associate/Full Professor (commensurate with level of appropriate relevant experience) to serve as Assistant Director of the program. The responsibilities of this position will include a combination of teaching and administration duties. The Assistant Director will 1) co-teach our first-year engineering physics honors courses which incorporate team projects and laboratory exercises, 2) lead the program’s effort in development and implementation of technology driven curricular elements (e.g., AI and XR), and 3) assist in the administration of our honors programs which includes Honors Concentrations and the National Academy of Engineering Grand Challenge Scholars (GCS) program. Effort allocation will be 80% to teaching and curriculum development and 20% to program administration. More information about the CGCHP is available at https://honors.tickle.utk/.edu . The Tickle College of Engineering is in the first year of a five-year undergraduate education plan with the goal of achieving comprehensive outcomes aligned with student success and competitiveness. The Assistant Director will lead this effort for the honors program by expanding an XR pilot program to help students learn physics concepts and integrate it into the honor physics sequence and potentially incorporate additional technology driven instruction as recommended by the educational plan. They will also assist with the recruitment of incoming students and the review of applicants. The University of Tennessee, Knoxville (UT), is the state's flagship institution, a campus of choice for outstanding undergraduates, and a premier graduate institution. As a land-grant university, UT is committed to excellence in learning, scholarship, and engagement with society. In all its activities, the university aims to advance the frontiers of human knowledge and enrich and elevate society. The Tickle College of Engineering has experienced rapid growth in its number of faculty and student enrollment. The position is a full-time, non-tenure-track, 9-month appointment. Candidates are required to have earned a terminal degree in engineering, engineering education, or a closely related field. Candidates should possess excellent communication skills, a solid commitment to innovative teaching methods (both traditional and technology-enabled), and the ability to promote an inclusive learning environment. Demonstrated interest in first-year engineering programs is expected. Faculty Rank Criteria Appointment rank will be determined based on the following criteria: ·         Teaching Assistant Professor – shows promise for excellence in teaching and related responsibilities ·         Teaching Associate Professor – minimum five years of experience teaching engineering and/or STEM topics at the post-secondary level and demonstrated record of excellence in teaching and related responsibilities ·         Teaching Professor (Full) – minimum eight years of experience teaching engineering and/or STEM topics at the post-secondary level and demonstrated record of excellence in teaching and related responsibilities; sustained, consistent record of excellence and evidence of instructional leadership (e.g., curriculum development, mentoring, pedagogical innovation) commensurate with senior rank. Applications must include the following four documents. Please upload documents as PDFs to ensure readability: One-page cover letter addressing qualifications and motivation, Teaching statement, including the candidate’s background and experience that make them an ideal candidate. This should include,   but is not limited to, examples of how the candidate has, or would employ active learning and technology-enhanced learning         strategies (especially the use of virtual and/or augmented reality). Comprehensive curriculum vitae, and Names and contact information (address, phone number, and e-mail address) for at least three professional references. Questions about the position should be directed to Dr. Nathan Hicks, Chair of the Search Committee, (email: nhicks7@utk.edu). Positions to be filled for a desired January 2027 start. Review of applications will begin October 1, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. Applications without all four required documents uploaded will not be reviewed."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191960": {
      "title": "Robotics and Automation for Advanced Manufacturing for Affordable Building Construction  - Research Assistant/Associate/Full Professor",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: UT-ORII Location: Knoxville, TN The University of Tennessee-Oak Ridge Innovation Institute (UT-ORII) seeks highly qualified research faculty candidates at any rank in robotics and automation. The successful candidate will contribute to an interdisciplinary collaboration focused on building construction to improve affordability, productivity, energy efficiency, and resilience. Potential research interests include, but are not limited to: Study the interaction and co-work between robots and people in the construction setting. Develop automation and robotics technologies for industrialized mass customization to maximize productivity, affordability, quality, and safety across the construction lifecycle. Develop and integrate onsite and offsite processes for the high-performance materials, component assembly, and whole building assembly that are being developed under the Buildings CRI. These activities will include robotic and automated manufacturing and automated quality assurance and quality control. Develop automatic and optimization processes to standardize and integrate onsite and offsite construction and industrialized mass customization in close collaboration with researchers from the Buildings CRI. This position will be associated with the UT-Oak Ridge Innovation Institute’s Advanced Manufacturing for Affordable Building Construction Convergent Research Initiative (Buildings CRI), a collaboration between Oak Ridge National Laboratory (ORNL) and the University of Tennessee. The Buildings CRI aims to transform the building industry through cutting-edge research in robotics, automation, advanced materials, and streamlined construction processes. At full strength, the Buildings CRI will recruit ~20 new investigators at ORNL and the University of Tennessee, Knoxville (UTK). The Buildings CRI supports its interdisciplinary researchers by offering networking opportunities with fellow researchers and construction sector stakeholders, facilitating collaborative experiments and proposal development, providing mentorship from senior CRI researchers at ORNL and UT, and offering tailored professional development opportunities. Roles and Responsibilities The successful candidate will be expected to: Develop a nationally and internationally recognized research program with interdisciplinary connections across the CRI network at UT and ORNL. Contribute building and construction expertise to collaborative studies on the transition of the building construction industry. Interface with UT-ORII programs to support the UT-ORII Buildings CRI's mission and goals. Build partnerships and collaborations through the Office of Research Innovation and Economic Development (ORIED), Institute for Energy and Environment (IEE) gateway, Institute for a Secure and Sustainable Environment, the Tickle College of Engineering, the School of Architecture, the UT Institute of Agriculture, and current and future partners. As a UT-ORII research faculty member, the successful candidate will collaborate with designated UT departments and researchers as well as ORNL researchers to address current and future affordability, productivity, energy-efficiency, and resilience requirements for buildings. This includes conducting research, preparing and submitting proposals, supporting existing research projects, and mentoring graduate and undergraduate students. PhD or terminal degree in Robotics, Automation, Advanced Manufacturing, Industrial Engineering, or related field. A track record of publishing and seeking funding. Collaborative research experience in areas supporting UT-ORII’s Buildings CRI mission. Demonstrated research track record supporting the candidate’s potential to establish a nationally and internationally recognized research program aligned with UT-ORII’s mission and goals. A clear path to independence as a researcher is desired, though collaboration with more senior investigators is also encouraged. Strong communication skills and the ability to work collaboratively in a team environment. This position requires the ability to be granted a site-access badge for the Oak Ridge National Laboratory. Additional Information This is a 12-month, non-tenure track position. The appointment will be with the University of Tennessee, Knoxville. The primary work location for this position is on the University of Tennessee campus in Knoxville and may include visits to collaborator sites on the campuses of ORNL and UTK. The position will be implemented as an initial three-year contract with full salary support for the first year, followed by a gradual increase in expected external salary funding in subsequent years. The applicant will be expected to recruit graduate students by year three. A competitive startup package including funds for graduate students and equipment will be offered. Salary and title/rank will be determined commensurate with experience. Benefits will be typical of exempt employees at the University of Tennessee. The candidate must be eligible for initial clearance at Oak Ridge National Laboratory. Application materials must be submitted to Interfolio at apply.interfolio.com/191960 Application reviews will begin October 15, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. Applications must include: (1) a letter of interest (2) a comprehensive curriculum vitae (3) research plan (4) 3 Professional References including names, addresses, and telephone numbers More About UT-ORII The University of Tennessee-Oak Ridge Innovation Institute was launched by the University of Tennessee and Oak Ridge National Laboratory in 2021, in response to America’s need for a stronger pool of science, technology, engineering and mathematics, and increasingly life science talent. UT-ORII is leveraging UT and ORNL’s best capabilities and resources to accelerate collaborative discovery, innovation, and interdisciplinary graduate education; and to prepare the next generation of talent in areas of critical importance to the nation and the State of Tennessee. The institute’s current research areas include Clean Manufacturing and Advanced Materials, Energy Storage and Transportation, Radiopharmaceutical Therapies, Circular Bioeconomy Systems, and Advanced Manufacturing for Affordable Building Construction. To learn more about UT-ORII visit utorii.com . More About UT Knoxville UT Knoxville, which includes the UT Space Institute and the UT Institute of Agriculture, serves the state by educating its citizens, enhancing its culture, and making a difference in people’s lives through research and service. It embodies excellence in teaching, research, scholarship, creative activity, outreach, and engagement. To learn more about UT Knoxville visit UTK.edu . More About ORNL Leveraging the talents of its world-class researchers and exceptional support staff, Oak Ridge National Laboratory is helping solve critical scientific and technical challenges and, in so doing, creating economic opportunity for the nation. ORNL provides access to unique facilities, laboratories, and equipment, drawing thousands of visiting researchers each year. To learn more about ORNL visit https://www.ornl.gov . For questions, please contact Prof. Mingzhou Jin ( jin@utk.edu )."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191977": {
      "title": "Construction Science and Management for Advanced Manufacturing for Affordable Building Construction  - Research Assistant/Associate/Full Professor",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: UT-ORII Location: Knoxville, TN The University of Tennessee-Oak Ridge Innovation Institute (UT-ORII) seeks highly qualified research faculty candidates at any rank in robotics and automation. The successful candidate will contribute to an interdisciplinary collaboration focused on building construction to improve affordability, productivity, energy efficiency, and resilience. Potential research interests include, but are not limited to: Develop modern construction management technologies and processes maximize productivity, affordability, quality, and safety across the construction lifecycle. Develop methods and systems to integrate design, estimating, construction, and operations of industrialized construction. Develop AI applications in construction science and management. Build models and frameworks for construction data interoperability & cybersecurity. Develop models and systems for resilient construction and supply chain to support modern construction, including industrialized construction. This position will be associated with the UT-Oak Ridge Innovation Institute’s Advanced Manufacturing for Affordable Building Construction Convergent Research Initiative (Buildings CRI), a collaboration between Oak Ridge National Laboratory (ORNL) and the University of Tennessee. The Buildings CRI aims to transform the building industry through cutting-edge research in robotics, automation, advanced materials, and streamlined construction processes. At full strength, the Buildings CRI will recruit ~20 new investigators at ORNL and the University of Tennessee, Knoxville (UTK). The Buildings CRI supports its interdisciplinary researchers by offering networking opportunities with fellow researchers and construction sector stakeholders, facilitating collaborative experiments and proposal development, providing mentorship from senior CRI researchers at ORNL and UT, and offering tailored professional development opportunities. Roles and Responsibilities The successful candidate will be expected to: Develop a nationally and internationally recognized research program with interdisciplinary connections across the CRI network at UT and ORNL. Contribute building and construction expertise to collaborative studies on the transition of the building construction industry. Interface with UT-ORII programs to support the UT-ORII Buildings CRI's mission and goals. Build partnerships and collaborations through the Office of Research Innovation and Economic Development (ORIED), Institute for Energy and Environment (IEE) gateway, Institute for a Secure and Sustainable Environment, the Tickle College of Engineering, the School of Architecture, the UT Institute of Agriculture, and current and future partners. As a UT-ORII research faculty member, the successful candidate will collaborate with designated UT departments and researchers as well as ORNL researchers to address current and future affordability, productivity, energy-efficiency, and resilience requirements for buildings. This includes conducting research, preparing and submitting proposals, supporting existing research projects, and mentoring graduate and undergraduate students. PhD or terminal degree in Construction Management, Construction Science, Civil Engineering, or related field. A track record of publishing and seeking funding. Collaborative research experience in areas supporting UT-ORII’s Buildings CRI mission. Demonstrated research track record supporting the candidate’s potential to establish a nationally and internationally recognized research program aligned with UT-ORII’s mission and goals. A clear path to independence as a researcher is desired, though collaboration with more senior investigators is also encouraged. Strong communication skills and the ability to work collaboratively in a team environment. This position requires the ability to be granted a site-access badge for the Oak Ridge National Laboratory. Additional Information This is a 12-month, non-tenure track position. The appointment will be with the University of Tennessee, Knoxville. The primary work location for this position is on the University of Tennessee campus in Knoxville and may include visits to collaborator sites on the campuses of ORNL and UTK. The position will be implemented as an initial three-year contract with full salary support for the first year, followed by a gradual increase in expected external salary funding in subsequent years. The applicant will be expected to recruit graduate students by year three. A competitive startup package including funds for graduate students and equipment will be offered. Salary and title/rank will be determined commensurate with experience. Benefits will be typical of exempt employees at the University of Tennessee. The candidate must be eligible for initial clearance at Oak Ridge National Laboratory. Application materials must be submitted to Interfolio at apply.interfolio.com/191977 Application reviews will begin October 15, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. Applications must include: a letter of interest a comprehensive curriculum vitae research plan 3 Professional References including names, addresses, and telephone numbers More About UT-ORII The University of Tennessee-Oak Ridge Innovation Institute was launched by the University of Tennessee and Oak Ridge National Laboratory in 2021, in response to America’s need for a stronger pool of science, technology, engineering and mathematics, and increasingly life science talent. UT-ORII is leveraging UT and ORNL’s best capabilities and resources to accelerate collaborative discovery, innovation, and interdisciplinary graduate education; and to prepare the next generation of talent in areas of critical importance to the nation and the State of Tennessee. The institute’s current research areas include Clean Manufacturing and Advanced Materials, Energy Storage and Transportation, Radiopharmaceutical Therapies, Circular Bioeconomy Systems, and Advanced Manufacturing for Affordable Building Construction. To learn more about UT-ORII visit utorii.com . More About UT Knoxville UT Knoxville, which includes the UT Space Institute and the UT Institute of Agriculture, serves the state by educating its citizens, enhancing its culture, and making a difference in people’s lives through research and service. It embodies excellence in teaching, research, scholarship, creative activity, outreach, and engagement. To learn more about UT Knoxville visit UTK.edu . More About ORNL Leveraging the talents of its world-class researchers and exceptional support staff, Oak Ridge National Laboratory is helping solve critical scientific and technical challenges and, in so doing, creating economic opportunity for the nation. ORNL provides access to unique facilities, laboratories, and equipment, drawing thousands of visiting researchers each year. To learn more about ORNL visit https://www.ornl.gov . For questions, please contact Prof. Husam Alshareef ( halshare@tennessee.edu )."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193294": {
      "title": "Sustainable Chemistry for Recyclable Biopolymers Research Assistant/Associate/Full Professor",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: UT-ORII Location: Knoxville, TN The University of Tennessee-Oak Ridge Innovation Institute (UT-ORII) invites applications from highly qualified research candidates to advance sustainable chemistry's fundamental science and engineering through the development of recyclable bio-derived monomers and polymers. The successful candidate will establish an externally funded, internationally visible research program that contributes to circular bioeconomy systems through innovative polymer synthesis, characterization, and recycling strategies. Research areas of interest for this position may include, but are not limited to: Developing efficient syntheses of bio-derived polymers with properties comparable to, or exceeding those of conventional synthetic polymers, with emphasis on recyclability-by-design (e.g., using dynamic covalent bonds). Elucidation of ideal bio-derived monomers and chemical building blocks that enable targeted polymers properties, potentially in collaboration with plant and material scientists. Development of advanced processing pathways for mechanical, chemical, or biological recycling of bio-derived polymers. This position is part of UT-ORII’s Circular Bioeconomy Systems (CBS) Convergent Research Initiative (CRI), a collaborative unit between ORNL and the University of Tennessee. The CBS CRI mission is to develop innovative, market-driven strategies to maximize the value of bio-based resources and create jobs in sustainable manufacturing and agriculture. By leveraging emerging science, this initiative aims to produce biomaterials from domestic sources, advancing American competitiveness while reducing reliance on foreign resources. The CBS CRI aims to recruit ~10 new investigators at Oak Ridge National Laboratory (ORNL), the University of Tennessee Institute of Agriculture (UTIA), and the University of Tennessee, Knoxville (UTK). The CBS CRI supports its interdisciplinary researchers by offering networking opportunities with fellow scientists and bioeconomy stakeholders, facilitating collaborative experiments and proposal development, providing mentorship from senior CRI researchers, and offering tailored professional development opportunities. The successful candidate will be expected to: Develop a nationally and internationally recognized research program with interdisciplinary connections across the network of UT-ORII CBS Convergent Research Initiative. Establish innovative approaches to synthesizing bio-based materials within circular bioeconomy systems. Undertake chemical syntheses such as chain-end modification of bio-derived renewable building blocks and synthesis of monomers from renewable resources Explore new approaches and technologies for synthesizing bio-based recyclable polymers with targeted properties using various plant-derived chemicals/building blocks, enabling the realization of a Circular Bioeconomy System. Interface with UT-ORII programs to support UT-ORII CBS CRI's mission and goals. Build partnerships and collaborations through UTK’s Institute for Advanced Materials and Manufacturing (IAMM), the Office of Research Innovation and Economic Development (ORIED), Global Energy Ecosystems (GE2), and current and future industrial partners. As a UT-ORII research faculty member, the successful candidate will collaborate with designated UT departments, researchers, and ORNL researchers. This includes conducting research, preparing and submitting proposals, supporting existing research projects, and mentoring graduate and undergraduate students. Required Qualifications: PhD in Chemistry, Polymer Science, Chemical Engineering, or closely related discipline. Research experience in areas supporting UT-ORII’s Circular Bioeconomy Systems mission, which include, but are not limited to: Synthesis of bio-derived monomers and polymers. Characterization tools and techniques to identify underlying chemical and physico-mechanical properties of preprocessed and recycled materials at various stages of the CBS loop. Chemical, biological, and/or thermochemical recycling of bio-derived polymers. Demonstrated research track record supporting the candidate’s potential to establish a nationally and internationally recognized research program aligned with UT-ORII’s mission and goals. Strong writing and communication skills and the ability to work collaboratively in a team environment. A clear path to independence as a researcher is desired, though collaboration with more senior investigators is also encouraged. This position requires the ability to be granted a site-access badge for the Oak Ridge National Laboratory. Preferred/Desired Qualifications: Experience in more than one discipline or interest in collaboration across these disciplines. Track record of funded research. Evidence of graduate and undergraduate student mentoring or the potential to mentor students successfully. Knowledge of, or experience in, conducting techno-economic analysis and life cycle assessment of preprocessing and recycling processes and technologies relevant to biorefineries and UT-ORII’s CBS CRI. Working knowledge and/or experience using artificial intelligence and machine learning tools to discover novel polymers and enable process optimization. This position will be implemented via a 3-year renewable appointment. Salary will be determined commensurate with experience. Benefits will be typical of those offered to exempt employees at the University of Tennessee. Start-up funds are available to outfit a lab, and a recurring budget is also available. Additional Information This is a 12-month, non-tenure track position. The appointment will be with the University of Tennessee, Knoxville, but may also include joint appointment at ORNL (assuming person meets ORNL requirements). The primary work location for this position is on the UTIA campus in Knoxville. The position will be implemented as an initial three-year appointment with full salary support for the first year, followed by a gradual decrease in salary coverage such that the successful applicant will receive 50% salary support in year 5 (if renewed) and is expected to receive extramural funding by year 3 or earlier. The applicant will be expected to recruit multiple graduate students by year three. A competitive startup package, including funds for graduate students and equipment, will be offered. Salary and title/rank to be determined commensurate with experience. Benefits will be typical of exempt employees at the University of Tennessee. Application reviews will begin October 15, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. All required application materials must be submitted to Interfolio at apply.interfolio.com/193294 Required Application Materials: Cover letter expressing your interest and motivations for the position Curriculum vitae Research plan (6 pages maximum) List of at least three professional references, including their complete contact information About UT-ORII Founded in 2019, the University of Tennessee-Oak Ridge Innovation Institute (UT-ORII) is a partnership between Oak Ridge National Laboratory (ORNL) and the University of Tennessee (UT). UT-ORII aims to maintain the U.S.'s leadership in innovation and discovery while creating a robust talent pipeline in national need and demand areas. Funded by the Department of Energy and the State of Tennessee, UT-ORII prepares interdisciplinary energy science and technology leaders and develops a world-class workforce for industry, government, and academia. Leveraging a 75-plus-year UT-ORNL partnership, UT and ORNL have developed joint institutes, joint facilities, interdisciplinary PhD programs, and comprehensive joint faculty arrangements, including 17 Governor’s Chairs recruited for their significant impact in their fields. UT-ORII aims to become a center for convergent research and talent development to help maintain U.S. prominence as a global innovation leader and provide tangible benefits to Tennessee. About the University of Tennessee Since its founding in 1794, the University of Tennessee, Knoxville (UTK) has been a leading institution dedicated to academic excellence, research innovation, and public service. With over 35,000 students from diverse backgrounds and more than 1,400 faculty members, UTK provides a dynamic environment for learning and discovery. The university excels in various fields, including engineering, agriculture, business, law, and the arts and sciences. UTK's renowned research programs contribute to energy, materials science, health, and environmental sustainability advancements. As Tennessee's flagship public university, UTK fosters strong partnerships with government agencies, industries, and national laboratories, including Oak Ridge National Laboratory, driving groundbreaking research and impactful collaborations that benefit society. About Oak Ridge National Laboratory Since its establishment in 1943, Oak Ridge National Laboratory (ORNL) has been delivering scientific discoveries and technical breakthroughs needed to realize energy and national security solutions and provide economic benefits to the nation. With more than 5,800 employees representing over 60 nations, ORNL applies unique facilities, sophisticated tools, and signature strengths in neutron science, high-performance computing, advanced materials, biology, and environmental science, clean energy, fission and fusion science and engineering, isotopes, and national security research to benefit science and society. ORNL’s wide range of partnerships with other U.S. Department of Energy (DOE) laboratories, universities, and industry has enabled outstanding scientific contributions, including Nobel Prizes, the discovery of elements, and hundreds of R&D 100 Awards."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190168": {
      "title": "Professor and Head, Department of Animal Science",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: UT Institute of Agriculture: Herbert College, AgResearch, Extension: Animal Science Location: University of Tennessee, Knoxville, Tennessee POSITION SUMMARY The University of Tennessee Institute of Agriculture (UTIA) seeks applications and nominations for the position of Professor and Head of the Department of Animal Science. This is a 12-month, tenured position. We seek a dynamic and visionary leader who will foster excellence in teaching, research, and Extension programs. The department head is responsible for executing a department-developed strategic vision for the department to support UTIA’s Land Grant mission of advancing agriculture and natural resources through research, education, and service. ABOUT THE DEPARTMENT The Department of Animal Science is a diverse and fascinating blend of applied and basic life sciences focused on enhancing the efficiency, profitability, and sustainability of domestic animal industries. The department’s activities are accomplished by its 24 tenured and tenure-track faculty, 5 lecturers, and 21 professional support staff who are located on the Knoxville campus and at facilities across Tennessee. Several faculty have joint appointments with the College of Veterinary Medicine. Approximately 500 undergraduate students are enrolled in the department’s bachelor of science degree programs with academic concentrations in Animal Industries, Biosciences, Veterinary Science, and BS-MS 4+1. In addition, there are approximately 45 graduate students enrolled in the department’s MS and PhD programs. New construction on the Center for Protein Innovation (meat processing facility) and the Broiler Innovation, Research and Demonstration (BIRD) facility should be completed next year and will support the department’s land grant effort in these areas.  Faculty are also active in five recently developed centers including the UTIA Genomics Center for the Advancement of Agriculture ( https://utiagenomics.tennessee.edu/ ), Precision Livestock Farming (https://plf.tennessee.edu/ ), the University of Tennessee One Health Initiative ( https://onehealth.tennessee.edu/ ), Center for Dairy Advancement and Sustainability ( https://utdairy.tennessee.edu/ ) , and UT Beef and Forage Center ( https://utbeef.tennessee.edu/ ). Research, teaching, and Extension programs in the department serve the areas of animal health and well-being, nutrition, physiology, genomics, reproduction, immunology, environment, and precision livestock farming technology. In addition to applied animal production work, multidisciplinary and multifunctional dual biomedical and agricultural purpose (animal and human health) programs are conducted with an emphasis on infectious disease, obesity, metabolic complications, and regenerative medicine. Extensive field and animal resources are available at ten UT AgResearch Research and Education Centers across Tennessee and the Veterinary Research and Education Center. Departmental Extension personnel lead adult and youth programs in beef cattle, dairy cattle, goats, horses, poultry, sheep, swine, and environmental management. Opportunities abound to continue to form new collaborative and interdisciplinary activities with the Tennessee Department of Agriculture, the nearby Oak Ridge National Laboratory, commodity and industrial partners, other university units and regional industries. Additional information about the department is available at https://animalscience.tennessee.edu/ , and more information on the Knoxville area can be found at https://www.utk.edu/about/knoxville . JOB DESCRIPTION The Department Head’s responsibilities include leadership of all departmental programs and administrative responsibility for planning, fiscal management, human resources, and facilities. The Department Head reports to the Deans of AgResearch, UT Extension, and the Herbert College of Agriculture, working with their offices to accomplish departmental goals and objectives. The Head also maintains a working relationship with the Dean of the College of Veterinary Medicine. The Head guides the Department in its mission to deliver nationally and internationally recognized programs in teaching, research, and Extension. The Head helps articulate and promote the Department’s vision to provide high quality, relevant education and training for undergraduate and graduate students that encompasses the vision of the University of Tennessee - a world enriched by our ideas, improved through action, and inspired by the Volunteer spirit of service and leadership - thus positioning students to take their places as academic and industry leaders in the state of Tennessee and the world. The Department Head will serve as the chief executive officer of the department and will be responsible for providing dynamic, visionary leadership in the following key areas: • Strategic Leadership: Recognize the importance of all three facets of the land grant mission as well as 4H and youth livestock programs. Provide direction for the department’s teaching, research, and Extension missions, ensuring alignment with institute, and university goals. • Stakeholder Engagement: Collaborate with faculty, staff, students, administration, alumni, industry partners, advisory groups, and external constituencies to establish priorities and guide departmental growth. • Faculty and Staff Development: Recruit, support, and evaluate a diverse team of faculty and staff; lead efforts in performance management, retention, and professional development. Implement and oversee a structured faculty mentoring program for tenure-track faculty, ensuring long-term support for successful advancement. • Student Success: Oversee student recruitment, advising, and support efforts to enhance enrollment, retention, and student achievement within the department’s disciplines. • Academic Program Quality: Ensure the health, accreditation, and continuous improvement of academic programs, maintaining high standards for teaching and learning outcomes. • Resource Management: Effectively manage departmental budgets, human resources, and facilities; identify and cultivate external funding and sponsored opportunities to support program growth. • Visibility and Advocacy: Enhance the visibility of departmental programs through the promotion of faculty achievements and advocacy efforts at the institute, university, state, and national levels. • Faculty Mentorship: Implement and oversee a structured faculty mentoring program for tenure-track faculty, ensuring long-term support for successful advancement. COMPENSATION AND BENEFITS Compensation is competitive and will be based on experience and qualifications. The University of Tennessee offers a comprehensive benefits package to all regular employees, including health and dental insurance, life insurance and multiple retirement plans. Additional information can be found at https://tennessee.edu/about/divisions/finance-admin/hr/payroll-benefits-retirement/ . ABOUT THE UNIVERSITY OF TENNESSEE AND THE INSTITUTE OF AGRICULTURE: The University of Tennessee, Knoxville (UTK), is the flagship campus of the University of Tennessee System and is classified as a Carnegie R1 (Doctoral Universities – Very High Research Activity) institution. The University of Tennessee Institute of Agriculture (UTIA) provides Real. Life. Solutions. through teaching, discovery, and outreach. UTIA is committed to excellence in developing solutions to agricultural, environmental, and natural resource challenges across Tennessee and beyond. UTIA is located in Knoxville, a vibrant city surrounded by beautiful natural areas including the Great Smoky Mountains National Park, and offers an excellent quality of life. REQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS The successful candidate must: • Qualify for the rank of Professor in the Department of Animal Science with an earned PhD in Animal, Dairy or Poultry Science or a closely related field and a distinguished record of scholarly achievement. • Be able to provide visionary leadership for Extension, research, and teaching programs. • Have demonstrated experience working within a land-grant university system, while also having an understanding and interest in the Land Grant philosophy. • Skills in effective management of personnel and a desire to encourage productive teamwork • Possess strong interpersonal, communication, and decision-making skills. • Have a strong commitment to development of faculty, staff, and students and a proven ability to foster collaboration across disciplines and institutions. • Demonstrate administrative and leadership experience, including supervision of personnel, fiscal management, and strategic planning. • Articulate strategic vision and long-term goals for the Department. • Experience in and a willingness to engage in assessing priorities, strategic planning and implementation, and adaptability to change in a multidisciplinary environment. NOMINATION AND APPLICATION PROCESS The Search Advisory Committee will review and continue to receive applications until an appointment is made. Nominations of qualified individuals are sought and should be sent via electronic means to Dr. Gary Bates ( gbates@utk.edu ), the Chair of the Search Advisory Committee. Applications will be reviewed beginning September 9, 2026. Applications should include: (1) a letter of application summarizing the applicant’s qualifications and vision of departmental leadership; (2) a complete curriculum vitae; and (3) names, addresses, e-mail addresses, and telephone numbers of at least five professional references that the Search Advisory Committee may contact. Application materials should be submitted via Interfolio at https://apply.interfolio.com/190168 ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/186768": {
      "title": "Teaching Assistant/Associate Professor of Agricultural and Resource Economics, Spring 2026",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: UT Institute of Agriculture: Herbert College, AgResearch, Extension: Agricultural and Resource Economics Location: The University of Tennessee, Knoxville, TN Agribusiness Online Masters Teaching The successful candidate will be expected to: 1) coordinate and develop a fully online masters program in agricultural business; 2) teach undergraduate and graduate courses in the Department of Agricultural and Resource Economics; 3) mentor students; and 4) participate in service activities of the Department, College, University, and profession. The expected start date is January 1, 2027. The candidate requires a PhD in agricultural economics or a closely related field. Strong knowledge of the subject matter taught in graduate courses is necessary. Successful candidates must have a strong commitment to quality teaching, excellent communication skills, and a desire to teach and mentor students in digital environments as well as work with industry stakeholders, faculty, and staff. Candidates for the Assistant Professor rank should have demonstrated promise of excellence in the areas of responsibility assigned to them. For the Associate Professor rank, candidates should have demonstrated excellence in areas of responsibility assigned to them, have at least 3 years of college teaching experience, and have taught graduate-level courses. To apply, please submit a letter detailing your interest in the position, a curriculum vitae, official transcripts, and a list of three references through Interfolio https://apply.interfolio.com/186768. Direct any questions regarding the position to Dr. T. Edward Yu, Search Committee Chair, Department of Agricultural & Resource Economics, at tyu1@utk.edu . Screening of applicants will begin on August 1 and continue until the position is filled."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/185899": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate/Full-Professor - Animal Waste Management and Farm Animal Environmental Housing Control Specialist",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: UT Institute of Agriculture: Herbert College, AgResearch, Extension: Biosystems Engineering and Soil Science Location: Knoxville, TN The Extension Animal Waste Management and Farm Animal Environmental Housing Control Specialist is expected to develop a comprehensive, statewide research and Extension program focusing on both animal waste and organic residues management and environmental control of confined farm animal (poultry and livestock) housing. These areas of expertise specifically encompass waste management, land application of manure, biosolids, and other organic waste streams, livestock mortality management, and environmental controls for farm animal confinement systems that protect animal welfare and promote efficient production. This role supports public health, animal health and welfare, minimizes air and surface/groundwater pollution, and poultry and livestock production efficiency. Responsibilities · Perform needs assessments and provide technical expertise for: o efficient management of manure and other organic residuals o environmental control of confined farm animal housing systems using precision livestock farming technologies ·         Advise stakeholders on: o   manure and other organic waste collection, storage, treatment, and disposal processes (e.g., composting, rendering, anaerobic digestion, incineration) o   compliance with air and surface/groundwater pollution regulations o   environmental control of confined farm animal housing systems that ensure welfare and promote efficient production · Seek extramural funding to support an Extension outreach and education program · Seek extramural funding to support basic and applied research that supports Extension programming · Conduct research and publish findings in referred journal articles · Develop outreach programs that train Extension Agents and other stakeholders on: o appropriate manure and biological residuals management methods and strategies o environmental control of confined farm animal housing systems · Disseminate research-based solutions using a variety of formats such as Extension publications, websites, as well at field days and technical meetings · Participate in the committee and governance structure of the Department, Institute, and University, and other duties as assigned · Travel as required to fulfill statewide educational responsibilities Required Qualifications PhD in Biosystems, Biological, Agricultural, Environmental, or other closely related engineering discipline Ability to develop and implement impactful Extension programming Capacity and desire to work cooperatively in multi-disciplinary teams and the ability to obtain and administer extramural funds Ability and desire to direct graduate student research, publish articles in professional journals, and prepare educational materials in support of program area Excellent verbal, writing, and presentation skills Preferred Qualifications Experience and a strong background in manure waste management and farm animal environmental housing control engineering Registration as a Professional Engineer or eligibility for P.E. licensure in Tennessee Candidates for Assistant Professor must show promise of developing a nationally recognized Extension and research program in waste management systems and livestock environmental control technologies. Candidates for Associate Professor must have achieved a nationally recognized record in Extension and research programming in waste management systems and/or  livestock environmental control technologies. Candidates for Professor must have achieved and maintained a significant, impactful record of a nationally recognized Extension and research program in waste management systems and/or livestock environmental control technologies. Applicant screening begins September 1, 2026 and continues until the position is filled. To apply, use the position link at https://apply.interfolio.com/185899 to submit a current CV, a 1-page letter describing your interest in and qualifications for the position, official transcripts, and three letters of reference. For questions, contact Dr. Robert Burns, Search Committee Chair, at rburns@utk.edu . Work Location University of Tennessee, Knoxville Compensation and Benefits Starting Salary Based on Education & Experience · Benefits: Group hospitalization and life insurance; State Retirement; Workers’ Compensation; sick and annual leave; numerous University of Tennessee and state benefits programs, including liberal time off for holidays, and longevity pay after three years of service; reimbursement for official travel. Find more information on UT Benefits here . About The BESS Department The Department of Biosystems Engineering and Soil Science brings broad engineering and scientific expertise to issues of sustainable development and resource stewardship.  Specific topics include productive and sustainable agriculture, soil and water resource management, impacts of climate change, machines and systems for optimized performance, construction science, and fuels and value-added products from renewable resources.  We produce well-prepared and motivated graduates (BS through PhD), discover new knowledge through research, and share that knowledge by engaging with scientific communities, industries, agencies, and individuals throughout Tennessee and the world. Biosystems Engineering and Soil Science (BESS) has three-degree programs: 1) Biosystems Engineering (BSE), 2) Environmental Science (ES), and 3) Construction Science and Management (CSM) and three MS programs (BSE, ESS and Biosystems Engineering Technology) and two PhD programs (BSE and ESS).  In addition to its undergraduate and graduate teaching duties, Biosystems Engineering and Soil Science also carries out a broad range of research, from basic through applied, and transfers that technology to its clientele via a range of Extension programs and other activities. Additional Information on all postings provided by UTK About UTK With over 225 years dedicated to education and research, and as one of the 100 largest employers in Tennessee, there is endless potential to discover at the University of Tennessee, Knoxville. This advanced academic center comprises 17 degree-granting colleges and schools, 900+ programs of study, 547 graduate programs, and is home to over 7,500 employees statewide who support the continued growth of the state’s flagship land-grant university. The University of Tennessee Institute of Agriculture, Anthropological Research Facility, Space Institute, and the UT Institute of Public Service are acclaimed entities of the UT system and with nearby affiliate, Oak Ridge National Laboratory, Rocky Top is a university on the rise. UT Knoxville considers its employees its number one asset and with values that focus on work-life balance, compensation, and innovative leadership, all Vols are supported to advance professionally. Employees have access to career development and coaching, continued education, and an extensive list of development and training possibilities. The Volunteer employee experience implements structures and practices that attract and retain a diverse community and that support a culture where everyone matters and belongs. Apply today and join the Vol Family for life! Ranked nationally as “Best Employer for New Graduates”, “One of America’s Best Large Employers”, “Best Workplace for Women,” and has been designated as “Best Place for Working Parents” by Forbes Magazine."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192195": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Applied Wildlife Management",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: UT Institute of Agriculture: Herbert College, AgResearch, Extension: School of Natural Resources Location: Knoxville, Tennessee The School of Natural Resources (SNR) at the University of Tennessee Institute of Agriculture (UTIA) in Knoxville, Tennessee, invites applications for a 12-month, tenure-track faculty position as an Assistant Professor of Applied Wildlife Management, 60% Research/40% Extension. The individual must be committed to the Land Grant University mission of research, teaching, and Extension, with a strong interest in mentoring students. The candidate’s expertise should complement and expand SNR’s existing research and Extension programs.  Anticipated start date:  August 2027. The successful candidate will develop a research and Extension program focused on applied wildlife management applicable to Tennessee landscapes. Examples of research and Extension program areas may include but are not limited to:  Habitat management, population dynamics, resource selection, and movement ecology. Applicants should have excellent verbal and written communication skills, and the ability and willingness to work in an interdisciplinary environment. The successful candidate is expected to seek collaboration with state and federal agencies, non-governmental organizations, and private landowners. In addition to securing extramural funding, the successful candidate is expected to publish in peer-reviewed journals, develop and promote Extension programs in applied wildlife management, conduct in-service training for UT Extension and state agencies as appropriate, direct graduate students, mentor undergraduate students, serve on School and Institute committees, and participate in professional societies. Required Qualifications : A Ph.D. in Wildlife Management, Wildlife Ecology, or a related field, completed before the position start date, experience conducting applied wildlife management research in ecosystems in or similar to Tennessee, and an understanding of Extension and the Land Grant mission. Desired Qualifications : A strong research and publication record, success in grant writing, experience working in Extension, and a record of collaboration and cooperative research with state and federal natural resource agencies and non-government organizations. To Apply : Upload (1) a cover letter, (2) a two-page research statement that includes how you would incorporate your research into an Extension program and the programs you foresee implementing, (3) copies of transcripts of all undergraduate and graduate course work, (4) a curriculum vitae detailing educational background, work experience, publications, and grants secured, and (5) names and full contact information of at least three professional references through the University’s online job portal.   Questions regarding the position may be directed to the Search Committee Chair, Emma Willcox, ewillcox@utk.edu . Review of applications will begin December 15, 2026. Salary will be commensurate with experience."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190124": {
      "title": "Lecturer/Teaching Assistant Professor - Plant Sciences",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: UT Institute of Agriculture: Herbert College, AgResearch, Extension: Plant Sciences Location: University of Tennessee, Knoxville, Tennessee Location Knoxville, Tennessee, or hybrid/remote. Hybrid-remote employees must be able to travel to Knoxville approximately one to three times per month for activities that may include faculty meetings, committee meetings, recording instructional materials in the Digital Learning Studio, and meetings with the Office of Digital Learning. Description The Plant Sciences Department in the Herbert College of Agriculture at the University of Tennessee, Knoxville is searching for a Lecturer or Teaching Assistant Professor to fill a 9-month position in the Plant Sciences (PLSC) undergraduate program. The successful candidate will be responsible for working with the PLSC program faculty, department head, and professional advisors to deliver coursework which will primarily complement the online delivery of PLSC undergraduate curriculum. The candidate should be willing to engage in innovative and interdisciplinary teaching and program development. Primary duties include: Teaching up to 12 credit hours per semester within the PLSC undergraduate curriculum. Promoting and advancing the PLSC program through activities that strengthen online programming, enrollment, and visibility. Supporting the continued development and modernization of departmental curriculum, including the emerging Plant Sciences online minor. Mentoring undergraduate students, supporting student enrichment activities, and supporting student professional development. Contributing to the development of a nationally recognized Plant Sciences online degree program by participating in professional organizations, contributing to the scholarship of teaching and learning, and incorporating innovative instructional methods, software, and equipment that enhance student learning outcomes. Meet regularly with the Office of Digital Learning (https://digitallearning.utk.edu/). Act as the main departmental liaison to the Office of Digital Learning and VolsOnline. Record online lectures in collaboration with UTK Learning Digital Studio ( https://digitallearning.utk.edu/support/faculty-support/media-studio/ ). COMPENSATION AND BENEFITS Compensation is competitive and will be based on experience and qualifications. The University of Tennessee offers a comprehensive benefits package to all regular employees, including health and dental insurance, life insurance and multiple retirement plans. Additional information can be found at https://tennessee.edu/about/divisions/finance-admin/hr/payroll-benefits-retirement/ . ABOUT THE UNIVERSITY OF TENNESSEE AND THE INSTITUTE OF AGRICULTURE: The University of Tennessee, Knoxville (UTK), is the flagship campus of the University of Tennessee System and is classified as a Carnegie R1 (Doctoral Universities – Very High Research Activity) institution. The University of Tennessee Institute of Agriculture (UTIA) provides Real. Life. Solutions. through teaching, discovery, and outreach. UTIA is committed to excellence in developing solutions to agricultural, environmental, and natural resource challenges across Tennessee and beyond. UTIA is located in Knoxville, a vibrant city surrounded by beautiful natural areas including the Great Smoky Mountains National Park, and offers an excellent quality of life. Required Qualifications: A master's degree in Plant Sciences, Horticulture, or a closely related field is required by the time of hire for appointment as a Lecturer . A Ph.D. or other terminal degree in Plant Sciences, Horticulture, or a closely related field is required by the time of hire for appointment as a Teaching Assistant Professor . Strong communication skills and the ability to integrate information technology into course delivery. Desired Qualifications: Demonstrated experience in online or hybrid instruction or other teaching experience. Demonstrated experience developing, designing, or significantly revising online or hybrid courses using evidence-based instructional practices. Horticulture or other Plant Sciences related work experience Experience with learning management systems (e.g., Canvas) Commitment to innovative teaching Demonstrated ability to work collaboratively Experience with outreach, recruitment, or initiatives that promote academic programs Experience with curriculum development and course assessment APPLICATION PROCESS The Search Committee will begin application review and continue to receive applications until an appointment is made. Any questions regarding this position can be emailed to Dr. Andy Pulte at apulte@utk.edu , the Chair of the Search Committee. Applications will be reviewed beginning September 15, 2026 in anticipation of a start date of January 1, 2027. Applications should include: (1) a letter of application summarizing the applicant’s qualifications and experience; (2) a complete curriculum vitae/resume; and (3) names, addresses, e-mail addresses, and telephone numbers of at least five professional references that the Search Committee may contact in the final stages of the interview process. Application materials should be submitted via Interfolio at https://apply.interfolio.com/190124."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192760": {
      "title": "Teaching Faculty in Data Science",
      "description": "Posted: Sep 07, 2026 Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: College of Emerging & Collaborative Studies Location: UTK Knoxville, Tennessee The College of Emerging and Collaborative Studies (CECS) at the University of Tennessee, Knoxville's (UTK) seeks a dynamic, collaborative, and innovative faculty member to contribute to its existing and future programs in Data Science. CECS has one position open in Data Science for a non-tenure track, nine-month, full-time appointment, on campus, beginning January 1, 2027. This is an open-rank search; appointment at the Teaching Assistant Professor, Teaching Associate Professor, or Teaching Professor level will be commensurate with qualifications and experience. The selected candidate will be responsible for teaching and service, with assignments made by the dean according to enrollment demands and scheduling. Primary teaching responsibilities will include courses in Data Science spanning introductory through graduate-level offerings, as well as other new courses launched by the College. We are seeking a colleague who brings deep applied expertise in one or more data science domains and who shares our commitment to education that is hands-on, intercollegiate, and workforce-relevant. Candidates are expected to maintain a scholarship focused on practice and impact; traditional academic research is welcome but not required. Expertise in the following teaching areas is expected: Data Science: The College is especially interested in candidates whose primary strength lies in modern data engineering and MLOps, including ETL/ELT pipeline development, workflow orchestration, containerization, cloud-based data engineering, CI/CD, experiment tracking, model deployment, and monitoring and observability for data and models, as this is a current strategic priority for the program. Beyond that focus, expertise is also expected in: foundational data science concepts including data collection, management, and exploration; data stewardship, ethics, and lifecycle management; data storage, warehousing, and governance; analytical methods including statistics, machine learning, and optimization; advanced data analysis including multivariate regression, clustering, topic modeling, and time series analysis; data wrangling and preprocessing; visual analytics; programming in Python and R; version control using Git, collaborative platforms such as GitHub, and reproducible computing environments such as Jupyter; database design and SQL; and communicating data science outcomes to technical and non-technical audiences. The ideal candidate will bring the knowledge and skills to teach courses such as Applied Cloud Computing for Data Science, Fundamentals of Data Engineering, Scalable Data Mining and Analysis, Edge and IoT Data Science, and Spatial Data Science, should the program choose to offer them in the future. Key Responsibilities Teach courses spanning introductory through graduate level in Data Science, including lab-intensive and applied learning  components Develop and regularly update course materials to reflect current tools, frameworks, and industry practice Collaborate with intercollegiate program faculty to design integrative learning experiences that connect technical skills with ethical, policy, and real-world application context Advise and mentor students, including supervision of capstone projects and applied research Maintain an active applied scholarly or professional practice profile relevant to your specialization; traditional academic research is welcome but not required Contribute to program assessment, continuous improvement, and accreditation processes Participate in college governance, committees, and professional community engagement Ph.D. in Data Science, Statistics, Applied Mathematics, Computer Science or a closely related quantitative field Demonstrated expertise in one or more of the data science teaching areas listed above Evidence of effective teaching at the university level, or substantial practitioner experience with instructional or mentoring responsibilities Experience or demonstrated ability in the design and delivery of courses in multiple formats, including face-to-face, synchronous online, asynchronous online, and hybrid modalities Experience with technology-enhanced teaching and team-based instructional practices Commitment to applied, hands-on, and workforce-oriented undergraduate education Preferred Qualifications Significant professional experience in a relevant industry or applied context (highly desirable) Relevant industry certifications, where applicable Record of applied scholarship: professional publications, conference presentations, tool or open-source software development, or practice-based projects Exp erience developing or delivering simulation-based, lab-intensive, or capstone learning experiences Demonstrated ability or interest in teaching across disciplinary boundaries (e.g., data science and applied AI, or data science and a domain application such as sport analytics or health data) Familiarity with curriculum development, program assessment, or accreditation processes (e.g., ABET, SACSCOC) Experience mentoring students from diverse backgrounds in technical fields Faculty Rank Criteria Appointment rank will be determined based on the following criteria: T eaching Assistant Professor – Holds a Ph.D. (or will hold at the time of appointment) in a related field with promise for excellence in teaching and related responsibilities, evidenced by early effectiveness and contributions. Teaching Associate Professor – Terminal degree with a demonstrated record of excellence in teaching and related responsibilities, with a minimum of three years of full-time teaching experience in a related field. Teaching Professor (Full) – Terminal degree with a sustained, consistent record of excellence and evidence of instructional leadership (e.g., curriculum development, mentoring, pedagogical innovation) commensurate with senior rank. Note: Applicants must be authorized to work in the United States. The college is unable to provide visa sponsorship for this position. About the College: The College of Emerging and Collaborative Studies (CECS) at the University of Tennessee, Knoxville, is at the forefront of changing the future of higher education. It is a first-of-its-kind college created to meet the needs of students seeking a customizable degree path in emerging fields such as artificial intelligence and data science that leads to rewarding careers upon graduation. CECS offers timely, innovative, student-centric degrees, minors, and stackable certificates at both undergraduate and graduate level that address the future talent gap and exposes students to experts and disciplines from across campus through cross-cutting curriculum. CECS utilizes strong industry partnerships to ensure students gain relevant skills and real-world experience, offering for-credit internships and multi-disciplinary projects. CECS emphasizes the cohort experience where students learn and interact with fellow students from across campus and disciplines, giving them the opportunity to learn from one another and work together to solve real-world problems. The University of Tennessee, Knoxville is the state's flagship institution, a campus of choice for outstanding undergraduates, and a premier graduate institution. As a land-grant university, UTK is committed to excellence in learning, scholarship, and engagement with society. Priority will be given to applications received on or before November 1 via our Interfolio application system. https://apply.interfolio.com/192760 Applications must include: 1) a one-page cover letter addressing qualifications and motivation, 2) a teaching statement, including the candidate's background and experience that make them an ideal candidate, please include teaching evaluations if available. 3) evidence of teaching effectiveness (e.g., course evaluations, performance review, or other relevant assessments) 4) a comprehensive curriculum vitae, and 5) the names and contact information (address, phone number, and e-mail address) for at least three professional references. Questions about the position should be directed to CECS Senior Director of Academic Operations, Elis Vllasi, email: evllasi@utk.edu . Positions to be filled as soon as possible. To apply go to https://apply.interfolio.com/192760"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192909": {
      "title": "Teaching Faculty in Applied Artificial Intelligence",
      "description": "Posted: Sep 07, 2026 Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: College of Emerging & Collaborative Studies Location: UTK Knoxville, Tennessee The College of Emerging and Collaborative Studies (CECS) at the University of Tennessee, Knoxville's (UTK) seeks a dynamic, collaborative, and innovative faculty member to contribute to its existing and future programs in Artificial Intelligence. CECS has one position open in Artificial Intelligence for a non-tenure track, nine-month, full-time appointment, on campus, beginning January 1, 2027. This is an open-rank search; appointment at the Teaching Assistant Professor, Teaching Associate Professor, or Teaching Professor level will be commensurate with qualifications and experience. The selected candidate will be responsible for teaching and service, with assignments made by the dean according to enrollment demands and scheduling. Primary teaching responsibilities will include courses in Artificial Intelligence spanning introductory through graduate-level offerings, as well as other new courses launched by the College. We are seeking a colleague who brings deep applied expertise in one or more artificial intelligence domains and who shares our commitment to education that is hands-on, intercollegiate, and workforce-relevant. Candidates are expected to maintain a scholarship focused on practice and impact; traditional academic research is welcome but not required. Expertise in the following teaching areas is expected: Artificial Intelligence: The College is especially interested in candidates whose primary strength lies in generative AI and large language model engineering, agentic AI and multi-agent systems, AI systems evaluation and safety, or AI forward deployed engineering — including retrieval-augmented generation, LLM fine-tuning, agent orchestration and tool use, prompt engineering, monitoring and evaluation of production AI systems (robustness testing, red-teaming, and hallucination detection), and the applied, client-facing deployment of AI solutions in real-world settings — as these are current strategic priorities for the program. Beyond that focus, expertise is also expected in: introductory artificial intelligence concepts and applications; natural language processing and conversational AI; human-AI interaction and user experience design; AI-based data handling, preprocessing, and visualization; and programming in Python using frameworks such as Scikit-learn, TensorFlow, and PyTorch. The ideal candidate will bring the knowledge and skills to teach courses such as Agentic AI and Multi-Agent Systems, Generative AI and Large Language Model Engineering, AI Systems Evaluation and Safety, and AI Forward Deployed Engineering, should the program choose to offer them in the future. Key Responsibilities Teach courses spanning introductory through graduate level in Artificial Intelligence, including lab-intensive and applied learning components Develop and regularly update course materials to reflect current tools, frameworks, and industry practice Collaborate with intercollegiate program faculty to design integrative learning experiences that connect technical skills with ethical, policy, and real-world application contexts Advise and mentor students, including supervision of capstone projects and applied research Maintain an active applied scholarly or professional practice profile relevant to your specialization; traditional academic research is welcome but not required Contribute to program assessment, continuous improvement, and accreditation processes Participate in college governance, committees, and professional community engagement Ph.D. in Artificial Intelligence, Computer Science, Data Science, or a closely related field Demonstrated expertise in one or more of the artificial intelligence teaching areas listed above Evidence of effective teaching at the university level, or substantial practitioner experience with instructional or mentoring responsibilities Experience or demonstrated ability in the design and delivery of courses in multiple formats, including face-to-face, synchronous online, asynchronous online, and hybrid modalities Experience with technology-enhanced teaching and team-based instructional practices Commitment to applied, hands-on, and workforce-oriented undergraduate education Preferred Qualifications Significant professional experience in a relevant industry or applied context (highly desirable) Relevant industry certifications, where applicable Record of applied scholarship: professional publications, conference presentations, tool or open-source software development, or practice-based projects Experience developing or delivering simulation-based, lab-intensive, or capstone learning experiences Demonstrated ability or interest in teaching across disciplinary boundaries (e.g., applied AI and data science, or applied AI and a domain application such as cybersecurity or health data) F amiliarity with curriculum development, program assessment, or accreditation processes (e.g., ABET, SACSCOC) Experience mentoring students from diverse backgrounds in technical fields Faculty Rank Criteria Appointment rank will be determined based on the following criteria: Teaching Assistant Professor – Holds a Ph.D. (or will hold at the time of appointment) in a related field with promise for excellence in teaching and related responsibilities, evidenced by early effectiveness and contributions. Teaching Associate Professor – Terminal degree with a demonstrated record of excellence in teaching and related responsibilities, with a minimum of three years of full-time teaching experience in a related field. Teaching Professor (Full) – Terminal degree with a sustained, consistent record of excellence and evidence of instructional leadership (e.g., curriculum development, mentoring, pedagogical innovation) commensurate with senior rank. Note: Applicants must be authorized to work in the United States. The college is unable to provide visa sponsorship for this position. About the College : The College of Emerging and Collaborative Studies (CECS) at the University of Tennessee, Knoxville, is at the forefront of changing the future of higher education. It is a first-of-its-kind college created to meet the needs of students seeking a customizable degree path in emerging fields such as artificial intelligence and data science that leads to rewarding careers upon graduation. CECS offers timely, innovative, student-centric degrees, minors, and stackable certificates at both undergraduate and graduate level that address the future talent gap and exposes students to experts and disciplines from across campus through cross-cutting curriculum. CECS utilizes strong industry partnerships to ensure students gain relevant skills and real-world experience, offering for-credit internships and multi-disciplinary projects. CECS emphasizes the cohort experience where students learn and interact with fellow students from across campus and disciplines, giving them the opportunity to learn from one another and work together to solve real-world problems. The University of Tennessee, Knoxville is the state's flagship institution, a campus of choice for outstanding undergraduates, and a premier graduate institution. As a land-grant university, UTK is committed to excellence in learning, scholarship, and engagement with society. Priority will be given to applications received on or before October 15 via our Interfolio application system.  https://apply.interfolio.com/192909 Applications must include: 1)       a one-page cover letter addressing qualifications and motivation, 2)       a teaching statement, including the candidate's background and experience that make them an ideal candidate, please include teaching evaluations if available. 3)       evidence of teaching effectiveness (e.g., course evaluations, performance review, or other relevant assessments), 4)       a comprehensive curriculum vitae, and 5)       the names and contact information (address, phone number, and e-mail address) for at least three professional references. Questions about the position should be directed to CECS Senior Director of Academic Operations, Elis Vllasi, email: evllasi@utk.edu . Positions to be filled as soon as possible. To apply go to https://apply.interfolio.com/192909"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190471": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Counselor Education School Counseling, Fall 2027",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: College of Education, Health, and Human Sciences: Counseling, Human Development, & Family Science Location: Knoxville The Department of Counseling, Human Development, and Family Science (CHDFS) at the University of Tennessee, Knoxville, invites applicants for a 9-month, tenure-track Assistant Professor position in Counselor Education with a specialization in school counseling. The position has a start date of August 1, 2027. The CHDFS mission is to promote optimal development and holistic well-being across the lifespan through research and practice that honors the complexities of individuals, families, and communities. We strive to achieve our mission by (a) conducting high-quality scientific research to advance our disciplines and inform practice and policy; (b) developing professionals who possess knowledge and skills to work alongside individuals, families, and communities; and (c) engaging with campus and community partners to design and deliver culturally responsive services grounded in research and theory. We value systemic and equity-oriented approaches that embrace the richness of human experiences. CHDFS currently has 19 full-time faculty members, including tenure-line and non-tenure-track faculty. CHDFS is home to programs in Counselor Education (MS, PhD) and Human Development and Family Science (BS, MS, PhD). Our CACREP-accredited programs are offered in a full-time, face-to-face, cohort-style format. We have growing undergraduate offerings in Counseling and Human Services and Addiction Counseling. To learn more about CHDFS and Counselor Education, please visit https://chdfs.utk.edu. We are housed in the College of Education, Health, and Human Sciences (CEHHS) – a dynamic college with exceptional investments in basic, translational, health-oriented, and community-engaged research and extramural funding support. Responsibilities Pursue and maintain an active program of research in professional counseling, leading to pursuit of external funding to advance the research program by the end of the probationary period Teach two graduate-level counselor education courses each fall and spring semester Actively participate as a core faculty member through advisement, mentorship, and engagement in program-level processes; serve on program, department, or college committees as needed Maintain requirements for serving as a core faculty member in the School Counseling specialized practice area as defined in CACREP 2024 Standard 1.AA. We seek candidates whose scholarship will meaningfully contribute to the CHDFS mission and values and to the UT System Aspirations ( https://tennessee.edu/statement-of-aspirations/ ) Required Qualifications Earned doctoral degree in counselor education (e.g., counseling, counselor education, counselor education and practice, counselor education and supervision), preferably from a CACREP-accredited program, no later than August 31, 2027 Counseling experience as a school counselor Evidence of a developing program of research relevant to professional counseling Demonstrated success or potential for successful teaching and supervising master’s-level counseling students or counselors Licensure and/or certifications relevant to the school counseling specialized practice area Desired Qualifications Two or more years of experience as a professional school counselor holding an active license Depth of professional experience in public, PK-12 educational settings beyond school counseling (e.g., teaching, administration, student services, or other school-based roles) Evidence of submitting research to high-impact outlets and seeking internal and external funding to support research Advanced research methodological expertise (e.g., quantitative, qualitative, mixed methods) Ability to teach school counseling specialty courses along with a range of core courses customized to the needs of professional counseling students in general Professional counseling dispositions including ability to work collegially in a collaborative team environment with strong professional counseling identity Review of applications will begin September 25, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. To apply, submit (1) a cover letter that emphasizes your overall qualifications, highlights your program of research, and describes your teaching and clinical perspectives (not to exceed three single-spaced pages), (2) curriculum vita, and (3) 1-page document listing the names and contact information for three references to https://apply.interfolio.com/190356 . Questions can be addressed to the Search Committee Chair, Dr. Jordan Westcott ( jbwestcott@utk.edu ). Applications received outside of Interfolio will not be considered."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190476": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Counselor Education CMHC or Community-Based Settings, Fall 2027",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: College of Education, Health, and Human Sciences: Counseling, Human Development, & Family Science Location: Knoxville The Department of Counseling, Human Development, and Family Science (CHDFS) at the University of Tennessee, Knoxville, invites applicants for a 9-month, tenure-track Assistant Professor position in Counselor Education with a specialization in a community-based setting such as Addiction, Clinical Mental Health, Clinical Rehabilitation, and/or Marriage, Couple, and Family Counseling. The position has a start date of August 1, 2027. The CHDFS mission is to promote optimal development and holistic well-being across the lifespan through research and practice that honors the complexities of individuals, families, and communities. We strive to achieve our mission by (a) conducting high-quality scientific research to advance our disciplines and inform practice and policy; (b) developing professionals who possess knowledge and skills to work alongside individuals, families, and communities; and (c) engaging with campus and community partners to design and deliver culturally responsive services grounded in research and theory. We value systemic and equity-oriented approaches that embrace the richness of human experiences. CHDFS currently has 19 full-time faculty members, including tenure-line and non-tenure-track faculty. CHDFS is home to programs in Counselor Education (MS, PhD) and Human Development and Family Science (BS, MS, PhD). Our CACREP-accredited programs are offered in a full-time, face-to-face, cohort style format. We have growing undergraduate offerings in Counseling and Human Services and Addiction Counseling. To learn more about CHDFS and Counselor Education, please visit https://chdfs.utk.edu. We are housed in the College of Education, Health, and Human Sciences (CEHHS) – a dynamic college with exceptional investments in basic, translational, health-oriented, and community-engaged research and extramural funding support. Responsibilities Pursue and maintain an active program of research in professional counseling, leading to pursuit of external funding to advance the research program by the end of the probationary period Teach two graduate-level counselor education courses each fall and spring semester Actively participate as a core faculty member through advisement, mentorship, and engagement in program-level processes; serve on program, department, or college committees as needed Maintain requirements for serving as a core faculty member as defined in CACREP 2024 Standards 1.Y and 1.Z We seek candidates whose scholarship will meaningfully contribute to the CHDFS mission and values and to the UT System Aspirations ( https://tennessee.edu/statement-of-aspirations/ ) Required Qualifications Earned doctoral degree in counselor education (e.g., counseling, counselor education, counselor education and practice, counselor education and supervision), preferably from a CACREP-accredited program, no later than August 31, 2027 Counseling experience in a clinical or community setting Evidence of a developing program of research relevant to professional counseling Demonstrated success or potential for successful teaching and supervising master’s-level counseling students or counselors Licensure and/or professional counseling certifications relevant to specialized practice area Desired Qualifications Specialization in couple, marriage, and/or family counseling, clinical rehabilitation counseling, and/or adolescent mental health Two or more years of experience as a licensed professional counselor (or equivalent) in a clinical or community-based setting with active license Evidence of submitting research to high-impact outlets and seeking internal and external funding to support research Ability to teach a range of core courses customized to the needs of professional counseling students in general, alongside specialized clinical courses Advanced research methodological expertise (e.g., quantitative, qualitative, mixed methods) Professional counseling dispositions including ability to work collegially in a collaborative team environment with strong professional counseling identity Review of applications will begin October 2, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. To apply, submit (1) a cover letter that emphasizes your overall qualifications, highlights your program of research, and describes your teaching and clinical perspectives (not to exceed three single-spaced pages), (2) curriculum vita, and (3) 1-page document listing the names and contact information for three references to apply.interfolio.com/190476 . Questions can be addressed to the Search Committee Chair, Dr. Jordan Westcott ( jbwestcott@utk.edu ). Applications received outside of Interfolio will not be considered."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/189172": {
      "title": "Biomedical Engineering Assistant/Associate Professor, Fall 2027, 2 positions available",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: Tickle College of Engineering: Biomedical Engineering Location: Knoxville, Tennessee The Department of Biomedical Engineering (BME) at the University of Tennessee, Knoxville (UTK) is hiring multiple tenure-track faculty at the Assistant and Associate Professor levels. Qualified applicants will be considered for tenure at the Associate Professor rank. This is a unique opportunity to help shape the identity of the BME department, expand its research strengths, and enrich its academic programs. The BME department is central to the university’s strategic priority in health and wellness to expand high-demand educational opportunities. The new faculty hires will directly contribute to the college’s goals in student enrollment, research innovation, workforce development, and external partnerships. We seek candidates whose research complements and expands BME and UTK’s current strengths in biomechanics, imaging, prosthetics, cardiovascular and neurological diseases, biomaterials, and robotics. Areas of interest include all aspects of biomedical engineering, including but not limited to drug delivery, advanced biomanufacturing, and new approach methodologies (NAMs). Preferred candidates should have a strong record of research accomplishment, a clear vision for building collaborative and interdisciplinary research programs, and a commitment to excellence in both undergraduate and graduate education. Applicants must hold a Ph.D. in Biomedical Engineering or a closely related field. Candidates should demonstrate excellence in research through a strong publication record and show potential for leadership in their area of study. All candidates are expected to have strong communication skills and the potential to engage in multidisciplinary and collaborative research. All candidates must also demonstrate eligibility to work in the United States. About the Department The University of Tennessee, Knoxville (UTK) has made biomedical research and human health a core institutional priority, supported through strong partnerships with the University of Tennessee Medical Center (UTMC). This collaboration provides a rich environment for translational research, where engineering innovation connects directly with clinical expertise to advance diagnostics, therapeutics, medical devices, and healthcare technologies. The University of Tennessee College of Veterinary Medicine offers additional collaboration opportunities and support for translational biomedical research through access to modernized animal housing facilities and histopathology services. Faculty in Biomedical Engineering will also benefit from access to UTK’s Clinical Core Facility Program and the Center for Precision Health (CPH), which provide state-of-the-art resources and infrastructure to support interdisciplinary research and accelerate the translation of discoveries from the laboratory to patient care. UTK is also a key partner in the Neuroscience Network of East Tennessee, NeuroNET, which fosters and supports cutting-edge research and training in brain sciences in collaboration with the UT Medical Center and the Oak Ridge National Laboratory. These shared facilities and programs foster collaboration across engineering, medicine, and public health, offering faculty unique opportunities to expand their research impact. BME at UTK benefits from strong institutional support, including new faculty hires and ongoing updates to the curriculum and facilities. The 2025–26 academic year marked BME’s first year as an independent department. For the prior 15 years, biomedical engineering had been administratively grouped with Mechanical and Aerospace Engineering. As BME enters its second year as a standalone department, it has experienced record growth, including an all-time-high freshman class of 165 students enrolled for fall 2026. The department recently welcomed three new faculty members engaged in high-impact research and is on track to nearly double its faculty size over the next three years. Faculty hired through these new positions will play an important role in shaping the trajectory of this young department and advancing BME at UTK toward becoming a prominent biomedical engineering program. About UT Knoxville and Tickle College of Engineering (TCE) The Tickle College of Engineering (TCE) is in the midst of an unprecedented period of growth and success, including adding over 30 new faculty at all levels to the college over 4 years as part of ambitious hiring campaigns led by Chancellor Donde Plowman and Dean Matthew Mench. The college has set records in research expenditures, enrollment, incoming student GPA, intellectual property development, and USNWR rank in the past three years. Key partnerships with Oak Ridge National Lab (ORNL)  through the University of Tennessee Oak Ridge Innovation Institute (UTORII) and Consolidated Nuclear Security (CNS) that manages the Y-12 National Security Complex have created an expanded opportunity space for faculty and students in the region.   New facilities include the state-of-the art Zeanah Engineering Complex, the University of Tennessee Manufacturing and Design Enterprise (TN-MADE) facility, and the Innovation South building that houses UTK’s Fibers and Composites Manufacturing Facility (FCMF), a National Prototype Research Center (NSPC) as part of a partnership with CNS Y-12 under construction, and new facilities for the Biomedical Engineering department and a quantum foundry in planning stages. TCE currently has 213 tenured/tenure track and over 80 non-tenure track faculty in its nine academic departments and offers 11 undergraduate, 16 MS, and 15 PhD/DE degree programs. Affiliated with TCE and located in Tullahoma, Tennessee, the UT Space Institute is a hub of aerospace and defense research. The college is also home to eight research centers and three interdisciplinary institutes. With approximately 4,800 undergraduate and 1,500 graduate students, the college sits 28th among public universities in the most recent U.S. News and World Report graduate rankings. Faculty in the college have won 33 early career awards (NSF, DOE, DARPA, AFOSR, AHA, and Sloan) since 2016. In FY25, the college had annual research expenditures of $113.9M. Knoxville, TN is a vibrant city with a beautiful and walkable downtown, active neighborhoods, an effervescent nightlife that includes numerous theaters and museums, a rich live music scene across all genres, and eclectic restaurants. Knoxville is nestled in the foothills of the Great Smoky Mountains, surrounded by lakes and the Tennessee River, providing amazing access to various outdoor activities. Knoxville is within easy driving distance of four major metropolitan areas that regularly host larger cultural and entertainment events. For the Assistant Professor position, the successful candidate will be expected to establish and maintain a vigorous, externally funded research program in their area of expertise. Additional responsibilities include teaching undergraduate and graduate courses, mentoring students in research, and contributing to departmental, university, and professional service activities. For the Associate Professor position, primary consideration will be given to applicants who have established a nationally recognized research program, demonstrated success in teaching and mentoring students, and articulated a compelling vision for future research at UTK. Candidates should also have a record of publishing in high-impact journals, securing and managing externally funded research from federal and/or industrial sponsors, and engaging in meaningful service to their professional community. Applications should include: (1) a cover letter articulating the applicant’s interest in and qualifications for this position, (2) a curriculum vitae, (3) a research statement outlining previous work and future directions, up to three pages (4) a teaching and mentoring statement, up to two pages (5) up to three representative publications, and (6) the names of three to five references. Evaluation of applications will begin November 1, 2026 and continue on a rolling basis. Any questions should be sent to Dr. Jindong Tan (search committee chair) at bmeadmin@utk.edu ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/189167": {
      "title": "Associate/Full Professor of Digital Health, Fall 2027",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: College of Education, Health, and Human Sciences: Nutrition and Public Health Sciences Location: Knoxville The College of Education, Health, and Human Sciences (CEHHS) at The University of Tennessee, Knoxville (UTK) is seeking applicants for a tenure-track associate or full professor of digital health to join the Department of Nutrition and Public Health Sciences (NPHS) for a nine-month position beginning August 1, 2027. Digital health generally involves advanced information and communication technologies (e.g., online and mobile applications, wearable sensors, computing platforms, connectivity, software, and machine learning/AI) to assess, intervene upon, and optimize human health behaviors and clinical outcomes. The successful candidate will be a nationally recognized scholar in the field and will hold a Gary and Becki Blauser endowed professorship position that provides additional support for high-quality scholarship. We seek a preeminent scholar who will contribute to the advancement of the department’s reputation in research and who will provide leadership in research and training in the area of digital health. Additionally, under the leadership of Dr. J. Graham Thomas, UTK is launching the Center for Applied Digital Health and Optimization Methods, which will enhance the university’s national footprint in digital health and optimization methods. This position will help support interdisciplinary collaborations in digital health within the center, across the campus, and within the UT system. Duties and responsibilities, with a focus in the area of digital health as appropriate, include: · Sustaining a nationally recognized, high-impact research agenda demonstrated through multiple indicators, such as publications in top-tier peer-reviewed journals, acquisition of competitive external funding, invited grant reviewer for federal funding, invited presentations, and recognition by professional associations. · Enhancing UTK’s ability to be a leader in digital health research demonstrated by supporting digital health research within the UT system and collaborating with health-focused industry and community partners to engage in impactful research. · Providing mentorship and research guidance for NPHS Ph.D. students, fostering their scholarly productivity and professional development. · Mentoring junior faculty to support their scholarly productivity, external funding success, and professional advancement. · Supporting graduate programs in NPHS through high-quality teaching, research-informed advising, and curriculum innovation. · Engaging actively in professional associations at the local, state, regional, and national/international levels, including presenting research, holding leadership positions, and contributing to the advancement of the field. · Providing meaningful service contributions to the department, college, university, and broader educational and professional communities. · Collaborating with faculty, staff, and students to advance interdisciplinary research, program development, and collective scholarly initiatives. · Demonstrating a strong commitment to the research, educational, and service missions of the university, college, and department. Required qualifications include: · Earned doctorate in psychology, public health, epidemiology, biostatistics, computer science, engineering, informatics, or a related discipline in the social, behavioral, or technical sciences. · Evidence of teaching effectiveness in graduate-level programs, as demonstrated by course evaluations, teaching awards, course syllabi, or documented instructional innovations. · Documented record of interdisciplinary collaboration, as demonstrated through co-authored publications, interdisciplinary projects, or service/leadership roles. · Evidence of engagement, as demonstrated through partnerships with industry, health-focused community-partners, or other engaged partners. Required qualifications for Associate Professors include: · Meet the qualifications for appointment as an Associate Professor as defined in the University of Tennessee, Knoxville’s Faculty Handbook and the NPHS bylaws (e.g., evidence of sustained contributions in research, teaching, and service). · Established research agenda and record of scholarly productivity as demonstrated by peer-reviewed publications, book chapters, monographs, invited presentations, editorships, or leadership roles in professional associations. · Evidence of success in securing external funding, such as serving as principal investigator (PI), co-PI, or in other senior personnel roles on funded grants/contracts, receiving fellowships, or contributing substantively to externally supported projects. · Evidence of mentoring doctoral and/or postdoctoral students documented by co-authored publications and dissertation supervision or serving as a mentor on a postdoctoral fellowship or career development award. Required qualifications for Full Professors include: · Meet the qualifications for appointment as a Full Professor as defined in the University of Tennessee, Knoxville’s Faculty Handbook and the NPHS bylaws (e.g., distinguished record of research, teaching, and service). · Extensive record of scholarly productivity as demonstrated by a sustained pattern of peer-reviewed publications, books or edited volumes, invited keynote addresses, leadership roles in professional associations, and other indicators of national/international recognition. · Extensive record of securing external funding, including documented experience serving as principal investigator (PI) or lead investigator on major federal, foundation, or state grants/contracts, with evidence of effective funded research project management. · Documented record of mentoring doctoral and/or postdoctoral students and junior faculty toward successful scholarly careers, as evidenced by co-authored publications, dissertation supervision or serving as a mentor on a postdoctoral fellowship or career development award, external grant collaborations, and the professional placement/advancement of mentees. Preferred qualifications include: · Experience with distance learning and innovative teaching methodologies, including hybrid and online course design and delivery. · Demonstrated ability to work collegially and collaboratively with faculty, staff, students, and community partners. · Demonstrated record of leadership within their department or college, and through active leadership roles in professional associations at the regional, national, and international levels. · Expertise in one or more technical aspects of digital health research (e.g., ecological momentary assessment, wearable sensors, machine learning/AI, electronic health record integration, geospatial tracking, virtual/augmented reality). Application Process Interested applicants should submit: 1. A letter of interest outlining how the applicant meets the stated qualifications; 2. A current curriculum vita; 3. Up to three representative publications/writing samples; and 4. Contact information for three references. These materials must be submitted electronically via the Interfolio portal. General inquiries may be directed to Dr. Hollie Raynor, search committee chair, at hraynor@utk.edu. Review of materials will begin on October 15, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. CEHHS is a multidisciplinary unit with 126 tenured/tenure-track faculty members housed in six departments. NPHS is a thriving department within CEHHS, currently with more than 18 full-time faculty, 237 graduate students, and 418 undergraduate students. Located near the beautiful Great Smoky Mountains National Park, The University of Tennessee, Knoxville (UTK) was founded in 1794 and is one of the oldest public universities in the country. UTK is a vibrant and diverse urban campus, located in the heart of Knoxville. It is the flagship campus for the UT system, a participating institution of the Association of Public and Land-grant Universities (APLU), and officially designated as a Research (Very High Research Activity) and Community Engagement University by the Carnegie Foundation. For more information about UTK, please visit: https://alumni.utk.edu/points-of-pride/#top."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194553": {
      "title": "Assistant or Associate Professor in Health Services Research and Healthcare Innovation - Fall 2027",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: College of Education, Health, and Human Sciences: Nutrition and Public Health Sciences Location: Knoxville, TN The Department of Nutrition and Public Health Sciences at the University of Tennessee, Knoxville invites applications for a nine-month, tenure-track faculty position at the Assistant or Associate Professor level. This position is part of an interdisciplinary faculty research cluster developed in partnership with River Valley Health (RVH), a large federally qualified health center (FQHC) network serving more than 60,000 patients annually across more than 20 clinical sites in East Tennessee. RVH provides comprehensive primary care and integrated health services to a diverse patient population, including patients from rural, underserved, and medically vulnerable communities. The scale and reach of the RVH network provide an exceptional environment for embedded research addressing chronic disease prevention and management, healthcare delivery, implementation, and population health. The RVH research cluster is designed to establish a sustained, collaborative research enterprise between the University of Tennessee and RVH . Cluster faculty work closely with RVH leadership, clinicians, staff, patients, and other partners to identify health-system and patient priorities; develop and implement interventions within real-world clinical settings; leverage clinical, patient-generated, and other health data; and evaluate outcomes at the patient, provider, clinic, and health-system levels. RVH is the primary healthcare partner and organizing research environment for the cluster, and most of the faculty members’ research effort is expected to be devoted to collaborative research conducted with RVH and the other cluster faculty. The cluster is supported by a dedicated Project Coordinator who facilitates research activities across the UT–RVH partnership, including coordination with RVH clinics and personnel, study implementation, participant recruitment and data collection, and other operational needs of collaborative projects. This shared infrastructure is intended to allow cluster faculty to efficiently develop and conduct multi-site, practice-based research across the RVH network and to support the growth of a sustained portfolio of externally funded research. We seek an innovative scholar whose research focuses on health services research, healthcare delivery and innovation, clinical or population health informatics, implementation science, learning health systems, or related areas. The successful candidate will join an interdisciplinary cluster with existing strengths in nutrition, digital health and technology, physical activity, behavioral intervention development, and implementation science. A central goal of this hire is to add expertise that complements, rather than substantially duplicates , the existing cluster. Candidates may conduct research involving nutrition, physical activity, obesity, cardiometabolic health, or related behavioral outcomes, but their primary scholarly contribution should extend the cluster into areas such as healthcare delivery, clinical systems, health services research, informatics, pragmatic trials, learning health systems, clinical workflow, organizational change, or other areas not already represented by the current faculty. The new faculty member will strengthen the cluster’s capacity to integrate research into healthcare delivery, leverage electronic health records and other real-world health data, evaluate interventions within routine clinical care, and study outcomes at the patient, provider, clinic, and health-system levels. Candidates with clinical backgrounds who can bridge public health research with clinical operations and healthcare delivery are particularly encouraged to apply. The cluster is intended to function as an integrated research team rather than as a collection of independent investigators using RVH primarily as a recruitment or data source (i.e., a Team -Science appr oach) . Faculty are expected to collaboratively develop a coordinated portfolio of externally funded research that addresses priorities identified with RVH while producing generalizable knowledge relevant to FQHCs, primary care, rural health, and other safety-net healthcare systems. Areas of research expertise may include: Health services and healthcare delivery research Healthcare innovation and practice transformation Population health and learning health systems Clinical or population health informatics Electronic health records and other real-world clinical data Pragmatic and embedded clinical trials Dissemination and implementation science Clinical workflow and organizational change Health information technology, interoperability, and clinical decision support Patient-generated health data, remote monitoring, and digital health Integration of evidence-based interventions into routine clinical care Healthcare-community linkages and community resource referral Prevention and management of cardiometabolic and other chronic diseases Food i s Medicine and interventions addressing health-related social needs Rural health, primary care, FQHCs, and safety-net healthcare delivery We particularly encourage candidates whose scholarship bridges public health and clinical care to apply. Clinical training, licensure, or credentials that support meaningful engagement and embedded research within healthcare delivery settings are strongly preferred. Relevant clinical backgrounds may include nursing or advanced practice nursing, medicine, psychology, pharmacy, or other health professions. Candidates with nonclinical backgrounds may also be considered if they demonstrate substantial expertise conducting embedded health services research and working collaboratively within healthcare systems. Clinical or professional training in nutrition, physical activity, behavioral health, or other related areas is not exclusionary; however, the successful candidate should bring a primary area of expertise that clearly complements the existing cluster and expands its scientific and methodological capabilities. The successful candidate will be expected to work closely with the other faculty and dedicated Project Coordinator in the RVH research cluster to develop a coordinated and externally funded research portfolio; establish sustained working relationships with RVH leadership, clinicians, staff, and patients; contribute complementary expertise in healthcare delivery, clinical systems, informatics, health services research, and/or real-world data; and participate in the development, implementation, and evaluation of interventions across the RVH network. The faculty member will also collaborate with colleagues across the University of Tennessee in nutrition, public health, kinesiology, health sciences, informatics, and related disciplines; mentor undergraduate and graduate students; and contribute to the Department’s teaching mission in areas aligned with the candidate’s expertise. Required of all candidates: Doctoral degree in public health, health services research, health policy or management, clinical or biomedical informatics, nursing, psychology, medicine, pharmacy, or a closely related field Evidence of a program of scholarship relevant to health services research, healthcare delivery, healthcare innovation, population health, clinical informatics, implementation science, or related areas A research program that provides expertise complementary to the existing cluster strengths in nutrition, digital health, physical activity, behavioral interventions, or implementation science Demonstrated interest in conducting collaborative, embedded research within healthcare delivery systems Ability and willingness to work as part of an interdisciplinary faculty research team and in sustained partnership with a healthcare organization Commitment to high-quality teaching and mentorship For appointment at the Assistant Professor level: Evidence of a developing independent research program Strong potential to obtain external research funding appropriate to career stage For appointment at the Associate Professor level: Established independent program of research with a sustained record of scholarly productivity Demonstrated success in obtaining external research funding Record of research, teaching, and mentorship appropriate for appointment at the Associate Professor level Preferred Qualifications Experience conducting research embedded within primary care or healthcare delivery systems Experience working with FQHCs, safety-net healthcare systems, rural populations, or other community-based healthcare organizations Expertise using electronic health record, clinical, administrative, or other real-world healthcare data for research Experience with pragmatic trials, implementation studies, quality improvement, or learning health system approaches Expertise that expands the cluster into healthcare delivery, clinical systems, informatics, health services research, organizational change, or another complementary area Research addressing chronic disease prevention and management, cardiometabolic health, or health-related social needs Experience integrating digital health, patient-generated health data, clinical decision support, or other technologies into healthcare delivery Clinical licensure or eligibility for clinical credentialing within healthcare settings Demonstrated ability to build research collaboratively with healthcare-system leaders, clinicians, staff, patients, and community partners Review of applications will begin on November 1, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. Individuals interested in applying for this position should submit electronically at https://apply.interfolio.com/194553 a letter of application outlining how the candidate fits the qualifications and expectations of the position, curriculum vita, and list of three references who can address the applicant’s capabilities with complete addresses, phone numbers, and email addresses. Questions can be directed to Dr. Jared McGuirt, search committee chair, at jmcguirt@utk.edu."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195980": {
      "title": "Assistant Director / Assistant Professor of Practice in Music Business and Music Communications, Natalie L. Haslam College of Music",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: Natalie L. Haslam College of Music Location: Knoxville, TN Posted: Oct 20, 2026 The Natalie L. Haslam College of Music at the University of Tennessee invites an accomplished music industry professional and educator to join its faculty as Assistant Director / Assistant Professor of Practice in Music Business and Music Communications, beginning on 1/1/2027 with flexibility for a later start date. The successful candidate will teach undergraduate courses such as Music Business, Music Publishing, Music Rights Management, Marketing/Promotions, as well as courses within the candidate’s expertise. The successful candidate will bring a demonstrated record of professional achievement in the music industry, a commitment to student mentorship, and a vision for connecting contemporary industry practices with impactful career-focused academic programming. Key responsibilities include collaborating and supporting admissions to our BA programs in Music Business Administration, Music Advertising & Public Relations, and Music Journalism and Media; bridging academic concepts with practical applications through case studies, industry-based projects, guest speakers, and creative learning opportunities; and contributing to strategic planning, program assessment, curriculum innovation, and initiatives that strengthen academic quality and professional relevance. Working collaboratively with faculty, university leadership, students, and industry partners, the Assistant Director will help advance the Music Business and Music Communications programs through curriculum development, experiential learning, internship expansion, and sustainable organizational growth. The ideal candidate has a background in music with demonstrated expertise in the music industry, with experience in areas such as A & R, Production Music, Record Label Operations, Booking Agencies, Management, Marketing, Distribution, DSPS/Streaming Structures, Synch/Licensing, Copyright Law, PROs/Public Performance, MLC/Digital Mechanicals, SoundExchange/Digital Performance, and CMOs/Neighboring Rights. The successful candidate will be an innovative educator, collaborative leader, and engaged industry professional who values academic excellence, practical learning, and the development of emerging talent. The Assistant Director will bring intellectual curiosity, entrepreneurial initiative, cultural awareness, and a forward-looking understanding of the music business and communications landscape. The anticipated workload may also include service activities relevant to these programs. This is a full-time, non-tenure-track appointment at the rank of an Assistant Director/ Assistant Professor of Practice, beginning spring 2027 with flexibility for a later start date. Web Page: www.music.utk.edu The University of Tennessee Natalie L. Haslam College of Music offers a broad and comprehensive professional music program at both the undergraduate and graduate levels and is an accredited institutional member of the National Association of Schools of Music. The College has approximately 65 faculty members and approximately 400 music majors. General Information: The University of Tennessee, Knoxville, is the state’s flagship research institution, a campus of choice for outstanding undergraduates, and a premier graduate institution. As a land-grant university, it is committed to excellence in learning, scholarship, and engagement with society. In all its activities, the university aims to advance the frontiers of human knowledge and enrich and elevate society. Knoxville’s cultural resources include a symphony orchestra, an opera company, a professional jazz orchestra, theatrical productions, ballet and modern dance, art galleries, and museums. Within an hour’s drive is the Great Smoky Mountains National Park with its scenic beauty and wide variety of recreational activities, plants, and wildlife. The Knoxville campus of the University of Tennessee encourages candidates who have the ability to contribute in meaningful ways to the access and engagement goals of the University. Contact ahuntoon@utk.edu with any questions. Required Qualifications Master's degree or equivalent professional degree in a related field, including but not limited to music, or commensurate professional experience. National and/or international career experience in the music industry, with evidence of an extensive industry network. Commitment to effective instruction and instruction methods in relation to the business of music. Strong interpersonal and communication skills Preferred Qualifications Broad experience in the music industry including knowledge of current and best practices in legal matters, label management, publishing/licensing, distribution, and/or marketing. Evidence of admin or leadership experience. Evidence of direct mentorship and workforce development of emerging industry professionals. Ability to design and implement curriculum and programs, including online, in-person and hybrid. The ability to attract resources, including students, partnerships, donors, grants or other sources of support to develop the music business and communications programs. Review of applications will begin on November 4, 2026 and continue until the position is filled. Upload letter of interest in leading and building our programs, a list of at least 3 professional references, curriculum vitae, and provide a document with links to any relevant industry work."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195404": {
      "title": "Teaching Assistant Professor - Economics - Fall 2027",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: Haslam College of Business: Economics Location: The University of Tennessee Knoxville Posted: Oct 23, 2026 The Department of Economics at the University of Tennessee seeks to fill a non-tenure-track Teaching Assistant Professor position with appointment to begin August 1, 2027. We seek candidates who are dedicated to a career of exceptional teaching of economics. This position provides a path to promotion to Associate and ultimately full Teaching Professor, which entail salary increases and extended appointments. All ranks of Teaching Professors teach a portfolio of courses typically including multiple large sections (typically 200-300 students) of principles of micro and/or macro and smaller classes in fields of expertise. Responsibilities include service and a commitment to fostering the goals of the department, college, and university. Candidates must have a PhD in economics or equivalent at the time of appointment. The Department of Economics is one of seven departments within the Haslam College of Business at the University of Tennessee. The University, College and Department are growing in terms of both number of students and faculty. The department has a strong PhD program and a new accelerated Master’s program. Candidates must have a PhD in economics or equivalent at the time of appointment. To apply, please submit a letter of interest, a curriculum vitae, statement of teaching philosophy, evidence of effective teaching, sample syllabus and exam for a course previously taught, and three recommendation letters through the Interfolio system linked below. Review of applications will begin two weeks after the posting, on November 6, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. Please reach out to Dr. Scott Holladay directly with any questions, jhollad3@utk.edu."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195836": {
      "title": "Non-tenure track Lecturer or Teaching Assistant Professor, Nuclear Engineering, Fall 2027",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: Tickle College of Engineering: Nuclear Engineering Location: Knoxville, TN Posted: Oct 19, 2026 The Department of Nuclear Engineering at the University of Tennessee, Knoxville (UTK) is seeking applications to fill a non-tenure track Lecturer or Teaching Assistant Professor starting on or around August 1, 2027. Exceptionally qualified individuals with very significant teaching experience may be considered at higher ranks. Duties will include teaching undergraduate, and possibly graduate, courses in nuclear engineering-related subjects, and providing service to the department, college, university, and community. The UT Department of Nuclear Engineering is the largest Nuclear Engineering PhD program in the United States, with a diverse research portfolio and close collaborations with Idaho National Laboratory (INL), Oak Ridge National Laboratory (ORNL), the Y-12 National Security Complex, and more than 150 nuclear-related companies within 50 miles of the campus. The department is housed in the state-of-the-art Zeanah Engineering Complex, home to more than 20 nuclear engineering laboratories and computational resources. The department is committed to cultivating work/life balance and a family-friendly environment for faculty, staff, and students. The Tickle College of Engineering (TCE), comprised of nine departments that house 213 tenure-track and 80+ non-tenure-track faculty, is in the midst of unprecedented growth under Chancellor Donde Plowman and Dean Matthew Mench, having added 30+ new faculty over the past 4 years and set records in research expenditures ($113.6M in FY24), enrollment (4,800 undergraduate and 1,500 graduate students), and increased ranking (now 28th among public universities) in the US News and World Report list of top engineering colleges. Key partnerships with Oak Ridge National Laboratory and Consolidated Nuclear Security (Y-12) and growth are supported by new facilities including the Zeanah Engineering Complex and the Innovation South building. Knoxville is a vibrant city with a beautiful, walkable downtown, active neighborhoods, a lively nightlife with numerous theaters and museums, a rich live music scene, and eclectic dining. Nestled in the foothills of the Great Smoky Mountains and surrounded by lakes and the Tennessee River, Knoxville offers outstanding access to outdoor recreation and is within 200 miles of four major metropolitan areas. Applicants must have a doctorate in Nuclear Engineering or a closely related field for appointment as a Teaching Assistant Professor or a Masters in Nuclear Engineering or a closely related field for appointment as a Lecturer by the effective date of appointment. Applicants should be able to contribute to existing courses in nuclear engineering, as well as to develop new avenues of teaching. The focus area for this search is inclusive to the department’s curriculum tracks in nuclear fission engineering and radiological engineering. Relevant teaching focus includes foundational nuclear engineering and/or the areas of nuclear power in addition to radiological engineering, including radiation interactions with matter, radiation detection, health physics, and nuclear chemistry. Teaching contributions may also include courses that are part of the Nuclear Power Engineering minor, or other academic minors, within the Department of Nuclear Engineering. Preference will be given to candidates who have demonstrated teaching success that complements existing University of Tennessee strengths and with the vision to develop collaborative teaching activities. Applications must be submitted electronically via Interfolio. A complete application should include: 1. A curriculum vitae 2. A statement of teaching interests and philosophy 3. A cover letter articulating the applicant's interest in and qualifications for the position 4. The names and contact information of three to five professional references Applications will be reviewed continuously, with priority to those received by 20 December 2026. Questions may be directed to Dr. Eric Lukosi (search committee chair) at elukosi@utk.edu."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195873": {
      "title": "Dean-Winston College of Law",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: Winston College of Law Location: Knoxville, TN  Posted: Oct 20, 2026 The University of Tennessee, Knoxville, has engaged WittKieffer to manage this search. Nominations and inquires can be directed to Werner Boel, LL.M, Ashlee Musser at UTKlawdean@wittkieffer.com . For fullest consideration, candidate materials should be received by December 7, 2026 . To review the full prospectus and a list of required materials, please visit: Dean, Winston College of Law | WittKieffer Nominations and inquires can be directed to Werner Boel, LL.M, Ashlee Musser at UTKlawdean@wittkieffer.com . For fullest consideration, candidate materials should be received by December 7, 2026 . To review the full prospectus and a list of required materials, please visit: Dean, Winston College of Law | WittKieffer"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/196218": {
      "title": "Research Assistant Professor for Advanced Construction Materials and Digital Construction",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: UT-ORII Location: Knoxville, TN  Posted: Oct 22, 2026 The University of Tennessee, Knoxville, invites applications for a Research Assistant Professor contributing to the Buildings Convergent Research Initiative (CRI) of the University of Tennessee–Oak Ridge Innovation Institute (UT–ORII). The position will advance construction materials and manufacturing approaches that support affordable, energy-efficient, resilient, and adaptable buildings. We seek an emerging scholar with expertise in concrete, cementitious systems, or other performative construction materials, and research interests connecting materials to digital construction and the adaptive built environment. Connections with artificial intelligence (AI), machine learning, and advanced computation are encouraged. Experimental, computational, and integrated research approaches are welcome. Research Emphasis Candidates should demonstrate depth in one or more of the following connected areas and a clear vision for contributing to the Buildings CRI. Advanced and performative construction materials. Concrete, alternative binders, composites, and multifunctional materials with engineered mechanical, thermal, environmental, or responsive behavior. Relevant topics include composition–processing–structure–property relationships, durability, resource efficiency, and performance over the material life cycle. Digital construction and advanced manufacturing. Concrete 3D printing, robotic fabrication, and automated construction, including printable mixture design, rheology, buildability, interlayer behavior, process monitoring, and relationships between fabrication parameters and component performance. Adaptive built environments. Materials, components, and building envelopes that respond to changing environmental conditions or operational needs. Examples include thermal buffering, integrated sensing, self-healing or other responsive functions, and designs that improve adaptability, energy performance, and resilience. Connections with AI and computation. AI-assisted materials discovery, inverse design, process optimization, computer vision, digital twins, or physics-informed modeling that connects material behavior, construction processes, and building performance. Research Environment The Buildings CRI brings together UT and Oak Ridge National Laboratory (ORNL) expertise in advanced materials, robotics, automation, and construction processes. The successful candidate will collaborate across disciplines and engage with university, national laboratory, and industry partners to connect fundamental research with scalable construction and building applications. Learn more: UT–ORII Buildings CRI Responsibilities Develop an original research direction aligned with the Buildings CRI and contribute to collaborative projects spanning materials, fabrication, and building performance. Lead research tasks from study design and execution through analysis, publication, and dissemination; deliver high-quality results and meet project milestones. Prepare and contribute to competitive proposals for external research funding in collaboration with UT, ORNL, and other partners. Mentor graduate and undergraduate researchers, contribute to team development, and promote rigorous, reproducible, and safe research practices. Build collaborations that support prototype development, validation, technology transfer, and translation of research findings into practical construction applications. Required Qualifications An earned PhD by the appointment start date in civil and environmental engineering, materials science and engineering, or a closely related field. Demonstrated research expertise relevant to construction materials, digital construction, or adaptive building technologies, supported by scholarly accomplishments appropriate to career stage. Evidence of potential to develop an impactful research program and contribute to externally funded, interdisciplinary research. Strong written and oral communication skills and the ability to work effectively with researchers from different disciplines. Preferred Qualifications Experience connecting material formulation, characterization, or modeling with manufacturing processes and component or building performance. Experience in concrete 3D printing, robotic fabrication, multifunctional materials, or adaptive building systems. Experience applying AI, machine learning, or advanced computational methods, or a compelling plan to establish these connections through collaboration. Contributions to proposal development, student mentorship, collaborative research, or partnerships with industry or national laboratories. Additional Information This is a 12-month, non-tenure track position. The appointment will be with the University of Tennessee, Knoxville. The primary work location for this position is on the University of Tennessee campus in Knoxville and may include visits to collaborator sites on the campuses of ORNL and UTK. The position will be implemented as an initial three-year appointment with full salary support for the first year, followed by a gradual increase in expected external salary funding in subsequent years. The applicant will be expected to recruit graduate students by year three. A competitive startup package including funds for graduate students and equipment will be offered. Salary and title/rank will be determined commensurate with experience. Benefits will be typical of exempt employees at the University of Tennessee. The candidate must be eligible for initial clearance at Oak Ridge National Laboratory. Application materials must be submitted to Interfolio at: https://apply.interfolio.com/196218 Cover letter describing their interest and fit A curriculum vitae A research statement outlining accomplishments and proposed contributions to the Buildings CRI Contact information for three professional references. Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis beginning immediately and will continue until the position is filled. Anticipated start date is January 2027. All further inquiries should be sent to Dr. Nick Zhou and Dr. Mingzhou Jin . More About UT-ORII The University of Tennessee-Oak Ridge Innovation Institute was launched by the University of Tennessee and Oak Ridge National Laboratory in 2021, in response to America’s need for a stronger pool of science, technology, engineering and mathematics, and increasingly life science talent. UT-ORII is leveraging UT and ORNL’s best capabilities and resources to accelerate collaborative discovery, innovation, and interdisciplinary graduate education; and to prepare the next generation of talent in areas of critical importance to the nation and the State of Tennessee. The institute’s current research areas include Clean Manufacturing and Advanced Materials, Energy Storage and Transportation, Radiopharmaceutical Therapies, Circular Bioeconomy Systems, and Advanced Manufacturing for Affordable Building Construction. To learn more about UT-ORII visit utorii.com . More About UT Knoxville UT Knoxville, which includes the UT Space Institute and the UT Institute of Agriculture, serves the state by educating its citizens, enhancing its culture, and making a difference in people’s lives through research and service. It embodies excellence in teaching, research, scholarship, creative activity, outreach, and engagement. To learn more about UT Knoxville visit UTK.edu . More About ORNL Leveraging the talents of its world-class researchers and exceptional support staff, Oak Ridge National Laboratory is helping solve critical scientific and technical challenges and, in so doing, creating economic opportunity for the nation. ORNL provides access to unique facilities, laboratories, and equipment, drawing thousands of visiting researchers each year. To learn more about ORNL visit https://www.ornl.gov ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/196224": {
      "title": "Design and Modeling of Plasma Facing Component Armor in Fusion Reactors - Research Assistant/Associate Professor",
      "description": "Department: The University of Tennessee - Knoxville: Knoxville Academic Units: UT-ORII Location: Knoxville, TN Posted: Oct 22, 2026 The University of Tennessee invites applications for a Research Assistant/Associate Professor position focused on integrated thermal–mechanical modeling of plasma–material interactions in fusion reactors. We seek candidates with expertise in micromechanics and/or nonlinear finite element modeling, a strong foundation in physics and continuum mechanics, and demonstrated expertise in advanced scientific code development and/or uncertainty quantification and propagation . Working with the University of Tennessee–Oak Ridge Innovation Institute (UT-ORII), the successful candidate will develop predictive multiphysics models to assess and guide the design of plasma-facing materials and components. The research will address the coupled thermal and mechanical response of plasma-facing components (PFCs) in magnetic fusion energy systems, with particular emphasis on the evolution of material properties and component performance under extreme operating conditions. These components experience high-energy particle irradiation, cyclic thermal and mechanical loading, and repeated bombardment by relatively low-energy light ions. Together, these processes alter surface morphology, thermophysical properties, and mechanical behavior, with consequences for component integrity and lifetime. Research priorities include developing micromechanical and nonlinear finite element models of deformation, damage, and failure under coupled irradiation, creep, and fatigue; developing and coupling computational models of plasma exposure, material evolution, and component response; and quantifying how uncertainties in material properties, loading conditions, and model parameters propagate through coupled simulations to affect predictions of performance and lifetime. The work will involve scientific software development, numerical algorithm development, and the integration of plasma and materials modeling codes into computational environments such as the Integrated Plasma Simulator (IPS) framework. The successful candidate will collaborate with faculty, postdoctoral researchers, and students in Nuclear Engineering, Mechanical Engineering, Materials Science and Engineering, and Physics, as well as scientists at Oak Ridge National Laboratory (ORNL). Required Qualifications: PhD in Nuclear Engineering, Mechanical Engineering, Civil Structural Engineering, Physics, Materials Science, Chemistry, Applied Mathematics or other discipline related to development of Materials for Extreme Environments. Significant research experience following the doctoral degree is desired, with a documented publication record. Demonstrated experience with code development for the mechanical response of materials or of components. Experience in statistical science/uncertainty quantification is beneficial Experience with obtaining funding and student mentoring is beneficial. Candidates with experience in more than one discipline, or interest in collaboration across these disciplines, are preferred. However, candidates who can define a unique area are also encouraged. Demonstrated Reserach Skills: Strong communication skills and a demonstrated ability to work in a collaborative team environment spanning experiments and modeling. A clear path to independence as a researcher is desired, although collaboration with more senior investigators will be expected. This position requires the ability to be granted a site-access badge for the Oak Ridge National Laboratory. Preferred Requirements: Since the investigator may participate in providing some level of instruction in accord with the UT-ORII mission, some formal teaching experience is desirable. A commitment to mentoring junior investigators who will work with the Research Faculty is important and, if available, demonstrated experience is a plus. Additional Information This is a 12-month, non-tenure track position. The appointment will be with the University of Tennessee, Knoxville. The primary work location for this position is on the University of Tennessee campus in Knoxville and may include visits to collaborator sites on the campuses of ORNL and UTK. The position will be implemented as an initial three-year contract with full salary support for the first year, followed by a gradual increase in expected external salary funding in subsequent years. The applicant will be expected to recruit graduate students by year three. A competitive startup package including funds for graduate students and equipment will be offered. Salary and title/rank will be determined commensurate with experience. Benefits will be typical of exempt employees at the University of Tennessee. The candidate must be eligible for initial clearance at Oak Ridge National Laboratory. Interested applicants should send inquiries to the Search Chair, Prof. Brian Wirth (Governor’s Chair Professor in Computational Nuclear Engineering) at bdwirth@utk.edu. All application material should be submitted in Interfolio at apply.interfolio.com/XXXXX. Applications must include: Letter of interest Comprehensive curriculum vitae Research plan Three professional references including names, addresses, and telephone numbers More About UT-ORII The University of Tennessee-Oak Ridge Innovation Institute (UT-ORII) was launched by the University of Tennessee and Oak Ridge National Laboratory in 2021, in response to  America’s need for a stronger pool of science, technology, engineering and mathematics (STEM) talent. UT-ORII is leveraging its joint UT-ORNL resources to foster an earlier interest in STEM in Tennessee schools, grow its interdisciplinary graduate programs, and accelerate collaborative discovery and innovation in convergent research initiatives of critical importance to the nation and to Tennessee. Learn more about the Fusion Technology and Materials for Extreme Environments Convergent Reserach Initaitive. More About UT Knoxville UT Knoxville, which includes the UT Space Institute and the UT Institute of Agriculture, serves the state by educating its citizens, enhancing its culture, and making a difference in people’s lives through research and service. It embodies excellence in teaching, research, scholarship, creative activity, outreach, and engagement. To learn more about UT Knoxville visit UTK.edu . More About ORNL Leveraging the talents of its world-class researchers and exceptional support staff, Oak Ridge National Laboratory is helping solve critical scientific and technical challenges and, in so doing, creating economic opportunity for the nation. ORNL provides access to unique facilities, laboratories, and equipment, drawing thousands of visiting researchers each year. To learn more about ORNL visit https://www.ornl.gov ."
    }
  },
